shift.gg</a> session), did not read as much as I wanted to, but I did try.</p><p>I am still trying.</p><p>From 23 till now, I have worked on different things, passed out on three occasions, and laughed in the face of death like Puss in Boots.</p><p>There’s a lot to look forward to. Not looking forward to social engineering as a recipient, but I am looking forward to learning how these guys use it to hack protocols. I am also looking forward to seeing how everyone turns out in the future, especially Tonbie, Tonye, and Wisdom - They will all grow. Some of them will never leave the walls of Bayelsa, some will, and it’s all a function of design.</p><hr><p>What I know for certain is that there is someone I live for, and I intend to live for Him fully - not partially, not when it’s convenient.</p><p>That’s what 24 looks like from the inside.</p><p>Now you be the judge — <em>“Remember who you are, mate”</em>, or <em>“Hogwarts, it’s Hammertime”</em>.</p>', 'https://picsum.photos/seed/hammertime-at-24-fojw/800/500', 'opinion', '{}', '7053023f-1869-4969-921b-0a6887c6c9c6', TRUE, FALSE, 9, 7, 1, 0, '2026-07-14T20:11:22.505Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:18.905Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('6d2387ab-26b1-438a-a096-da6ee15bc168', 'Copy of The Girl Who Kept Going', 'the-girl-who-kept-going-k365-copy-445', 'There was a girl who kept telling herself, “I’ll try again tomorrow.”Not because she was lazy.Not because she didn’t have dreams.She was simply tired.Tired of s…', '<p>There was a girl who kept telling herself, “I’ll try again tomorrow.”</p><p>Not because she was lazy.</p><p>Not because she didn’t have dreams.</p><p>She was simply tired.</p><p>Tired of starting things and wondering if they would work out. Tired of watching other people move forward while she felt like she was standing in the same place. Tired of pretending she had everything figured out when, deep down, she was still trying to find her way.</p><p>But there was something she refused to do.</p><p>Give up.</p><p>Some mornings, she woke up with confidence. Other mornings, she woke up with questions.</p><p>Am I good enough?</p><p>Will things ever change?</p><p>What if I fail again?</p><p>Still, she got up.</p><p>She learned something new.</p><p>She wrote another page.</p><p>She applied for another opportunity.</p><p>She tried again.</p><p>And slowly, she began to understand something important:</p><p>Progress doesn''t always look like success.</p><p>Sometimes, progress is simply refusing to stop.</p><p>The girl didn''t know exactly where her journey would take her. She didn''t have all the answers. She didn''t even know how long it would take.</p><p>But she knew she wanted a different life.</p><p>So she kept going.</p><p>One word.</p><p>One lesson.</p><p>One opportunity.</p><p>One day at a time.</p><p>Maybe that''s what courage really is not being certain that everything will work out, but choosing to continue even when you''re not sure.</p><p>And perhaps, somewhere along the way, the girl will look back and realize that all those small steps she almost dismissed were actually taking her somewhere.</p><p>She just had to keep going.</p><p>Winny ✍🏽</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789156264/echo-gist/62580ebc-d0a6-462b-b0a2-612472fca3e2/lrboajzdclf1sej9miov.png', 'technology', '{}', '62580ebc-d0a6-462b-b0a2-612472fca3e2', FALSE, FALSE, 1, 0, 0, 0, '2026-09-18T10:58:26.083Z', '2026-09-18T10:58:26.083Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('30f4c8a3-b346-4ee8-a142-91ffd150d052', 'Nigeria’s Tech Growth in 2026​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​', 'nigerias-tech-growth-in-2026-khao', 'Nigeria’s digital economy is no longer just expanding rapidly it’s becoming more structured. Fresh legislation, a flagship regional tech expo, and advancing AI …', '<p></p><p>Nigeria’s digital economy is no longer just expanding rapidly it’s becoming more structured. Fresh legislation, a flagship regional tech expo, and advancing AI oversight are helping the country cement its role as the digital hub of West Africa.</p><p><strong>Digital Economy Projected to Reach $18.3 Billion</strong><br>Industry forecasts put Nigeria’s digital economy revenue at $18.3 billion this year, fueled by rising AI use, a more mature fintech landscape, growing cloud services, and broader 5G and broadband coverage. Experts view 2026 as a pivotal year: the focus is moving from rapid but unstructured expansion toward steadier, higher-value innovation, backed by stronger legal foundations for the sector.</p><p>A key piece of that foundation is the National Digital Economy and E-Governance Bill, which is expected to become law this year. Once enacted, it would elevate the National Information Technology Development Agency (NITDA) to a central “super-regulator” role empowering it to categorize AI systems by risk level, mandate algorithmic transparency, and certify AI auditors. This would complement the work of the Nigeria Data Protection Commission, creating a dual regulatory layer for technology.</p><p>Telecoms already accounted for more than 9% of real GDP last year, a share likely to rise further as fibre to the home networks expand and mobile access improves. Internet penetration is forecast to approach 60% this year, largely thanks to Nigeria’s mobile-first user base.</p><p><strong>GITEX Nigeria 2026 Heads to Abuja and Lagos</strong><br>West Africa’s premier tech, AI and startup gathering, GITEX Nigeria, takes place from 31 August to 3 September across Abuja and Lagos, with the backing of President Bola Ahmed Tinubu. Organizers describe the event as evidence that Nigeria and the wider region are prepared to convert innovation into tangible economic gains, creating platforms for partnerships, investment and collaboration across the digital economy.</p><p>Communications, Innovation and Digital Economy Minister Bosun Tijani emphasizes that the ambition goes beyond importing technology: Nigeria aims to develop a digital economy in which Africa creates AI and digital solutions rather than merely using them.</p><p><strong>Africa’s Frontrunner in Responsible AI</strong><br>Recent assessments show Nigeria leading African countries in responsible AI governance. Policy frameworks, however, are only the starting point. The greater task is expanding real-world AI adoption across sectors while strengthening infrastructure, skills and regulatory systems. Nations that convert solid governance into measurable productivity and broad-based growth will capture the greatest long-term benefits from AI.</p><p><strong>Growing Regional Ties</strong><br>Nigeria and Ghana are examining joint digital innovation projects, with the National Agency for Science and Engineering Infrastructure (NASENI) central to Nigeria’s industrial shift. Information Minister Mohammed Idris notes that initiatives under the Renewed Hope Agenda are delivering concrete results jobs, projects and innovation in areas such as local manufacturing, renewable energy, agri-tech and digital skills training.</p><p><strong>The Bottom Line</strong><br>Nigerian tech in 2026 is less about isolated headline launches and more about infrastructure finally matching ambition. Sharper regulation, leading AI governance, expanding telecoms and broadband, plus a major regional showcase in Lagos and Abuja, are all aligning. Should the digital economy bill pass and GITEX meet expectations, 2026 may be the year Nigeria moves from “emerging” tech market to established player.</p><p> And Ecogist is also one of the space making wave and you can be a member of this growing community. Register with us today for free, turn on our notifications on your mail and get all updated tech news from here. </p>', NULL, 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 10, 2, 0, '2026-08-06T17:28:49.208Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:07.277Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('cdd073e5-62fb-4076-b5e8-a7db349bc823', 'You’re 3 States Away and Your Dad Is in Front of Your Hostel? (Part 2)', 'youre-3-states-away-and-your-dad-is-in-front-of-your-hostel-part-2-efbb', 'The lie got bigger. Then I opened his door.', '<p>I tried to calm myself down by thinking of my boyfriend. I pictured the cute reaction—the hugging, the emotional moment, the “<em>I can’t believe you are here</em>”. You know that moment you’re day dreaming and smiling to yourself like a fool only to look beside you and realize someone has been looking at you? Yeahhhh, I quickly adjusted myself and continued being optimistic. No one will spoil my joyyy….lolll🤧🙂</p><p>When I got to his city, I carried my bag like someone going to her husband''s house. I ordered a ride straight to his place. My stomach was doing excitement, not knowing it should have been doing warning.</p><p>I reached his door and didn''t knock. I wanted to set up my phone for the surprise video. I positioned the camera small, patted my face. I rehearsed my smile.</p><p><em>I wish someone slapped me at this point. Like one hot slap instantly because...Kai... God Ahbeg🥲</em></p><p>I opened the door—thankfully, it was unlocked. And I didn''t see my boyfriend initially sef.</p><p>Like "Everywhere first blurrrrr."</p><p>Then I looked again</p><p>The first thing I saw...</p><p>Legs...</p><p>Legs in the air....</p><p>Legs doing activities my own legs had never done. Ehnnnn???</p><p>I just stood still. I was trying to process what I am seeing. I mean, I’ve got eye issues so that might not be my boyfriend, right? —<em>first stage: denial.</em></p><p>I dropped my bag and I just stood like that. My boyfriend turned, saw me and shouted like he just saw a ghost.</p><p>The girl jumped up and behaved like I was the intruder. She said, "Can''t you knock?" I said, "On my boyfriend''s door?" She said, "YOUR what?"</p><p><em>Oh the pain I was feeling was indescribable.</em></p><p>My boyfriend was begging, kneeling, trying to touch me. He kept saying the girl meant nothing. But the girl was shouting that she was his real girlfriend and I was the side chick.</p><p>I just wanted to leave there but this goat was still holding me down, begging.</p><p>My dad’s message came in to my phone saying he’s sending my uncle to the hotel we are “staying” and that he will be around for the competition to cheer me on.&nbsp; All the color drained from my body at that moment. I wasn’t even in Lagos State to start with, I just lied that I was there🤧😭.</p><p><strong><em>Can you see how complicated it is now?Omooo God punish you Olaaaa😭.</em></strong></p><p>I managed to free myself from the stupid boy and I stepped outside. Then, it started raining heavily. My boyfriend followed me, still trying to beg, while my dad kept texting that he was sending my uncle to verify my location.</p><p>My life was crashing before my eyes. In HD. In 4k. I was whirling in disgrace. My uncle is on his way to a hotel I was never even in.</p><p></p><p>What will you do if you were me???🥹 I need a story asapppp.</p><p></p><p>To Be Continued.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788529137/echo-gist/a16fce57-7b1d-41de-934e-3b6d207eac01/jw1gd0igjebqelesdouq.jpg', 'lifestyle', '{"storytime","relationshipdrama","funnystories","universitylife","lifestyle"}', 'a16fce57-7b1d-41de-934e-3b6d207eac01', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 22, 3, 1, '2026-09-04T13:45:53.922Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:45.632Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('0ec44aad-fc4d-46a5-adeb-4b42ce3d7d2b', 'Equality? That''s a false narrative', 'equality-thats-a-false-narrative--3a22', 'Would  a father who killed his children and claimed insanity get this kind of sympathy?    Well, i hate to burst your bubble but the answer is No. Take the case…', '<p><strong>Would  a father who killed his children and claimed insanity get this kind of sympathy?  </strong></p><p><strong>  Well, i hate to burst your bubble but the answer is No. Take the case of  Timothy Ray Jones Jr as a reference.  The contrast with Lindsay Clancy is not subtle. Both parents admitted causing the deaths of their young children and advanced mental-illness defenses. The public and media responses have been strikingly different<br></strong></p><p><strong>Timothy Ray Jones Jr., a divorced software engineer and primary custodial parent, killed his five children on August 28, 2014, in their Lexington County, South Carolina, home. After one of the children died during harsh physical punishment for breaking electrical outlets, Jones strangled the other four (two with his hands, two with a belt). He then drove their bodies around the Southeast for eight or nine days before dumping them in garbage bags on a rural Alabama road. He used synthetic marijuana. His defense pleaded not guilty by reason of insanity, citing family history of schizophrenia, possible brain damage from a teenage car accident, stress as a single father, religious turmoil, and voices, including a “demonic gremlin.”</strong></p><p><strong> A court-appointed psychiatrist diagnosed substance-induced psychosis from Spice, concluded Jones knew right from wrong, and suggested he may have exaggerated symptoms to live with what he had done. A jury rejected the insanity claim in 2019, convicted him of five murders, and recommended death. The South Carolina Supreme Court upheld the conviction and sentence in 2023. There were no pink-shirt vigils, no mass identification campaigns, and no comparable fundraising wave. Coverage treated the crime as “obscene” and “outrageous.” Jurors later described lasting trauma. Jones’s own father expressed personal guilt over not intervening sooner, but the dominant public frame was of a father who panicked after one death and then methodically finished the rest. <br></strong></p><p><strong>Cultural scripts still treat maternal filicide as a possible medical catastrophe and paternal filicide as a failure of character or simple evil. Andrea Yates, who drowned her five children in 2001 and was eventually found not guilty by reason of insanity, is frequently compared to Clancy; Jones is not. That difference in narrative power, not just the facts of each crime, explains why one parent’s insanity claim generated identification and the other’s generated a death sentence. The dead children in both families received no second chance to benefit from either story.<br></strong></p><p></p><p><strong>It''s such a crazy world we live in.  I hear a mistrial has been declared already. Such a shitty society. </strong></p><p></p><p> 3enchnad</p><p></p><p><strong><br></strong></p><p><strong><br><br></strong></p><p></p><p><strong><br></strong></p><p><strong><br></strong></p><p></p>', NULL, 'culture', '{}', '173ebb0c-2ac0-4f1b-ba8e-16b7b14dac7d', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 4, 3, 0, '2026-09-04T17:35:41.742Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:45.413Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('9f1a6512-d222-460f-aa12-749b5afd6eec', 'The Night We Played Truth or Dare', 'the-night-we-played-truth-or-dare-5f6j', 'What started as a game slowly became something else', '<p> It was a few minutes past 10 p.m. My eyes were still wide open despite having a stressful day in school. I''d finished all my chores so I could use my mum''s phone without interruption.</p><p>I got chatting with my stranger friend, and everything was going smoothly until he asked if we could play Truth or Dare. I agreed because I''d always heard my friends talk about their funny dare experiences.</p><p>After all, I wanted the experience too.</p><p>We started with truth. The questions were harmless.</p><p>"What''s your favourite colour?"</p><p>"Who''s your celebrity crush?"</p><p>"Have you ever had a boyfriend?"</p><p>We laughed through every answer, and somehow it made us even more comfortable with each other. Then we switched to dares.</p><p>"Change your profile picture for five minutes."</p><p>"Send a random emoji."</p><p>Easy enough.</p><p>But as the game went on, the dares became more personal. He asked me to send a picture. At first, it sounded innocent, so I did. Then another. Then another. Before I knew it, the requests became uncomfortable. He kept saying things like, "Come on, it''s just a game," or, "Don''t you trust me?"</p><p>I remember staring at my screen, wondering why I suddenly felt uneasy. I didn''t want to seem rude or boring, but deep down I knew I didn''t want to do what he was asking.</p><p>That was the first time I realised that people don''t always cross your boundaries all at once. Sometimes they do it little by little until saying "no" feels harder than saying "yes."</p><p>I eventually refused, and the atmosphere changed immediately. The friendly guy I''d been talking to disappeared. He became cold, dismissive, and eventually stopped talking to me altogether.</p><p>For a while, I blamed myself. I wondered if I''d overreacted or ruined the friendship. But with time, I realised something important.</p><p>A genuine friend will never make you feel guilty for protecting your boundaries. Anyone who walks away because you said "no" wasn''t staying because they valued you, they were staying because they hoped to get something from you.</p><p>Looking back now, I''m actually grateful that happened. It taught me that not everyone who is kind to you has good intentions. Some people earn your trust first before testing your limits.</p><p>That stranger didn''t just become my friend. He became one of the biggest lessons of my life.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1786554668/echo-gist/ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731/u5bdak8nysgarpsolf3v.png', 'technology', '{}', 'ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731', FALSE, FALSE, 2, 36, 0, 0, '2026-08-12T17:18:51.769Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:00.744Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('82028da2-b199-4c99-b4b9-d3259be01951', 'Everyone has an excuse.', 'everyone-has-an-excuse-epev', 'People create justifications that let their side off the hook. We do it constantly. Once we pick a team mothers, mental-health advocates, “tough on crime” types…', '<p>People create justifications that let their side off the hook. We do it constantly. Once we pick a team mothers, mental-health advocates, “tough on crime” types, whoever we start collecting reasons why our people aren’t really at fault. The facts get rearranged so the people we identify with come out looking more sympathetic, more victimized, or more reasonable than they actually were. The Lindsay Clancy case is a brutal example of this habit in action.</p><p></p><p>On January 24, 2023, Lindsay Clancy strangled her three children  with exercise bands in the basement of their house in Duxbury, Massachusetts. Then she cut her wrists and neck and jumped out a second-story window. She survived, paralyzed from the waist down. She has never denied doing it. Her defense is that she was in the grip of postpartum psychosis and should not be held criminally responsible. Prosecutors say she planned it: she sent her husband out for takeout and medicine so she would have the house to herself, then acted methodically. The trial has stretched on for weeks. As of early September 2026 the jury is still deliberating and has already told the judge they are deadlocked.</p><p></p><p>That is the core of what happened. Everything after that is people choosing sides and then inventing stories that make their side look better.</p><p></p><p>One large group of people mostly other mothers, mental-health advocates, and a surprising number of conservative women treat Clancy as the real victim. They point to the months she spent seeking help, the revolving door of doctors, the long list of medications, the hospital stay that ended only weeks before the killings. They say the system failed her. They wear pink T-shirts outside the courthouse. Online they post “I am Lindsay” or “same, Lindsay” next to pictures of their own kids. The implication is clear, any of us could have snapped if the doctors had treated us this badly. The justification lets an entire category of people off the hook. Motherhood is hard, the medical system is broken, society doesn’t support women therefore the woman who actually killed three children is not the main problem. The problem is everyone else.</p><p></p><p>The other side does the same thing in the opposite direction. They focus on the planning. She texted her husband to leave. She chose a quiet moment. She used objects already in the house. She told the children “go to God, baby” while she did it. To this group, postpartum psychosis is either rare enough to be almost irrelevant or being stretched into an all-purpose excuse. Some commentators went further and suggested a lot of postpartum talk is just selfishness women who don’t want to sacrifice their old lives. That version lets a different tribe off the hook: the people who believe in personal responsibility, who think “mental illness” has become a get-out-of-jail-free card, who are tired of seeing children’s deaths treated as a talking point about healthcare. If you start from that worldview, Clancy is simply a murderer who happened to have a convenient diagnosis.</p><p></p><p>Both versions require ignoring pieces of the story. The first side downplays how organized the afternoon looked. The second side downplays how long Clancy had been describing intrusive thoughts, insomnia, and a sense that she was losing control and how many professionals saw her and still sent her home. Neither side likes holding both ideas at once: that she was severely ill and that three children are still dead because of what she did.</p><p></p><p>You can see the same pattern in smaller ways. Some people decided the husband must have done it, even though Clancy has admitted the acts and the 911 call is public. Others decided the doctors should be in prison instead of her. Each theory exists because it protects a preferred narrative. If the husband is the villain, then the story becomes one about men who don’t help enough. If the doctors are the villains, then the story becomes one about a broken medical system. The actual sequence of events is less convenient for either team.</p><p></p><p>This is not unique to this case. People have always done it. We just have more platforms now, so the justifications get louder and more detailed. The Clancy trial happens to be a clean illustration because almost nothing about the basic facts is in dispute. She killed the children. She tried to kill herself. She had been unwell for months. The argument is only about what those facts mean and meaning is where the justifications start.</p><p></p><p>The uncomfortable part is that most of us would do the same thing if the person involved belonged to our group. We would look for the details that make our side look less responsible and the other side look more so. We would call it “context” or “nuance.” It is just ordinary tribal thinking with better vocabulary. Three children are still gone either way. The justifications do not change that. They only make it easier for the rest of us to keep our preferred story intact.</p><p></p><p>Until humans begin to take responsibility of their actions i see no difference between us humans and animals. Yes i said that and that is my own view.</p><p></p><p>                  </p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788277861/echo-gist/173ebb0c-2ac0-4f1b-ba8e-16b7b14dac7d/uzarfeiboxo1gr5tvvim.jpg', 'opinion', '{}', '173ebb0c-2ac0-4f1b-ba8e-16b7b14dac7d', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 38, 8, 4, '2026-09-01T15:56:10.721Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:53.685Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('10904387-f46c-4270-9b26-aa61c3a4163b', 'SWITCH: THE FINAL TRIAL AND ERROR, THEN RESULTS.', 'switch-the-final-trial-and-error-then-results--rvlt', '&nbsp;SWITCH: THE FINAL TRIAL &amp; ERROR, THEN RESULTSI am the best at my craft and I am confident in my skillsMy product is so great and I am sure if people k…', '<p>&nbsp;SWITCH: THE FINAL TRIAL &amp; ERROR, THEN RESULTS</p><ul><li><p>I am the best at my craft and I am confident in my skills</p></li><li><p>My product is so great and I am sure if people know about it they’ll line up.</p></li><li><p>Five posts a week on Linkedin, Instagram, X and Facebook and I should make those sales.</p></li></ul><p>And yet,&nbsp;</p><ul><li><p>No leads</p></li><li><p>No sales</p></li><li><p>Random “congratulations on the new role” on linkedin on a brand/business you just started.</p></li></ul><p>Yeah, this may be you. You need something, and right now you may not really know exactly what it is you need but you just know there’s something missing. Frankly, I am not here to be a Mr. Genie swooshing out of a lamp to give you solutions to your problems but I can hold your hand and we can figure things out together.</p><p>You want visibility, sales, traction/traffic, more likes, more views, growth, better marketing strategies, and to be respected in your industry. You need these;</p><p><br></p><ol><li><p></p></li></ol><h1>Feel it to understand it.</h1><p>It is in frustration, that is where problems are identified. In today’s world, we avoid the things that make us uncomfortable and we seek comfort in quotes about failure and watch videos on how to cope with failure and, here’s the really sad one, “flop proud”.&nbsp; Emotion is a variable that cannot be removed from progress in any form, how you feel doesn’t just portray what you go through in a period of time, it also dictates your next step. It says, “are you just going to fail and start from square one or are you going to break down the information you have from failing and fix the problem?”&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;&nbsp;Disregarding public opinion, the “don’t be too hard on yourself”, YOU should dissect your own actions and strategies and chastise yourself for mistakes, unavoidable or not. That way there’s room for HOW DO I GET THIS RIGHT? In your chain of thoughts.&nbsp;</p><p>You have to think; I am not visible enough, my work/effort are not aligned with my goal, I got too greedy, too hasty, too comfortable, or a hundred and one other issues in order to MAKE IT WORK. A banner and random post/video not clearly telling your story or mission is not enough.</p><p>Let these stay with you for an hour or two and no more. For in that agonizing comes the hard truth that sets you free, and once you are free and see clearly, nothing can stop the next step.</p><p><br></p><ol start="2"><li><p></p></li></ol><h1>Game board it</h1><p>Being a business or brand owner is synonymous to being a chess master, your strategy must be future oriented. You must at all times, use the data in front of you to predict the world around you as well as move strategically, thereby positioning yourself where opportunity meets you.</p><p>This is where you draw a mind and road map based on information retrieved and information acquired, and then you SWOT.&nbsp;</p><p>S- strength (what you currently do very well or excel at)</p><p>W-weaknesses&nbsp; (chinks and loopholes)</p><p>O- opportunities&nbsp; (what you stand to gain)</p><p>T- threats&nbsp; (potential situations that may lead to losses)</p><p>Now, find a way to use your strengths to grab opportunities and curtail threats. And prevent observed threats by plugging weaknesses. The aim is to have at least a semblance of structure and a plan. It is a BEING UNTOP OF IT line of thought. Basically, a new way of thinking/problem solving.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><ol start="3"><li><p></p></li></ol><h1>SYSTEMIZE IT&nbsp;</h1><p>So now you’ve reached the tricky, fun, delicate part. Building a system.&nbsp; Coming up with a stable system with guaranteed results and flexibility capable of accommodating any scenario/situation. This requires patience, and an understanding of yourself and the living entity you’ve built and dubbed “business”.</p><p>Bruce lee was unto something when he said “be like water”. You want whatever you build to adapt to the world around it and the world itself is ever changing. Your system should be so fluid that it doesn’t combat the passing of time or the seasons that are ever in motion, or the rise and fall of a market, but adapt to it.</p><p>You get this by thinking like a mad scientist on a mission to prove the existence of a new phenomenon. Come up with new strategies, test each one, collect data from each outcome, and then use the data and results acquired to build the customized system that works for you in your industry.</p><p><br></p><ol start="4"><li><p></p></li></ol><h1>UTILIZE IT</h1><p>And now you’re finally here. Feel free to call yourself a chef, or artist or any other cool name you find and you think suits you, it probably does. For my analogy, I will go with the “chef”. The art and science of cooking requires utensils and ingredients to prepare the consumable substance we call food.</p><p>&nbsp;Take that logic, and we have; ideas, capital, resources, and reach, and we call them ingredients. And then, with your already established way of solving problems/mode of operation, you have the fire and your system the pot you cook with.&nbsp;</p><p>And now it is as simple as throwing in ingredients to a pot and cooking a hot tasty meal bound to attract the neighbors (customers/clients).&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><br><br></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong><em>“WHEN IT COMES BUSINESS, WHEN IT COMES TO STRATEGY, YOU ARE THE MASTER, THE ARTIST AND THE CREATOR.”</em></strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;WOMO G FRANCIS</em></strong></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788569370/echo-gist/fc3405b9-4f71-4dfc-880e-dfba45c138c7/mzz3cszcjajeetvtpzv3.png', 'career', '{}', 'fc3405b9-4f71-4dfc-880e-dfba45c138c7', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 4, 1, 0, '2026-09-05T00:52:36.401Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:45.163Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('8b43304f-26d6-4f25-9b2b-ba62b9b8d037', 'Broken Mirror, Smile Face 2', 'broken-mirror-smile-face-2-3rlx', 'The house on Douglas Road was the kind of house that had opinions. Adaeze had felt it the first time she walked through its door as a young bride, twenty-four a…', '<p>The house on Douglas Road was the kind of house that had opinions. Adaeze had felt it the first time she walked through its door as a young bride, twenty-four and luminous with the specific hope of a woman who believes she has chosen well — the high ceilings, the dark wood banister worn smooth by other hands, the way the afternoon light came slanting gold through the parlor windows like something out of an old photograph. Chidi had bought it from an aging widower who wept when he handed over the keys, and she had thought, then, that a house loved that much by its previous owner must hold something good inside its walls. Some residue of tenderness in the plaster.</p><p>She had been wrong, or she had been right and the house had simply absorbed too much else in the years since — arguments swallowed whole by the thick old walls, apologies that were never really apologies, silences stretched taut across dinner tables until they hummed. Houses remember. She believed that now, in the way she believed few things anymore, with the flat certainty of someone who has stopped needing anyone else to agree with her.</p><p>In the weeks after Chidi left, the house grew stranger. Not haunted — she didn’t believe in haunted, not in the cinematic sense, not in the sense of things that rattled chains and moved cutlery in the night. But inhabited, somehow, by the residue of who she had been inside it. She would walk past the study and feel, for just a moment, the ghost of her younger self standing in the doorway asking permission to speak. She would pass the broken mirror — she had never had it replaced, had in fact instructed the housekeeper never to touch it — and catch her own face looking back at her from seven different angles, seven different women, none of whom she entirely recognized.</p><p>That was the strangest part. Not the loneliness — she had expected the loneliness, had steeled herself for it the way you steel yourself for cold water, knowing the shock will pass. What she hadn’t expected was the stranger in the glass. Some mornings the woman looking back seemed harder around the eyes than Adaeze remembered being. Some mornings she seemed impossibly young, undone, a girl playing dress-up in her mother’s jewelry. And some mornings — the worst mornings, the ones that left her sitting on the edge of the bathtub with her face in her hands — the woman in the mirror seemed to be waiting. For something. As if the breaking hadn’t been an ending at all, but the opening of a door that had been locked for a very long time.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1784120606/echo-gist/b3132995-e2de-4cc1-9705-e9671bcf3423/s0z4jslhfdvjmpkg7sxo.png', 'opinion', '{}', 'b3132995-e2de-4cc1-9705-e9671bcf3423', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 4, 1, 0, '2026-07-15T13:05:16.829Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:14.434Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('1ec04aa6-ebbd-467b-b099-eee408f5202a', 'Dangote Refinery Goes Public Today: What Nigerians Need to Know About the ₦2.15tn IPO', 'dangote-refinery-ipo-opens-today-as-nigerians-get-chance-to-own-stake-in-africas-j7h7', 'Dangote Refinery IPO Opens Today as Nigerians Get Chance to Own Stake in Africa’s Biggest RefineryThe Dangote Petroleum Refinery and Petrochemicals has opened i…', '<p>Dangote Refinery IPO Opens Today as Nigerians Get Chance to Own Stake in Africa’s Biggest Refinery</p><p></p><p>The Dangote Petroleum Refinery and Petrochemicals has opened its highly anticipated initial public offering (IPO), giving Nigerians and other eligible investors an opportunity to become shareholders in Africa’s largest oil refinery.</p><p></p><p>The public offer opened on Monday, September 14, 2026, as Dangote Group seeks to raise about ₦2.15 trillion from the sale of 4.1 billion shares. The shares are priced at ₦525 each.</p><p></p><p>The refinery, owned by Nigerian billionaire Aliko Dangote, is offering the shares as part of plans to raise additional funds for expansion. The company plans to increase its refining capacity from about 700,000 barrels per day to 1.4 million barrels per day over the coming years.</p><p></p><p>The public offer has been promoted as an “IPO for the People,” with the company seeking to make ownership accessible to ordinary Nigerians and retail investors.</p><p></p><p>Eligible investors can subscribe for as little as 10 shares, costing ₦5,250 at the offer price, although investors are advised to read the offer documents and understand the risks before investing.</p><p></p><p>The refinery, located in the Lekki Free Zone in Lagos, began operations in 2024 and has a processing capacity of about 700,000 barrels of crude oil per day. Its operations have helped Nigeria move from relying heavily on imported refined petroleum products toward becoming an exporter of refined products.</p><p></p><p>The IPO represents a major development for Nigeria’s capital market. If fully subscribed, the ₦2.15 trillion offer would rank among the country''s biggest public share offerings and give retail investors an opportunity to participate in the ownership of a major Nigerian industrial asset.</p><p></p><p>The refinery is also seeking to strengthen its position in the African energy market. Its planned expansion to 1.4 million barrels per day would significantly increase its ability to supply refined petroleum products to Nigeria and other African markets.</p><p></p><p>However, investors should remember that buying shares does not guarantee profit. The value of shares can rise or fall, while dividends are only paid when declared by the company. The official IPO information also warns investors to consider the risks before subscribing.</p><p></p><p>The Dangote Refinery public offer is scheduled to close on October 13, 2026, according to the company''s official IPO information.</p><p></p><p>With strong interest from Nigerian retail investors, the Dangote Refinery IPO is expected to be closely watched as a major test of investor appetite and participation in Nigeria''s capital market.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789407493/echo-gist/c8a6cb2b-83cc-4b91-8bb9-b3e2ec813a12/mttur1krj4iycpyrnwgh.jpg', 'business', '{}', 'c8a6cb2b-83cc-4b91-8bb9-b3e2ec813a12', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 16, 1, 0, '2026-09-14T17:18:27.897Z', '2026-09-19T23:00:09.792Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('f8f5365a-1334-4673-996b-213946ca5388', 'Dancing With The Devil', 'dancing-with-the-devil-ahud', 'Because when you''re desperate enough to take the devil''s hand, you don''t realize you''re walking yourself straight into the fire.', '<p>I was so tired of drowning that I didn''t care who pulled me out.</p><p>The ballroom was quiet; only the melody of a cello playing a tune that felt boring.</p><p>I have lived my entirely life watching other people live, watching doors slam shut in my face, and counting every single money left in my pocket. <strong><em>When the walls are closing in, you stop looking for angels.</em></strong></p><p>The world had made it abundantly clear that it was completely done offering me mercy.</p><p>That’s when he showed up. No horns, no scary monster face, just a warm smile, and an easy way out. He looked like the answer to a prayer whispered at midnight when you''re completely out of options.</p><p>"You look like someone who is tired of losing," he whispered into my ear.</p><p>"I am," I said, my voice shaking.</p><p>"Then let me lead," he said, offering his hand.</p><p>And without thinking, without pausing for even a second to know the cost, or to wonder why an escape route was being handed to me so easily, I placed my palm right into his.</p><p>The moment our fingers locked, the music swelled. He pulled me straight into the center of the floor, and for a brief, blinding second, it felt like true grace. The weight of everything and desperation left my shoulders.</p><p>He spun me effortlessly, catching me every time I stumbled. The floor beneath us turned into clear, fragile glass, revealing a roaring furnace deep down below, but he just smiled at me, his gaze locking into mine. <strong><em>“Don''t look down,”</em> </strong>he said. <strong><em>“Just look at me.”</em></strong></p><p>So I did. I let myself get completely lost in the rhythm. I loved the speed of it, the sudden, rush of power, the dangerous thrill of feeling important to <strong><em>someone</em></strong>, even if that someone was the devil.</p><p>And then, right in the middle of a spin, the music stopped.</p><p>I looked down at our joined hands, his hand split wide open, and underneath wasn''t skin at all; it was monstrous, hot and burning my own hands, eating away at my flesh. The sudden pain didn''t even shock me. What truly broke me, was when I finally stopped spinning and looked around the ballroom.</p><p>I wasn''t a guest at a masquerade ball at all.</p><p>Everyone there were in chains and on their knees, weeping. There were monsters all around them, whipping their backs. They had a mark; some had on their foreheads while some, on their necks. I looked at my hand that was still holding his and noticed the mark was at the back of my right hand.</p><p>I started feeling hot; I was burning up. That was when I realized that the floor underneath our feet was shattering and before long, we went down. I thought I was taking his hand to escape the fire not knowing all along, I was walking deeper into it.</p><p>Then he left my hands and I finally saw the monster that was hiding all along in him. He began to laugh and then before long, I was in chains like the others and on my knees. </p><blockquote><p><strong><em>And just like that, I had created hell for myself.</em></strong></p></blockquote><p></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789479270/echo-gist/7e7eba8c-65af-4b6d-a36c-519340f55539/qkxggaynjrvybmsovvwb.png', 'writing', '{"lifelessons","darkfiction","shortstories","moralchoices","writing"}', '7e7eba8c-65af-4b6d-a36c-519340f55539', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 8, 1, 0, '2026-09-14T19:02:40.896Z', '2026-09-29T10:21:29.417Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('5b66c2db-f7af-4fe9-8490-3de9734dda94', 'How to Manage a High-Performing Remote Team in 2026', 'how-to-manage-a-high-performing-remote-team-in-2026-2h5i', 'Remote and hybrid work are now established ways of working for millions of professionals. Managing people across locations, though, takes more than video calls, project-management software, and a busy…', '<p>Remote and hybrid work are now established ways of working for millions of professionals. Managing people across locations, though, takes more than video calls, project-management software, and a busy Slack channel.</p><p>The real challenge is building a system where people understand their priorities, communicate well, collaborate across time zones, and stay accountable without feeling micromanaged. In 2026, AI adds another factor. It is increasingly used for meeting summaries, reporting, research, and task management, which changes how managers can spend their time. Meanwhile, Gallup data shows that among U.S. employees whose jobs can be done remotely, 52% work hybrid and 26% work fully remotely.</p><p>So how do you build a remote team that actually performs? These eight practices are a good place to start.</p><p>1. Start With Clarity</p><p>Remote teams can''t rely on overheard conversations or a quick word at someone''s desk, so expectations have to be explicit. Every team member should be able to answer six questions:</p><p>What are our priorities?</p><p>What am I responsible for?</p><p>What does success look like?</p><p>Who owns each task?</p><p>When is the work due?</p><p>Where is information stored?</p><p>If people can''t answer these, adding more software won''t help. Clarity comes before technology. When goals, responsibilities, and deadlines are clear, employees can make decisions on their own instead of waiting for a manager to respond, which matters most when your team spans several time zones.</p><p>2. Build a Strong Remote Culture</p><p>Culture doesn''t disappear when people leave the office. It has to be designed more deliberately. Start by agreeing on team norms for:</p><p>Communication channels and etiquette</p><p>Working hours and time-zone overlap</p><p>Expected response times</p><p>What counts as urgent</p><p>Documentation habits</p><p>Then add simple rituals: a weekly team update, a monthly retrospective, and regular recognition of good work. The goal isn''t to force people to socialize online. It''s to create an environment where they feel connected, trusted, and included. Small, consistent gestures, such as publicly crediting a colleague''s contribution, do more for remote culture than an occasional virtual happy hour.</p><p>3. Make Communication "Async First"</p><p>Not everything needs a meeting. A project update that takes two minutes to read shouldn''t cost 30 minutes of everyone''s time.</p><p>For routine updates, use asynchronous communication with a consistent format:</p><p>Project: Website redesign</p><p>Completed: Homepage</p><p>In progress: Mobile version</p><p>Blocked by: Final copy approval</p><p>Next step: Complete testing</p><p>A shared template like this lets colleagues in any time zone catch up quickly and spot problems early. Save live meetings for what they do best: discussion, decision-making, brainstorming, and relationship building. Drawing this line clearly can dramatically reduce unnecessary meetings and gives people longer stretches of uninterrupted time.</p><p>4. Make Meetings Outcome-Driven</p><p>Before scheduling any meeting, ask one question: what needs to be different when this meeting ends? If you can''t answer, you probably don''t need the meeting.</p><p>Every meeting that does go ahead should have:</p><p>A clear purpose</p><p>A shared agenda</p><p>Decisions that need to be made</p><p>Assigned action items</p><p>Deadlines for those actions</p><p>Keep routine meetings short and record important decisions afterward in a place everyone can find. A meeting should produce an outcome, not simply consume time. Circulating notes and owners within a day also gives absent colleagues, often those in other time zones, the same information as everyone else.</p><p>5. Use AI as a Productivity Assistant</p><p>AI shouldn''t replace leadership, but it can remove a significant amount of administrative work. A remote manager can use it to:</p><p>Summarize team updates</p><p>Prepare meeting agendas</p><p>Extract action items from meeting notes</p><p>Draft weekly reports</p><p>Flag overdue tasks</p><p>Organize research</p><p>Create onboarding resources</p><p>Try this workflow. At the end of each week, collect your team''s updates and ask an AI assistant: "Summarize these updates into three key wins, three challenges, unresolved issues, and the top priorities for next week." Instead of reading several separate reports, you get a structured overview that helps you focus on decisions. Review the output before acting on it, as AI summaries can miss context or nuance.</p><p>Microsoft''s Work Trend Index has highlighted the growing role of AI agents and human-AI collaboration at work. The principle for managers is simple: use AI to reduce administrative friction, not to replace human judgment.</p><p>6. Measure Results, Not Online Activity</p><p>One of the biggest mistakes in remote management is confusing being online with being productive. Someone can spend eight hours logged in and accomplish very little, while another person completes their most important work in four focused hours.</p><p>Instead of tracking:</p><p>Online status</p><p>Message counts</p><p>Hours spent in meetings</p><p>Keyboard activity</p><p>focus on outcomes such as:</p><p>Projects completed</p><p>Deadlines met</p><p>Customer satisfaction</p><p>Sales generated</p><p>Response times</p><p>Quality of work</p><p>Progress on strategic milestones</p><p>When expectations are clear, employees need less surveillance. Trust-based management also tends to attract and keep stronger people, because capable professionals rarely thrive under constant monitoring.</p><p>7. Protect People From Digital Overload</p><p>Remote work has a side effect: the workday can start to feel endless. Microsoft''s research has described the "infinite workday," in which employees increasingly check messages and email outside traditional working hours.</p><p>Leaders can push back by:</p><p>Protecting blocks of focus time</p><p>Reducing unnecessary meetings</p><p>Defining what counts as urgent, and how to escalate it</p><p>Encouraging people to disconnect</p><p>Avoiding after-hours messages unless truly necessary</p><p>Managers set the tone here. If you send messages at 10 p.m., people will feel they need to reply at 10 p.m. Productivity isn''t about keeping people busy every minute. It''s about helping them do meaningful work without unnecessary friction, and sustainably enough to keep doing it.</p><p>8. Keep Developing Your Team</p><p>Technology is changing quickly, so employees need ongoing chances to build new skills. The World Economic Forum''s Future of Jobs Report 2025 lists AI and big data, technological literacy, creative thinking, resilience, and leadership among the skills shaping the future of work.</p><p>Build learning into the team''s routine through:</p><p>Short training sessions</p><p>Peer learning and knowledge sharing</p><p>Mentorship</p><p>AI skills training</p><p>Quarterly career conversations</p><p>Remote employees can feel invisible when it comes to growth, so making development an explicit, scheduled part of the job matters. A high-performing team isn''t just productive today. It becomes more capable over time.</p><p>The Remote Team Manager''s Checklist</p><p>Before each week begins, ask yourself:</p><p>Are our priorities clear?</p><p>Does everyone know what they own?</p><p>Are unnecessary meetings being eliminated?</p><p>Are important decisions documented?</p><p>Are we using AI where it genuinely saves time?</p><p>Are we measuring outcomes rather than activity?</p><p>Is the workload sustainable?</p><p>Are people learning and developing?</p><p>If you can answer "yes" consistently, you''re building the foundations of a strong remote team.</p><p>Final Thoughts</p><p>Managing a high-performing remote team in 2026 isn''t about keeping everyone online. It''s about clarity, communication, coordination, capability, and trust. Technology and AI can make the system faster, but leadership still determines how well it works.</p><p>The best remote leaders don''t simply monitor work. They design better ways of working.</p><p>Start small: identify one process that wastes your team''s time, fix it, document it, and then look for a way to automate part of it with AI. Small improvements in how a team works add up to significant gains over time.</p><p>For more practical insights on AI workflows, executive productivity, digital tools, and the future of work, follow Dennis Njoku on Echo Gist.</p><p>Sources</p><ul><li><p>Gallup: Hybrid Work and Remote Work Trends</p></li><li><p>Microsoft: Work Trend Index</p></li><li><p>Microsoft: The "Infinite Workday"</p></li><li><p>World Economic Forum: Future of Jobs Report 2025</p></li></ul><p></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1790149852/echo-gist/20b1d7a8-babb-44f2-9ad0-e99f7b2b4149/enw6asoxrcbhhacvqxt8.png', 'productivity', '{"leadershipmanagement","executiveproductivity","futureofwork","aidigitalworkflows","remotework"}', '20b1d7a8-babb-44f2-9ad0-e99f7b2b4149', TRUE, FALSE, 6, 2, 1, 0, '2026-09-23T07:40:49.256Z', '2026-09-23T21:25:39.447Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('6c987fe3-6b32-4101-b459-94e3220f6429', 'SWITCH', 'switch-n0q0', 'THE FINAL TRIAL AND ERROR, THEN REULTS.', '<p></p><ul><li><p>I am the best at my craft and I am confident in my skills</p></li><li><p>My product is so great and I am sure if people know about it they’ll line up.</p></li><li><p>Five posts a week on Linkedin, Instagram, X and Facebook and I should make those sales.</p></li></ul><p>And yet,&nbsp;</p><ul><li><p>No leads</p></li><li><p>No sales</p></li><li><p>Random “congratulations on the new role” on linkedin on a brand/business you just started.</p></li></ul><p>Yeah, this may be you. You need something, and right now you may not really know exactly what it is you need but you just know there’s something missing. Frankly, I am not here to be a Mr. Genie swooshing out of a lamp to give you solutions to your problems but I can hold your hand and we can figure things out together.</p><p>You want visibility, sales, traction/traffic, more likes, more views, growth, better marketing strategies, and to be respected in your industry. You need these;</p><p><br></p><ol><li><p></p></li></ol><h1>Feel it to understand it.</h1><p>It is in frustration, that is where problems are identified. In today’s world, we avoid the things that make us uncomfortable and we seek comfort in quotes about failure and watch videos on how to cope with failure and, here’s the really sad one, “flop proud”.&nbsp; Emotion is a variable that cannot be removed from progress in any form, how you feel doesn’t just portray what you go through in a period of time, it also dictates your next step. It says, “are you just going to fail and start from square one or are you going to break down the information you have from failing and fix the problem?”&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;&nbsp;Disregarding public opinion, the “don’t be too hard on yourself”, YOU should dissect your own actions and strategies and chastise yourself for mistakes, unavoidable or not. That way there’s room for HOW DO I GET THIS RIGHT? In your chain of thoughts.&nbsp;</p><p>You have to think; I am not visible enough, my work/effort are not aligned with my goal, I got too greedy, too hasty, too comfortable, or a hundred and one other issues in order to MAKE IT WORK. A banner and random post/video not clearly telling your story or mission is not enough.</p><p>Let these stay with you for an hour or two and no more. For in that agonizing comes the hard truth that sets you free, and once you are free and see clearly, nothing can stop the next step.</p><p><br></p><ol start="2"><li><p></p></li></ol><h1>Game board it</h1><p>Being a business or brand owner is synonymous to being a chess master, your strategy must be future oriented. You must at all times, use the data in front of you to predict the world around you as well as move strategically, thereby positioning yourself where opportunity meets you.</p><p>This is where you draw a mind and road map based on information retrieved and information acquired, and then you SWOT.&nbsp;</p><p>S- strength (what you currently do very well or excel at)</p><p>W-weaknesses&nbsp; (chinks and loopholes)</p><p>O- opportunities&nbsp; (what you stand to gain)</p><p>T- threats&nbsp; (potential situations that may lead to losses)</p><p>Now, find a way to use your strengths to grab opportunities and curtail threats. And prevent observed threats by plugging weaknesses. The aim is to have at least a semblance of structure and a plan. It is a BEING UNTOP OF IT line of thought. Basically, a new way of thinking/problem solving.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><ol start="3"><li><p></p></li></ol><h1>SYSTEMIZE IT&nbsp;</h1><p>So now you’ve reached the tricky, fun, delicate part. Building a system.&nbsp; Coming up with a stable system with guaranteed results and flexibility capable of accommodating any scenario/situation. This requires patience, and an understanding of yourself and the living entity you’ve built and dubbed “business”.</p><p>Bruce lee was unto something when he said “be like water”. You want whatever you build to adapt to the world around it and the world itself is ever changing. Your system should be so fluid that it doesn’t combat the passing of time or the seasons that are ever in motion, or the rise and fall of a market, but adapt to it.</p><p>You get this by thinking like a mad scientist on a mission to prove the existence of a new phenomenon. Come up with new strategies, test each one, collect data from each outcome, and then use the data and results acquired to build the customized system that works for you in your industry.</p><p><br></p><ol start="4"><li><p></p></li></ol><h1>UTILIZE IT</h1><p>And now you’re finally here. Feel free to call yourself a chef, or artist or any other cool name you find and you think suits you, it probably does. For my analogy, I will go with the “chef”. The art and science of cooking requires utensils and ingredients to prepare the consumable substance we call food.</p><p>&nbsp;Take that logic, and we have; ideas, capital, resources, and reach, and we call them ingredients. And then, with your already established way of solving problems/mode of operation, you have the fire and your system the pot you cook with.&nbsp;</p><p>And now it is as simple as throwing in ingredients to a pot and cooking a hot tasty meal bound to attract the neighbors (customers/clients).&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><br><br></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong><em>“WHEN IT COMES BUSINESS, WHEN IT COMES TO STRATEGY, YOU ARE THE MASTER, THE ARTIST AND THE CREATOR.”</em></strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;~WOMO G FRANCIS</em></strong></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788569742/echo-gist/fc3405b9-4f71-4dfc-880e-dfba45c138c7/sb3gyftyfrpbkgrj9krx.png', 'career', '{}', 'fc3405b9-4f71-4dfc-880e-dfba45c138c7', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 5, 1, 0, '2026-09-05T00:58:12.866Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:44.839Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('217c3f95-e419-4843-8039-61b5e4f26f7a', 'WE''RE LOSING THE ABILITY TO BE BORED', 'were-losing-the-ability-to-be-bored--9cb6', 'The Elevator Test You Didn''t Know You Were Failing', '<p>You are in a lift for eleven seconds. Phone''s out before the doors even close. You''re standing in a queue, waiting for water to boil, sitting in a parked car waiting for someone and in every single one of those gaps, your hand has already found your phone before your brain even registered there was a gap to fill.</p><p>If you can''t remember the last time you did nothing, on purpose, for more than thirty seconds, this is for you.</p><p><strong>BOREDOM USED TO BE A WAITING ROOM, NOT AN EMERGENCY</strong> </p><p>There was a version of life, not even that long ago, where waiting meant actually waiting, staring at a wall, watching people, thinking about nothing in particular until a thought eventually wandered in. Boredom was uncomfortable, sure, but it was tolerable. It was just a normal texture of being alive. Now boredom registers almost like a malfunction, something to fix immediately, the second it starts. The gap between feeling under-stimulated and reaching for your phone has shrunk to almost nothing. Most people don''t experience boredom anymore. They intercept it.</p><p><strong>WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS IN AN UNFILLED MOMENT </strong></p><p>Here''s what''s easy to forget, boredom was never actually empty. It was your brain''s background processing time, sorting through the day, connecting ideas that had nothing to do with each other, occasionally producing the exact insight or idea you''d been stuck on for days, except it arrives at 2pm in a queue, not at your desk while you are trying to force it. Constant stimulation does not just remove discomfort. It removes the raw material creativity actually runs on, unstructured, undirected thinking time that can''t be scheduled, only allowed to happen.</p><p><strong>WHY THIS IS HARDER TO NOTICE THAN IT SOUNDS </strong></p><p>Nobody feels the absence of an idea they never had. That''s what makes this loss so quiet, you don''t experience "I would have thought of something interesting just now if I hadn''t filled the gap." You just fill the gap, move on, and never know what didn''t get to surface. The cost is invisible precisely because it''s a cost of something that never happened. That''s very different from, say, tiredness, which announces itself. This one doesn''t. It just slowly narrows what your mind produces, without ever telling you it''s happening.</p><p><strong>THE TRUTH YOU NEED TO KNOW </strong></p><p>A lot of what feels like "I''m just not that creative" or "I never have good ideas anymore" is not actually a personality trait. It''s a symptom of never giving your mind the unfilled time it needs to wander somewhere useful. You can''t out scroll your way into insight. The two are, in a fairly direct sense, in competition with each other.</p><p>It''s also worth admitting, reaching for the phone in these gaps is not really about entertainment most of the time. It''s about avoiding the mild discomfort of being alone with your own thinking, even for eleven seconds in a lift. That discomfort used to be tolerable. Now it barely gets the chance to start before it''s interrupted.</p><p><strong>WHY "JUST PUT THE PHONE DOWN" DOESN''T ACTUALLY WORK </strong></p><p>Telling yourself to simply resist reaching for it treats this as a willpower problem, when it''s closer to a withdrawal problem. The urge to fill silence is not really about wanting content. It''s the discomfort of an under-stimulated brain that''s been trained, through years of instant intercepts, to expect input constantly. Removing the phone without addressing that trained expectation usually just leads to restlessness, not reflection.</p><p><strong>WHAT REBUILDING BOREDOM TOLERANCE ACTUALLY LOOKS LIKE </strong></p><p>Not a dramatic digital detox. Smaller, more repeatable interruptions to the automatic reach:</p><p>- Let one small wait go unfilled on purpose: the queue, the lift, the kettle and notice how uncomfortable even that short a gap feels at first.</p><p>- Keep a place to catch the thoughts that show up in those gaps: They''re often oddly specific and easy to lose the moment you distract yourself again.</p><p>- Treat restlessness as a normal part of the process, not a sign you''re doing it wrong:  The discomfort fades with repetition; it doesn''t disappear immediately.</p><p><strong>SO WHAT NOW? AS I ALWAYS ASK</strong></p><p>Next time you feel that reflex, the reach for your phone before you have even registered why, pause for just a few seconds before you do it. Not forever. Just long enough to ask what your mind might have done with that moment if you''d let it. </p><p>You''ll probably never know what you have been missing until you start leaving the gaps open long enough to find out.</p><p>I hope you find this helpful.</p><p>Don''t forget to like. Share. Follow. Comment</p><p>Shalom!</p>', NULL, 'technology', '{"techgadgetsboredscrolling"}', '817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 6, 1, 0, '2026-09-15T16:41:08.095Z', '2026-09-30T20:19:59.475Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('01924dc6-017f-48c5-9001-bb2b6f51fd0f', '5 Digital Skills Every Nigerian Youth Needs to Make Money in 2026', '5-digital-skills-every-nigerian-youth-needs-to-make-money-in-2026-dst3', 'Practical digital skills you can learn and turn into income opportunities, even as a beginner.', '<p>Nigeria’s job market is changing quickly. A university degree is still valuable, but having practical digital skills can open additional opportunities for young Nigerians.</p><p>From writing and designing to managing social media and using artificial intelligence, many services can now be delivered online to businesses and clients in Nigeria and abroad.</p><p>The Nigerian government is also putting increasing attention on digital skills and the digital economy. In 2026, the Federal Government launched initiatives aimed at expanding digital skills, employment and opportunities for Nigerians to work with international clients.</p><p>The good news is that you don’t need to learn everything at once.</p><p>Here are <strong>five digital skills worth considering in 2026.</strong></p><p><strong>1. Content Writing</strong></p><p>If you can research a topic and explain it clearly, content writing can become a useful skill.</p><p>Businesses need written content for websites, blogs, newsletters, social media pages, product descriptions and other platforms. Writers can also work with individuals who need articles, scripts, captions or other forms of written communication.</p><p>A beginner can start by learning:</p><ul><li><p>Blog writing</p></li><li><p>SEO writing</p></li><li><p>Copywriting</p></li><li><p>Social media captions</p></li><li><p>Script writing</p></li><li><p>Product descriptions</p></li></ul><p>The important thing is not simply learning how to write. You also need to understand your audience, research information properly and produce content that solves a problem.</p><p><strong>How to start:</strong> Pick one type of writing, create three to five sample pieces and use them as your portfolio when approaching potential clients.</p><p><strong>2. Social Media Management</strong></p><p>Almost every business wants an online presence, but many business owners don’t have enough time to manage their social media accounts.</p><p>That’s where social media managers can help.</p><p>A social media manager may create posts, write captions, schedule content, respond to comments, research trends and analyse the performance of a page.</p><p>You can begin by learning how platforms such as Instagram, TikTok, Facebook and LinkedIn work.</p><p>You should also learn basic content strategy. Posting randomly is not enough. You need to understand <strong>who the audience is, what they want and what type of content makes them take action.</strong></p><p><strong>How to start:</strong> Choose a small business, create a sample seven-day content plan for it and use that as part of your portfolio.</p><p><strong>3. Graphic Design and Canva</strong></p><p>You don’t necessarily need expensive design software to begin learning graphic design.</p><p>Tools such as Canva allow beginners to create social media graphics, flyers, presentations, thumbnails, posters and other visual content.</p><p>Graphic design can become particularly useful when combined with another skill. For example, someone who understands both social media management and design can offer a business a complete content package instead of only creating individual graphics.</p><p><strong>How to start:</strong> Learn basic principles such as typography, spacing, alignment and colour combinations. Then practise by recreating different types of professional designs.</p><p>Your goal should eventually be to create original work rather than simply relying on templates.</p><p><strong>4. Artificial Intelligence (AI) Tools</strong></p><p>AI is becoming an important part of many digital jobs.</p><p>However, learning AI doesn’t necessarily mean becoming a computer scientist.</p><p>For many beginners, the useful skill is learning how to use AI tools to research ideas, brainstorm content, analyse information, improve workflows and complete repetitive tasks more efficiently.</p><p>The key is to <strong>combine AI with another valuable skill</strong>.</p><p>For example:</p><p><strong>Writing + AI = faster content production</strong></p><p><strong>Design + AI = faster creative workflows</strong></p><p><strong>Marketing + AI = faster research and content planning</strong></p><p>Don’t think of AI as a replacement for learning. Think of it as a tool that can make your existing skills more productive.</p><p><strong>5. Digital Marketing</strong></p><p>Digital marketing brings several skills together.</p><p>It includes areas such as search engine optimisation (SEO), social media marketing, email marketing, online advertising, content marketing and analytics.</p><p>Businesses need people who can help them attract potential customers online and turn attention into sales.</p><p>You don’t have to master everything immediately. Start with one area and gradually expand your knowledge.</p><p>For example, Google currently offers a Digital Marketing &amp; E-commerce Career Certificate designed for beginners, covering areas including social media marketing, search, email, e-commerce and analytics.</p><p><strong>Where Can You Learn These Skills?</strong></p><p>One of the biggest advantages of learning digital skills today is the number of online resources available.</p><p>You can start with free educational videos on YouTube and practise what you learn immediately.</p><p>There are also structured learning opportunities. For example, Nigeria’s government-backed Digital Training Academy says it provides access to free digital and professional training through partnerships with platforms including Coursera and Pluralsight.</p><p>MTN Nigeria also operates its <strong>Digital Skills for Digital Jobs</strong> initiative, which provides curated learning resources aimed at helping young Nigerians develop digital skills for employment and entrepreneurship.</p><p>The most important thing is to avoid becoming a <strong>professional course collector</strong>.</p><p>You don’t need 20 certificates before you start.</p><p>Learn → practise → build a portfolio → look for clients.</p><p><strong>How Can You Start Making Money?</strong></p><p>Learning a skill is only the first step.</p><p>The next step is turning that skill into a service that somebody is willing to pay for.</p><p>For example, instead of saying:</p><p>“I know Canva.”</p><p>You could offer:</p><p>“I create social media graphics for small businesses.”</p><p>Instead of saying:</p><p>“I can write.”</p><p>You could offer:</p><p>“I write SEO-friendly blog articles for businesses.”</p><p>This difference is important because clients usually pay for <strong>solutions</strong>, not simply for someone’s knowledge.</p><p>Start small. Create sample work, build a simple portfolio and approach businesses, creators and potential clients who could benefit from your service.</p><p>You can eventually explore freelance platforms, direct outreach, social media and professional networks to find opportunities.</p><p><strong>Final Thoughts</strong></p><p>You don’t need to master all five skills.</p><p>In fact, trying to learn everything at once can slow you down.</p><p><strong>Pick one skill.</strong></p><p>Spend the next 30 days learning and practising it. Create real examples of your work, build a portfolio and start looking for opportunities.</p><p>As your experience grows, add complementary skills.</p><p>Nigeria’s digital economy is creating new opportunities, but opportunity alone isn’t enough. You need a skill that can solve a real problem.</p><p><strong>The best time to start building that skill is before you need it.</strong></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788858052/echo-gist/f52280a4-722f-4591-a509-c385360c9f1a/p8lxcnzpgiwqj2ldnm0z.jpg', 'technology', '{"digitalskills","nigeria","makemoneyonline","youth"}', 'f52280a4-722f-4591-a509-c385360c9f1a', TRUE, FALSE, 5, 267, 48, 12, '2026-09-08T09:17:34.110Z', '2026-09-30T13:01:53.234Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('6708a994-fbf5-42c3-a466-9891d287007d', 'Broken Mirror, Smile Face 1', 'broken-mirror-smile-face-1-dlyx', 'The mirror had hung in that house for eleven years before Adaeze ever raised a hand against it, and even then, she hadn’t meant to.It happened on a Tuesday — sh…', '<p>The mirror had hung in that house for eleven years before Adaeze ever raised a hand against it, and even then, she hadn’t meant to.</p><p>It happened on a Tuesday — she would remember that detail long after she forgot the color of the walls, the smell of the hallway, the sound her own voice used to make before it learned to whisper. A Tuesday, ordinary as bread, the kind of day that asks nothing of you and receives nothing in return. She had been standing in front of the mirror doing something so mundane it embarrassed her later to recall it: pinning her hair back, tilting her chin, deciding whether the woman looking back at her still deserved to be looked at.</p><p>And then Chidi’s voice had come up the stairs, low and even, the way it always was right before it wasn’t. Adaeze. Just her name. Nothing more. But she knew, the way a house knows a storm is coming before the first drop falls — some pressure change in the air, some tightening behind the ribs — and her hand had closed into a fist before she’d told it to, and the fist had found the glass, and the glass had found seven ways to break.</p><p>She stood there afterward with blood beading along her knuckles like small red stars, and she did not cry. That was the thing she would think about for years — she did not cry. She simply looked at the wreckage of her own reflection, fractured into panes of light and shadow, and felt something in her chest exhale for the first time in longer than she could remember.</p><p>He never came up the stairs that day. She would learn later that he’d simply turned around and walked back out to the car, as if her silence — her refusal to answer, her refusal to make herself small and available for whatever was coming — had confused some internal calculation he’d relied on for years. As if the woman who broke mirrors instead of apologizing was a woman he no longer recognized, and didn’t know how to want.</p><p>He left three weeks after that. Not because of the mirror. Because of everything the mirror had let loose in her.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1784063346/echo-gist/b3132995-e2de-4cc1-9705-e9671bcf3423/r0bdcldqlkm8w1dzkxym.png', 'opinion', '{}', 'b3132995-e2de-4cc1-9705-e9671bcf3423', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 7, 2, 0, '2026-07-14T21:10:02.672Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:15.025Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('89d66fe7-1968-4a0c-91f3-70e965f08506', 'AI Isn’t Taking Your Job — But Someone Who Knows How to Use It Might', 'ai-isnt-taking-your-job-but-someone-who-knows-how-to-use-it-might-8lca', 'A few years ago, knowing how to use a computer was an advantage.Today, knowing how to use AI might be becoming one.Everywhere you look, artificial intelligence …', '<p>A few years ago, knowing how to use a computer was an advantage.</p><p>Today, knowing how to use AI might be becoming one.</p><p>Everywhere you look, artificial intelligence is changing the way people work. It can write, research, analyse information, generate ideas, create images and even help people learn new skills.</p><p>And naturally, people are worried.</p><p>Will AI take our jobs?</p><p>Maybe that’s not the question we should be asking.</p><p>A more uncomfortable question is:</p><p>What happens when someone who knows how to use AI competes for the same job as someone who doesn’t?</p><p><strong>AI is becoming more than a chatbot</strong></p><p>For many people, AI still means opening a chatbot and asking it a question.</p><p>But the technology is moving beyond that.</p><p>AI is increasingly being used to handle repetitive tasks, assist with research, analyse information and help businesses get work done faster.</p><p>Think about a content writer.</p><p>One writer might spend hours researching a topic, creating an outline and working through a first draft.</p><p>Another might use AI to brainstorm ideas, organise research and improve their draft.</p><p>The second writer hasn’t necessarily become a worse writer.</p><p>They may simply have more time to focus on the things that require a human touch: creativity, judgment, personality and understanding their audience.</p><p>That’s where the real advantage begins.</p><p><strong>The workplace is changing</strong></p><p>It’s easy to imagine AI as a machine whose only purpose is to replace humans.</p><p>But that’s not necessarily how this transition will happen.</p><p>Some jobs will change, and some tasks will become automated. At the same time, new roles and new ways of working will emerge.</p><p>We’ve seen this happen before.</p><p>Technology has always changed the workplace. The people who adapt to those changes often find themselves with new opportunities.</p><p>AI may be doing the same thing.</p><p>The question isn’t whether your current job will look exactly the same in five years.</p><p>It probably won’t.</p><p>The question is whether you’ll be willing to learn as it changes.</p><p><strong>But AI still needs you</strong></p><p>Here’s something people sometimes forget:</p><p>AI can be wrong.</p><p>It can give you information that sounds convincing but isn’t true. It can misunderstand what you want. It can produce something generic when what you really need is creativity or human understanding.</p><p>That’s why knowing how to use AI isn’t the same as letting AI do everything for you.</p><p>The valuable skill is knowing when to use it, what to ask it, what to verify and what to do yourself.</p><p><strong>So, should you learn AI?</strong></p><p>You don’t need to become a programmer.</p><p>You don’t need to understand every technical detail behind artificial intelligence.</p><p>Start with your own field.</p><p>Ask yourself:</p><p>What part of my work takes too much time?</p><p>What tasks are repetitive?</p><p>Where could AI help me research, organise or generate ideas?</p><p>Then learn how to use it responsibly.</p><p>Because the goal isn’t to become dependent on AI.</p><p>The goal is to become better at what you already do.</p><p>Maybe the future of work isn’t humans versus AI.</p><p>Maybe it’s humans who know how to work with AI versus those who refuse to learn.</p><p>AI might not take your job.</p><p>But someone who knows how to use it might become better prepared for the opportunities you both want.</p>', NULL, 'technology', '{}', '43014ec1-637a-4b51-9800-065d611de765', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 73, 9, 10, '2026-09-08T09:09:33.324Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:23.870Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('51b09c0a-6af3-4063-b0a4-4e7fafd8173a', '100MB.. Is It Enough', '100mb-is-it-enough--82m0', 'The government announced 100MB of free daily data for students like it was a scholarship. Like it was rent paid, like it was a laptop, like it was anything clos…', '<p></p><p></p><p>The government announced 100MB of free daily data for students like it was a scholarship. Like it was rent paid, like it was a laptop, like it was anything close to what "digital learning" actually requires. I did the math before I did the gratitude.</p><p></p><p>100MB is one video call, if you turn your camera off. It''s a handful of PDF downloads, if they''re not scanned images pretending to be PDFs. </p><p></p><p>I''m not ungrateful for gestures. I''ve lived here long enough to know something is technically better than nothing, and I know somewhere a student with zero data now has a little more than zero. That matters, even at this size. But I''m tired of being asked to celebrate the shape of help instead of the substance of it.</p><p></p><p>And of course it''s election season again. Funny how the announcements multiply right when the votes are about to matter  data today, maybe light tomorrow, a scholarship scheme by December. I''m not saying the timing is deliberate. I''m saying I''ve seen this calendar before, and I know what month it usually stops being generous.</p><p></p><p>Meanwhile, the man who oversaw a decade of our elections just landed an ambassador post in Doha. So Yakubu will manage. The rest of us will get 100MB.</p><p></p><p>This is the pattern, not the exception. Light comes back for two hours and it''s a "restoration." A road gets one lane resurfaced and it''s a "commissioning." A scholarship covers registration but not tuition and it''s still called a scholarship. We''ve been trained to receive announcements as if they were outcomes, to clap at the press release before anyone''s checked whether the thing actually works.</p><p></p><p>I think about what 100MB says about who they think we are students who''ll be moved by the sound of "free" more than the fact of "enough." It''s not malice, I don''t think. It''s just cheaper to announce data than to fix electricity, cheaper to whitelist a few platforms than to make the internet actually affordable, cheaper to give us a headline than a system.</p><p></p><p>So I''ll take the 100MB. I''m not too proud to use what''s offered. But I''m also not going to pretend it solves what it claims to solve, or thank anyone like it did. There''s a difference between gratitude and amnesia, and I''d rather stay a little ungrateful than forget, every single time, what we actually needed.</p><p></p><p>We are handed peanuts to sway us. Laughable, honestly. And the maddening part isn''t even the peanuts  it''s that they''re not wrong to try it. It''s worked before.</p>', NULL, 'technology', '{}', '5b0486c4-e2be-442d-87a9-7c80c6d955cd', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 3, 3, 0, '2026-09-15T18:49:14.232Z', '2026-09-19T14:56:29.796Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('c3660e5a-afde-46b3-b8a2-6ec6bcc98c8b', 'Apple has officially announced the iPhone 18 series release date. The pressure is on.', 'apple-has-officially-announced-the-iphone-18-series-release-date-the-pressure-is-hzjh', 'Apple has confirmed its iPhone 18 series release date onWednesday, September 9, at 10 a.m. PT and it''s shaping up to be one of the biggest launches in years. Th…', '<p>Apple has confirmed its iPhone 18 series release date onWednesday, September 9, at 10 a.m. PT and it''s shaping up to be one of the biggest launches in years. The event will introduce the premium iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max, but the real headline is Apple''s first ever foldable iPhone, widely rumored to be called the iPhone Ultra. It''s also the first Apple keynote hosted by new CEO John Ternus, marking a major leadership shift after Tim Cook''s 15 year run. Notably, the standard iPhone 18 won''t be part of this launch.</p><p>On pricing, a particular price hasn’t been fixed but they have been rumors. Reports suggest the iPhone 18 Pro could start anywhere from $999 to $1,199, while the foldable is the real wildcard estimates for the Ultra range from around $1,928 up to over $3,000 for top storage tiers. And here''s what''ll really get people talking, even at a possible $2,000+ starting price for a first generation folding iphone, one analyst predicts the Ultra could still sell 10 million units in its first year proving that when it comes to Apple, people rarely slow down with the orders and are more fantasized about it. Will you be buying the latest 18 series or you will only just be feeding your eyes?</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788621775/echo-gist/40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1/w26twoe2a0fzvbxgl1up.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 1, 51, 1, 0, '2026-09-05T15:23:15.626Z', '2026-09-15T19:20:50.101Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('f0502e72-380d-4e31-b99b-5cb684db2763', 'Health is wealth. But we only learn that the expensive way.', 'health-is-wealth-but-we-only-learn-that-the-expensive-way--ag4v', 'Think about the last time you told yourself "I''ll rest later." Did later ever actually come? For most of us, it doesn''t, because we keep pushing it down the lis…', '<blockquote><p>Think about the last time you told yourself "I''ll rest later." Did later ever actually come? For most of us, it doesn''t, because we keep pushing it down the list until our body makes the decision for us, usually in the form of a headache that won''t quit, a cold that lingers too long, or an exhaustion that sleep alone can''t fix anymore. That''s the thing about rest, it''s not negotiable, and it''s definitely not something that should be postponed. Listen to your body, go for regular checkups, and add rest into your routine the same way you''d add anything else that matters. </p><p>Health is wealth, they say, but it''s quite a bit to understand until you actually fall ill. Sickness can be avoidable, but only with a proper routine, one that includes eating healthy, exercising regularly, going for checkups, and yes, resting.</p><p>But there''s one thing people skip the most, and that''s rest. We wear tiredness like a badge of honor, as if pushing through exhaustion means we''re winning at life, when really it just means we''re running on borrowed time. Your body isn''t a machine that runs forever on empty, it''s a system that needs downtime to keep working properly, and the moment you start ignoring that, everything else begins to slip.</p><p>While you sleep, your muscles repair themselves, your cells regenerate, and your immune system quietly rebuilds its defenses, which is why skimping on rest leaves you more vulnerable to illness than you''d expect. It also sharpens your mind in ways you don''t notice until it''s missing, because everything feels harder when you''re exhausted, focus fades, memory slips, and a tired brain ends up making tired choices. Your heart feels it too, since chronic lack of rest has been linked to high blood pressure and added strain, proving that your heart needs the slowdown just as much as your muscles do. Even your mood depends on it more than people realize, as irritability, anxiety, and low spirits often trace straight back to poor rest, while a well rested mind simply handles stress better. And perhaps most importantly, rest is what stands between you and burnout, because constant hustle without recovery doesn''t make you more productive, it only speeds up the crash, and rest is what makes consistency possible in the first place.</p><p>So don''t wait until your body forces you to stop. Build rest into your routine the same way you''d build in exercise or meals, take the naps, sleep the full hours, take the day off when you need it, because it isn''t laziness, it''s maintenance. In the end, a body that''s well rested is a body that''s well prepared, for work, for life, for everything.</p><p>Also, Echogist cares about your health. We''ll be dropping more health tips right here on Ecosgist, simple, practical advice to help you live better, feel better, and take charge of your wellbeing one habit at a time. If this resonated with you, don''t let it end here, click the link below to join the community and stay updated every time we drop something new. Your health deserves the attention, and we''re here to make sure you never miss a tip.</p></blockquote><p></p>', NULL, 'technology', '{}', '40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 14, 1, 0, '2026-08-08T08:18:23.289Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:06.463Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('5fabaa74-fed4-4822-8624-9b3acc43fd52', 'Broken mirror-smile-face 🪞 3', 'broken-mirror-smile-face-3--6szv', 'Her daughter, Ify, was seven when it happened, too young to understand the precise architecture of what had fallen apart, old enough to feel the change in the a…', '<p>Her daughter, Ify, was seven when it happened, too young to understand the precise architecture of what had fallen apart, old enough to feel the change in the air the way children always do — in the length of the silences at dinner, in the new sharpness of her mother’s laugh, in the sudden absence of a father’s shoes by the door. She didn’t ask many questions. She was a quiet child, watchful, the kind of watchful that worried Adaeze more than tears would have.</p><p>It was Ify who found her, three months after the mirror broke, sitting on the floor of the bedroom with a single shard of glass held up to catch the evening light.</p><p>“Mommy,” the girl said, from the doorway, in the small careful voice she used when she suspected she’d walked into something she wasn’t meant to see. “What are you doing?”</p><p>Adaeze looked at her daughter over the fractured edge of glass, and for a moment she considered lying — considered saying something soft and forgettable, the kind of thing mothers say to close a door gently rather than slam it. But something in her had grown too tired for softness that wasn’t true.</p><p>“I’m trying to see myself,” she said. “Properly. For the first time in a long time.”</p><p>Ify came and sat beside her on the floor, cross-legged, her school uniform still creased from the day. She didn’t ask what that meant. She simply looked at the shard in her mother’s hand, at the small sliver of face reflected there — one eye, part of a cheekbone, the corner of a mouth that hadn’t yet decided whether to smile.</p><p>“It’s broken,” Ify said. Not an accusation. Just a fact, offered up the way children offer up facts, testing their weight in the air.</p><p>“Yes,” Adaeze said. “It is.”</p><p>“Are you going to fix it?”</p><p>Adaeze looked down at the glass for a long moment. Outside, the evening call to prayer drifted faint and distant from the mosque three streets over, threading through the ordinary evening sounds of Owerri — okada engines, a neighbor’s radio, someone’s laughter carrying on the warm air. Ordinary sounds, for a house that no longer felt ordinary at all.</p><p>“I don’t think so,” she said finally. “I think I want to leave it broken for a while. I want to see what it shows me before I decide to put it back together.”</p><p>Ify considered this with the grave seriousness only children can summon for things that make no logical sense to them. Then she reached out one small finger and touched the edge of the largest shard — carefully, the way she’d been taught to touch things that could cut.</p><p>“It’s like a puzzle,” she said. “But sad.”</p><p>“Yes,” Adaeze said, and something in her throat tightened unexpectedly, fiercely, a grief she hadn’t given herself permission to name. “That’s exactly what it’s like.”</p>', 'https://picsum.photos/seed/broken-mirror-smile-face-3--6szv/800/500', 'opinion', '{}', 'b3132995-e2de-4cc1-9705-e9671bcf3423', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 4, 0, 0, '2026-07-16T15:57:22.951Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:13.106Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('ff969ac5-da28-43b0-ba54-81dd59700c19', 'SEO Is Not Dead. But the Old Way of Doing SEO Is', 'seo-is-not-dead-but-the-old-way-of-doing-seo-is-t0hx', 'How AI is changing what it means to be discovered online', '<p>For years, the SEO playbook was fairly simple.</p><p>Find a keyword. Write an article. Add the keyword to your title and headings. Build some backlinks. Wait for Google to rank you.</p><p>That strategy can still work.</p><p>But search has changed.</p><p>People are asking longer, more specific questions. They are using artificial intelligence to research products, compare options, solve problems, and explore topics through conversations rather than a series of simple searches.</p><p>Google is changing with them.</p><p>In 2026, Google says AI Overviews have more than <strong>2.5 billion monthly active users</strong>, while AI Mode has surpassed <strong>one billion monthly users</strong>. Google also says people are searching more frequently and asking more complex questions as these AI features become part of Search.</p><p>So, is SEO dead?</p><p><strong>No.</strong></p><p>But the old way of thinking about SEO is.</p><h2>Search Is Becoming a Conversation</h2><p>Imagine searching for:</p><blockquote><p>“Best laptop for students.”</p></blockquote><p>Traditional search gives you a list of pages to investigate.</p><p>Now imagine asking:</p><blockquote><p>“I am a computer science student with a limited budget. I need a laptop for programming, occasional video editing, and long study sessions. What should I buy?”</p></blockquote><p>That is not simply a keyword.</p><p>It is a problem.</p><p>AI-powered search is designed to understand these more complex questions and continue the conversation through follow-up questions. Google''s AI Mode can break a question into multiple searches to explore different parts of a topic and bring together more relevant information from across the web.</p><p>This changes what content creators need to think about.</p><p>The goal is no longer simply to rank for a phrase.</p><p>The goal is to <strong>be useful when someone needs an answer.</strong></p><h2>Keywords Still Matter. But They Are Not Enough</h2><p>This does not mean keywords are dead.</p><p>They are still useful for understanding what people are searching for and helping search engines understand what a page is about.</p><p>The problem is relying on keywords alone.</p><p>Consider two articles about artificial intelligence.</p><p>The first says:</p><blockquote><p>“10 Benefits of Artificial Intelligence.”</p></blockquote><p>It repeats information that readers can find on hundreds of other websites.</p><p>The second says:</p><blockquote><p>“I Used AI to Automate Five Tasks in My Small Business. Here Is What Actually Worked.”</p></blockquote><p>Which one would you rather read?</p><p>The second article has something the first one lacks:</p><p><strong>experience.</strong></p><p>It has a reason to exist.</p><p>That matters because Google is increasingly emphasizing original, useful and non-commodity content in its AI search guidance. Its documentation specifically encourages unique perspectives, firsthand experience and information that goes beyond what is already widely available.</p><h2>AI Has Created a Content Problem</h2><p>Artificial intelligence has made producing content easier than ever.</p><p>That sounds great until everyone starts doing it.</p><p>Today, someone can generate an article about almost any topic in seconds. The result is an internet filled with content that may look different but often says the same thing.</p><p>That creates a new problem.</p><p>It is no longer difficult to produce content.</p><p><strong>It is difficult to produce content worth remembering.</strong></p><p>If you want your article to stand out, give readers something they cannot get from a generic AI-generated summary.</p><p>Share an experiment.</p><p>Explain something from personal experience.</p><p>Analyze original data.</p><p>Interview someone.</p><p>Tell a story.</p><p>Challenge an assumption.</p><p>Give a perspective that makes the reader stop and think.</p><p>The more information becomes automated, the more valuable <strong>experience and judgment</strong> become.</p><h2>The New SEO Question</h2><p>Instead of asking:</p><blockquote><p>“How do I rank for this keyword?”</p></blockquote><p>Start asking:</p><blockquote><p><strong>“Why would Google, an AI system, or a reader choose my content over everything else?”</strong></p></blockquote><p>That question will improve your content strategy.</p><p>Suppose you want to write about remote work.</p><p>You could publish:</p><p><strong>“10 Benefits of Remote Work.”</strong></p><p>Or you could investigate:</p><p><strong>“I Asked 20 Remote Workers What They Wish They Knew Before Working From Home.”</strong></p><p>The second idea has originality.</p><p>It creates information rather than simply rearranging existing information.</p><p>That is the kind of thinking content creators need in the age of AI.</p><h2>What Should You Do Differently?</h2><p>You do not need to abandon SEO.</p><p>You need to make it more useful.</p><h3>1. Stop Creating Content Just Because a Keyword Has Search Volume</h3><p>Search volume tells you that people are looking for something.</p><p>It does not tell you that you have something valuable to say.</p><p>Before writing, ask:</p><p><strong>What can I add that readers cannot easily find somewhere else?</strong></p><h3>2. Bring Experience Into Your Content</h3><p>If you tested something, tell people what happened.</p><p>If you made a mistake, explain it.</p><p>If you achieved a result, show how.</p><p>Firsthand experience makes content more credible and gives readers a reason to trust you.</p><h3>3. Solve the Problem Behind the Search</h3><p>People do not really search for keywords.</p><p>They search because they want something.</p><p>A person searching for “SEO” may actually be asking:</p><blockquote><p>“Why has my website traffic dropped?”</p></blockquote><p>Someone searching for “AI automation” may really mean:</p><blockquote><p>“What can I automate in my business without hiring another employee?”</p></blockquote><p>Understand the problem, and you will create better content.</p><h3>4. Make Your Content Worth Citing</h3><p>Google''s AI search features are not removing the web. Google is actively adding ways for people to discover articles, websites, original perspectives and other sources through AI-powered Search.</p><p>That means there is still an enormous opportunity for publishers.</p><p>But your content needs to give people—and search systems—a reason to discover it.</p><h2>The Future of SEO Is More Human Than You Think</h2><p>There is an interesting irony here.</p><p>The more artificial intelligence improves at producing generic information, the less valuable generic information becomes.</p><p>That makes human qualities more important:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Experience.</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Expertise.</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Original research.</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Creativity.</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Judgment.</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Trust.</strong></p></li></ul><p>AI can help you research, brainstorm, organize and edit.</p><p>Use it.</p><p>But do not let it replace your thinking.</p><p>The strongest content in the coming years will not necessarily come from people who avoid AI.</p><p>It will come from people who know how to use AI <strong>without losing their own voice, experience and judgment.</strong></p><p>SEO is not dead.</p><p><strong>It is evolving from the art of ranking pages to the art of becoming worth discovering.</strong></p><p>And for writers, businesses and creators willing to adapt, that is not a threat. It is an opportunity.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789060762/echo-gist/290c2973-016c-40b3-aee0-77b5eb493875/i7fbyl6h7jijhhzzlvij.png', 'technology', '{"technology","seo","writing","business","digitalmarketing"}', '290c2973-016c-40b3-aee0-77b5eb493875', TRUE, FALSE, 5, 4, 0, 0, '2026-09-10T17:21:45.537Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:02.967Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('6dee6e03-c5ad-4aca-a980-4686899bb0e2', 'Copy of The Girl Who Kept Going', 'the-girl-who-kept-going-k365-copy-733', 'There was a girl who kept telling herself, “I’ll try again tomorrow.”Not because she was lazy.Not because she didn’t have dreams.She was simply tired.Tired of s…', '<p>There was a girl who kept telling herself, “I’ll try again tomorrow.”</p><p>Not because she was lazy.</p><p>Not because she didn’t have dreams.</p><p>She was simply tired.</p><p>Tired of starting things and wondering if they would work out. Tired of watching other people move forward while she felt like she was standing in the same place. Tired of pretending she had everything figured out when, deep down, she was still trying to find her way.</p><p>But there was something she refused to do.</p><p>Give up.</p><p>Some mornings, she woke up with confidence. Other mornings, she woke up with questions.</p><p>Am I good enough?</p><p>Will things ever change?</p><p>What if I fail again?</p><p>Still, she got up.</p><p>She learned something new.</p><p>She wrote another page.</p><p>She applied for another opportunity.</p><p>She tried again.</p><p>And slowly, she began to understand something important:</p><p>Progress doesn''t always look like success.</p><p>Sometimes, progress is simply refusing to stop.</p><p>The girl didn''t know exactly where her journey would take her. She didn''t have all the answers. She didn''t even know how long it would take.</p><p>But she knew she wanted a different life.</p><p>So she kept going.</p><p>One word.</p><p>One lesson.</p><p>One opportunity.</p><p>One day at a time.</p><p>Maybe that''s what courage really is not being certain that everything will work out, but choosing to continue even when you''re not sure.</p><p>And perhaps, somewhere along the way, the girl will look back and realize that all those small steps she almost dismissed were actually taking her somewhere.</p><p>She just had to keep going.</p><p>Winny ✍🏽</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789156264/echo-gist/62580ebc-d0a6-462b-b0a2-612472fca3e2/lrboajzdclf1sej9miov.png', 'technology', '{}', '62580ebc-d0a6-462b-b0a2-612472fca3e2', FALSE, FALSE, 1, 0, 0, 0, '2026-09-18T10:58:26.148Z', '2026-09-18T10:58:26.148Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('4e1457af-3428-48c3-a976-6a55ae590075', 'You Don''t Have to Be an AI Expert to Start a Career in AI', 'you-dont-have-to-be-an-ai-expert-to-start-a-career-in-ai-qst3', 'AI is creating more career paths than just machine learning and advanced programming. Here''s where beginners can actually start.', '<p>You Don''t Have to Be an AI Expert to Start a Career in AI</p><p>When people hear “career in AI,” they often imagine someone writing thousands of lines of Python code or building complicated machine-learning models.</p><p>That can be one path.</p><p>But it isn''t the only one.</p><p>Artificial intelligence is becoming part of many different types of work. That means there are more ways to enter the AI industry than many beginners realize.</p><p>AI Is Bigger Than Coding</p><p>AI isn''t just about building systems like ChatGPT.</p><p>Companies also need people who can work with data, test AI systems, support AI-powered products, manage projects, research information and help businesses understand how to use new technology.</p><p>This means someone interested in AI doesn''t necessarily have to start by becoming an AI engineer.</p><p>The first step can be finding the part of AI that matches your existing strengths.</p><p>So What Should a Beginner Learn?</p><p>There is no single AI skill that guarantees a career.</p><p>But some foundations can make a big difference.</p><p>Problem-solving is one of them.</p><p>Knowing how to research, communicate, analyze information and solve real problems can be just as important as knowing how to use an AI tool.</p><p>AI can help you produce an answer.</p><p>But you still need to understand the problem you''re trying to solve.</p><p>You Don''t Need to Learn Everything at Once</p><p>One mistake beginners make is trying to learn everything connected to AI at the same time.</p><p>Python.</p><p>Machine learning.</p><p>Prompt engineering.</p><p>Data science.</p><p>Cloud computing.</p><p>LLMs.</p><p>Robotics.</p><p>Before long, you have watched twenty tutorials and built nothing.</p><p>A better approach is to start with one direction.</p><p>If you enjoy numbers and finding patterns, data analysis might interest you.</p><p>If you enjoy building things, software development could be a starting point.</p><p>If you enjoy communication and business, you could explore AI-assisted content, customer support, research or operations.</p><p>And if you eventually discover that you enjoy programming and mathematics, you can move deeper into machine learning and AI engineering.</p><p>AI Skills Still Need Human Skills</p><p>Knowing how to use an AI tool isn''t the same as understanding the work it produces.</p><p>AI can generate an answer that looks impressive while still containing mistakes.</p><p>That''s why critical thinking matters.</p><p>You need to be able to ask:</p><p>Does this make sense?</p><p>Is this information accurate?</p><p>Can I explain what the tool produced?</p><p>What happens if the answer is wrong?</p><p>Those questions become more important as AI becomes more involved in everyday work.</p><p>Start With Something Real</p><p>You don''t need a huge AI project to begin building experience.</p><p>You could create a simple data-analysis project.</p><p>Build a small application using an AI API.</p><p>Create an AI-assisted research workflow.</p><p>Test different AI tools and document what works.</p><p>Or solve a small problem that people actually have.</p><p>The important thing is that you are producing something you can explain.</p><p>A project gives you something more useful than another certificate sitting in a folder.</p><p>It gives you evidence that you can actually apply what you have learned.</p><p>The AI Career Is Still Being Written</p><p>AI is changing quickly.</p><p>New tools are appearing, existing jobs are changing and new roles are developing.</p><p>That can make the industry confusing for beginners.</p><p>But it also means you don''t have to know exactly where your career will end before taking the first step.</p><p>Start with a foundation.</p><p>Choose one area.</p><p>Build something.</p><p>Learn from it.</p><p>Then move forward.</p><p>You Don''t Have to Start at the Top</p><p>You don''t need to become an AI expert overnight.</p><p>You don''t need to know every programming language.</p><p>And you don''t need to understand every new AI model that gets released.</p><p>You just need a starting point.</p><p>The bigger question isn''t:</p><p>“Do I know enough about AI?”</p><p>It might be:</p><p>“What can I start learning and building today?”</p><p>Because the people who enter the AI industry tomorrow will also be people who started somewhere today.</p><p>What part of AI would you be interested in learning?</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789731442/echo-gist/32428e86-4409-45bd-8488-60480b94c940/nayowqqfu2uqfu0pzfhg.jpg', 'career', '{"ai","career","technology","beginners"}', '32428e86-4409-45bd-8488-60480b94c940', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 2, 2, 1, '2026-09-18T11:38:29.272Z', '2026-09-19T15:09:26.493Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('e27d43cc-4017-4fda-8df0-c9d4c475e085', 'Standard Online Jobs You Can Do From your Home in 2026', 'standard-online-jobs-you-can-do-from-your-home-in-2026-0npe', 'Working from home has become increasingly common, with opportunities available in technology, customer service, writing, education, marketing, design and other fields.', '<p>Working from home has become increasingly common, with opportunities available in technology, customer service, writing, education, marketing, design and other fields.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">However, not every online job advertisement is genuine. Fake recruiters, upfront-payment scams and unrealistic income promises are common, so it''s important to verify opportunities before accepting them.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">In this guide, you''ll discover <strong>several legitimate online jobs in 2026</strong>, including what each job involves and the skills you need to get started.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>What Are Online Jobs?</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;">Online jobs are work opportunities that can be performed remotely using a computer, smartphone or internet connection.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Depending on the role, you may work for an employer, serve clients as a freelancer or operate your own online business.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Popular online jobs include:</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Virtual assistant</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Freelance writer</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Graphic designer</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Social media manager</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Online tutor</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Customer service representative</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Data entry specialist</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Legitimate Online Jobs in 2026</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>1. Virtual Assistant</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> Organized people with good communication skills.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Virtual assistants help businesses with tasks such as managing emails, scheduling appointments, organizing documents and communicating with customers.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Useful skills:</strong> Communication, organization, time management and basic computer knowledge.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>2. Freelance Writer</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> People who enjoy writing. I fall in this category</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Freelance writers create blog posts, website content, product descriptions, newsletters and marketing materials. A degree isn''t always necessary, but strong writing and research skills are important.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Getting started:</strong> Choose a niche, create writing samples and approach potential clients.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>3. Graphic Designer</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> Creative people with visual-design skills.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Graphic designers create logos, social media graphics, advertisements, presentations and other visual materials. Beginners can start with tools such as Canva, while experienced designers may use professional design software.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>4. Social Media Manager</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> People who understand social media and content creation.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Social media managers create posts, schedule content, respond to followers and monitor account performance.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Useful skills:</strong> Copywriting, content creation, analytics and basic design.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>5. Online Tutor</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> Individuals with knowledge of a particular subject.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Online tutors teach subjects such as mathematics, English, science, programming and music through online platforms.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Some positions require formal qualifications, while others focus on knowledge and teaching ability.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>6. Customer Service Representative</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> Good communicators.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Remote customer service workers assist customers through phone, email or live chat. You typically need good communication, patience, problem-solving ability and reliable internet access.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>7. Data Entry Specialist</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> Detail-oriented beginners. Data-entry workers enter, update and organize information in digital systems.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>8. Web Developer</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> People interested in programming.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Web developers build and maintain websites and web applications. Learning HTML, CSS and JavaScript can provide a foundation, while advanced positions may require additional programming languages and technologies.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Fun fact: there are AI tools that can help with this</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>9. Software Developer</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> Experienced programmers.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Software developers create applications, websites and digital products.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Remote opportunities exist across areas such as:</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Web development</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mobile development</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Backend development</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Cloud computing</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; DevOps</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>10. Video Editor</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> Creative people interested in video.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Video editors turn raw footage into finished content for YouTube, social media, advertisements, podcasts and online courses. There are also AI tools for this</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>11. Digital Marketing Specialist</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> People interested in growing businesses online.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Digital marketers help businesses attract customers through SEO, social media, email, paid advertising and content marketing. You can improve your prospects by specializing in one area rather than trying to master everything at once.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>12. Translator</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> People fluent in multiple languages.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Translators convert written content between languages while maintaining its meaning and context.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Professional translation requires strong writing ability, accuracy and cultural understanding.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>13. Remote Sales Representative</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> Confident communicators.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Remote sales representatives contact potential customers, explain products and services and help close deals. Some positions offer salaries, while others combine a base payment with commission.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>14. Content Creator</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> People who enjoy producing digital content.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Content creators make videos, articles, podcasts and social media posts.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Potential income sources include advertising, sponsorships, memberships, affiliate marketing and product sales.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>15. Voice-Over Artist</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> People with strong speaking skills.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Voice-over artists record narration for advertisements, videos, courses, audiobooks and other projects.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">A quality microphone, quiet environment and professional demo can help attract clients.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>16. AI Trainer or Evaluator</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> People with strong language, analytical or technical skills.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">This is one of the few new online hustles that arises with the emergence of AI. AI companies may hire workers to review AI-generated responses, classify information or provide feedback used to improve AI systems.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>17. UX/UI Designer</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> Creative people interested in technology.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">UX/UI designers work on the usability and appearance of websites and applications.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Learning tools such as Figma and creating practical case studies can help beginners build a portfolio.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>18. Affiliate Marketer</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best for:</strong> People with an audience or content platform.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Affiliate marketers promote products or services using tracking links and may earn commissions from qualifying sales.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Promotion can happen through blogs, YouTube, social media or email newsletters.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Best Online Jobs for Beginners</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;">If you don''t have much experience, consider starting with:</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Virtual assistance</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Customer support</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Data entry</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Transcription</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Freelance writing</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Online research</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Social media assistance</p><p style="text-align: justify;">·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Content creation</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Beginner-friendly doesn''t mean effortless. You still need to develop useful skills and provide reliable work.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>How to Find Legitimate Online Jobs</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;">1. Research the Employer: check whether the company has an official website, professional contact information and a verifiable business presence.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">2. Don''t Pay to Get Hired: be suspicious of anyone demanding money for guaranteed employment, registration or special access to jobs.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">3. Avoid Unrealistic Offers: promises of extremely high income for almost no work are major warning signs.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">4. Use Reputable Sources: search established job platforms, professional networks and company career pages for vacancies.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">5. Protect Your Information: verify a recruiter and company before providing sensitive personal or financial information.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>How to Start Working Online</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;">Step 1: Choose a skill - Select something you already know or can realistically learn. For example: <strong>Writing → Freelance writer</strong>/ <strong>Organization → Virtual assistant</strong>/ <strong>Design → Graphic designer</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;">Step 2: Build your skills - Use courses, tutorials and practical projects to improve.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Step 3: Create a portfolio -Show potential employers or clients what you can actually do.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Step 4: Apply for opportunities - Search for suitable jobs and customize your applications instead of sending the same message everywhere.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Step 5: Keep improving - Your first opportunity may not be your ideal job. Use early experience to build skills, references and a stronger portfolio.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Conclusion</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify;">There are many legitimate ways to <strong>work from home in 2026</strong>, but finding a genuine opportunity requires patience and research.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Don''t focus only on advertised income. Consider the skills required, the credibility of the employer and whether the opportunity can provide sustainable work. Start with <strong>one skill, one service and one clear goal</strong>.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">&nbsp;</p>', NULL, 'technology', '{}', '89f720e3-1f90-484c-80c1-ed14f3ce9ec4', FALSE, FALSE, 5, 44, 0, 0, '2026-08-27T22:53:29.201Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:14.093Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('99f131cf-c1fc-4530-957b-86ba6457e099', 'Monday’s, 9-5 nightmare', 'mondays-9-5-nightmare--1jki', 'I always notice how people complain about Mondays, and I think it comes from that feeling of just wishing the weekend could stretch a little longer. For me thou…', '<p>I always notice how people complain about Mondays, and I think it comes from that feeling of just wishing the weekend could stretch a little longer. For me though, Mondays are actually my best days. I walk in knowing I have a long meeting ahead, I map out strategy for the week, and I just move through the day with purpose until it ends.</p><p>It makes me think about how this shows up in our personal lives too. Sometimes we freak out over basic things simply because we''ve given them more meaning than they deserve. We let fear creep in and turn something small into a mountain, when really, we could handle it if we just looked at it plainly. Monday is just a regular day. Nothing more.</p><p>I know not everyone will agree with me on this, but I hope we can all welcome this week with a little more ease. Happy new week, everyone. Let''s make this one count.</p>', NULL, 'technology', '{}', '40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1', TRUE, FALSE, 1, 2, 0, 0, '2026-08-24T09:12:30.543Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:39.041Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('8e651c3b-3a11-4976-8eeb-992d6ffae0ca', 'Chapter 2', 'chapter-2-6sd0', 'heheh welcome back!', '<p>Aria’s point of view </p><p>I can’t remember how long I walked but boy,am I tired. My legs hurt. I’m honestly surprised I hadn’t fainted yet, cause i literally just gave birth to this baby like five seconds ago and now I’m out here hiking through a haunted forest like it’s a Tuesday.</p><p>Shaking my head, I yelled, “you people don’t know how to treat a lady!” To no one in particular. </p><p>Call me crazy I didn’t care. I probably even looked crazy. Hair everywhere, Blanket-wrapped baby om my hip, talking to a forest like it owed me money.</p><p>I looked down at him. He blinked. I blinked nack. Then he just looked away like I’d bored him already. One day and already too cool for me.</p><p>“Okay wow,” i muttered.” You’re gonna be one of those babies huh.”</p><p>I honestly can’t even tell if he can see me His eyes are white as snow, no pupils, nothing just this endless pale stare that makes it real hard to know if he’s studying my soul or just really interested in a leaf behind my head.</p><p>What should I name him? I thought, trudging along. Black Heart wasn’t going to cut it forever, that was just me describing the fire situation, not an actual name.</p><p>I couldn’t exactly introduce him to people like “hey, this is my fire baby, he’s got a mysterious demon dad.”</p><p>“What do you think?” I asked him. “Any preferences? Blink twice For Marcus.”</p><p>Nothing he just kept staring off at that leaf like it held the secrets of the universe.</p><p>“Cool talk.” I said. “Real productive conversation we’re having.”</p><p>That’s when I heard it. A twig snap, somewhere behind me. I stopped walking so fast i nearly tripped over my own exhausted legs.</p><p>Another snap.</p><p>Every joke in my head evaporated instantly. I pulled him tight against my chest and turned around slow, and there stepping out of the mist like he’d been waiting for his cue was a figure in dark robes, face swallowed by his hood.</p><p>I didn’t need to see his eyes. I could feel the power coming off him, and it felt exactly like the power I remembered from Black Hearts father.</p><p>My knees nearly gave out right there.</p><p>“Who are you?” I asked. My voice came out thinner than I wanted, less “fierce protective mother” more like “ please don’t kill me, I just did childbirth.”</p><p>“You should not have brought him here.” His voice was low and layered not fully human.</p><p>I rolled my eyes. “Can you tell me where to go then? It’s not easy carrying a demon baby around, you know.”</p><p>He didn’t answer that. Rude.</p><p>“They’re hunting us,” i said. “In case you were wondering why I’m out here looking like this.”</p><p>“A demon’s child born to a mortal. Of course they hunt him.” Just straight into the lore dump.</p><p>“Cool. Thanks. Very helpful.” I shifted Black heart higher on my arm.</p><p>“Anything else or did you just show up to judge my parenting choices?” </p><p>“He does not belong to this world, Aria.”</p><p>I froze. “Okay, first of all how do you know my name ?. Second of all rude.”</p><p>He just stared. Well I assumed he stared. Hood. Hard to tell.</p><p>“He’s my son,” i said. “I’ll keep him safe, whether you approve or not, mystery man.”</p><p>He Lifted a hand not towards me, towards my baby and flames flared up like he was showing off for company. “Show-off” i muttered.</p><p>“His power will grow. The question is what he bec-“</p><p>“Great. Love a good cryptic warning. Really ties the whole ‘worst night of my life’ thing together.”</p><p>“You will try to outrun this,” he said “and you will fail. Like all others .”</p><p>“Cool speech. Very inspiring. You should do birthdays.” I said.</p><p>And with that he was just gone. Just vanished like he’d gotten bored of me being sarcastic at him. Honestly fair.</p><p>I stood there for a second. “Okay,” i told the empty forest. “That happened.” Black heart made a little sound against my chest.</p><p>“Yeah I know,” i said, I attract weirdos. It’s a gift.” I kissed his forehead, cool flame brushing my lips.</p><p>“I’ll keep you safe. No matter what.” Bet .” Even if step one is apparently getting heckled by a cloak guy in the woods.”</p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p>Don’t forget to like the chapter 💕</p><p></p><p></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789132071/echo-gist/fb04bbb6-dd2f-4de7-b6e4-95ce3b890d50/zksyqtlgdaiqhxo0xapn.jpg', 'writing', '{}', 'fb04bbb6-dd2f-4de7-b6e4-95ce3b890d50', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 25, 3, 0, '2026-09-11T13:12:53.026Z', '2026-09-19T20:57:35.335Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('ccdb71b3-151d-499f-8883-f10772e2e48d', 'When Everyone Can Type, Who’s Actually Worth Reading?', 'when-everyone-can-type-whos-actually-worth-reading-p5eo', 'A couple of years back, stringing together clean sentences was enough to get you hired. If you could meet a deadline, nail subject-verb agreement, and weave thr…', '<p>A couple of years back, stringing together clean sentences was enough to get you hired. If you could meet a deadline, nail subject-verb agreement, and weave three industry keywords into a paragraph without sounding like a broken robot, you had an edge.</p><p>Not anymore.</p><p>Right now, a single prompt spits out a decent sales email, a clean white paper summary, five LinkedIn carousels, and an 800-word blog post before you can even stand up to refill your mug.</p><p>It leaves writers with an uncomfortable question: If the machine generates the text, why do we need you?</p><p>The answer isn''t that comforting, bumper-sticker lie: <em>“Machines will never replace humans!”</em></p><p>They already are. Routine writing tasks—filler SEO posts, generic real estate listings, robotic corporate updates—are evaporating. Good riddance. But losing low-effort writing tasks doesn''t kill the craft; it just resets the bar.</p><p>The definition of a writer had to change, because the internet is choking on words.</p><p><strong>The Sea of Identical Sludge</strong></p><p>Cheap production kills value. We''ve known this since the assembly line, yet digital marketing acted shocked when it happened to text.</p><p>When generating an article takes twelve seconds, the internet gets flooded with polite, frictionless mediocrity. You''ve felt this every time you search for something online. You open four tabs from four different brands, written by four different names.</p><p>They all open with the exact same lukewarm throat-clearing. They hit the exact same bullet points. They cite the exact same two studies.</p><p>Different logos, identical soup.</p><p>eMarketer flagged this in their recent look at content marketing: volume is exploding, but differentiation and genuine reader trust are bottoming out. Nobody needs more sentences. We’re drowning in sentences. What we''re starving for is point of view.</p><p><strong>Data vs. Scars</strong></p><p>Say you’re shopping for a camera.</p><p>You ask an LLM to pit two Sony mirrorless bodies against each other. It’ll generate a pristine, sterile spec comparison. Sensor size. Burst rate. Battery life. Clean, useful, instantly forgettable.</p><p>Now picture a beat-up wedding photographer chiming in:</p><blockquote><p><em>“I bought Camera B for an outdoor shoot in Austin last July. The autofocus hunts every time the sun hits a backlight, and the grip gave me wrist cramps by hour four. Get Camera A, but pack three spare batteries because the power draw is a joke.”</em></p></blockquote><p>That isn’t data. That’s skin in the game.</p><p>Even search engines have caught on. Look at how Google’s AI search guidelines have tightened: they actively downrank recycled summaries and reward firsthand proof of work. Algorithms can scrape facts; they cannot drop a lens on pavement or blow a client deadline.</p><p>Your edge isn''t typing speed. It’s the scar tissue you acquired while learning your trade.</p><p><strong>The New Job Description</strong></p><p>For decades, we confused writing with mechanics. Good grammar, an expensive vocabulary, tidy syntax.</p><p>Table stakes. A computer handles syntax better than most college grads anyway.</p><p>The heavy lifting happens long before you hit backspace:</p><ul><li><p>Sniffing out a weak argument before you publish it.</p></li><li><p>Knowing which industry “best practice” is actually an expensive waste of time.</p></li><li><p>Asking the awkward follow-up question during an interview.</p></li><li><p>Translating tangled technical garbage into plain English without losing the nuance.</p></li><li><p>Having the spine to take a stance that might annoy someone.</p></li></ul><p>Software generates answers. Humans decide which questions matter.</p><p><strong>Own Your Biases (They''re Your Asset)</strong></p><p>Hand the exact same research brief to two different thinkers, and you should get two completely different pieces. If you don''t, fire one of them.</p><p>One looks at an industry trend and sees operational waste. The other looks at it and spots an employee retention problem. One is a hardened cynic; the other sees an angle nobody capitalized on.</p><p>That lens is your moat. The moment you scrub out your quirks, your failures, and your weird, specific working history to sound “authoritative,” you become replaceable by a 15-cent API call. Your perspective isn''t fluff. It''s the only reason anyone stays past paragraph two.</p><p><strong>Stop Fighting the Tool</strong></p><p>Does this mean taking a vow of digital poverty and typing essays on a 1940s Underwood?</p><p>Don''t be dense. Use the tech.</p><p>Use it to tear apart your counterarguments. Use it to scan an eighty-page regulatory filing for the three numbers you need. Use it to unstick your brain when you’ve been staring at a blank document for forty minutes.</p><p>Just recognize the line between assistance and outsourcing:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Lazy:</strong> <em>“Write a 1,000-word post about remote work culture.”</em> (Enjoy your bland, unreadable slop.)</p></li><li><p><strong>Effective:</strong> <em>“Here are my interview notes with three burnt-out engineering leads. Poke holes in my theory that hybrid schedules are hurting mid-level retention, then suggest three counterarguments I need to address.”</em></p></li></ul><p>One makes the machine think for you. The other uses the machine to test your thinking.</p><p><strong>The Split</strong></p><p>The coming years won''t reward the luddites who pretend the tech doesn''t exist, nor will they reward the prompt “engineers” flooding LinkedIn with regurgitated drivel.</p><p>The upside belongs to people who understand how to layer:</p><ul><li><p><strong>The engine</strong> for heavy lifting, research sorting, and brute-force outlining.</p></li><li><p><strong>Firsthand experience</strong> to build genuine authority.</p></li><li><p><strong>Taste and editorial judgment</strong> to know what to cut.</p></li><li><p><strong>A distinct human voice</strong> to make the reader actually care.</p></li></ul><p>Almost anyone can turn a blinking cursor into five hundred words of clean text now. The craft hasn’t died—it just shed its bloat.</p><p>Stop worrying about whether you can write. The real question is whether you have anything to say.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789501915/echo-gist/290c2973-016c-40b3-aee0-77b5eb493875/vhjegibsxqyesdnfh5fb.jpg', 'ai', '{"ai","artificialintelligence","technology","human","writing"}', '290c2973-016c-40b3-aee0-77b5eb493875', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 1, 0, 0, '2026-09-15T19:53:25.155Z', '2026-09-15T19:53:37.643Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('642a9b50-6d68-44a0-b5ed-357ffcffbf7c', 'Faith In The Midst Of The Storm', 'faith-in-the-midst-of-the-storm-8dv1', 'Topic: Faith In The Midst Of The StormText: (Mark 4:35–41)From our text, the Bible records that when Jesus and His disciples set out on the sea to cross over to…', '<p>Topic: Faith In The Midst Of The Storm</p><p>Text: (Mark 4:35–41)</p><p>From our text, the Bible records that when Jesus and His disciples set out on the sea to cross over to the other side, a violent storm arose, causing their boat to fill with water and threatening to capsize. Seeing the violent storm, the disciples became greatly afraid and called upon their Master, saying, "Master, carest thou not that we perish?" In that situation, the disciples saw Jesus as a Master who did not care about their condition because they could not imagine how He could be sleeping comfortably in such a turbulent and frightening situation.The Bible records that when Jesus awoke, He rebuked the wind and said to them, "Why are ye so fearful? How is it that ye have no faith?"</p><p>This brings us to our topic this morning: "Faith in the Midst of the Storm."</p><p>What is a Storm?</p><p>According to our Bible text (Mark 4:35–41), a storm is any situation that threatens your peace, tests your faith, and challenges your trust in God. Before the storm came, the disciples were travelling peacefully. However, when the storm arose, their peace was threatened, and they became terrified. Their faith was tested, but instead of holding on to the Master who was with them in the boat, they accused Him of not caring whether they perished.</p><p>What is Faith?</p><p>Faith is remaining calm and standing firm in the midst of turbulent situations. In verse 38 of our Bible text, it was faith that enabled Jesus to remain at peace in the boat. Though He appeared to be sleeping, Jesus was full of faith. He had the assurance that nothing unpleasant would result from the storm.</p><p>At a time like this in our country, a time filled with many storms, the currency for our survival and livelihood is faith. We live in a period when many things have failed us because of economic instability and societal challenges.</p><p>Examples of Storms in Our Country (Nigeria)</p><p>Below are some examples of storms we are currently facing in Nigeria:</p><p>1. The Storm of Financial Difficulties:</p><p>This is a situation that has weakened the economy and made our welfare uncertain. The prices of goods continue to rise daily. Businesses are no longer moving as usual, yet there are many expenses and bills to settle.</p><p>2. The Storm of Insecurity and Threats to Life:</p><p>This is a situation where our security is uncertain and our lives are under threat because of banditry, terrorism, and other forms of violence.</p><p>3. The Storm of Family Problems:</p><p>At a time like this, many families are going through various challenges such as sicknesses and health issues, the loss of loved ones, land disputes, court cases, and other difficulties.</p><p>4. The Storm of Persecution and Opposition:</p><p>This is a time when speaking or standing for the truth often attracts opposition and persecution. Many people are now afraid to speak the truth because of fear of being harmed or rejected.</p><p>The application of faith amidst these storms implies holding on tightly to God and never giving up.</p><p>#Faith is seeing the possible in the impossible. Though many things may seem impossible in our country today, faith enables us to see possibilities where others see impossibilities.</p><p>I do not know what your storm may be or what situation appears impossible before you. I encourage you to trust God one more time. Look through the eyes of faith and see possibilities in the midst of impossibilities.</p><p>#Faith is having the assurance of God''s presence in your life amidst every storm (Jeremiah 1:19). Are people threatening to harm you because you stood for the truth concerning that land matter? Hear the Word of God in Jeremiah 1:19: "They shall fight against thee; but they shall not prevail against thee."</p><p>#Faith is trusting God so completely that His promises become more real to you than your present challenges. Beloved, do not allow your challenges to overshadow God''s promises for your life.</p><p>#Faith is believing God even when you have not yet seen the answer. Are you still waiting for answers to prayers you have been offering since January? Keep trusting in God. He will surely come through for you.</p><p>#Faith is believing that God is working things out for your good, even when you do not understand the situation.</p><p>Conclusion:</p><p>In conclusion, storms are inevitable, but they are temporary. With Christ in your life, you can face every storm with confidence because He is able to speak peace into every situation. As recorded in Mark 4:39, He said, "Peace, be still."</p><p>Brethren, in this time of storms, run to Jesus for your safety, and you will come out victorious. Even the disciples ran to Jesus, and they were saved. Jesus is able to save you from every storm. Come to Him today and do not delay.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787595476/echo-gist/f760c85d-4249-4313-a4d3-8b9a5c89355f/hsvbo70smqi0mizkqy6t.png', 'technology', '{}', 'f760c85d-4249-4313-a4d3-8b9a5c89355f', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 5, 0, 0, '2026-08-24T18:21:09.210Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:37.877Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('f8b8f4fa-9a46-4f44-9afd-dc8644c2cd65', 'Barcelona''s Summer of Reinvention', 'barcelonas-summer-of-reinvention-uetg', 'Every era has to end for a new one to begin, and this summer, Barcelona let go of an old chapter to write a bolder one. Lewandowski''s goals now live in memory a…', '<p></p><p>Every era has to end for a new one to begin, and this summer, Barcelona let go of an old chapter to write a bolder one. Lewandowski''s goals now live in memory as the club turned its attention forward, splashing £70 million on Anthony Gordon, a signing that felt less like business and more like a declaration. Add Karim Adeyemi''s blistering pace for £18 million, and suddenly Flick''s attack looks like something built for chaos, the good kind, the kind defenders dread facing on a Saturday afternoon.</p><p>Then came the twist nobody quite believed until it was real.  Rodri, the calm at the center of Manchester City''s storm, now walking towards Camp Nou. Alongside him, Joao Cancelo stays put, ready to give this new look side even more balance. But the biggest question still hangs in the air, unanswered and irresistible, will Julian Alvarez finally arrive to fill the striker shaped hole Lewandowski left behind? Nobody''s sure yet, and honestly, that uncertainty is half the fun.</p><p>Familiar names have moved on to write their own stories elsewhere, and in their place stands a squad hungry to prove something bigger than a title defense. Preseason has been unpredictable, thrilling one moment and frustrating the next, but that''s exactly what makes this season feel alive. Barcelona are building something, and the pieces are finally starting to click. Can''t wait to see what other teams are preparing for this season.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787244441/echo-gist/40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1/fonupb2a9guuyv1x66s0.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 18, 1, 1, '2026-08-20T16:47:44.684Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:50.615Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('36cd4df6-4013-4f14-9c74-af4bd8300131', 'To Everyone Waiting for Their Season to Change', 'to-everyone-waiting-for-their-season-to-change--aoge', 'Nobody warns you how fast childhood ends. One day you''re laughing carelessly with friends, chasing evenings that felt endless, and the next you''re standing in a…', '<p></p><hr><p>Nobody warns you how fast childhood ends. One day you''re laughing carelessly with friends, chasing evenings that felt endless, and the next you''re standing in adulthood, wondering when the weight got so heavy. The bills. The expectations. The pressure to have a plan, a skill, a job, a way to survive and thrive all at once. Nobody hands you a manual for this.</p><p>We see you. We understand the ache of missing simpler days, and we understand the quiet fear that comes with not knowing what''s next. That''s exactly why we built Echogist, a space for the dreamers, the strugglers, the ones still finding their footing. A place where your voice matters, your story counts, and your growth isn''t just noticed, it''s championed.</p><p>You don''t have to carry this alone. This is a community built to walk with you through the hard seasons, the uncertain ones, the ones that test your patience but are quietly shaping you into who you''re becoming.</p><p>So if you''re in a difficult chapter right now, hold on. The waiting isn''t wasted. It''s preparing you for something greater. And when it comes, you won''t face it alone. We''ll be here, growing with you, every step of the way. </p><p></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787165698/echo-gist/40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1/z330z2yhopnqxtldntxu.jpg', 'technology', '{}', '40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1', TRUE, FALSE, 1, 27, 4, 0, '2026-08-19T18:55:30.200Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:51.811Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('d3c98107-a611-4ba5-ad71-3d019d05e5a5', 'Inside the Digital Engine Room: How Technology Powers Online Games!', 'inside-the-digital-engine-room-how-technology-powers-online-games-ospm', 'Have you ever wondered what happens behind the screen when you tap "play" on an online game?It looks like magic, but behind all the flashing colors and spinning…', '<p>Have you ever wondered what happens behind the screen when you tap "play" on an online game?</p><p>It looks like magic, but behind all the flashing colors and spinning wheels is a giant digital engine room filled with clever computer tricks. Let’s take a peek at the invisible machines working behind the scenes!</p><p><strong>1. The Ultimate Dice Roller: Random Number Generators</strong></p><p>Imagine a super-speed robot rolling a pair of dice millions of times every single second. In computer language, this is called a <strong>Random Number Generator</strong> (or <strong>RNG</strong>).</p><p>An RNG is like an electronic referee. It uses complicated math formulas to ensure every spin, shuffle, and move is a complete surprise. Because no human can predict what number pops out next, games stay fair and square for everyone playing.</p><p><strong>2. The Secret Code Armor: Real-Time Encryption</strong></p><p>Whenever a game sends information across the internet, it travels down a giant digital highway filled with billions of data packets. To keep player details and game moves safe from sneaky snoops, computers use <strong>encryption</strong>.</p><p>Think of encryption like a spy lockbox. Your phone scrambles the message into gibberish, puts it inside an armored vault, and zips it across the web. Only the game server has the special secret key to unlock it and read the contents in the blink of an eye!</p><p><strong>3. The Shape-Shifter: Mobile-First Design</strong></p><p>Have you ever tried wearing a jacket that was three sizes too big? It feels awkward and gets in the way! Games used to be designed only for giant desktop monitors, but today they are built with <strong>mobile-first design</strong>.</p><p>This means the digital code acts like stretchy clay. Whether you play on a tiny pocket phone or a giant living room tablet, the buttons, animations, and graphics automatically shrink or stretch to fit your screen perfectly without stuttering or slowing down.</p><blockquote><p><strong>Fun Fact:</strong> The math engines inside game servers can create billions of unpredictable number combinations in less time than it takes you to blink!</p></blockquote><p><strong>Think About This:</strong> If you could design your own secret code language that only your best friend could read, what symbols would you use?</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789509919/echo-gist/290c2973-016c-40b3-aee0-77b5eb493875/lxvxw1r7ipj93skgliys.jpg', 'technology', '{"technology","ai","tech","digitalengine","world"}', '290c2973-016c-40b3-aee0-77b5eb493875', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 0, 0, 0, '2026-09-15T22:05:42.730Z', '2026-09-18T15:11:04.616Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('d5239a83-23d9-42b6-a922-56c845858d7c', '10 Myths In Agriculture We Need To Stop Believing', '10-myths-in-agriculture-we-need-to-stop-believing--p8qa', '🌱 𝟭𝟬 𝗠𝗬𝗧𝗛𝗦 𝗜𝗡 𝗔𝗚𝗥𝗜𝗖𝗨𝗟𝗧𝗨𝗥𝗘 𝗪𝗘 𝗡𝗘𝗘𝗗 𝗧𝗢 𝗦𝗧𝗢𝗣 𝗕𝗘𝗟𝗜𝗘𝗩𝗜𝗡𝗚Agriculture is changing rapidly, but some old misconceptions are st…', '<p>🌱 <strong>𝟭𝟬 𝗠𝗬𝗧𝗛𝗦 𝗜𝗡 𝗔𝗚𝗥𝗜𝗖𝗨𝗟𝗧𝗨𝗥𝗘 𝗪𝗘 𝗡𝗘𝗘𝗗 𝗧𝗢 𝗦𝗧𝗢𝗣 𝗕𝗘𝗟𝗜𝗘𝗩𝗜𝗡𝗚</strong></p><p>Agriculture is changing rapidly, but some old misconceptions are still holding people back.</p><p>Here are 10 myths worth challenging:</p><p>𝟭. 🚜 <strong>“𝗔𝗴𝗿𝗶𝗰𝘂𝗹𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲 𝗶𝘀 𝗼𝗻𝗹𝘆 𝗳𝗼𝗿 𝗽𝗲𝗼𝗽𝗹𝗲 𝘄𝗵𝗼 𝗱𝗶𝗱𝗻’𝘁 𝗴𝗼 𝘁𝗼 𝘀𝗰𝗵𝗼𝗼𝗹.”</strong><br>𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁: Modern agriculture requires knowledge in science, economics, technology, engineering, marketing, and business.</p><p>𝟮. 🌱 <strong>“𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗻𝗲𝗲𝗱 𝗮 𝗹𝗮𝗿𝗴𝗲 𝗽𝗶𝗲𝗰𝗲 𝗼𝗳 𝗹𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝘁𝗼 𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗿𝘁 𝗳𝗮𝗿𝗺𝗶𝗻𝗴.”</strong><br>𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁: Urban farming, container gardening, hydroponics, and other systems can work with limited space.</p><p>𝟯. 💰 <strong>“𝗙𝗮𝗿𝗺𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗶𝘀 𝗮𝗹𝘄𝗮𝘆𝘀 𝗮 𝗽𝗼𝗼𝗿 𝗺𝗮𝗻’𝘀 𝗯𝘂𝘀𝗶𝗻𝗲𝘀𝘀.”</strong><br>𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁: Agriculture can be highly profitable when production, costs, markets, and risks are properly managed.</p><p>𝟰. 🌾 <strong>“𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗺𝗼𝗿𝗲 𝗳𝗲𝗿𝘁𝗶𝗹𝗶𝘇𝗲𝗿 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝗮𝗽𝗽𝗹𝘆, 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗺𝗼𝗿𝗲 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝗵𝗮𝗿𝘃𝗲𝘀𝘁.”</strong><br>𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁: Crops need the right nutrient, in the right amount, at the right time. More is not always better.</p><p>𝟱. ☀️ <strong>“𝗚𝗼𝗼𝗱 𝗿𝗮𝗶𝗻𝗳𝗮𝗹𝗹 𝗮𝘂𝘁𝗼𝗺𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗹𝘆 𝗺𝗲𝗮𝗻𝘀 𝗮 𝗴𝗼𝗼𝗱 𝗵𝗮𝗿𝘃𝗲𝘀𝘁.”</strong><br>𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁: Yield also depends on seeds, soil fertility, pests, diseases, farm management, and weather conditions.</p><p>𝟲. 📱 <strong>“𝗙𝗮𝗿𝗺𝗲𝗿𝘀 𝗱𝗼𝗻’𝘁 𝗻𝗲𝗲𝗱 𝘁𝗲𝗰𝗵𝗻𝗼𝗹𝗼𝗴𝘆.”</strong><br>𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁: Technology can improve productivity, decision-making, market access, irrigation, mechanization, and farm management.</p><p>𝟳. 🌽 <strong>“𝗦𝗺𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗵𝗼𝗹𝗱𝗲𝗿 𝗳𝗮𝗿𝗺𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗰𝗮𝗻𝗻𝗼𝘁 𝗯𝗲𝗰𝗼𝗺𝗲 𝗮 𝘀𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗼𝘂𝘀 𝗯𝘂𝘀𝗶𝗻𝗲𝘀𝘀.”</strong><br>𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁: With better productivity, finance, aggregation, market access, and value addition, small farms can become profitable enterprises.</p><p>𝟴. 🐝 <strong>“𝗘𝘃𝗲𝗿𝘆 𝗶𝗻𝘀𝗲𝗰𝘁 𝗼𝗻 𝗮 𝗳𝗮𝗿𝗺 𝗶𝘀 𝗵𝗮𝗿𝗺𝗳𝘂𝗹.”</strong><br>𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁: Some insects pollinate crops, while others naturally control agricultural pests.</p><p>𝟵. 🌍 <strong>“𝗔𝗴𝗿𝗶𝗰𝘂𝗹𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗲𝗻𝘃𝗶𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗮𝗹 𝗽𝗿𝗼𝘁𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝗰𝗮𝗻𝗻𝗼𝘁 𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸 𝘁𝗼𝗴𝗲𝘁𝗵𝗲𝗿.”</strong><br>𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁: Agroforestry, conservation agriculture, integrated farming, and better soil management can support both food production and environmental sustainability.</p><p>𝟭𝟬. 📈 <strong>“𝗜𝗳 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗱𝘂𝗰𝗲 𝗺𝗼𝗿𝗲, 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝘄𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗮𝘂𝘁𝗼𝗺𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗹𝘆 𝗺𝗮𝗸𝗲 𝗺𝗼𝗿𝗲 𝗺𝗼𝗻𝗲𝘆.”</strong><br>𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁: Production is not the same as profitability. High costs, low prices, poor market access, and post-harvest losses can turn higher production into bigger losses.</p><p>𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗯𝗶𝗴𝗴𝗲𝗿 𝗽𝗶𝗰𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲?</p><p><strong>𝗔𝗴𝗿𝗶𝗰𝘂𝗹𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲 𝗶𝘀 𝗻𝗼 𝗹𝗼𝗻𝗴𝗲𝗿 𝗷𝘂𝘀𝘁 𝗮𝗯𝗼𝘂𝘁 𝗽𝗹𝗮𝗻𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗵𝗮𝗿𝘃𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴.</strong></p><p>It is a system involving <strong>people, technology, finance, markets, policies, climate, and consumers.</strong></p><p>Which agricultural myth did you believe for the longest?</p><p>#Agriculture #Agribusiness #SustainableAgriculture #FoodSecurity #AgriculturalDevelopment</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789508284/echo-gist/6ab7749a-4f8a-421e-8a47-80d578b9ee69/dkummybyl5vmtarpc4wt.jpg', 'career', '{}', '6ab7749a-4f8a-421e-8a47-80d578b9ee69', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 3, 1, 0, '2026-09-15T21:38:40.723Z', '2026-09-18T11:21:54.693Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('d1ab17df-7bfa-4e1b-9036-200b2aa5edcb', 'If AI can code, what Happens to Beginner Developers?', 'if-ai-can-code-what-happens-to-beginner-developers-gjf0', 'AI is changing the work developers do, but the bigger question may be how the next generation gets its first opportunity.', '<p>If AI Can Code, What Happens to Beginner Developers?</p><p>A few years ago, learning to code often meant starting small.</p><p>You might spend hours fixing a simple bug, building a basic website, or trying to understand why one line of code broke everything else.</p><p>Those small tasks were part of how beginners gained experience.</p><p>Now, AI can handle many of those tasks in seconds.</p><p>You can describe what you want, and an AI tool can generate code almost immediately.</p><p>That sounds helpful.</p><p>But it raises an important question:</p><p>If AI can handle some of the tasks beginners traditionally used to learn, how do beginner developers get the experience they need?</p><p>AI Is Changing the Beginner''s Role</p><p>It is easy to look at AI and assume that developers will simply be replaced.</p><p>The reality is more complicated.</p><p>AI can generate code, explain errors, suggest solutions and help developers work through programming problems. But someone still needs to understand the code, test it and decide whether it actually solves the problem.</p><p>The World Economic Forum recently highlighted this challenge: as AI takes over more routine tasks, companies may need to rethink how junior developers gain experience and develop the skills needed to become senior developers. 1</p><p>So the important question may no longer be:</p><p>"Can AI write the code?"</p><p>Instead, it may be:</p><p>"Can the developer understand what the AI wrote and know whether it is correct?"</p><p>Learning to Code Still Matters</p><p>AI can make programming faster, but that does not mean beginners can skip learning the fundamentals.</p><p>A developer who understands programming logic, databases, APIs and debugging can use AI much more effectively than someone who simply copies whatever it produces.</p><p>Imagine an AI generates 200 lines of code for you.</p><p>If everything works, great.</p><p>But what happens when something breaks?</p><p>If you understand the code, you can investigate the problem.</p><p>If you don''t, you may have no idea where to start.</p><p>That is why understanding the technology behind the tools may become even more important as AI becomes better at generating code.</p><p>The Skills May Be Changing</p><p>Being a good developer has never been only about typing code quickly.</p><p>Problem-solving matters.</p><p>Critical thinking matters.</p><p>Understanding what a user actually needs matters.</p><p>Knowing how different parts of a system work together matters.</p><p>These skills become especially important when AI is involved.</p><p>The World Economic Forum''s recent discussion of entry-level software work points to skills such as critical thinking, technical understanding, domain knowledge and real-world project experience as important parts of preparing developers for an AI-assisted workplace. 2</p><p>In other words, beginners may need to learn how to work with AI rather than compete against it.</p><p>But There Is Still a Big Question</p><p>There is a problem that AI cannot solve by itself.</p><p>Beginners need opportunities to gain experience.</p><p>Traditionally, junior developers learned by working on smaller tasks, reviewing code, fixing bugs and learning from experienced developers.</p><p>If AI takes over more of those routine tasks, companies need to find new ways for beginners to learn.</p><p>Otherwise, there could be fewer opportunities for people to gain the experience needed to become senior developers later.</p><p>That is one of the biggest questions surrounding AI and entry-level technology jobs today.</p><p>So, What Happens Next?</p><p>AI is unlikely to make learning software development pointless.</p><p>Instead, it may change what it means to be a beginner developer.</p><p>A new developer may need to spend less time memorizing every piece of syntax and more time understanding how software works, solving problems, checking AI-generated code and building real projects.</p><p>The technology will continue changing.</p><p>The tools will continue improving.</p><p>But someone still has to decide what should be built, why it should be built and whether the result actually works.</p><p>That person still needs knowledge.</p><p>And that is why the future of beginner developers may not be about competing with AI.</p><p>It may be about learning how to use it without losing the ability to think for yourself.</p><p>What do you think?</p><p>Will AI make it harder for beginners to enter software development, or will it create a new way for them to learn?</p><p>Share your thoughts in the comments.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789511765/echo-gist/32428e86-4409-45bd-8488-60480b94c940/q35fxqdygo3gjhkgxfk8.jpg', 'career', '{"ai","softwaredevelopement","techcareers","beginners"}', '32428e86-4409-45bd-8488-60480b94c940', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 5, 2, 1, '2026-09-15T22:36:59.602Z', '2026-09-19T15:10:01.747Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('cd1425d5-9bbf-40c2-83a3-15097a144646', 'The Battle of the Superphones: iPhone Duo vs. iPhone 18 Pro Max!', 'the-battle-of-the-superphones-iphone-duo-vs-iphone-18-pro-max-94js', 'Have you ever wondered what would happen if a secret agent notebook and a giant rocket ship had a friendly showdown?Well, in the magical world of gadgets, two i…', '<p>Have you ever wondered what would happen if a secret agent notebook and a giant rocket ship had a friendly showdown?</p><p>Well, in the magical world of gadgets, two incredible pocket-sized computers are showing off their coolest tricks: the clever <strong>iPhone Duo</strong> and the mighty <strong>iPhone 18 Pro Max</strong>! Let’s peek inside their digital toolboxes to see how they work.</p><h3>1. The Origami Wonder: A Screen That Folds Like Magic!</h3><p>Imagine a tasty sandwich. When it’s closed, it fits right in your lunchbox. But open it wide, and boom—you have a big, delicious feast! That is exactly how the <strong>iPhone Duo</strong> works.</p><p>It is a foldable phone made with a tiny, super-smooth hinge—which works just like the flexible elbow in your arm. When folded in half, it slips easily into the tiny coin pocket of your jeans. But unfold it, and it blossoms into two screens side-by-side! You can watch your favorite cartoon on the top half while drawing a silly mustache on a photo on the bottom half. It’s like carrying two mini-tablets that snap together like magic puzzle pieces!</p><h3>2. The Heavyweight Champion: A Giant Screen with Falcon Eyes!</h3><p>Now meet the giant: the <strong>iPhone 18 Pro Max</strong>. This phone doesn’t bend or fold—instead, it is built like an unbreakable shield!</p><p>Think of its brain like a bustling highway where billions of tiny electric cars zoom by at light speed without ever causing a traffic jam. Because it’s so big and powerful, it never slows down, even during the wildest 3D video games. And its cameras? They act just like the sharp eyes of a high-flying falcon! You could stand on one side of a soccer field and zoom all the way across to count the spots on a ladybug sitting on the goalpost.</p><h3>3. Which Pocket Pal Wins the Crown?</h3><p>So, who wins the trophy? It depends on your superhero mission!</p><p>If you love secret-agent gadgets, drawing, and having a phone that flips open like a treasure chest, the <strong>iPhone Duo</strong> is your ultimate sidekick. But if you want a giant movie screen, marathon battery life that lasts all weekend, and superstar cameras, the <strong>iPhone 18 Pro Max</strong> takes the gold ribbon!</p><blockquote><p><strong>Fun Fact:</strong> The ultra-strong glass on these phones is baked in super-hot ovens so tough that it’s harder than steel—making it strong enough to survive accidental drops from your treehouse!</p></blockquote><p><strong>Think About This:</strong> If you could invent your very own dream phone, would it fold into a tiny cube, fly like a mini drone, or make you fresh warm cookies on demand?</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789509029/echo-gist/290c2973-016c-40b3-aee0-77b5eb493875/xzvjumcrkrnhzxpohi1d.jpg', 'technology', '{"technology","iphone18promax","apple","iphoneduo","latestapple"}', '290c2973-016c-40b3-aee0-77b5eb493875', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 0, 0, 0, '2026-09-15T21:51:52.272Z', '2026-09-16T11:04:22.170Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('eecb8556-a755-43ba-b158-91d13f2f3267', 'The Stranger Who Became My Friend', 'the-stranger-who-became-my-friend-ji63', '…', '', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1786378480/echo-gist/ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731/fbzoristfu7nfmn9xu8l.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731', FALSE, FALSE, 1, 22, 0, 0, '2026-08-10T16:16:02.206Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:03.911Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('411e49a5-db28-4b12-84d2-3808b2a7493b', 'The Girl Who Kept Going', 'the-girl-who-kept-going-k365', 'There was a girl who kept telling herself, “I’ll try again tomorrow.”Not because she was lazy.Not because she didn’t have dreams.She was simply tired.Tired of s…', '<p>There was a girl who kept telling herself, “I’ll try again tomorrow.”</p><p>Not because she was lazy.</p><p>Not because she didn’t have dreams.</p><p>She was simply tired.</p><p>Tired of starting things and wondering if they would work out. Tired of watching other people move forward while she felt like she was standing in the same place. Tired of pretending she had everything figured out when, deep down, she was still trying to find her way.</p><p>But there was something she refused to do.</p><p>Give up.</p><p>Some mornings, she woke up with confidence. Other mornings, she woke up with questions.</p><p>Am I good enough?</p><p>Will things ever change?</p><p>What if I fail again?</p><p>Still, she got up.</p><p>She learned something new.</p><p>She wrote another page.</p><p>She applied for another opportunity.</p><p>She tried again.</p><p>And slowly, she began to understand something important:</p><p>Progress doesn''t always look like success.</p><p>Sometimes, progress is simply refusing to stop.</p><p>The girl didn''t know exactly where her journey would take her. She didn''t have all the answers. She didn''t even know how long it would take.</p><p>But she knew she wanted a different life.</p><p>So she kept going.</p><p>One word.</p><p>One lesson.</p><p>One opportunity.</p><p>One day at a time.</p><p>Maybe that''s what courage really is not being certain that everything will work out, but choosing to continue even when you''re not sure.</p><p>And perhaps, somewhere along the way, the girl will look back and realize that all those small steps she almost dismissed were actually taking her somewhere.</p><p>She just had to keep going.</p><p>Winny ✍🏽</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789156264/echo-gist/62580ebc-d0a6-462b-b0a2-612472fca3e2/lrboajzdclf1sej9miov.png', 'technology', '{}', '62580ebc-d0a6-462b-b0a2-612472fca3e2', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 6, 2, 1, '2026-09-11T19:54:02.531Z', '2026-09-18T11:10:31.192Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('6e919d46-62fb-4d53-91ff-09ceaff179d6', 'The Future Isn''t Just AI. It''s Knowing What to Do With It.', 'the-future-isnt-just-ai-its-knowing-what-to-do-with-it-ahpf', 'I asked AI to help me explain a scholarship.It gave me an answer.It just wasn''t the answer I needed.That small interaction ended up teaching me something I didn…', '<p>I asked AI to help me explain a scholarship.</p><p>It gave me an answer.</p><p>It just wasn''t the answer I needed.</p><p>That small interaction ended up teaching me something I didn''t expect about the future of AI.</p><p>A few years ago, knowing how to use a computer was an advantage.</p><p>Then it became knowing how to use the internet.</p><p>Now we''re entering another shift.</p><p>AI is becoming part of how we learn, work, create, research, and solve problems.</p><p>But I don''t think the biggest advantage will belong to the people who simply know how to open ChatGPT and type a question.</p><p>It will belong to the people who know what to ask, why they''re asking it, and what to do with the answer.</p><p>I got a small glimpse of this while working on a final project for an AI course.</p><p>I wanted to solve a problem that felt simple:</p><p>What happens when a student finds a scholarship opportunity but has no idea where to start?</p><p>A scholarship page can contain eligibility requirements, required documents, deadlines, application instructions, and several other details.</p><p>For someone already overwhelmed by the process, knowing that the opportunity exists isn''t always the problem.</p><p>Knowing what to do next is.</p><p>So I created ScholarGuide AI a concept designed to help students break down scholarship information and turn it into a clearer application plan.</p><p>At first, I did what most people probably would.</p><p>I asked:</p><p>"Can you explain this scholarship?"</p><p>AI explained it.</p><p>But the response wasn''t particularly useful.</p><p>So I tried again.</p><p>I gave it more information about the applicant and asked for guidance that was more specific to their situation.</p><p>The response improved.</p><p>Then I realized something.</p><p>Maybe the problem wasn''t the AI.</p><p>Maybe I hadn''t told it enough about the problem I was trying to solve.</p><p>So I changed the way I prompted it.</p><p>I structured the prompt around four things:</p><p>Goal.</p><p>Context.</p><p>Source.</p><p>Expectations.</p><p>And suddenly, the interaction became much more useful.</p><p>Instead of simply summarizing the scholarship, the AI could help organize things like:</p><p>- likely eligibility</p><p>- required documents</p><p>- missing information</p><p>- application steps</p><p>- preparation timelines</p><p>The technology hadn''t suddenly become smarter.</p><p>I had simply learned how to work with it better.</p><p>And that changed the way I think about AI.</p><p>AI isn''t necessarily valuable because it can give you an answer.</p><p>It''s valuable when you understand the problem you''re trying to solve, give it the right context, set the right expectations, and know how to evaluate what comes back.</p><p>That''s a very different skill from simply knowing how to use ChatGPT.</p><p>And I think this distinction is going to matter more and more.</p><p>Because AI is becoming easier to access.</p><p>The barrier is no longer simply "Can you use AI?"</p><p>The more interesting question is:</p><p>"What can you actually do with it?"</p><p>Two people can have access to the same AI tool and walk away with completely different results.</p><p>One asks for an answer.</p><p>The other uses it to explore a problem, challenge an idea, organize information, find gaps, test possibilities, and make better decisions.</p><p>The difference isn''t necessarily the tool.</p><p>It''s the person using it.</p><p>The future may be powered by AI, but I don''t think everyone will benefit from it equally.</p><p>The people who learn to work with AI intentionally will have an advantage not because AI makes them smarter, but because they learn how to turn a powerful tool into something useful.</p><p>And perhaps that''s one of the skills worth developing now:</p><p>Don''t just learn AI.</p><p>Learn how to think with it.</p><p>That''s what building ScholarGuide AI taught me.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1786340822/echo-gist/6110aa8e-6f0d-4df4-ad0b-2ac775af342f/ydpdw4yq7omfulj1cdyz.png', 'ai', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 16, 2, 0, '2026-08-10T05:54:49.768Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:04.418Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('a355cadd-260f-4851-9ffb-cb9a6d59b54f', 'The hardest part was never the writing. It was believing in yourself and your story.', 'the-hardest-part-was-never-the-writing-it-was-believing-in-yourself-and-your-sto-itjq', 'We know that feeling staring at a blank page, typing something, deleting it, typing it again. Wondering if it''s good enough. Wondering if anyone will care. Wond…', '<p></p><p>We know that feeling staring at a blank page, typing something, deleting it, typing it again. Wondering if it''s good enough. Wondering if anyone will care. Wondering if you should just keep it to yourself, like you always do.</p><p>At Echogist, we want you to stop wondering and start sharing.</p><p>This is a place built for writers who''ve never called themselves writers. For the person who''s been keeping notes on their phone for years but never showed anyone. For the one who writes better than they speak. For the one who''s been told "you should write a book" but never believed it enough to try.</p><p>You don''t need credentials here. You don''t need a following. You don''t need perfect grammar or a fancy vocabulary. You just need something true  a memory, an opinion, a feeling, a moment that changed you  and the willingness to let it exist somewhere other than your notes app.</p><p>Because the truth is, someone out there has been waiting for exactly the words you haven''t said yet. Your story might be the mirror they need, or the permission they''ve been looking for to finally write their own.</p><p>So this is your sign. Write the thing you''ve been avoiding. Share the piece you weren''t sure was "good enough." Let your story exist in the world, not just in your head.</p><p>Welcome to Echogist where every writer, seen or unseen, finally gets their stage.</p><p>We can''t wait to read what you''ve been holding onto 💜</p><p></p>', NULL, 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 12, 1, 0, '2026-08-05T17:18:32.402Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:08.421Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('54d6ab89-62ca-4562-8d39-73b5863e997c', 'What Happened After Heartbreak?', 'what-happened-after-heartbreak-kdpo', 'So, they left you. And what happened next? Did the world stop spinning? Did the sun refuse to rise the next morning because the person you loved was no longer i…', '<p>So, they left you. And what happened next? Did the world stop spinning? Did the sun refuse to rise the next morning because the person you loved was no longer in your life? Did their departure somehow put your own life on hold? Of course not. Their life continued, and whether you like it or not, yours continued too. The only question is: <strong>what are you going to do with the life that continued after they left?</strong></p><p>Maybe you think their life will be paused because they left you. Maybe you think their light will stop shining because they walked away. Perhaps you are sitting somewhere waiting for their life to fall apart, hoping that one day they will finally realize what they lost and come running back to you. Then think again. Someone leaving you does not automatically mean their destiny will be denied, just as your heartbreak does not mean yours has been cancelled. Life does not pause because a relationship ended.</p><p>And honestly, some of us need to stop watching the person who left us and start watching ourselves. We spend so much time checking whether they are happy, whether they have found someone else, whether they are struggling, or whether karma has finally arrived at their doorstep. We become spectators in someone else''s life while our own life is waiting for us to participate in it. You are sitting there waiting for karma to deal with someone while your own dreams are waiting for you to deal with them. You must be a comedian.</p><p>Yes, you were disappointed. Yes, you may have been hurt. Maybe you genuinely believed that person was going to be part of your future, and suddenly you had to imagine your life without them. Give yourself permission to acknowledge that. You don''t have to pretend that nothing happened or force yourself to be fine the next morning. But there has to be a point where disappointment stops being an experience and starts becoming a lifestyle. You cannot keep building your identity around the person who left you.</p><p>Sometimes, the space created by someone''s absence can become the space where you finally start doing things for yourself. Maybe you have been putting off that business because you were too occupied with the relationship. Maybe there is a skill you have always wanted to learn but never made time for. Maybe you have always wanted to write, design, study, create content, start a small business, improve your career, or simply become more disciplined with your life. Now you have time. What are you going to do with it?</p><p>This is where many people get it wrong. They say, "I am going to become successful so that they will regret leaving me." But why should your success still revolve around the person who left? Why are they still the centre of your plans? Build because you want to build. Learn because you want to grow. Start the business because you have something to offer. Improve yourself because you deserve to become better, not because you are waiting for someone to notice the difference.</p><p>And please, don''t turn your entire healing process into a competition with someone who may not even know they are competing with you. You don''t need to become richer than them, prettier than their new partner, more successful than their friends, or more visible on social media just to prove a point. That is still allowing their existence to control your decisions. The real win is when you stop asking, "Will they see what I have become?" and start asking, <strong>"What kind of person do I want to become?"</strong></p><p>There is also something we don''t talk about enough: sometimes people leave at a time when we desperately needed a change. That doesn''t necessarily mean they left <em>for our benefit</em>, because we cannot always know another person''s reasons for leaving. But their departure can still become an opportunity if we choose to use it. A closed door can leave you standing outside complaining about the door, or it can make you look around and realize there are other doors you have never tried to open.</p><p>Imagine spending six months waiting for someone to regret leaving you when you could have spent those six months building something. Imagine checking their page every morning while someone else is spending those same mornings learning a skill that will change their income. Imagine hoping they will come back while the life you said you wanted is sitting untouched because you are still waiting for the past to give you permission to move forward.</p><p>Don''t do that to yourself.</p><p>Let the relationship end if it has ended. Let the disappointment be real, but let it have an expiry date. Pick yourself up and look at the things you have neglected. What did you stop doing? What dream have you postponed? What skill have you always wanted to develop? What business idea have you been talking about for years? What kind of person have you always wanted to become? Start there.</p><p>Maybe you can start small. You don''t need a huge amount of money or a perfect plan before you begin. You can start with what you already have. If you can design, design. If you can write, write. If you can bake, bake. If you know how to sell, sell. If you have a talent that you have been hiding because you were too busy worrying about a relationship, bring it back to life. Your next chapter does not have to begin with something dramatic. Sometimes it begins with one decision to stop wasting your own potential.</p><p>And don''t be surprised if your life starts looking different after you make that decision. You may discover abilities you never knew you had because you finally have the time and freedom to explore them. You may meet new people, learn new things, make mistakes, start again, and eventually build something you are genuinely proud of. The person who left may never see any of it. They may see it and say nothing. They may hear about it years later. They may even come back. But none of those possibilities should determine whether you move forward.</p><p>Because your life cannot be built around the hope that someone who left will one day return.</p><p>One day, you may look back and realize that the biggest thing you gained after that relationship was not another person. It was yourself. You became more intentional with your time. You learned to make decisions without constantly seeking someone''s approval. You became better at recognizing what you want and what you will no longer accept. You built something. You failed at something and tried again. You became more confident. You discovered that there was a whole life waiting for you outside the relationship you thought was your whole world.</p><p>That is why you shouldn''t be afraid of the question, <strong>"What happens after heartbreak?"</strong></p><p>Something happens after heartbreak.</p><p>Life happens.</p><p>Bills still need to be paid. Dreams still need to be pursued. Opportunities still come. New skills can still be learned. Businesses can still be started. Books can still be written. Careers can still grow. Friendships can still be made. There are still places you haven''t visited, things you haven''t learned, people you haven''t met, and versions of yourself you haven''t discovered.<br></p><p>So don''t sit around waiting for the person who left you to lose their light. Their light is not your responsibility, and neither is their destiny. Your responsibility is what you do with yours.</p><p>Maybe they left you at a chapter you thought was supposed to last forever. Fine. Turn the page.</p><p>Don''t let them meet you where they left you.</p><p>Let them meet a person who kept going. Better still, don''t spend your life hoping they meet you at all. Build a life so meaningful that whether they return, regret leaving, or never look back becomes irrelevant.</p><p>Because heartbreak may have ended something, but it does not have to end <strong>you</strong>.</p><p>So, what happened after heartbreak?</p><p>That depends on you.</p><p>Did you see the end of a relationship and mistake it for the end of your life?</p><p>Or did you look at the empty space it left behind and say, <strong>"Alright. What can I build here?"</strong></p><p><br></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789482542/echo-gist/26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2/pa5egiey4pwtsxpmklfi.jpg', 'opinion', '{"whathappenedafterheartbreak","lifeafterheartbreak","focusonyou","growthmindset","buildyourlife"}', '26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2', TRUE, FALSE, 7, 1, 1, 0, '2026-09-15T14:32:35.595Z', '2026-09-15T15:54:19.350Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('a333c7c5-8c83-4e96-805b-7d34a682fa38', 'You Don''t Have To Have Your Life Figured Out Yet', 'you-dont-have-to-have-your-life-figured-out-yet-k09p', 'Why feeling behind doesn''t mean you''re actually behind.', '<p>Have you ever looked at your life  and wondered, "<strong>What</strong> <strong>am I even doing?"</strong></p><p></p><p>You open social media and someone your age has graduated.  Someone has landed their dream job. Someone is starting a business. Someone is getting married. Someone is traveling to places you have only seen in pictures. And then there''s you.</p><p>Still trying to figure things out. </p><p>Sometimes, it can make you feel like everyone received a manual for life except you. </p><p>You start asking questions you don''t even have answers to.</p><p>Am I falling behind?</p><p>Why does everyone seems to know what they want except me? </p><p>If you ever felt this way, I want you to know something: </p><p><strong>You are not necessarily behind. </strong></p><p>You may simply be on a different part of your journey.</p><p></p><h3>Life Isn''t a Race</h3><p>Growing up can sometimes feel like there is a deadline for everything.  You are supposed to finish school at a certain age. Get a job. Become financially stable. Find love. Get married.  Build a home. Become successful.</p><p>And somehow,  you are expected to accomplish all of these things according to a timeline that nobody actually gave you.</p><p>But who decided that everyone''s life has to follow the same schedule?</p><p>Some people find their passion when they''re young. Others spend years trying different things before discovering what truly makes them happy.</p><p>Some people get their dream job immediately after school. </p><p>Others have to apply again and again before someone finally gives them a chance.</p><p>Some people know exactly what they want.</p><p>Others are still discovering themselves. </p><p>Neither person is automatically doing better.</p><p><strong>Different doesn''t mean behind.</strong></p><h3>Social Media Doesn''t Show You The Whole Story </h3><p>This is something I have had to remind myself of too.</p><p>When you look at someone''s social media page, you''re actually seeing the part of their life they chose to show you. You see the graduation picture. You see the car. You see the engagement announcement. You see business launch. You see the Vacation.</p><p>But you don’t see the uncertainty that came before those moments.</p><p>You don''t see the applications that were rejected. You don''t see the night they questioned themselves. You don’t see the mistakes. </p><p>You don''t see the moments  when they wondered whether they were making the right decisions. </p><p>You''re comparing your <strong>behind-the-scenes </strong>to someone else''s <strong>highlight reel.</strong></p><p>And that''s an unfair comparison.</p><p>So celebrate people when good things happen to them. Be happy for them. But don''t turn their achievements into evidence that you''re not doing enough.  </p><p><strong>Someone else''s progress does not cancel yours.</strong></p><p></p><h3>You Are Allowed to Change Your Mind </h3><p>Maybe you once thought you knew exactly what you wanted to do with your life.</p><p>Then things changed. </p><p>Maybe the career you imagined for yourself no longer feels right. Maybe the dream you had years ago doesn''t excite you anymore.  Maybe you started something and realized you wanted something completely different.</p><p>That''s okay.</p><p>You are growing. And growth sometimes changes your perspective.  You are allowed to say: <strong>"This isn''t what I want anymore".</strong></p><p>You''re allowed to start over.</p><p>You are allowed to try something new.</p><p>You are allowed to take a different path.</p><p>Changing direction doesn''t automatically mean you wasted your time. Sometimes the wrong road teaches you enough to recognize the one.</p><p></p><h3>Start With What You Have</h3><p>One of the biggest mistakes we make is waiting until we have everything figured out before we begin. We tell ourselves: </p><p>"I''ll start when I''m ready". </p><p>"I''ll do it when I have enough money."</p><p>"I''ll begin when I know exactly what I''m doing". </p><p>But sometimes, readiness comes <strong>after </strong>you start. </p><p>Write the first page. </p><p>Take the course.</p><p>Send the application.</p><p>Start the small business.</p><p>Make the phone call.</p><p>Try the thing you''ve been thinking about.</p><p>It doesn''t have to be perfect. </p><p>It just has to begin.</p><p>You don''t need to see the entire staircase before taking the first step</p><p></p><h3>Give Yourself Time</h3><p>Not every season of your life will look impressive.</p><p>Some seasons are loud.</p><p>Some are exciting.</p><p>Some are full of achievements.</p><p>But some seasons are quiet.</p><p>They are the seasons where you are learning, making mistakes, discovering yourself and slowly becoming stronger.</p><p>And just because nothing exciting seems to be happening doesn''t mean you''re not growing.</p><p>Think about a seed.</p><p>When you first put it into the soil, there is nothing impressive to look at.</p><p>For a while, it seems like nothing is happening.</p><p>But underneath the surface, something is taking place.</p><p>Roots are forming.</p><p>Growth is happening where nobody can see it.</p><p>Maybe you''re in that kind of season right now.</p><p>Maybe your progress is simply happening quietly.</p><p>Don''t give up on yourself just because your growth isn''t visible yet.</p><h3>So, What If You Don''t Have It All Figured Out?</h3><p>Honestly?</p><p>That''s okay.</p><p>You don''t need to know exactly where you''ll be five years from now.</p><p>You don''t need to have every answer today.</p><p>You don''t need to become everything at once.</p><p>Focus on the next step.</p><p>Learn what you can.</p><p>Try what interests you.</p><p>Make mistakes.</p><p>Start again when you need to.</p><p>And give yourself permission to grow at your own pace.</p><p>One day, you may look back at this confusing season and realize that you were not actually lost.</p><p>You were learning.</p><p>You were growing.</p><p>You were becoming the person you were meant to be.</p><p>So the next time you feel like everyone is moving ahead while you''re standing still, remember this:</p><p><strong>You are not running their race.</strong></p><p>Your journey is yours.</p><p>Your timing is yours.</p><p>And your story is still being written.</p><p><strong>Don''t judge your entire life by a chapter that hasn''t finished yet.</strong></p><p></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787694744/echo-gist/26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2/hvo7pinx7wgizuazf6mz.png', 'technology', '{"personalgrowth","lifeadvice","selfdiscovery","motivation","lifelessons"}', '26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 104, 11, 7, '2026-08-25T21:58:08.418Z', '2026-09-22T17:22:36.769Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('ee00d0ae-d1e2-4bfb-98cd-90c02fc23a73', 'WHY NIGERIAN INTERNET DRAMA GETS MORE ATTENTION THAN IMPORTANT CONVERSATIONS', 'why-nigerian-internet-drama-gets-more-attention-than-important-conversations--cik3', 'We react to everything. But are we paying attention to what matters?', '<p>A serious thread about the naira, or fuel prices, or a bill that will actually affect your life gets just three retweets, maybe a sigh emoji, just maybe. A celebrity''s ex posts a cryptic story at 2am and by morning it''s a full trending topic, with think pieces, screenshots, and strangers arguing in your mentions about a relationship they have no stake in.</p><p>You have noticed it. You have  probably even taken part in it, I know. Not because you are shallow but because the drama was simply built better than the conversation.</p><p> It''s Not That People Don''t Care.</p><p>The easy explanation is that Nigerians on the internet are more interested in gossip than substance. That''s lazy, and it''s not even true. People care deeply, you can see it in how quickly a fundraiser moves when it''s framed right, or how fired up a comment section gets when a real issue is presented as a story instead of a statistic.</p><p>The difference is not the caring. It''s the packaging. Drama arrives pre-loaded with characters, stakes, a villain, and a cliffhanger. A policy issue arrives as a paragraph you have to work to understand. One asks nothing of you but your attention. The other asks for effort you may not have left after a full day of surviving Lagos, or Abuja, or wherever you''re doing your own version of surviving.</p><p>THE ALGORITHM IS NOT NEUTRAL, IT''S A CO-AUTHOR </p><p>Here''s the part most people skip over, you are not just choosing drama over depth. Something is choosing it for you, repeatedly, until it feels like your own preference.</p><p>Engagement: not truth, not important, is what gets rewarded. A screenshot of someone''s messy breakup will always outperform a thread explaining subsidy removal, because outrage and curiosity are cheaper to trigger than understanding. The apps are not asking "what does this person need to know." It''s asking "what will keep this person scrolling for four more minutes." Those are rarely the same question.</p><p>THE UNCOMFORTABLE PART </p><p>Here''s what stings a little, drama gives you something real conversations often don''t, a clear villain, a clean side to take, and the satisfying feeling of having an opinion without the responsibility of holding it for long. Tomorrow there will be a new scandal, and today''s outrage will be forgotten with no consequences to you. But a real issue, insecurity, unemployment, a failing system does not resolve by tomorrow. It just sits there, asking you to keep caring long after it stopped being entertaining. Drama is a story with an ending. Real life rarely gives you one.</p><p>SO WHAT DO YOU ACTUALLY DO WITH THIS?Not "log off" that advice rarely survives past the weekend. You know what I''m talking about.Try noticing instead. Before you engage with the next trending topic, ask what it''s actually asking of you, your attention, or your outrage, or nothing at all. That one second pause does not make you above it. It just makes the choice yours again, instead of the algorithm''s.</p><p>Maybe the goal was never to care about the important things and ignore the noise completely. Maybe it''s learning to tell, in real time, which one you''re actually being handed, the story, or the thing that was trying to look like one.</p><p>How relatable is this to you?</p><p>We are eager to hear from you at the comment.</p><p>Like. Share. Follow. Comment.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787726139/echo-gist/817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4/gzre0bpewcywcllj3hhw.png', 'lifestyle', '{"lifestylesocialmediaentertainmentsocialdrama"}', '817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 153, 5, 1, '2026-08-26T06:50:00.354Z', '2026-09-22T19:34:57.085Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('d138cee3-4731-4d7d-a861-7d3c3feae46a', 'Should You Call Your Boss by His First Name? Tony Elumelu Video Has Nigerians Divided', 'should-you-call-your-boss-by-his-first-name-tony-elumelu-video-has-nigerians-div-5cp2', 'She said “Good morning, Tony.” He replied that she should call him “Mr Elumelu” or “TOE.” Now Nigerians are debating something bigger than the viral video: if your workplace teaches you to use first names, should that rule still apply when you''re speaking to the chairman?', '<p><strong>A young UBA trainee’s “Good morning, Tony” greeting to Tony Elumelu has sparked a wider debate about respect, workplace hierarchy and whether first-name culture belongs in Nigerian offices.</strong></p><p>A brief exchange between United Bank for Africa (UBA) Group Chairman Tony Elumelu and a young graduate trainee has turned into a much bigger conversation about how Nigerians should address senior colleagues and bosses.</p><p>The incident happened during the graduation ceremony of UBA’s Graduate Management Accelerated Programme (GMAP), where a trainee addressed Elumelu simply as “Tony” while speaking during an interactive session. UBA itself confirms that the 2026 GMAP graduation ceremony involved more than 700 young professionals in Lagos.&nbsp;</p><p>Elumelu appeared to initially mishear the greeting, asking whether the trainee had said “Toyin.” When she clarified that she had said, “Good morning, Tony,” he stopped her and told her to address him as “Mr Elumelu” or “TOE.”&nbsp;</p><p>The exchange was brief, but the question it raised has proved anything but simple: <strong>when you work for an organisation that encourages first-name communication, does that mean you should address the chairman by his first name too?</strong></p><h2><strong>The Viral Moment</strong></h2><p>In the video circulating online, the young woman stands to interact with Elumelu and begins her greeting with “Good morning, Tony.”</p><p>Elumelu initially appears to think she said “Toyin.” She repeats herself and makes it clear that she is addressing him as Tony.</p><p>He then corrects her.</p><p>According to multiple reports of the video, Elumelu tells her she should call him “Mr Elumelu” or “TOE,” rather than Tony. He also says he does not subscribe to what he describes as “Oyinbo life.”&nbsp;</p><p>The trainee subsequently addresses him as “Mr Elumelu” before continuing with her question.</p><p>There is no indication from the available reporting that the exchange developed into a serious confrontation. What happened afterward, however, was largely outside the room.</p><p>The clip went viral.</p><h2><strong>Why Did a Simple Greeting Become Such a Big Deal?</strong></h2><p>For many Nigerians, the answer is obvious.</p><p>How you address someone can communicate much more than their name.</p><p>In traditional Nigerian settings, calling an older person “Sir” or “Ma” is often considered basic courtesy. Senior executives may be addressed as “Chairman,” “Boss,” “Oga,” “Mr [surname]” or by a professional title.</p><p>That is particularly true when there is a significant difference in age or status.</p><p>So, to some Nigerians watching the video, hearing a young trainee address one of the country''s most prominent businessmen simply as “Tony” sounded unnecessarily casual.</p><p>But there is another side to the story.</p><h2><strong>What If Your Workplace Teaches You to Use First Names?</strong></h2><p>This is where the situation becomes more complicated.</p><p>Some people who have worked in Nigerian banks and corporate organisations have come forward to argue that the trainee may not have intended any disrespect.</p><p>One business leader, Modupe Okeowo, said in a reaction shared on X that she understood the woman to be a new recruit and suggested that her training may have contributed to the situation.</p><p>Okeowo said new employees can be taught to address people within an organisation by their first names and suggested that the woman may simply have carried that practice into the interaction with Elumelu.&nbsp;</p><p>Other former bank employees have made similar arguments, saying first-name culture can be deliberately built into corporate training.</p><p>And this is important because the woman was not simply a random member of the public approaching a billionaire on the street.</p><p>She was reportedly a graduate trainee participating in a UBA programme.</p><p>That changes the context.</p><h2><strong>Is Calling Your Boss by His First Name Actually Disrespectful?</strong></h2><p>Not necessarily.</p><p>In some modern workplaces, using someone''s first name is completely normal.</p><p>It can make communication feel less hierarchical and encourage employees to speak more freely. Some companies deliberately adopt this culture because they want employees to feel comfortable approaching senior executives.</p><p>And there is evidence that first-name culture has existed within parts of the Nigerian banking industry for years.</p><p>So, if an organisation tells its employees, “Call everyone by their first name,” would it really be fair to accuse an employee of disrespect for following that culture?</p><p>That is where the debate gets interesting.</p><h2><strong>But There Is Also a Difference Between Company Culture and Personal Preference</strong></h2><p>There is another reasonable argument.</p><p>Even if a company encourages informal communication, an individual may still have a personal preference about how they want to be addressed.</p><p>In this case, Elumelu made his preference clear.</p><p>He did not tell the trainee that she could not speak to him. He simply told her what he preferred to be called in that setting.</p><p>And once someone has clearly stated a preferred form of address, there is a strong argument for respecting it.</p><p>The disagreement, therefore, may not really be about whether “Tony” is inherently disrespectful.</p><p>It may be about <strong>knowing when a general workplace rule meets an individual''s personal preference.</strong></p><h2><strong>The Nigerian Workplace Is Changing</strong></h2><p>This incident also exposes a larger generational shift.</p><p>Older Nigerians generally grew up in environments where age and position carried significant weight.</p><p>You did not casually call your teacher by their first name.</p><p>You did not call your parents'' friends by their first names.</p><p>And calling an older person without a title could easily be interpreted as poor home training.</p><p>But younger professionals are entering workplaces that increasingly operate differently.</p><p>They are working in multinational companies, startups, and modern corporate environments where CEOs can sit with junior employees, encourage them to challenge ideas, and even ask to be addressed by their first names.</p><p>The result is an interesting collision between <strong>traditional Nigerian respect culture and modern corporate culture.</strong></p><p>Neither side necessarily has to be completely wrong.</p><h2><strong>What Are Nigerians Saying?</strong></h2><p>The reactions to the Elumelu video have reflected this divide.</p><p>Some Nigerians believe the trainee should have known better and addressed Elumelu as “Sir,” “Chairman” or “Mr Elumelu.”</p><p>Others believe she was simply following the culture she had been taught during her training and that the incident should not have become a public spectacle.</p><p>Some former bank employees have particularly defended the young woman, arguing that first-name communication is not unusual in the financial sector.&nbsp;</p><p>Others have taken a more nuanced position: first-name culture may be acceptable inside an organisation, but employees should still learn to read the room when dealing with a very senior executive at a formal event.</p><p>That may be the most practical lesson from the entire incident.</p><h2><strong>So, Who Was Right?</strong></h2><p>Perhaps the better question is not whether Tony Elumelu was right or whether the young woman was wrong.</p><p>It is whether <strong>respect should depend on a person''s name, their tone, the workplace culture or the preference of the person being addressed.</strong></p><p>Imagine working in a company where everyone, from the newest recruit to the CEO, is called by their first name.</p><p>You finish your training and walk into a room where the company''s chairman is standing in front of you.</p><p>Do you automatically switch back to “Sir”?</p><p>Or do you assume that the company''s first-name culture still applies?</p><p>There is no universal answer.</p><p>The safest approach is to understand the culture of the organisation, pay attention to the setting and, most importantly, respect the individual''s stated preference.</p><p>Because while “Good morning, Tony” may sound perfectly normal in one office, it may produce a very different reaction in another.</p><p>And perhaps that is the real lesson from this viral moment: <strong>professional etiquette is not always about knowing the right word. Sometimes, it is about knowing the room.</strong></p><p><strong>If your boss told you to call him by his first name, would you actually do it?</strong></p><hr><p></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787328713/echo-gist/31cf9361-36e5-4829-8ae2-b81652b35275/t36elcrs8yzdu9dkj2px.jpg', 'culture', '{"tonyelumeluubanigerianworkplaceworkplaceculturenigeriannews"}', '31cf9361-36e5-4829-8ae2-b81652b35275', TRUE, FALSE, 6, 7, 0, 0, '2026-08-21T16:15:06.670Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:48.065Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('b5d492d6-d013-45b6-818f-776742e2544d', 'Looks premeditated to me.', 'looks-premeditated-to-me-v3lm', 'When prosecutors argue that an act was premeditated rather than the result of a sudden mental break, they rely on evidence of sequence, timing, and deliberate c…', '<p>When prosecutors argue that an act was premeditated rather than the result of a sudden mental break, they rely on evidence of sequence, timing, and deliberate choice. In the case of Lindsay Clancy, arguments that the deaths of her three children were planned center on specific, documented steps taken on the evening of January 24, 2023.</p><p>The core argument for premeditation rests on the creation of a guaranteed, uninterrupted window of time. Prosecutors highlighted that Clancy sent her husband on two specific errands picking up a prescription at a pharmacy and getting takeout from a restaurant further away in a neighboring town. Digital records from her phone showed she checked the restaurant''s route and travel duration on Apple Maps just before asking him to go. Prosecutors contend this search was a deliberate calculation to confirm she would have roughly 45 to 50 minutes alone in the house.</p><p>Additionally, prosecutors pointed to the physical execution of the acts and her demeanor immediately beforehand. Retrieving exercise bands and using them sequentially required deliberate choice and sustained effort over several minutes, which meets the legal definition of reflection prior to action. Furthermore, during a brief phone call with her husband while he was out, her tone was described as completely calm and ordinary an interaction prosecutors interpret as a conscious attempt to avoid raising alarm or causing him to return early.</p><p>Conversely, defense attorneys and mental health experts argue that these structured actions do not rule out severe mental illness. They contend that individuals suffering from acute postpartum psychosis or severe depressive delusions can still execute multi-step tasks and display outward calm while entirely detached from reality. Whether these actions reflect cold, conscious calculation or the internal logic of a profound psychotic breakdown remains the central point of contention in the case.</p><p></p><p>Note that everything here is based off of what i have read and is made public about this case and I''ll keep keeping y''all posted.  Remember this is a safe place to express ur thoughts.</p><p>       -3enchnad</p>', NULL, 'philosophy', '{}', '173ebb0c-2ac0-4f1b-ba8e-16b7b14dac7d', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 4, 2, 0, '2026-09-03T18:05:42.713Z', '2026-09-19T21:14:10.769Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('c71d6f03-0e72-4515-a03c-f3a2f60e7e03', 'YOU''RE TIRED ALL THE TIME? IT MAY NOT JUST BE LAZINESS', 'youre-tired-all-the-time-it-may-not-just-be-laziness-pc79', 'The Exhaustion That Sleep Doesn''t Fix', '<p>You slept eight hours. You still feel like you fought someone in your dreams. You get up, and within an hour, you are already counting down to when you can lie back down. Somewhere along the way, someone, a parent, a boss, your own inner voice, labeled this "laziness," and you believed it, because it was easier than asking what was actually going on. If rest has not been fixing your tiredness in a while, this is for you. Journey with me. </p><p>THE GUILT BEFORE THE QUESTION </p><p>Most people don''t start by asking "why am I this tired." They start by asking "what''s wrong with me that I can''t just push through like everyone else." The exhaustion gets buried under self judgment before anyone even considers a cause. That order matters. Guilt makes you hide the tiredness, powered through meetings, fake energy in conversations, blaming your own discipline instead of actually investigating it. You end up managing the shame of being tired more than the tiredness itself.</p><p> IT''S RARELY JUST ONE THING </p><p>Fatigue is almost never a single clean cause, which is part of why it''s so easy to dismiss. It''s the combination of broken sleep from a mind that won''t switch off, meals skipped or grabbed in a rush, a body that has not moved properly in weeks, and a nervous system that''s been quietly running on alert for months without a real break. Each piece alone might seem manageable. Stacked together, they add up to a tiredness that no amount of "sleeping earlier tonight" can undo. I''m sure you''re getting my point.</p><p>WHY REST DOESN''T ALWAYS FIX IT </p><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788351977/echo-gist/817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4/ktj39gahbsipugwpbgmj.png" alt="1000761490.png" style="max-width: 100%; border-radius: 8px; margin: 24px 0px; display: block;"><p>Here''s something people don''t say enough, sleep and rest are not always the same thing. I hope you are not surprised? You can get hours of sleep and still be in a state of stress the entire time, jaw tight, mind racing, body never actually relaxing. That''s sleep without recovery, and it explains why you can wake up from a full night feeling like you barely closed your eyes. Real rest requires your nervous system to believe it''s safe to slow down. If you are carrying unresolved stress, unspoken worry, or a body that''s been running on adrenaline, sleep alone will not reach that.</p><p> THE UNCOMFORTABLE TRUTH </p><p>Calling yourself lazy is, in a strange way, easier than asking whether something in your life or your body needs attention. Laziness has a simple fix, try harder. A deeper cause means facing something that might take longer to sort out, cost money, or require admitting you have been ignoring your own warning signs for a while. It''s worth asking honestly, is this actually a discipline problem? or have I been quietly running on empty for longer than I have let myself notice?</p><p>WHEN IT''S WORTH PAYING CLOSER ATTENTION </p><p>Occasional tiredness after a rough week is normal. But fatigue that doesn''t lift after real rest, that comes with other changes in your body, mood, or ability to function, that''s not something to keep explaining away with willpower. That pattern is worth taking to a doctor, not just a better sleep schedule.</p><p>So What Now?</p><p>Stop asking how to be less lazy, and start asking what your tiredness has actually been trying to tell you about your sleep, your stress, your body, or all three. The answer is not always comfortable, but it''s more useful than another pep talk about discipline. Sometimes tired is not a character flaw. Sometimes it''s information you''ve been too busy pushing through to read.</p><p>Did you find this helpful?</p><p>Tell us in the comments.</p><p>Like. Follow. Share. Comment</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788350034/echo-gist/817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4/nrue9fxtqdaho7i0tg96.png', 'health', '{}', '817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 46, 3, 0, '2026-09-02T12:31:52.670Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:50.294Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('da08c929-3ffa-4fb5-8c45-59d08c30add3', '5 AI-Powered Services Beginners Can Sell Online in 2026', '5-ai-powered-services-beginners-can-sell-online-in-2026-rjqp', 'You don''t need to build an AI model to make money with AI. You can use existing tools to solve problems people already pay for.', '<p>5 AI-Powered Services Beginners Can Sell Online in 2026</p><p>When people hear “making money with AI,” they often imagine building the next big AI company.</p><p>But you don''t need to build an AI model to start earning from the technology.</p><p>A simpler approach is to use AI to help you deliver a service that people already need.</p><p>Businesses still need content. They need social media posts. They need videos, research, designs and customer support.</p><p>AI can help you do some of that work faster.</p><p>The important part is understanding that the AI tool itself isn''t what clients are paying for.</p><p>They are paying for the result.</p><p>Here are five services a beginner can start exploring.</p><p>1. AI-Assisted Content Writing</p><p>Businesses and creators constantly need captions, blog posts, product descriptions, emails and other written content.</p><p>AI can help with research, brainstorming and first drafts, while you provide editing, fact-checking and the human touch.</p><p>You could offer services such as:</p><p>• Social media captions</p><p>• Blog articles</p><p>• Product descriptions</p><p>• Email newsletters</p><p>• Website copy</p><p>The goal isn''t to copy and paste whatever AI produces.</p><p>The value comes from turning that first draft into something accurate, useful and appropriate for the client.</p><p>2. AI-Assisted Social Media Management</p><p>Many small businesses struggle to consistently create content for their social media pages.</p><p>AI can help generate content ideas, captions, content calendars and variations of posts.</p><p>You can combine that with tools such as Canva to create graphics and help a business maintain a consistent online presence.</p><p>You don''t necessarily need hundreds of followers yourself.</p><p>You need to understand what the business wants to communicate and create content that fits its audience.</p><p>3. AI-Assisted Video Content</p><p>Short-form video has become an important part of online marketing.</p><p>AI tools can help with tasks such as generating scripts, creating captions, removing unnecessary sections and speeding up parts of the editing process.</p><p>A beginner could offer services such as:</p><p>• Short video editing</p><p>• TikTok or Reels scripts</p><p>• Captions and subtitles</p><p>• YouTube Shorts preparation</p><p>• Promotional videos</p><p>You still need to understand storytelling and what makes a video worth watching.</p><p>AI simply helps you work faster.</p><p>4. AI-Assisted Research and Data Work</p><p>AI can read, summarize and organize large amounts of information much faster than doing everything manually.</p><p>That can be useful for businesses that need research.</p><p>For example, you could help organize competitor information, summarize documents, collect publicly available information or prepare research reports.</p><p>But accuracy matters.</p><p>AI can make mistakes, so anything delivered to a client should be checked before it is presented as fact.</p><p>5. AI-Powered Business Support</p><p>This is where things can become more interesting.</p><p>Businesses are increasingly using AI inside their existing workflows.</p><p>Upwork''s 2026 research shows growing demand for people who can apply AI to existing work, including AI integration, chatbots, data work and other specialized services. 1</p><p>That means you don''t necessarily have to sell “AI” as the product.</p><p>You can sell the result.</p><p>For example, instead of saying:</p><p>“I know how to use ChatGPT.”</p><p>You could say:</p><p>“I can help you create a weekly social media content system.”</p><p>That is a much clearer service.</p><p>Don''t Try to Sell Everything</p><p>One mistake beginners make is trying to become an expert in every AI tool at once.</p><p>You don''t need to.</p><p>Choose one service.</p><p>Learn the tools needed for that service.</p><p>Create two or three sample projects.</p><p>Then start looking for people who might need the service.</p><p>Your first samples don''t have to come from paying clients.</p><p>You can create examples for imaginary businesses or improve sample projects to demonstrate what you can do.</p><p>AI Doesn''t Remove the Need for Skill</p><p>There is an important warning here.</p><p>AI has made producing basic content easier, which also means basic AI-generated work is becoming easier for everyone to produce.</p><p>Upwork''s recent research found that demand is increasingly shifting toward people who can improve, refine and finish AI-generated work rather than simply generate an output. 2</p><p>So learning how to press a button isn''t enough.</p><p>You need to learn how to judge the result.</p><p>Edit it.</p><p>Improve it.</p><p>Fact-check it.</p><p>And understand what the client actually needs.</p><p>Start Small</p><p>You don''t need an expensive AI subscription to begin experimenting.</p><p>Start with tools you can access.</p><p>Pick one service.</p><p>Build a few samples.</p><p>Find potential clients.</p><p>And improve with every project.</p><p>The opportunity isn''t really about asking:</p><p>“How can AI make me money?”</p><p>A better question is:</p><p>“What problem can I solve faster or better with AI?”</p><p>Because clients don''t pay for AI.</p><p>They pay for solutions.</p><p>What AI-powered service would you try first?</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1790161543/echo-gist/32428e86-4409-45bd-8488-60480b94c940/pacq2nmq4hfuhwa7zf1y.jpg', 'technology', '{"ai","freelancing","makingmoney","technology"}', '32428e86-4409-45bd-8488-60480b94c940', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 1, 1, 0, '2026-09-23T11:06:21.139Z', '2026-09-23T11:06:50.527Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('f5326b16-3809-4eb1-bb81-d16d6d1b8f9e', 'Google Is Increasing Its Prices in Nigeria Tomorrow — Here''s How Much You Will Now Pay', 'google-is-increasing-its-prices-in-nigeria-tomorrow-heres-how-much-you-will-now--8k23', 'If you pay for Google One in Nigeria, your next renewal could cost significantly more. The 100GB plan is moving from ₦1,900 to ₦2,950, while 200GB rises to ₦4,400. But the exact date depends on how you subscribed. Here’s what you need to know before your next payment.', '<p><strong>Google One subscribers in Nigeria are facing higher storage costs, with the new rates taking effect for certain renewals from August 26. Here is what is changing and what users need to know before their next payment.</strong></p><p>Google is increasing the price of its Google One cloud-storage plans in Nigeria, with existing subscribers on Google Play and annual Apple App Store subscriptions being charged the new rates when they renew on or after <strong>August 26, 2026</strong>. Apple App Store monthly subscribers began moving to the new rates from August 23.&nbsp;</p><p>For Nigerian users, the increase means the popular <strong>100GB plan moves from ₦1,900 to ₦2,950 per month</strong>, while the <strong>200GB plan rises from ₦2,900 to ₦4,400 per month</strong>.&nbsp;</p><p>That means some Nigerians who use Google Photos, Gmail or Google Drive for their everyday storage will soon have to decide whether the additional space is still worth the higher monthly bill.</p><h2><strong>Google One Prices Are Going Up in Nigeria</strong></h2><p>Google One gives users additional cloud storage that can be used across Google Drive, Gmail and Google Photos. A standard Google Account comes with up to <strong>15GB of storage at no charge</strong>, while paid Google One plans increase that capacity.&nbsp;</p><p>The latest Nigerian prices are:</p><p><strong>Google One 100GB</strong><br>Old price: <strong>₦1,900/month</strong><br>New price: <strong>₦2,950/month</strong><br>Increase: <strong>₦1,050/month (55.3%)</strong></p><p><strong>Google One 200GB</strong><br>Old price: <strong>₦2,900/month</strong><br>New price: <strong>₦4,400/month</strong><br>Increase: <strong>₦1,500/month (51.7%)</strong></p><p>The 100GB plan therefore costs <strong>₦12,600 more per year</strong> at the new monthly rate, while the 200GB plan costs <strong>₦18,000 more per year</strong>.</p><p>The current Nigerian App Store listing also shows the new monthly prices of ₦2,950 for 100GB and ₦4,400 for 200GB.&nbsp;</p><h2><strong>When Does the New Price Actually Start?</strong></h2><p>This is where Nigerian subscribers need to pay attention.</p><p>The new prices are not necessarily charged to every existing subscriber on August 26.</p><p>Google says new members in affected countries, including Nigeria, have been able to subscribe at the updated prices since <strong>July 27, 2026</strong>. For existing members, however, the timing depends on where the subscription was purchased.&nbsp;</p><p>For <strong>Google Play subscriptions</strong>, the new price applies to renewals on or after <strong>August 26</strong>.</p><p>For <strong>Apple App Store annual subscriptions</strong>, the new price also applies to renewals on or after <strong>August 26</strong>.</p><p>For <strong>Apple App Store monthly subscriptions</strong>, the new price started applying to renewals on or after <strong>August 23</strong>.&nbsp;</p><p>So if your Google One subscription does not renew on August 26, that does not necessarily mean you will be charged the new price on that exact day.</p><p>Google says existing subscribers receive advance notification of the price change by email, while a banner notification can also appear when they log into Google One on the web or through the Android or iOS app.&nbsp;</p><h2><strong>Why Is Google Increasing the Price?</strong></h2><p>Google has confirmed the price increase but its official notice does <strong>not provide a specific Nigeria-focused explanation for why the prices are being raised</strong>. It says the increase affects selected countries and that the amount varies according to the plan and local currency.&nbsp;</p><p>That means users should be careful about attributing the increase specifically to inflation, exchange rates, taxes, artificial intelligence costs or any other factor unless Google directly confirms it.</p><p>For now, the important fact for Nigerian subscribers is simply that the new prices have been announced and are being introduced according to the relevant subscription and renewal dates.</p><h2><strong>What Is Google One and Why Do Nigerians Use It?</strong></h2><p>Google One is essentially an expanded storage subscription for Google''s ecosystem.</p><p>Your storage is shared across services including:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Google Drive</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Gmail</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Google Photos</strong></p></li></ul><p>A normal Google Account comes with up to 15GB of free storage shared across these services. Once that space becomes insufficient, users can purchase Google One to increase their storage capacity.&nbsp;</p><p>For someone who regularly backs up photos and videos, stores documents in Drive and keeps years of Gmail messages, the subscription can become difficult to give up.</p><h2><strong>What Happens If You Cancel Google One?</strong></h2><p>Cancelling does <strong>not</strong> immediately wipe out your Google One storage or benefits.</p><p>Google says users who cancel retain their paid Google One benefits until the end of their current billing cycle. After that, their storage returns to the amount available under their account''s free storage allowance.&nbsp;</p><p>The important issue comes if you are using more storage than your free allowance.</p><p>For example, someone with 80GB of data on a 100GB Google One plan would be above the free 15GB limit after cancellation.</p><p>In that situation, Google says restrictions can affect Gmail, Google Drive and Google Photos. Users may be unable to send or receive Gmail messages, upload or sync new files in Drive, or back up new photos and videos until they reduce their storage usage or purchase additional storage.&nbsp;</p><p>So cancelling is not as simple as pressing a button and forgetting about the account. Users should first check how much storage they are actually using.</p><h2><strong>Should You Cancel or Keep Your Google One Subscription?</strong></h2><p>There is no universal answer.</p><p>If your Google account contains thousands of photos, important documents, large files and years of emails, paying for additional storage may still be worthwhile.</p><p>The question becomes more difficult for someone who is barely using their current storage allowance.</p><p>If you are considering cancelling, first check whether you can:</p><ul><li><p>Delete unnecessary photos and videos.</p></li><li><p>Remove large files from Google Drive.</p></li><li><p>Clear old emails and attachments.</p></li><li><p>Empty your Gmail and Drive trash.</p></li><li><p>Move important files to another reliable storage location.</p></li></ul><p>Google provides storage-management tools to help users see what is consuming their space.&nbsp;</p><h2><strong>How to Check How Much Google Storage You Are Using</strong></h2><p>Before deciding whether the new price is worth paying, check your current storage usage.</p><p>You can open Google One while signed into the relevant Google Account and check the storage section. Google also provides storage-management tools that break down usage across services.&nbsp;</p><p>This is particularly useful because some people may be paying for 100GB without realising that they are using only a small portion of it.</p><p>On the other hand, someone who is already close to the 100GB limit may find that cancelling would create problems.</p><h2><strong>Is Google One Still Worth It at the New Price?</strong></h2><p>The answer largely depends on how much you rely on Google''s ecosystem.</p><p>At the old ₦1,900 monthly price, a 100GB subscriber spent about <strong>₦22,800 over 12 months</strong> if billed monthly.</p><p>At ₦2,950 per month, the same plan costs about <strong>₦35,400 over a year</strong>.</p><p>For the 200GB plan, the annual monthly-billing cost rises from approximately <strong>₦34,800 to ₦52,800</strong>.</p><p>That is a significant difference for Nigerian consumers, particularly at a time when many households are already watching their monthly expenses closely.</p><p>But for someone whose Google account is effectively their digital filing cabinet, the additional cost may still be preferable to losing convenient access to cloud storage.</p><p>The smarter approach is therefore to <strong>check your actual storage usage before deciding</strong>.</p><h2><strong>What Nigerian Google One Subscribers Should Do Now</strong></h2><p>If you are an existing subscriber, check the email or notification Google sent you and look at the renewal date attached to your account.</p><p>Google''s official schedule says Google Play subscriptions and annual Apple App Store subscriptions renewing from August 26 will be charged the new price, while Apple monthly subscriptions have already moved to the new rate.&nbsp;</p><p>If you do not want to continue at the higher rate, Google says eligible existing members who do not want to renew at the new price can cancel by <strong>August 25</strong>; Apple monthly subscribers had an earlier August 22 deadline.&nbsp;</p><p>The important thing is not to cancel blindly. <strong>Check your storage first.</strong></p><p>If you are already above the free storage limit, make a plan for where your files, photos and other data will go before your paid storage expires.</p><h2><strong>The Bigger Question</strong></h2><p>For years, ₦1,900 a month made Google One''s 100GB plan relatively easy to overlook as a small digital expense.</p><p>The new ₦2,950 price changes that calculation.</p><p>A user who barely needs the extra storage may decide that it is time to clean up their account. Someone whose entire digital life is stored across Google Photos, Drive and Gmail may decide that the convenience is still worth paying for.</p><p>Either way, Nigerian subscribers now have a reason to look at exactly how much space they are using before their next renewal.</p><p><strong>Will you keep paying for Google One at the new price, or are you considering cancelling?</strong></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787686910/echo-gist/31cf9361-36e5-4829-8ae2-b81652b35275/yq0dwxnm5gcdiaqtiq9y.jpg', 'technology', '{}', '31cf9361-36e5-4829-8ae2-b81652b35275', TRUE, FALSE, 6, 1, 0, 0, '2026-08-25T19:46:35.913Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:31.054Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('7dc59e96-de3c-4151-8aa8-057432f2b313', 'The Old Clock That Thought Me Patience', 'the-old-clock-that-thought-me-patience--m0ia', 'Some things are not broken; they are simply taking the time they need to become whole', '<p></p><p>The first thing Elena noticed when she stepped into her grandfather''s house that summer was the silence. Not the peaceful kind of silence that settles over a house at rest. The strange hollow silence of something missing. The kind you don''t notice until it''s gone.</p><p>It took her a moment to realize what it was.</p><p>The clock.</p><p>For as long as she could remember her grandfather''s house had been filled with the steady comforting tick of the old grandfather clock that stood in the hallway between the kitchen and the living room. It had ticked through her childhood. Through birthday parties, rainy afternoons, summer evenings when she and her cousins would sit on the porch steps and listen to it chime through the open window. It had always been so constant that she had stopped hearing it the way you stop hearing a river once you''ve lived beside it long enough.</p><p>Now it stood silent, its hands frozen at twenty minutes past three as if time itself had simply given up somewhere in that hallway and decided to stay forever.</p><p>"Grandpa," Elena called, setting her bag down by the door. "Your clock stopped."</p><p>Her grandfather, Antonio looked up from the armchair where he''d been reading his glasses pushed low on his nose.&nbsp;</p><p>"I know, " he said and returned to his book.</p><p>"For how long?"</p><p>" three months now."</p><p>Elena walked over to the clock and studied it. Dust had settled faintly along the top of its case and when she opened the small glass door at the front she could see the pendulum hanging motionless caught in mid-swing forever. It looked, she thought, a little sad. Like something that had once had a purpose and had quietly lost it.</p><p>"Why haven''t you thrown it out?" she asked. "You could get one. A nice digital. Something modern. This old thing is just taking up space now."</p><p>Her grandfather set down his book with the patience of a man who had never once in his life been in a rush to finish a sentence. He looked at her for a moment the way he always did when he was about to say something he wanted her to actually hear.</p><p>"Some things " he said, "are not broken. They are simply taking the time they need to become whole."</p><p>Elena frowned. "That clock hasn''t moved in three months Grandpa. That''s broken."</p><p>"Or it''s waiting for someone enough to fix it properly " he said and smiled the small knowing smile that had frustrated and comforted her in equal measure her whole life. "Come. I''ll show you."</p><p>That evening after dinner Antonio brought out a wooden toolbox that Elena had never seen before. Worn at the corners the brass latches tarnished with age. He set it on the table beside the clock and began, piece by piece to take the clock apart.</p><p>Elena pulled up a chair to watch out of politeness rather than real interest at first. She had errands to think about, messages to answer a life waiting for her on the phone in her pocket.. There was something about the way her grandfather worked that slowly pulled her attention away from all of it.</p><p>He didn''t rush. He lifted the pendulum free. Set it down with the same gentleness he might have used to lift a sleeping child. He removed the small brass gears one at a time laying each one on a cloth in the exact order he''d taken them out so that putting them back would be no different than reading a sentence from left to right.</p><p>"This one " he said, holding up a gear no bigger than a coin "is worn down. See how the teeth here are smoother than they should be? That''s why the clock kept losing time before it stopped completely. It wasn''t sudden, you know. It didn''t just die one day. It had been struggling for a while before anyone noticed."</p><p>Elena leaned in. "I didn''t even know clocks could struggle."</p><p>"Everything struggles before it fails, " Antonio said. "The failing is loud. The struggling is always quiet."</p><p>He set the gear aside and reached for a small tin of oil, applying it with careful unhurried drops to each joint and spring inside the mechanism. He worked in silence for a while. Elena found herself simply watching his hands. Hands that had built furniture, fixed radios repaired the roof after storms and now moved just as carefully over pieces of brass so small they seemed almost imaginary between his thick fingers.</p><p>"You could have just bought a clock, " she said, again softer this time.</p><p>"I could have, " he agreed. ". This clock was for your grandmother before it was mine. It has kept time through fifty-two years of this house. A new clock would tell the hour as well.. It wouldn''t have kept any of this." He gestured, not at the clock exactly. In the room around them. The worn armchair, the photographs on the wall, the quiet weight of decades that seemed to live inside the house itself. "Some things are worth repairing or replacing, Elena. Not because they''re perfect. Because of what they carry."</p><p>They worked on the clock together for three evenings. Elena found herself looking forward to it in a way that surprised her. Coming home earlier than she needed to putting her phone away without being asked, pulling her chair to the table eagerly.</p><p>In the evening as her grandfather cleaned a small spring with a soft brush Elena finally said the thing she had been carrying since she arrived.</p><p>"I feel like that clock, Grandpa."</p><p>He didn''t look up but she could tell he was listening the way he always listened. Fully without hurry.</p><p>"I''m twenty-six " she continued. "I thought by now I''d have. I don''t know. A career I was proud of. A place of my own. Something that looked like progress. Instead I feel like I''m just... Stuck. Frozen at twenty past three like the clock. Everyone around me seems to be moving and I''m just standing still gathering dust."</p><p>Antonio set down his brush. Looked at her for a long moment. Then he picked up the gear from earlier and held it out to her in his palm.</p><p>"Do you see this?" he said. "This gear was working when the clock was slowing down. It didn''t stop trying. It kept turning and kept doing its part even as it wore thin even as it struggled more each day than the day before. The clock didn''t fail because this gear gave up. It failed because it needed rest and repair and someone was patient enough to notice what was happening underneath the surface."</p><p>He closed her fingers gently around the brass piece.</p><p>"You are not frozen, Elena. You are being repaired. There is a difference. A frozen thing has given up. A thing being repaired is simply being tended to piece by piece in an order that makes sense to the one doing the tending. Even if that order doesn''t make sense to the one being fixed."</p><p>Elena felt her throat tighten unexpectedly. ". How do you know when the repair is almost done? How do you know it''s not just... Broken for good?"</p><p>"You don''t always know, " he admitted. "That''s the part. You keep going. You keep cleaning the gears, oiling the joints, checking each piece when the whole thing still looks silent and useless from the outside.. One day —" he shrugged, the simplest gesture in the world "— one day you wind it back up and it starts ticking again. Not because you rushed it. Because you didn''t."</p><p>In the evening the clock was finally whole again. Every gear cleaned, every worn piece either repaired or carefully replaced, every joint oiled and patient under her grandfather''s steady hands. He lifted the pendulum. Hung it back in its place and then with the same unhurried calm he''d shown through the entire process he gave it a gentle push.</p><p>For a moment nothing. Elena''s heart caught in her chest the way it had when she was small and terrified something wouldn''t work.</p><p>Then the pendulum swung.</p><p>The clock began to tick.</p><p>Not loudly. Not dramatically. Just a soft steady sound, the sound that had filled this house for fifty-two years returning now as though it had never truly left. As though it had simply been resting somewhere deep inside the mechanism waiting for someone patient enough to bring it back.</p><p>Elena sat still listening. Tears rose in her eyes before she understood why.</p><p>"It''s working, " she whispered.</p><p>"It never stopped being worth fixing, " her grandfather said. "It just needed time."</p><p>He looked at her then. There was nothing else he needed to say. She understood.</p><p>Elena stayed at her grandfather''s house for two weeks that summer and every evening she sat beside the old clock and listened to it tick feeling something, in her own chest slowly loosen with each steady beat. She thought of the gear resting now among the others doing its quiet work again, no longer straining, simply turning at its own pace exactly as it was meant to.</p><p>She thought of herself the way. Not frozen, not broken, not failing. Just being repaired, piece by piece in a way that would make sense in time even if it didn''t make sense yet.</p><p>Years later, long after she had built the career and the life she once thought she was failing to reach Elena would still remember that clock whenever things felt slow or uncertain or unfinished. She would remember her grandfather''s hands moving without hurry over pieces small to rush and his voice steady, as the ticking itself:</p><p>Not everything that is delayed is denied. Some things need time, care and patience before they work again.</p><p>Somewhere quietly she would begin to tick forward again too.</p><p></p><h3>Lesson:</h3><p>Not everything that is delayed is denied.</p><p>Some things need time, care and patience before they work again.</p><p>Healing is not the absence of brokenness. It is the presence of tending.</p><p>Keep showing up for the parts of yourself that are still being repaired.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788371389/echo-gist/26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2/oxwrufzb3yaflr9szixc.png', 'lifestyle', '{"lifelesson","personalgrowth","healingjourney","patience","inspirationalstories"}', '26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2', TRUE, FALSE, 8, 12, 3, 1, '2026-09-02T17:54:24.768Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:49.077Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('5c177ade-53bc-49e7-a470-9271ebd152a6', 'Can You Go to Prison for Beating a Suspected Thief? What Nigerian Law Says', 'can-you-go-to-prison-for-beating-a-suspected-thief-what-nigerian-law-says-wblr', 'You catch someone you believe has stolen from you. Everyone around you starts shouting “thief!” and someone suggests beating the person. But where does the law draw the line? A recent warning from IGP Tunji Disu has raised an important question every Nigerian should understand.', '<p><strong>A recent warning from the Inspector-General of Police has put a serious question back in the spotlight: if you catch someone you believe is stealing, are you legally allowed to beat them?</strong></p><p>The answer is not as simple as “yes” or “no”. Nigerian law recognises circumstances in which force may be legally justified, particularly self-defence, but being angry at someone you suspect of stealing does not give you the automatic right to punish that person.</p><p>The issue has gained fresh attention after Inspector-General of Police <strong>Tunji Disu</strong> ordered police commissioners across the 36 states and the Federal Capital Territory to treat cases of mob action as homicide and investigate them accordingly.&nbsp;</p><h2><strong>The Police Warning That Has Nigerians Talking</strong></h2><p>Disu delivered a strong warning against what Nigerians commonly call <strong>jungle justice</strong>.</p><p>In his statement, the IGP said: <strong>“Jungle justice is murder. It is not justice.”</strong> He stressed that Nigeria operates under the rule of law and that no citizen, community or group has the authority to try, sentence or execute another person.&nbsp;</p><p>He also pointed to Sections 33, 34 and 36 of the 1999 Constitution, saying that suspects retain rights to life, dignity and fair hearing.</p><p>According to reports of the statement, Disu said suspicion, allegations, photographs, videos or rumours cannot replace proper police investigation and judicial proceedings.&nbsp;</p><p>The warning is particularly significant because the police chief has directed commissioners of police nationwide to investigate mob action as homicide and pursue those who participated in such attacks.&nbsp;</p><p>So, what does that mean for an ordinary Nigerian who catches someone allegedly stealing?</p><h2><strong>So, Can You Go to Prison for Beating a Suspected Thief?</strong></h2><p><strong>Yes, potentially.</strong></p><p>But it is important to understand what that actually means.</p><p>There is no rule saying that every person who gets into a physical confrontation with a suspected thief will automatically be sent to prison. Criminal liability depends on the circumstances, what happened during the incident, the injuries caused, the person''s intention and the applicable law.</p><p>However, the fact that someone is <strong>suspected</strong> of stealing does not give members of the public a free licence to assault or punish that person.</p><p>That distinction is crucial.</p><p>If someone is accused of stealing a phone, money or another item, the allegation still has to be investigated. The person does not become legally guilty simply because someone shouted “thief” or because a video appears to show something suspicious.</p><p>The IGP specifically warned that suspicion and allegations cannot replace investigation and a court''s determination of guilt.&nbsp;</p><h2><strong>What Is the Difference Between Arrest and Assault?</strong></h2><p>This is where the situation can become confusing.</p><p>There is an important difference between <strong>bringing a suspected offender to the attention of law enforcement</strong> and <strong>punishing that person yourself</strong>.</p><p>The police warning is essentially that members of the public cannot become the police, prosecutor and judge simply because they believe someone has committed a crime.</p><p>Once a person is caught, the appropriate response is to involve law enforcement rather than turning the situation into a punishment session.</p><p>The Constitution also protects the right to life and provides exceptions involving lawful use of force, including certain circumstances involving defence and lawful arrest.&nbsp;</p><p>That does not mean every physical act carried out during an alleged arrest is automatically lawful.</p><p>The circumstances matter.</p><h2><strong>What If the Suspected Thief Attacks You First?</strong></h2><p>This is where <strong>self-defence</strong> becomes important.</p><p>Nigerian law does recognise self-defence in appropriate circumstances.</p><p>Section 286 of the Criminal Code provides that where a person is unlawfully assaulted and has not provoked the assault, the person may use force that is reasonably necessary to defend against the assault. The provision also places limits on the use of force, with different considerations where there is a reasonable apprehension of death or grievous harm.&nbsp;</p><p>The key idea is <strong>defence</strong>, not revenge.</p><p>For example, there is an obvious legal difference between trying to protect yourself from an ongoing attack and continuing to beat someone after the immediate danger has ended.</p><p>Nigerian courts have also considered whether force used in alleged self-defence was reasonably necessary in the circumstances.&nbsp;</p><p>So if someone attacks you, the existence of self-defence as a legal principle does not mean that every response will automatically be protected.</p><p>The facts matter.</p><h2><strong>What If a Suspected Thief Is Beaten and Dies?</strong></h2><p>This is where the consequences can become extremely serious.</p><p>The IGP has specifically warned that people involved in mob killings can face murder charges. Reports of his statement also indicate that he referenced <strong>Section 319 of the Criminal Code and Section 221 of the Penal Code</strong> when discussing the severe punishment attached to unlawful killing.&nbsp;</p><p>Under Section 319 of the Criminal Code, a person convicted of murder is subject to the prescribed punishment of death, subject to the provisions of the section. Nigerian courts have upheld murder convictions under the Criminal Code where the prosecution established the necessary elements of the offence.</p><p>But there is an important distinction:</p><p><strong>Being involved in a fight that results in death does not automatically mean a person is guilty of murder.</strong></p><p>The prosecution still has to establish the offence under the applicable law. Nigerian Supreme Court authority has, for example, identified elements that must be proved for a murder conviction, including the death of the victim, causation and the required intention or knowledge regarding death or grievous harm.&nbsp;</p><p>That is why it is dangerous to reduce every mob-related death to a simple slogan.</p><p>The exact circumstances matter legally.</p><h2><strong>Why Do Nigerians Still Take Matters Into Their Own Hands?</strong></h2><p>If the law is clear that mob justice is unlawful, why does it continue to happen?</p><p>There is no single explanation.</p><p>Distrust of the justice system can play a role. So can fear of criminals, frustration with crime, anger after an alleged theft and the belief that suspects will escape punishment.</p><p>There is also the power of crowds.</p><p>A person who might not attack someone alone may behave differently when surrounded by dozens of angry people.</p><p>Phones can make the situation even worse. Someone starts recording. Another person shares the footage. More people arrive. Before the allegation has been properly investigated, the crowd can begin treating it as established fact.</p><p>But none of these factors turns an allegation into a conviction.</p><h2><strong>What Should You Do If You Catch Someone You Suspect Has Stolen Something?</strong></h2><p>The safest approach is to <strong>involve the appropriate authorities rather than administer punishment yourself</strong>.</p><p>If there is evidence relating to the alleged offence, preserve it and provide it to law enforcement.</p><p>If the situation is dangerous, prioritise the safety of yourself and others and contact the police or relevant emergency authorities.</p><p>Most importantly, avoid turning an accusation into a physical punishment.</p><p>The IGP has specifically directed police commanders to identify, arrest and prosecute people involved in mob attacks, including those who plan, incite, encourage or otherwise participate in such actions.&nbsp;</p><h2><strong>Why Being a Suspect Is Not the Same as Being Guilty</strong></h2><p>The word <strong>“suspect”</strong> is perhaps the most important word in this entire discussion.</p><p>A suspect is someone who is suspected of committing an offence.</p><p>That is not the same thing as being convicted.</p><p>Section 36 of the Constitution protects fair hearing, and the IGP specifically emphasised that suspects are entitled to these constitutional protections.&nbsp;</p><p>This matters because mistakes happen.</p><p>Someone can be accused because of a misunderstanding.</p><p>Someone can be wrongly identified.</p><p>A video can leave out important context.</p><p>A crowd can believe something that later turns out to be false.</p><p>And once violence begins, correcting a mistake may become impossible.</p><h2><strong>The Bigger Problem With Mob Justice</strong></h2><p>Mob justice does not only create a danger for suspected criminals.</p><p>It creates danger for everyone.</p><p>An innocent person can be killed because of a false accusation. Someone attempting to intervene can become a victim. And people who participate in unlawful violence can potentially expose themselves to criminal prosecution.</p><p>The Uromi killings of 16 travellers in Edo State in March 2025 remain one of the cases cited by Disu while discussing the dangers of mob justice.&nbsp;</p><p>The broader lesson is simple: <strong>an accusation is not evidence, and a crowd is not a court.</strong></p><h2><strong>What the IGP''s Warning Really Means</strong></h2><p>Disu''s warning is not simply about people who kill suspects.</p><p>It is also about changing the way Nigerians respond when they believe someone has committed a crime.</p><p>His position is that the determination of guilt belongs to the legal system, not to an angry crowd.</p><p>That does not mean Nigerians have to ignore crime or allow themselves to be attacked.</p><p>It means the response to suspected criminal activity must remain within the boundaries of the law.</p><p>Self-defence, where legally applicable, is different from revenge.</p><p>Reporting a suspected thief is different from beating one.</p><p>And an allegation is different from a conviction.</p><h3><strong>The Bottom Line</strong></h3><p>So, <strong>can you go to prison for beating a suspected thief in Nigeria?</strong></p><p>You potentially can, depending on the circumstances and the offence established.</p><p>The fact that someone is suspected of stealing does not by itself give you the legal right to punish that person.</p><p>And if a confrontation escalates into serious injury or death, the potential criminal consequences can become far more severe.</p><p>The safest principle is straightforward: <strong>if someone is suspected of committing a crime, report the matter and allow the authorities and courts to determine what happens next.</strong></p><p>If you caught someone you believed had stolen from you, would you hand the person over to the police or would you be tempted to take matters into your own hands?</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787688406/echo-gist/31cf9361-36e5-4829-8ae2-b81652b35275/qebsfbnoamjgmgs0tfzx.jpg', 'culture', '{}', '31cf9361-36e5-4829-8ae2-b81652b35275', TRUE, FALSE, 7, 2, 0, 0, '2026-08-25T20:13:50.986Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:30.716Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('e4acaebf-f241-4c55-9c6c-76b8bd853e1d', 'I No Go Gree', 'i-no-go-gree-lw9j', 'The Cost of A Plate', '<p>"How far na? I wan come your shop on Saturday make I come charge my phone," Jacko said, leaning against the wooden frame of the kiosk. "You know say light no dey ever dey on Saturdays."</p><p>"Omo, true," Malo replied, wiping down his counter. "But I get wedding to attend."</p><p>Jacko’s eyes lit up slightly. "Wedding? Ah, I go fit go?"</p><p>"Yes, you fit," Malo said with a casual shrug.</p><p>To anyone else, a wedding was a celebration of love. To Jacko, it was a guarantee of a warm meal, something that could sustain him for the entire day. His daily routine had long devolved into a forced fast, enduring empty mornings and hollow afternoons just to eat a single meal at night.</p><p>Tall, lanky, and visibly malnourished, his bones practically pressed against his faded shirt.</p><p>Whenever the hunger reached a breaking point, Jacko would show up at Malo’s shop. On good days, Malo would offer him a bowl of soaked garri or whatever leftovers he had to spare.</p><p>Being an undergraduate had become a slow, painful endurance test. His coursemates had repeatedly advised him to drop out, pointing out that the financial burden was destroying him.</p><p>Surviving on a ruthless budget of three thousand Naira a month, he trekked long, grueling miles to campus and back every single day. </p><p>His grades were slipping under the fog of exhaustion, but he kept showing up. He crashed church programs, claiming to be a first-time guest just to get the free refreshment packages. </p><p>He would follow almost anyone who offered to buy him a few pieces of hot <em>awara</em> or <em>buns</em>. </p><p>He had even attempted to date, hoping a girlfriend might cook for him, but no one wanted a guy who could barely afford to feed himself.</p><p>When Saturday arrived, Jacko didn''t care about the music, the decor, or the couple. He immediately began hovering around the catering staff, trailing the waiters before the ceremony had even reached the refreshment phase.</p><p>His desperate, jittery movements made him look suspicious, like a thief scoping out a target, when all he was really hunting for was <em>jollof rice.</em></p><p>Malo was across the grand hall, deep in conversation with a few guests, when a sudden commotion shattered the music.</p><p>Near the catering station, Jacko was in a fierce tug-of-war, wrestling a heavy thermal cooler away from a frantic waitress.</p><p>"Shey una go still share the food?!" Jacko screamed, his voice cracking with rage and starvation. "Greedy people! I say I wan chop now! I dey hungry! Wicked people!"</p><p>Mortified, Malo stepped back into the crowd, completely pretending not to know him to escape the second-hand embarrassment.</p><p>The shouting grew so chaotic that it drew the attention of the entire room. The music cut out as the bride and groom rushed over to quell the scene. But as the groom stepped forward, Jacko froze.</p><p>His grip on the cooler loosened, and his hollow eyes locked onto the man in the tailored suit.</p><p>"Odoba?!" Jacko gasped, his voice turning icy. "Na you be this?!"</p><p>The groom''s face drained of color.</p><p>"So you leave us dey suffer!" Jacko roared, stepping past the waitress. "When time come for you to settle us, you run... now you wan marry?!"</p><p>The truth hit the room like a physical blow. Odoba wasn''t just a wealthy groom, he was Jacko’s elder brother. Years ago, he had gathered money from Jacko and their struggling siblings under the promise of investing it to lift the family out of poverty, only to disappear with every last kobo to build a lavish new life.</p><p>Years of starvation, humiliation, and bone-deep agony snapped inside Jacko all at once.</p><p>Before anyone could react, Jacko moved like a whirlwind of pure wrath. He tore down the sheer fabric decor, kicked over the floral arrangements, and lunged toward the head table. </p><p>With a furious shove, he sent the elaborate three-tiered wedding cake crashing to the floor in a heap of ruined icing. He marched over to the catering line and flipped the very food coolers he had been fighting for, spilling <em>jollof rice</em> and meat across the shiny tiles.</p><p>Panic erupted. Guests scrambled out of the hall, whispering and gasping in total disarray as the event unraveled in minutes.</p><p>The wedding was officially over.</p><p>Malo stood frozen by the entrance, his jaw dropped, watching the destruction in utter shock.</p><p>In the center of the ruined hall, surrounded by spilt food and crushed cake, Jacko stood towering over his trembling brother, his chest heaving.</p><p>"Tell your wife who you really are!" Jacko shouted, tears of rage cutting through the dust on his face. "Kai! I don suffer... I no go gree!"</p><p><strong>What would you do if you were Malo?</strong></p><p><strong>Would you have done worse if you were Jacko? </strong></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787696757/echo-gist/64a4435f-e4df-4c17-8422-16439c81d14b/zjfztxjvqrgosa9csvy2.jpg', 'writing', '{"writing","drama","art"}', '64a4435f-e4df-4c17-8422-16439c81d14b', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 25, 1, 4, '2026-08-25T22:26:56.747Z', '2026-09-20T20:43:26.139Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('264c0910-9825-4604-9de3-b2a46d847efa', 'You’re 3 States Away and Your Dad Is in Front of Your Hostel?', 'youre-3-states-away-and-your-dad-is-in-front-of-your-hostel-ug12', 'The one time I decided to misbehave, and the universe made sure I regretted it.', '<p>I traveled interstate to surprise my boyfriend on his 27th birthday. No heads-up, no "I''m coming", nothing. As an undergraduate with strict parents who never allowed you to go anywhere, this was like my breaking of the shell thing. Also, it is going to be very cuteeee and romantic for my baby and I🥹.</p><p>But life had other plans…</p><p>Not long after I left the hostel, my parents kept calling me. At first, I thought it was their usual check ins- I still didn’t pick up. The calls were getting much tho as if it was a loan app calling one of their debtors. I had already entered the bus by this time. Like... we had even gone far already. I was settling into the trip, eating groundnut and minding my business, when my dad''s name popped up on my phone again, he was calling and this time, I picked up.</p><p>At first it was normal check-up. "Where are you? Hope you''re okay?" I said I was fine and I’m in my hostel. It’s not the first time he will be asking me these questions so I answered without thinking too much of it. My voice was shaking small tho and I was trying to sound like someone in the hostel, not someone currently on the road. I think he bought it because he said okay and hung up.</p><p>30 minutes later, this man called again. But this time he said, "I''m in your school. Come outside. I''m standing in front of your hostel"</p><p>My spirit left my body. Everything inside me scattered. How do I explain to my dad that I am no where near the hostel after telling him few minutes ago that I am in the hostel????</p><p>Helppppp. What do I say? What do I do? Should I say lie that I don’t where I am and act like I was kidnapped and had a memory loss? Why me, God? Why today? Any day but today???? No nawwww!!! I’ve been a good girlll just this one timeee🤧.</p><p>I told him calmly, “Daddy, I’m…um…I’m on my way to Lagos State for the AIC competition. I was shaking so bad hoping he will believe it.</p><p>There was a long pause. Then he said, “Without telling me or your mother?”. I said, “It was sudden. Very important!. Out of those selected to go, one of them fell ill and I was asked to replace her. Everything was last minute”. Then, he said “Okay”. I heaved a sigh of relief.</p><p>“Send me the location and all the details”, my dad said. At that point, my soul left me quietly. Is he planning to travel down to Lagos or what? I quickly texted my friend, Bola, who was actually selected to go for the competition.</p><p><em>-Oh, that part of the competition is actually true, just that I was not selected. I’m not a liar🌚</em></p><p>Bola got back to me with the details and location and was wondering what’s going on but I just told her I’ll fill her in later. By this time, I was sweating like someone that had different problems in life. I sent them my dad and he said okay and wished me luck.</p><p><em>Phew,free at last….or so I thought. Little did I know that the real wahala hadn’t even started yet.</em></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788346189/echo-gist/a16fce57-7b1d-41de-934e-3b6d207eac01/xiynerogf1dfkkakrm1n.jpg', 'lifestyle', '{"storytime","relationshipdrama","funnystories","lifestyle","university"}', 'a16fce57-7b1d-41de-934e-3b6d207eac01', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 206, 10, 3, '2026-09-02T11:02:23.426Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:50.735Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('f1010001-0000-4000-a000-000000000025', 'Zero-Downtime Database Migrations: Safe Schema Changes in Production', 'zero-downtime-database-migrations-safe-schema-changes', 'How to safely rename columns, add NOT NULL constraints, and migrate large PostgreSQL tables without locking rows or interrupting active users.', '<h1>Zero-Downtime Database Migrations: Safe Schema Changes in Production</h1>
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('7589fdaa-3382-4ade-858b-2e25b36ebf02', 'The Japa syndrome', 'the-japa-syndrome--sfuv', 'There''s a word every young Nigerian knows without a dictionary. “Japa” Yoruba for "to flee." It''s become the name for a generation''s desperate urge to leave Nig…', '<p>There''s a word every young Nigerian knows without a dictionary. “Japa” Yoruba for "to flee." It''s become the name for a generation''s desperate urge to leave Nigeria by any means, for anywhere that will have them. That urge just sparked a heated online argument, a popular creator publicly told a friend not to attempt the Libya route to Europe, and what should''ve been a private conversation exploded into a public fight some insisting no hardship is worth risking your life or body for, others arguing Libya is just a "closer," faster shortcut than waiting years for a proper visa.</p><p>Both sides are talking about the same road, but not the same risk. IOM''s counter-trafficking data shows Nigerians make up over half of all identified trafficking victims in Libya the largest single nationality by far, ahead of Libyans and Somalis with victims most commonly funneled into forced labor, sex trafficking, and kidnapping for extortion. By early 2026, Nigeria remained one of the top nationalities among the roughly 936,000 migrants IOM counted moving through Libya, and in Europe, a 2022 snapshot found Italy home to over 120,000 Nigerians is the single most significant destination for Nigerian trafficking victims specifically. Between 2014 and 2016 alone, IOM recorded a nearly 600% spike in potential sex-trafficking victims arriving in Italy via that same Mediterranean crossing. </p><p>Someone''s cousin really did make it through and is now sending money home. Someone''s neighbor really did disappear on that same road. Both stories are true, and that''s exactly why the argument won''t die.</p><p>Japa syndrome won''t be solved by a viral thread or a warning from a well meaning friend it''s a symptom of an economy that keeps pushing its most ambitious people outward, and a legal migration system too slow and expensive for the people most desperate to use it. But the numbers aren''t ambiguous, even when the feelings are, for a lot of people, the Libya route isn''t a shortcut to Europe, it''s a pipeline into exploitation. So is the Libya route ever worth the risk for a shot at a better life, or is no hardship in Nigeria worth those odds?</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787068040/echo-gist/40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1/vtdya6wgbtwzncsaafsn.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 9, 0, 0, '2026-08-18T15:48:57.985Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:54.664Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('0c1132d9-c246-49d5-8876-d6654f032376', 'The Monday no body talks about', 'the-monday-no-body-talks-about--oeix', 'Today is another Monday, but do you know that today brings you one step closer to your greatness?
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('e05f66bd-8bb0-4fbd-a97f-cf1f7d481648', 'How to Stop Procrastinating: Practical Strategies', 'how-to-stop-procrastinating-practical-strategies-z34m', 'Four, that’s the number of books I’ve been working on. Two should have been completed at least a year ago, but I never seem to be able to put the words down......', '<p>Four, that’s the number of books I’ve been working on. Two should have been completed at least a year ago, but I never seem to be able to put the words down. What’s stopping me from becoming a published author? Writer’s block or maybe the feeling of inadequacy? Nah, PROCRASTINATION: the dominant act of delaying that which needs to be done. That’s what stopping me. I keep delaying, and holding myself back. </p><p>So how do we get over this? </p><p>What is procrastination? </p><p>Procrastination is simply the act of delaying important tasks you need to finish. It usually involves doing less strenuous activities despite potentially negative consequences. </p><p>In most studies, it has been said that Procrastination can have a detrimental effect on both you and your work. It increases the feelings of guilt and anxiety. It also reduced work performance.</p><p>The fear of failure is usually the leading cause of procrastination, because as human beings we often postpone tasks because we fear making mistakes.</p><p>Ways to Stop Procrastinating </p><p>Break your work into steps</p><p>One of the reason we procrastinate is that we find the work too overwhelming for us, so breaking it down into little parts, and then focusing on one part at a time makes it easier to do the task step by step. If you still procrastinate on the task after breaking it down, then break it down more. </p><p>Prioritize tasks</p><p>Make a list of what you have to do on a daily or weekly basis. Then spend a few minutes figuring out what needs to be done and what doesn’t, so you can identify, prioritize and work accordingly.</p><p>Change your environment</p><p>This really worked for me. After finishing my degree and moving to another state, I realized the new environment have an impact on my productivity. So by changing or finding a better and different environment, you can focus more on what’s before you.</p><p>Minimize distractions</p><p>Limit distractions when you commit to focusing on a task that could cause you to procrastinate. You can turn off your phone or stay away from social media.</p><p>Clarify your goals</p><p>If you’ve been procrastinating for too long, it show there’s a misalignment between what you want and what you are currently doing. So take a break from your work and use the time to regroup yourself. </p><p>Move with people who inspire you to act</p><p>Identify individuals who trigger you and hang out with them more often. Soon you will emulate their drive and spirit too. </p><p>I, personally joined a writing community whereby most of the members are published authors. This pushed me further because I want to join the clique of published authors.</p><p>Get a motivational friend</p><p>Having a companion makes the whole process much easier and more fun. Your friend should be someone who has his/her own set of goals. Both of you will hold each other accountable to your goals and plans. </p><p>My good friend Ekundayo Femi was the one for me (No homo). I’m sure he’s going to read this and smile. </p><p>Give yourself a break</p><p>Finally, don’t be too hard on yourself if you are having a tough time beating procrastination.  Just reschedule and get back on track later.</p><p>Beating procrastination is not exactly a walk in the park, but with dedication, you can do it. </p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787974486/echo-gist/89f720e3-1f90-484c-80c1-ed14f3ce9ec4/vhhaaysg5lvynsz1e38j.png', 'productivity', '{}', '89f720e3-1f90-484c-80c1-ed14f3ce9ec4', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 78, 4, 0, '2026-08-29T03:38:15.878Z', '2026-09-28T04:08:29.485Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('b9eb4efd-0a0f-4dd5-9453-4847e406c2d2', 'Atiku Abubakar, presidential candidate of ADC has vowed to review Nelfund and introduce debt relief for qualifying Nigerian students if elected in 2027.', 'atiku-abubakar-presidential-candidate-of-adc-has-vowed-to-review-nelfund-and-int-nopr', 'Atiku Abubakar, Nigeria''s former Vice President (1999–2007) and presidential candidate of the African Democratic Congress (ADC) for the 2027 election, has inten…', '<p>Atiku Abubakar, Nigeria''s former Vice President (1999–2007) and presidential candidate of the African Democratic Congress (ADC) for the 2027 election, has intensified his campaign against President Bola Tinubu by targeting the administration''s signature economic reforms. Central to his pitch is the Atiku Economic Recovery Plan (AERP) 2027, under which he has pledged to replace Nigeria''s old fuel-import subsidy with what his campaign describes as a capped, transparently budgeted, and independently audited production subsidy tied to domestic refining. Atiku has framed this as distinct from the pre-2023 subsidy regime, arguing that support would flow to Nigerian refineries rather than importers, with crude supplied at preferential rates in exchange for verified production and consumer benefits, and a built-in sunset clause as local capacity grows. He has also called for a review of the Nigerian Education Loan Fund (NELFUND), including debt forgiveness for students, as part of a broader critique of economic hardship, insecurity including violence in Benue State and what he characterizes as Tinubu''s mismanagement of subsidy-removal savings.</p><p>Beyond economic policy, Atiku''s team has taken legal action challenging Tinubu''s eligibility to contest the 2027 election, alleging that the NYSC discharge certificate Tinubu submitted to Nigeria''s electoral commission (INEC) bears the name "Tinubu Bola Adekunle" rather than his official name, "Bola Ahmed Tinubu," and that the document amounts to forgery. The Federal High Court in Abuja, presided over by Justice Inyang Ekwo, adjourned the matter to September 28, 2026, after Atiku''s legal team was unable to personally serve Tinubu with the originating summons a procedural dispute that has stalled substantive arguments for now. On the regional front, Atiku has also called for reviving the ECOMOG model as a way to restore unity and collective security within ECOWAS, framing it as part of a broader vision for regional stability alongside his domestic reform agenda.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788373639/echo-gist/40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1/j9r5mgvk1ybllnxyoeoi.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 3, 0, 0, '2026-09-02T18:29:25.581Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:48.835Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('b0ebe3db-8879-47e8-97d7-82758880c1c7', 'HAPPY NEW MONTH!🤭🤭', 'happy-new-month-7uqo', 'Welcome to the month of September, wishing you the very best as you journey through the days of this beautiful month.🤩😌…', '<p>Welcome to the month of September, wishing you the very best as you journey through the days of this beautiful month.🤩😌</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788280341/echo-gist/7d2ad71d-e26d-4985-9fbe-8b4c1570f087/s17ijqpqei7sxbrntjrl.heic', 'technology', '{}', '7d2ad71d-e26d-4985-9fbe-8b4c1570f087', FALSE, FALSE, 1, 2, 0, 0, '2026-09-01T16:40:00.506Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:53.472Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('e3a18343-ed22-4380-8214-fff3cfbab8c5', 'Before you go into today’s activities, don’t forget this:', 'before-you-go-into-todays-activities-dont-forget-this-jt12', 'Today is another opportunity to get closer to your dreams. Keep pushing, forge ahead, and never stop believing in yourself and in what God can do through you.
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('42b00b45-821a-4295-9a76-f63cfe4d622f', 'The Real Battle Isn''t the Device. It''s the Interface.', 'the-real-battle-isnt-the-device-its-the-interface-a0q6', 'For two decades, the tech industry''s biggest wars were fought over hardware and platforms  who made the best phone, who owned the app store, who controlled the …', '<p>For two decades, the tech industry''s biggest wars were fought over hardware and platforms  who made the best phone, who owned the app store, who controlled the browser. That era is ending. The next battleground is the layer that sits between humans and their machines, the AI system you talk to, delegate tasks to, and increasingly trust to act on your behalf. Whoever controls that layer doesn''t just sell a product they become the default intermediary for how people access information, make decisions, and get things done. </p><p>This is why Apple, OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google are no longer just competitors in their traditional categories. They''re all racing toward the same prize becoming the operating system of human intention.</p><p>Each player is approaching this from a different angle, which makes the competition more interesting than a simple feature race. Apple holds the distribution advantage a billion pockets and the operating system those pockets run on but has moved cautiously on building its own frontier AI, leaning instead on partnerships to fill the gap while it protects its walled garden. </p><p>OpenAI has consumer mindshare and a head start in making AI feel like a default habit, but lacks Apple''s hardware reach and Google''s data pipes. Anthropic has staked its position on being the trusted, safety conscious choice for developers and enterprises betting that reliability and alignment become the deciding factor as AI takes on more consequential tasks. </p><p>Google has the broadest reach of all search, Android, Workspace, and a research engine that rivals anyone''s, giving it the rare ability to embed AI into products people already use daily without asking them to switch anything.</p><p>What makes this fight existential rather than incremental is that the AI layer, once entrenched, is remarkably sticky. A search engine can be swapped with a new tab, an AI assistant that knows your calendar, your writing style, your preferences, and your history is much harder to walk away from. </p><p>That''s why the real contest isn''t about who ships the flashiest demo this quarter it''s about who becomes the assumed default that people route every question, task, and decision through. </p><p>The company that wins that position doesn''t just gain a product line. It gains a foothold in nearly every digital interaction that follows, which is exactly why Apple, Google, OpenAI, and Anthropic are treating this moment with the same intensity once reserved for the smartphone wars except this time, the stakes are the interface to everything.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787944530/echo-gist/40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1/iuu21wmyubvxtdxk2hbr.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 8, 1, 0, '2026-08-28T19:17:43.603Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:11.571Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('537ce1b7-7e72-410e-8d46-ca90cb9ba5b1', 'Your bar soap grew hair overnight. Has to be your village people at work right?', 'your-bar-soap-grew-hair-overnight-has-to-be-your-village-people-at-work-right-eg6g', 'Microchemistry in a layman''s fashion', '<p>Your bar soap grew <strong>hair</strong> overnight.</p><p>No, it is not <strong>mold</strong>.</p><p>No, it is not cotton fibers.</p><p>No, it is not your <strong>village people</strong>. 😄</p><p>It is chemistry, happening right on your bathroom counter.</p><p>I was supposed to get ready for an outing and I wanted to grab a quick bath.</p><p>But then as I reached for my white Premier bar soap, I noticed it had grown white hair.</p><p><strong>Worrisgoingonhere!!</strong> 😂</p><p>Sharpally, I picked up my Redmi Note 14 and took some sharp shots that inspired this fine piece you are reading right now.</p><p>Here is what was actually going on 👇</p><p>When soap gets wet and dries slowly in a humid bathroom, dissolved sodium ions migrate to the surface through capillary action.</p><p>Capillary action? What is that?</p><p>Don''t worry — I got you!</p><p>The independent ability of fluids to flow into narrow spaces without the help of external forces like gravity is called capillary action.</p><p>Simple!</p><p>Those ions, remember the dissolved sodium ions, they hit the air.</p><p>They react with carbon dioxide.</p><p>They crystallize into tiny fibrous needles.</p><p>The result?</p><p>A fine, white, woolly coat of sodium carbonate growing right along the edge of your soap.</p><p>This is called soap efflorescence.</p><p>Or soap bloom.</p><p>And here is the fascinating part.</p><p>It happens most dramatically on thin slivers of soap, not fresh bars.</p><p>(Check the attached picture 📸)</p><p>Don''t check "slivers" in the dictionary — I have got you as always.</p><p>It simply refers to those small, flat, leftover pieces of bar soap.</p><p>That time when the soap is almost completely used up?</p><p>Yes. Exactly that.</p><p>But why does it happen on slivers specifically?</p><p>As a bar soap wears down, its surface-area-to-volume ratio increases exponentially.</p><p>All I mean is it becomes far more exposed to evaporation and air activity, especially when left out in the open, compared to when it was still new and fresh.</p><p>The ions have less distance to travel.</p><p>The crystals form faster.</p><p>The bloom is more dramatic.</p><p>Is it dangerous?</p><p>Not at all.</p><p>Run it under warm water and the crystals dissolve instantly.</p><p>Your soap is perfectly fine to use.</p><p>You have probably seen this dozens of times and never knew what it was.</p><p>Now you do.😊</p><p>What did you think it was the first time you saw it?</p><p>As a chemist, do you think this explanation is without corrections?</p><p>Repost — someone in your network needs to know this today.</p><p>#ScienceCommunication #Chemistry #EverydayScience #ScientificWriting #Microbiology #Nigeria</p><p></p><p><br></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788866068/echo-gist/822d6b89-834b-40bf-a86b-a9a08d95380a/fyqv5b7pb7vgjocsgx2n.jpg', 'science', '{"writing","science","sciencecommunication","scientificwriting","cosmetology"}', '822d6b89-834b-40bf-a86b-a9a08d95380a', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 57, 5, 9, '2026-09-08T11:18:36.951Z', '2026-09-23T11:12:20.221Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('3db983af-d1e4-4083-8922-cda248575189', 'The Stranger Who Became My Friend', 'the-stranger-who-became-my-friend-96j1', 'The conversations that made a stranger feel like home', '<p>I said that was only the beginning because who would have thought that a random stranger I met on Facebook one cold Tuesday evening would become my friend?</p><p>We started chatting, and for a girl who didn''t know much, at least not yet, he filled a void I didn''t even know existed.</p><p>You could tell how excited I was every time we texted. It didn''t feel like I was talking to a stranger anymore. We just clicked.</p><p>The conversations flowed effortlessly. The energy was there, the vibe was unmatched, and talking to him felt like I''d known him my whole life.</p><p>I didn''t mind if my Mum''s old android phone, which lagged almost all the time, wasn''t available. I''d gladly pick up my Dad''s button phone and spend what felt like forever trying to connect to Facebook. On a bad day, it could take ages. On a good day, it loaded just fast enough to keep me smiling.</p><p>I didn''t even mind the stress of typing by pressing the same button over and over just to get the right letter.</p><p>And I definitely didn''t enjoy the heartbreaking moments when I accidentally pressed the cancel button and watched my carefully typed message disappear.</p><p>None of it mattered.</p><p>Not the slow internet.</p><p>Not the lagging phones.</p><p>Not the frustration of starting a message all over again.</p><p>As long as I could talk to my stranger.</p><p>Back then, all I saw was a friend.</p><p>I had no idea that this friendship would eventually teach me one of the biggest lessons of my life.</p><p>To be continued...</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1786378854/echo-gist/ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731/xyixgxtytlfjdsvkynkk.jpg', 'lifestyle', '{"socialmedia","nostalgia","growingup","personalstory"}', 'ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 37, 5, 1, '2026-08-10T16:22:34.831Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:03.401Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('3830a6c0-246a-4414-b8c8-c890eb69c1d0', 'Starting Was Never the Hard Part', 'starting-was-never-the-hard-part--0lk0', 'What nobody tells you about staying when the excitement wears off.', '<p>I remember the excitement that comes with starting something new.</p><p>That feeling when an idea suddenly makes sense in your head.</p><p>You start imagining what it could become. You tell yourself, “This time, I’ll actually do it.”</p><p>Maybe you even see someone else doing something similar and suddenly, you want to start too.</p><p>You create the account.</p><p>You write the first post.</p><p>You make the first sale.</p><p>You publish the first video.</p><p>And for a little while, everything feels exciting.</p><p>Then reality happens.</p><p>The excitement wears off.</p><p>The numbers aren''t moving the way you imagined. Nobody seems to be noticing. The thing you were so excited about yesterday suddenly feels like another responsibility today.</p><p>And that''s when you realise:</p><p>Starting was never the hard part.</p><p>Nobody talks about how easy it is to start a thing.</p><p>Nobody talks about how often we want to start a thing.</p><p>Sometimes, all it takes is seeing someone else doing what we''ve always wanted to do. Suddenly, we''re inspired. We want the momentum. We want to experience the feeling of “I''m finally doing something with this.”</p><p>But nobody talks enough about what happens after the excitement.</p><p>When you have to show up even when you don''t feel inspired.</p><p>When you have to keep building when nobody is clapping.</p><p>When you have to keep learning because what you thought would work isn''t working.</p><p>When you have to look at what you''ve built so far and still believe it can become something bigger.</p><p>That''s where the real work begins.</p><p>Consistency is not always glamorous.</p><p>Sometimes, consistency is publishing the post that gets three views.</p><p>Sometimes, it''s trying again after something you were sure would work doesn''t.</p><p>Sometimes, it''s choosing to believe in the process when there is very little evidence that the process is working.</p><p>And perhaps that''s why so many things never get to become what they could have been.</p><p>Not because people couldn''t start.</p><p>But because they couldn''t stay.</p><p>Because starting gives you a rush.</p><p>Building requires patience.</p><p>And growing requires you to remain optimistic even when the results haven''t caught up with your effort yet.</p><p>I think we''re too fascinated by beginnings.</p><p>We celebrate the announcement, the first post, the launch, the new idea.</p><p>But there is something beautiful about the person who quietly keeps going long after the excitement is gone.</p><p>Maybe the real question isn''t, “What do I want to start?”</p><p>Maybe it''s:</p><p>“What am I willing to keep building when starting no longer feels exciting?”</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787078415/echo-gist/ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731/bafjvajehi6vr2j6o011.png', 'lifestyle', '{"consistency"}', 'ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 13, 3, 0, '2026-08-18T18:43:01.112Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:54.036Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('6d188f03-aa34-4a1e-a04d-8fda6623c4c1', 'ChatGPT Is Not Your Friend', 'chatgpt-is-not-your-friend--k6zd', 'ChatGPT is not your friendBut hear me out.You can give it a name. You can tell it about your terrible day. You can ask it for advice at 2 a.m. You can even have…', '<p>ChatGPT is not your friend</p><p>But hear me out.</p><p>You can give it a name. You can tell it about your terrible day. You can ask it for advice at 2 a.m. You can even have it talk to you like your best friend and after a while, it can start feeling strangely personal but ChatGPT doesn''t actually know you the way your best friend does.</p><p>It doesn''t worry about you when you disappear for three days. It doesn''t laugh because it remembers that embarrassing thing you did in secondary school. It doesn''t sit around wondering whether you''re okay.</p><p>It''s an AI and I think remembering that is important.</p><p>Not because ChatGPT is bad. Actually, it''s one of the most useful tools I''ve personally come across.</p><p>You can use it to explain something you don''t understand, brainstorm ideas when your brain is completely empty, help organize your thoughts, practice for an interview, create study questions, improve your writing, or simply help you figure out where to start when you have absolutely no idea what you''re doing but useful doesn''t mean human, and that''s where we sometimes get carried away.</p><p>We can become so comfortable talking to AI that we forget to question what it tells us.</p><p>ChatGPT can be wrong.</p><p>It can misunderstand you.</p><p>It can give you an answer that sounds incredibly confident and still be completely incorrect.</p><p>So please don''t let a beautifully written paragraph convince you that something must be true.</p><p>And maybe don''t hand over every private detail of your life either. Be careful of what you share with it. Your AI doesn''t need your passwords, financial information, or deeply sensitive personal details just because you''re having a late-night conversation.</p><p>Use it.</p><p>Question it.</p><p>Learn from it.</p><p>Let it help you but don''t forget that there''s a difference between having a useful tool and having a human relationship.</p><p>So no, ChatGPT isn''t your friend.</p><p>But it can be a pretty damn useful companion when you know how to use it and yes, before you ask,</p><p>I still love ChatGPT. </p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788038759/echo-gist/38d8e5b3-d4ed-43af-892e-1649c9410730/rjyik1aedhkzlgbkhspm.png', 'technology', '{}', '38d8e5b3-d4ed-43af-892e-1649c9410730', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 20, 9, 0, '2026-08-29T21:32:24.171Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:09.741Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('4a779cd5-dfb0-415e-93d7-4cb4eba75726', '7 Things Nigerians Can Use ChatGPT For That Could Actually Save Them Money', '7-things-nigerians-can-use-chatgpt-for-that-could-actually-save-them-money-hbdn', 'ChatGPT isn’t only for answering questions. If you use it properly, it can help you spend less, learn faster, and even find new ways to make money.', '<p>With the rising cost of everyday expenses in Nigeria, many people are looking for ways to make their money go further. At the same time, AI tools like ChatGPT are becoming easier to access.</p><p>But instead of using AI only for school assignments, captions, or random questions, you can use it as a practical tool for managing your money and making better decisions.</p><p>Here are seven useful ways Nigerians can start using ChatGPT today.</p><p><strong>1. Use ChatGPT to Plan Your Budget</strong></p><p>You don’t always need a complicated spreadsheet to understand where your money is going.</p><p>Give ChatGPT your income and regular expenses, and ask it to help you create a realistic budget.</p><p>For example, you could write:</p><p>“I earn ₦100,000 per month. My transport is ₦20,000, food is ₦30,000, data is ₦10,000, and other expenses are ₦15,000. Help me create a budget and show me where I can reduce spending.”</p><p>It can organize your expenses and help you identify areas where you may be spending more than necessary.</p><p>The important part is to give it <strong>real numbers</strong> rather than guessing.</p><p><strong>2. Find Cheaper Alternatives Before You Buy</strong></p><p>Before spending money on something, ask ChatGPT whether there are cheaper alternatives.</p><p>For example, if you need software, a study resource, a design tool, or another digital service, you can ask:</p><p>“What free alternatives can I use instead of this paid tool?”</p><p>You can then research the alternatives yourself and compare their prices, features, and limitations.</p><p>This doesn’t mean ChatGPT will always know the cheapest option. Prices change, so verify current prices before buying anything.</p><p>But it can give you a starting point instead of immediately reaching for your wallet.</p><p><strong>3. Use It to Learn Skills for Free</strong></p><p>One of the biggest advantages of AI is that you can turn it into a personal tutor.</p><p>Instead of paying for a course immediately, you can ask ChatGPT to teach you a skill step by step.</p><p>For example:</p><p>“Teach me copywriting from beginner to advanced. Give me a 30-day learning plan and practical exercises every day.”</p><p>You can do the same with graphic design, coding, digital marketing, Excel, content writing, or many other skills.</p><p>You still need practice and real-world experience, but AI can help you understand concepts and get feedback while you’re learning.</p><p><strong>4. Improve Your CV and Job Applications</strong></p><p>Applying for jobs can be exhausting, especially when you keep sending the same CV everywhere.</p><p>ChatGPT can help you improve your CV for a particular position.</p><p>Instead of saying:</p><p>“Make my CV better.”</p><p>Try:</p><p>“Here is my CV and here is the job description. Identify the skills the employer is looking for and help me rewrite my CV to clearly show my relevant experience. Don’t invent experience I don’t have.”</p><p>You can also use it to prepare for interviews, generate practice questions, and improve your answers.</p><p>That can save you time and potentially help you submit stronger applications.</p><p><strong>5. Help a Small Business Create Marketing Content</strong></p><p>You don’t have to own a large company to benefit from AI.</p><p>If you sell clothes, food, cosmetics, digital products, or services, ChatGPT can help you create marketing ideas.</p><p>For example:</p><p>“I sell handmade perfumes in Nigeria. Give me 10 Instagram content ideas that can attract potential customers without sounding too salesy.”</p><p>You can also ask for product descriptions, captions, promotional ideas, email messages, and content calendars.</p><p>And there’s another opportunity here.</p><p>If you become good at using AI for these tasks, you could eventually offer <strong>content-writing or social-media services to businesses</strong> instead of only using AI for yourself.</p><p><strong>6. Turn Your Existing Knowledge Into a Service</strong></p><p>You don’t necessarily need to learn an entirely new skill before trying to earn online.</p><p>Think about what you already know.</p><p>Maybe you’re good at writing. Maybe you understand social media. Maybe you can edit videos, design simple graphics, tutor students, translate languages, or do research.</p><p>You can ask ChatGPT:</p><p>“I know how to [insert your skill]. Give me 10 simple services I could offer online, who would pay for them, and examples of what I could deliver.”</p><p>AI can help you turn a vague skill into a specific service.</p><p>For example, instead of saying:</p><p><strong>“I am a writer.”</strong></p><p>You could offer:</p><p><strong>“I write social-media captions and short promotional posts for small businesses.”</strong></p><p>A specific service is often easier for a potential client to understand.</p><p><strong>7. Use ChatGPT to Avoid Bad Spending Decisions</strong></p><p>One of the simplest uses of AI is asking it to challenge your purchase before you make it.</p><p>Suppose you want to buy something expensive.</p><p>Ask:</p><p>“I want to spend ₦80,000 on this item. Give me five questions I should ask myself before buying it.”</p><p>You can also ask it to create a simple <strong>need vs. want</strong> comparison.</p><p>For example:</p><p><strong>Need:</strong> Something essential for school, work, or daily life.</p><p><strong>Want:</strong> Something you would enjoy having but can live without.</p><p>This doesn’t mean ChatGPT should decide what you buy. You should make the final decision based on your own financial situation.</p><p><strong>The Bigger Opportunity</strong></p><p>The real value of ChatGPT isn’t simply getting free answers.</p><p>It’s learning how to <strong>use AI to do useful work faster</strong>.</p><p>Someone might use ChatGPT to write a random poem.</p><p>Someone else might use it to build a study plan, improve their CV, organize a business, create marketing content, research an idea, or develop a service they can sell.</p><p>The difference isn’t necessarily the tool.</p><p>It’s <strong>how you use it</strong>.</p><p>And you don’t need to become an AI expert overnight.</p><p>Start with one problem you already have.</p><p>Ask ChatGPT to help you solve it.</p><p>Check the information it gives you.</p><p>Make your own decisions.</p><p>Then repeat the process.</p><p><strong>Final Thought</strong></p><p>With the cost of living putting pressure on many Nigerians, saving ₦1,000, ₦5,000, or ₦10,000 where possible can matter.</p><p>At the same time, learning how to use AI effectively could help you become more productive and potentially create new income opportunities.</p><p>So don’t just ask ChatGPT:</p><p><strong>“What can you do?”</strong></p><p>Ask:</p><p><strong>“What problem can you help me solve?”</strong></p><p>That question can lead to much more useful answers.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789743706/echo-gist/f52280a4-722f-4591-a509-c385360c9f1a/zaasew7ktsr56l8ihirk.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'f52280a4-722f-4591-a509-c385360c9f1a', TRUE, FALSE, 5, 8, 4, 1, '2026-09-18T15:03:12.125Z', '2026-09-29T09:20:48.578Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('2dfa21b5-ce13-4a3b-9595-7018a935c3ad', '"Is It Not Just Food?"', 'is-it-not-just-food-jbei', 'Fool Me Once', '<p>Living off a student budget means every single naira counts. Tired of watching her allowance vanish into the pockets of campus food vendors, Amaka spent her Saturday afternoon in the kitchen.</p><p>She cooked enough to survive the brutal stretch of upcoming lectures and assignments ahead.</p><p>A rich, fragrant batch of chicken stew and a thick, well-seasoned pot of egusi soup. </p><p>Living in a shared apartment with two other girls, she took no chances. She neatly portioned the food into plastic bowls, bolded her name across every lid with a permanent marker, and stacked them securely in the communal fridge.</p><p>By Tuesday evening, the stew looked suspiciously light. "Maybe I ate more than I remembered", she thought, brushing it off.</p><p>By Wednesday, half her egusi soup was outright gone. There was no denying it now; someone was helping themselves. </p><p>Amaka stood by the fridge, her blood pressure rising. She immediately called out to her two roommates, demanding to know who had been raiding her shelf. Both met her gaze with wide, exaggerated eyes of innocence, swearing on their lives they hadn''t touched her food.</p><p>Then came Thursday.</p><p>When Amaka opened the fridge, her stew and egusi soup were completely gone and the containers sat empty, licked clean, and left unwashed. It wasn''t just theft anymore; it was pure disrespect.</p><p>She didn''t bother asking twice. She threw open the bedroom door and confronted them point-blank. Cornered by the sheer fury in her eyes, the roommates finally abandoned their denials and copped to the crime, though their defense was nothing short of tragicomedy.</p><p>"Haba, Amaka, why are you vexing over ordinary food?", standard guilt-tripping from Chidinma the vocal one. </p><p>"Is it not just food?", Chidinma said rolling her eyes as if Amaka was the unreasonable one. "You should pity us naw. You know my father lost all his shop capital recently, so things have been tight. We''ve been starving."</p><p>Amaka stared at her in utter disbelief. Only forty-eight hours earlier, Chidinma had strutted into the room wrapped in a brand-new bone-straight wig, clutching a designer shopping bag, with a fresh bottle of Hennessy resting on her study table. Starving, indeed.</p><p>Instead of shouting, Amaka let out a cold, quiet breath. She didn''t yell. She didn''t threaten. She simply nodded and walked away.</p><p>Friday evening, she went back to the kitchen. She whipped up a fresh, delicious batch of stew, labeled it with her name just like before, and slid it into the fridge. </p><p>Only this time, the secret ingredient wasn''t rosemary, it was a heavy-handed dose of medical-grade laxatives, thoroughly blended into the rich, spicy sauce.</p><p>She didn''t have to wait long. Chidinma and her co-conspirator didn''t just eat the stolen stew; they treated it like a weekend buffet, inviting an unsuspecting friend over to help them finish it.</p><p>When Amaka returned from her evening classes, she walked into a lively room. The three girls were sitting together, gisting and laughing loudly, completely unbothered by her presence. Amaka didn''t say a word about the food. She simply grabbed her bag, casually stepped out into the hallway, and locked the bedroom door securely from the outside with a heavy padlock she had bought just for the occasion.</p><p>Not two minutes later, the first rumble sounded.</p><p>What started as a frantic knock quickly escalated into desperate, frantic pounding against the wood. Screams of panic pierced the air as the chemical time bomb detonated simultaneously inside three digestive tracts.</p><p>With the single bathroom trapped on the other side of the locked bedroom door, total and catastrophic chaos erupted.</p><p>By the time Amaka strolled back to the room an hour later, the noise had died down to pathetic, muffled whimpers.</p><p>She unlocked the door and pushed it open. The room smelled like a biological hazard zone. Three broken, defeated girls sat in absolute silence, staring at the floor, utterly stripped of their pride.</p><p>Amaka leaned casually against the doorframe, her expression entirely deadpan.</p><p>"Let this be a permanent lesson," she said smoothly. "Never touch what doesn''t belong to you. Next time, the consequences won''t be flushed away."</p><p>With that, she left the door wide open for them to figure out their own cleanup, turned on her heel, and walked out into the evening breeze.</p><p></p><p>What would you have done if you were Amaka?</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787087256/echo-gist/64a4435f-e4df-4c17-8422-16439c81d14b/eub8iqhepsf88mp4fip4.jpg', 'writing', '{"drama","stories","reality","wow"}', '64a4435f-e4df-4c17-8422-16439c81d14b', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 25, 4, 2, '2026-08-18T21:25:56.425Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:52.813Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('ba858c73-7fa5-4d49-b18b-0215b1af1e1d', 'He Saw People Getting Paid. Then He Sent ₦50,000.', 'he-saw-people-getting-paid-then-he-sent-50000-atrr', 'What looked like an opportunity turned out to be an expensive lesson.', '<p>My friend thought he had found an easy way to make money.</p><p>He had been seeing people participate in this online "fast fingers" challenge. Someone would drop a word or phrase, and whoever typed it first could win money.</p><p>People were posting screenshots.</p><p>People were celebrating.</p><p>Someone he knew was even participating and appearing on the winners'' list.</p><p>So, naturally, it started looking real.</p><p>Then came the bigger opportunity.</p><p><strong>Money doubling.</strong></p><p>Send ₦50,000.</p><p>Get much more back.</p><p>It sounded almost too good to be true.</p><p>But he''d already seen the screenshots.</p><p>So he tried it.</p><p>He went to the bank, made the transfer and sent the receipt.</p><p>The response was simple:</p><p><strong>“I''ve received it. Wait for your payment.”</strong></p><p>He waited.</p><p>One hour.</p><p>Then another.</p><p>Nothing.</p><p>Eventually, he realized the ₦50,000 wasn''t coming back.</p><p>And then he learned something even more interesting about the whole thing.</p><p>Some of the "proof" people had been seeing wasn''t proof at all.</p><p>Screenshots could be manipulated.</p><p>Conversations could be carefully selected.</p><p>Even identities could be used to make the opportunity look more trustworthy.</p><p>And suddenly, the whole thing made sense.</p><p><strong>The scam wasn''t just asking people for money.</strong></p><p>It was first giving them a reason to believe.</p><p>That''s what makes online scams so dangerous.</p><p>We often think we''ll recognize a scam because it''ll look suspicious.</p><p>But what if it doesn''t?</p><p>What if there''s a screenshot?</p><p>What if other people appear to be getting paid?</p><p>What if someone you know is participating?</p><p>What if the account looks like someone you already trust?</p><p>At that point, you''re no longer asking:</p><p><strong>“Is this real?”</strong></p><p>You''re asking:</p><p><strong>“How much should I send?”</strong></p><p>And that''s the part we need to be careful about.</p><p>A screenshot isn''t proof.</p><p>A testimonial isn''t proof.</p><p>A crowded WhatsApp group isn''t proof.</p><p>And someone looking successful online doesn''t automatically make their offer legitimate.</p><p>Before you send your money, <strong>verify what you''re being shown.</strong></p><p>Because sometimes the most convincing part of a scam is not the promise of getting rich.</p><p><strong>It''s the fake evidence that makes you believe everyone else already did.</strong></p><p>And my friend?</p><p>He learned that lesson for <strong>₦50,000.</strong></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787749452/echo-gist/6110aa8e-6f0d-4df4-ad0b-2ac775af342f/tzkxulehily6fmrehvd5.png', 'lifestyle', '{}', '6110aa8e-6f0d-4df4-ad0b-2ac775af342f', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 10, 3, 1, '2026-08-26T13:07:21.024Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:24.316Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('219cff8a-28fd-4d37-b627-54d9ab6a751b', 'Chelsea Secure 2-0 Win Over Luton
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('3b3dea33-1ea7-47f8-9917-001e19944d8f', 'WHY WE CAN''T LOOK AWAY FROM OTHER PEOPLE''S MESS', 'why-we-cant-look-away-from-other-peoples-mess-zjiy', 'It''s Not Gossip. It''s Something Older Than That.', '<p>You told yourself you were not going to click on it. Then you clicked on it. Then you watched the screen recording twice, read every quote tweet, checked if there was a part two, and somehow ended up more emotionally invested in two strangers'' breakup than in your own unanswered messages. You know this feeling, I''m sure you do. Everyone does. Nobody quite admits how often they engage in it.</p><p><strong>THE MESS YOU DON''T HAVE TO CLEAN</strong> There''s a specific relief in watching someone else''s disaster unfold, you get all the drama, the stakes, the emotional charge, with none of the consequences. You don''t have to apologize to anyone tomorrow. You don''t have to live with the fallout. It''s conflict with the safety rail on, and that combination is almost impossible to resist.</p><p>Compare that to your own unresolved situations,  the friend you are avoiding, the decision you keep postponing. Those come with real weight. A stranger''s scandal comes with none. Of course the second one is easier to sit with.</p><p><strong>WHAT YOU''RE ACTUALLY WATCHING FOR </strong>It''s rarely really about the specific celebrity or the specific fight. It''s about permission, permission to feel a strong emotion (outrage, judgment, secondhand embarrassment) without having earned it through your own mess. Watching someone else''s public unraveling lets you feel intensely without being exposed yourself. That''s the quiet trade happening every time a scandal goes viral, millions of people borrowing someone else''s chaos to feel something, safely, from a distance.</p><p><strong>THE COMPARISON NOBODY SAYS OUT LOUD </strong></p><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788867726/echo-gist/817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4/nrm8gjwjq1t2c9heany5.png" alt="1000774752.png" style="max-width: 100%; border-radius: 8px; margin: 24px 0px; display: block;"><p>Here''s the part that doesn''t get admitted enough, watching someone else fall apart publicly makes your own private mess feel smaller by comparison. If someone''s entire reputation can collapse over a leaked voice note, your unpaid bill or unfinished project suddenly feels almost manageable. It''s a strange, unspoken comfort, not because you wish anyone harm, but because someone else''s public disaster quietly recalibrates what "doing badly" looks like, and you come out of the comparison feeling slightly less alone in your own. True or false?</p><p><strong>THE UNCOMFORTABLE TRUTH</strong> </p><p>Some of the loudest reactions to a scandal come from people who recognize a piece of themselves in it and are relieved it''s someone else''s name attached instead of theirs. The intensity of the judgment is sometimes a measure of how close it hit, not how far removed the viewer actually is.</p><p>That does not make the outrage fake. It just means it''s rarely only about the person being dragged. It''s also about what their mess reflects back.</p><p><strong>WHY THIS IS NOT ACTUALLY ABOUT BEING A BAD PERSON </strong></p><p>It''s tempting to read all of this as evidence that people are cruel or shallow. That''s too simple. Curiosity about other people''s lives is old, older than the internet, older than celebrity culture. What changed is the volume and speed. You used to hear about someone''s business weeks later, filtered through a few people. Now you get it live, raw, and endlessly replayable, with a comment section pushing you to react immediately instead of just observing quietly.</p><p>The fascination is not new. The infrastructure around it is what makes it feel inescapable now.</p><p><strong>WHAT TO ACTUALLY DO WITH THIS </strong></p><p>Not guilt, and not forced detachment either, pretending you don''t care about any of it usually doesn''t last. Just a small honest check: notice what a scandal makes you feel before you comment on it. Relief that it''s not you? Recognition of something you''ve done yourself, quietly?</p><p>That question won''t stop you from watching. It just stops you from mistaking watching for something more innocent than it is.</p><p>Maybe the real story was never really about them, yes. Maybe it''s about what their unraveling gave you permission to feel, and what that says about what you''ve been sitting on all this while.</p><p>Think about it for a while, is it really about them?</p><p>Shalom!</p><p>Don''t forget to like. Follow. Share. Comment</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788867465/echo-gist/817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4/pq5l4iordkvstz8m1uvx.png', 'lifestyle', '{"lifestylesocialmediadramagossipstrendssocialmediamess"}', '817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 32, 2, 0, '2026-09-08T11:46:26.118Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:22.764Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('8796bde0-8ec2-4e2d-a53d-73d3beea7bd9', 'We Don''t Have an Information Problem. We Have a Trust Problem.', 'we-dont-have-an-information-problem-we-have-a-trust-problem-tx74', 'A screenshot felt like proof.', '<p>A few years ago, if someone showed you a video, you probably believed what you were seeing.</p><p>A screenshot felt like proof.</p><p>A voice recording sounded like evidence.</p><p>And if something came from a familiar-looking news page, you probably didn''t think twice before sharing it.</p><p>But things have changed.</p><p>The internet has become very good at showing us things that look real.</p><p>And sometimes, that''s exactly the problem.</p><p>You wake up.</p><p>Someone sends a message to the group chat.</p><p>"Have you seen this?"</p><p>There''s a screenshot.</p><p>Maybe a video too.</p><p>It looks serious.</p><p>You read it, react to it, perhaps send it to someone else.</p><p>Then later, you discover that it wasn''t true.</p><p>Maybe the picture was generated.</p><p>Maybe the video was edited.</p><p>Maybe the person in the audio never actually said those words.</p><p>And you find yourself thinking:</p><p>How was I supposed to know?</p><p>That''s the difficult part.</p><p>Sometimes, you weren''t supposed to know immediately.</p><p>The content was made to look believable.</p><p>We used to trust what we could see.</p><p>If someone told you something happened, you might ask for a picture.</p><p>"Send me proof."</p><p>And if they sent you a screenshot, that often settled the argument.</p><p>But a screenshot can be created.</p><p>A picture can be changed.</p><p>A video can be manipulated.</p><p>A voice can be generated.</p><p>And now AI makes creating convincing content easier than it used to be.</p><p>This doesn''t mean that everything you see online is fake.</p><p>It means something has changed:</p><p>Seeing is no longer enough.</p><p>But here''s the part we don''t talk about enough.</p><p>The problem isn''t only that people create false information.</p><p>It''s that we''ve become very comfortable believing things that feel right.</p><p>If a story confirms something we already believe, we''re more likely to accept it.</p><p>If everyone in our WhatsApp group is talking about it, it feels true.</p><p>If the person sharing it is someone we trust, we don''t always question it.</p><p>If there''s a screenshot, we think:</p><p>"There. That''s the evidence."</p><p>Sometimes, we don''t even realize we''ve stopped checking.</p><p>We''re no longer asking:</p><p>"Is this true?"</p><p>We''re asking:</p><p>"Does this look true?"</p><p>And those are two very different questions.</p><p>So what do we do now?</p><p>I''m not saying you should become suspicious of everything.</p><p>That would be exhausting.</p><p>You don''t need to investigate every WhatsApp message like you''re solving a crime.</p><p>But maybe we need to develop a small habit:</p><p>Pause before you believe.</p><p>Who posted this?</p><p>Where did it originally come from?</p><p>Can I find the same story somewhere else?</p><p>Does the original source actually say what this screenshot claims?</p><p>And perhaps the hardest question:</p><p>Am I believing this because it''s true, or because I want it to be true?</p><p>Sometimes, that question is enough to stop us from spreading something we haven''t actually verified.</p><p>The internet has given us access to more information than any generation before us.</p><p>We can learn almost anything.</p><p>We can hear from people on the other side of the world.</p><p>We can see events almost as they happen.</p><p>AI can help us create, research and understand things faster than ever.</p><p>But with all that access comes another responsibility.</p><p>We have to become better at deciding what deserves our trust.</p><p>Because we don''t have an information problem.</p><p>We have a trust problem.</p><p>And perhaps one of the most important digital skills we can develop isn''t learning how to find more information.</p><p>It''s learning when to stop, question what we''re seeing, and decide whether it deserves to be believed.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788174498/echo-gist/6110aa8e-6f0d-4df4-ad0b-2ac775af342f/sdneohfklbetuo5mhlr1.jpg', 'technology', '{"digitalera","ai","awareness"}', '6110aa8e-6f0d-4df4-ad0b-2ac775af342f', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 3, 2, 0, '2026-08-31T11:10:32.007Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:01.957Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('3cd7c367-4005-4a5d-85ad-5e5986ed30ff', 'Found myself  here.', 'found-myself-here-frqh', 'Hello everyone, I joined from the writer''s review community. So glad to be here. Can''t wait to participate with everyone.…', '<p>Hello everyone, I joined from the writer''s review community. So glad to be here. Can''t wait to participate with everyone.</p>', NULL, 'technology', '{}', '9a9d4c27-a8f1-4e8d-a615-f6c38b5c6383', FALSE, FALSE, 1, 19, 2, 2, '2026-08-17T09:41:15.602Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:55.650Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('31349563-cb11-478e-9551-ab84815c267b', 'A Splash of Color', 'a-splash-of-color--yzmd', 'If love was always perfect, it would probably mean a lot less.', '<p>A friend once called me and asked,</p><p>“Can you be faithful to one woman all your life?”</p><p>Four years ago, I wouldn’t even think twice.</p><p>Of course.</p><p>Back then, I really thought I understood love.<br>Lowkey felt like I had it figured out.</p><p>But now I know better.<br>You can’t be a master at something you’ve barely experienced.</p><p>Then she asked something deeper.</p><p>Why does God allow certain things to happen?</p><p>And honestly, the first thing that came to my mind was simple.</p><p>A splash of color.</p><p>Imagine this for a second.</p><p>You meet the right person the very first time.<br>No heartbreak. No confusion. No wrong choices.</p><p>Just straight love.<br>Clean. Easy. Perfect.</p><p>You meet, you click, you marry.<br>End of story.</p><p>Sounds nice, right?</p><p>But if you really think about it…</p><p>that kind of life would be boring.</p><p>Too smooth.</p><p>Like a painting with just one color.</p><p>Because love as we know it doesn’t come like that.</p><p>It’s built in contrast.</p><p>In the ups and downs.<br>In the moments that excite you and the ones that break you a little.</p><p>The confusion.<br>The disappointment.<br>The “what is even going on” phases.</p><p>That’s what gives it depth.</p><p>Even the parts we hate,<br>betrayal, mistrust, heartbreak,</p><p>they’re not from God.</p><p>But somehow, they still shape us.</p><p>Because in those moments, you’re forced to decide who you are.</p><p>You choose to be kind, not because life has been kind to you,<br>but because you want to be.</p><p>You choose to forgive when everything in you is saying don’t.</p><p>And that choice?</p><p>That’s where things get real.</p><p>That’s where you meet yourself.</p><p>Not the version your parents raised.<br>Not the one shaped by rules or expectations.</p><p>But the one that shows up<br>when you have every reason to do wrong<br>and you still choose right.</p><p>So maybe life without all this,</p><p>without the chaos,<br>without the uncertainty,</p><p>would actually be missing something.</p><p>Because certainty sounds nice<br>until it becomes your entire life.</p><p>Then it starts to feel empty.</p><p>Predictable.</p><p>Like you already know how everything ends.</p><p>And where’s the fun in that?</p><p>So maybe the goal isn’t to avoid the chaos.</p><p>Maybe it’s to step into it.</p><p>To experience it fully.</p><p>To learn from it without letting it define you.</p><p>Because somewhere inside all that mess,</p><p>you might just find your answer<br>to why.</p><p>P.S I later said yes, please I don’t cheat 🤣</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789541368/echo-gist/82b4b527-06d6-42d5-8cba-cb7e09cda146/f1x49cwc2ibckpxkdoaa.jpg', 'lifestyle', '{"selfdiscovery","personalgrowth","faithlife","humanexperience"}', '82b4b527-06d6-42d5-8cba-cb7e09cda146', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 16, 3, 5, '2026-09-16T06:55:13.532Z', '2026-09-19T14:32:19.813Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('6c0bb10e-5dd7-46bd-a27a-b22a1ac5d827', 'Comfort is a slow trap', 'comfort-is-a-slow-trap--bg8j', 'When you stop chasing and start settling without realizing it', '<p>Comfort</p><p>Comfort used to mean something different.</p><p>It was what our parents chased. Stability. Safety. A life that didn’t constantly feel like survival. And somehow, that desire got passed down to us.</p><p>But somewhere along the line, something changed.</p><p>Because now, comfort doesn’t always feel like safety.</p><p>Sometimes, it feels like a cage.</p><p>I try to imagine how it started. Maybe they didn’t set out to raise people who would settle. Maybe they were once like us. Young. Certain. Full of dreams. Talking about the future like it was guaranteed.</p><p>So what really went wrong?</p><p>Let me paint it simply.</p><p>Between 15 and 25, almost everyone is a dreamer. It doesn’t matter if you’re rich, average, or below. You have ideas. Big ones. You see a version of your life that feels possible.</p><p>But here’s the truth.</p><p>Most people dream.<br>Very few act.</p><p>Then comes 25 to 30.</p><p>Reality starts to tap you on the shoulder. Some of your mates are already living the life you imagined for yourself. And you’re left with two choices.</p><p>You either sink…<br>or you push.</p><p>But even when you try to push, something fights you.</p><p>Your brain.</p><p>People don’t talk about this enough.</p><p>Your brain doesn’t understand dreams. It understands patterns.</p><p>Its job is simple. Keep you alive.</p><p>And to do that, it resists change.</p><p>Because change feels like risk. Risk feels like danger. And danger is something your brain is wired to avoid.</p><p>So what does it do?</p><p>It creates comfort.</p><p>Not real comfort.<br>False comfort.</p><p>Procrastination becomes its best tool. Delay. Avoid. Stay where it’s safe.</p><p>And slowly, without even realizing it, you settle.</p><p>That’s where the real danger is.</p><p>Because comfort, when left unchecked, becomes the beginning of decline.</p><p>You start to justify things.</p><p>You call it being “realistic.”<br>You tell yourself certain levels of success aren’t for you.</p><p>And before you know it, you’re no longer chasing anything.</p><p>You’re just… maintaining.</p><p>Living hand to mouth. Surviving instead of building.</p><p>I call that the second fall.</p><p>But I’m not here to preach doom.</p><p>Because there’s good news.</p><p>Your brain can be rewired.</p><p>Not overnight. Not with one big decision. But with small, consistent actions.</p><p>Micro moves.</p><p>Doing something, no matter how small, that moves you forward. Then doing it again. And again.</p><p>It won’t feel easy. In fact, it will feel unnatural at first.</p><p>That’s the point.</p><p>You’re breaking a pattern.</p><p>And if you really want to go further, build structure around yourself.</p><p>Put yourself around people who are ahead of you. People who won’t clap for your excuses. People who will call you out when you start slipping.</p><p>Because growth doesn’t happen in isolation.</p><p>And comfort, left alone, will always win.</p><p>The ages I mentioned are just examples.</p><p>Change isn’t tied to time.</p><p>It’s tied to action.</p><p>So no matter where you are right now…</p><p>Start.</p><p>Even if it’s small.</p><p>Especially if it’s small.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1786477097/echo-gist/82b4b527-06d6-42d5-8cba-cb7e09cda146/jh9oikznmdm9vecyvklt.jpg', 'productivity', '{"persongrowth","mindset","selfimprovement","mentalhealth","motivation"}', '82b4b527-06d6-42d5-8cba-cb7e09cda146', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 50, 6, 5, '2026-08-11T19:41:30.856Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:03.160Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('31da4965-84e7-43e0-a9d3-600ffd27c31b', 'Why Nobody Is Reading Your Content — How to Get Your First 1,000 Readers as a Beginner Content Writer', 'why-nobody-is-reading-your-content-how-to-get-your-first-1000-readers-as-a-begin-b1ka', 'Starting a career as a content writer can be exciting, but the early stages are rarely easy.You may have good writing skills, plenty of ideas and the motivation…', '<p>Starting a career as a content writer can be exciting, but the early stages are rarely easy.</p><p></p><p>You may have good writing skills, plenty of ideas and the motivation to create content, yet still struggle to get people to read your work. Sometimes, you can spend hours researching and writing an article only to get a handful of views.</p><p></p><p>This can be discouraging, especially when you see other writers posting content that receives hundreds or thousands of views.</p><p></p><p>But getting traffic is not only about being a good writer. It also requires understanding your audience, choosing the right topics, promoting your work and consistently creating content that people actually want to consume.</p><p></p><p>If you are a beginner wondering why your content is not getting enough attention, here are some common challenges you may face and practical steps you can take to grow your audience.</p><p></p><h4>Challenges Content Writers Face in the Early Stages</h4><p></p><h3>1. Getting Your First Readers</h3><p></p><p>One of the biggest challenges for a new content writer is getting people to read their work.</p><p></p><p>When you are just starting, you may not have an established audience on your blog or social media platforms. Search engines may also take time to discover and rank your content.</p><p></p><p>This means that even a well-written article can remain almost invisible.</p><p></p><p>The solution is not to give up. You need to actively put your content in front of the people who are most likely to find it useful.</p><p></p><h3>2. Not Knowing What to Write About</h3><p></p><p>Another common problem is choosing topics.</p><p></p><p>Some writers write about whatever comes to mind without first considering whether anyone is interested in the subject.</p><p></p><p>Writing about your interests is important, but if your goal is to attract traffic, you also need to understand what your target audience is searching for, discussing and struggling with.</p><p></p><p>A topic that solves a real problem will usually have more potential than a random topic that provides little value.</p><p></p><h3>3. Comparing Yourself With Established Writers</h3><p></p><p>Social media can make the early stages of writing feel harder than they actually are.</p><p></p><p>You may see another writer with thousands of followers, viral posts and major publications and wonder why your own content is not performing the same way.</p><h3></h3><p>What you may not see is the amount of time that person has spent building their audience.</p><p></p><p>Instead of comparing your beginning to someone else''s progress, study what successful writers are doing and learn from their strategies.</p><p></p><h3>4. Inconsistent Content Creation</h3><p></p><p>Many beginners start with enthusiasm.</p><p></p><p>They publish several articles in their first few weeks and then stop when the expected traffic does not come.</p><p></p><p>Building an audience takes time. People need to see your work repeatedly before they become regular readers.</p><p></p><p>Consistency does not necessarily mean publishing several times every day. It means creating a realistic schedule and sticking to it.</p><p></p><h3>5. Poor Content Promotion</h3><p></p><p>Publishing an article and waiting for people to find it is rarely enough.</p><p></p><p>Even excellent content needs distribution.</p><p></p><p>A new writer may have a useful article on their website but fail to share it on LinkedIn, Facebook, X, WhatsApp communities, Reddit or other platforms where potential readers spend time.</p><p></p><p>Promotion should therefore be treated as part of content creation, not an afterthought.</p><p></p><h3>6. Writing for Everyone</h3><p></p><p>Trying to appeal to everyone can make your content less useful to anyone.</p><p></p><p>A beginner writer should have a clear understanding of who they are writing for.</p><p></p><p>For example, instead of simply writing about "technology," you could focus on technology for Nigerian students, AI tools for freelancers, digital skills for young professionals or remote-work opportunities for Africans.</p><p></p><p>The more clearly you understand your audience, the easier it becomes to create relevant content.</p><p></p><h3>How Content Writers Can Build Huge Traffic and a Strong Audience</h3><p></p><p>There is no secret button that guarantees thousands of visitors overnight. However, there is a process that can significantly improve your chances of attracting consistent traffic.</p><p></p><p>Here is a step-by-step approach.</p><p></p><h3>Step 1: Choose a Clear Niche</h3><p></p><p>Start by deciding what you want people to know you for.</p><p></p><p>Your niche could be:</p><p></p><p>- Technology</p><p>- Artificial intelligence</p><p>- Education</p><p>- Remote work</p><p>- Career development</p><p>- Business</p><p>- Digital marketing</p><p>- Personal finance</p><p>- Lifestyle</p><p>- Health and wellness</p><p></p><p>You do not have to limit yourself to one subject forever. However, having a clear direction makes it easier to attract a specific audience.</p><p></p><p>For example, a writer who consistently publishes useful information about AI tools and remote work may gradually become known by people interested in those areas.</p><p></p><h3>Step 2: Understand Your Target Audience</h3><p></p><p>Before writing an article, ask yourself:</p><p></p><p>Who am I writing this for?</p><p></p><p>Then think about their problems.</p><p></p><p>What are they searching for?</p><p></p><p>What questions do they ask?</p><p></p><p>What information do they struggle to find?</p><p></p><p>What would make them click on your article?</p><p></p><p>For example, if your audience consists of Nigerian graduates, topics such as "How to Find Remote Jobs From Nigeria," "Best Digital Skills to Learn in 2026" or "Free Online Courses With Certificates" may be more relevant than a general article about technology.</p><p></p><p>The closer your content is to your audience''s actual needs, the better your chances of attracting them.</p><p></p><h3>Step 3: Find Topics People Are Already Searching For</h3><p></p><p>Do not always start with a blank page and simply ask yourself what to write.</p><p></p><p>Research first.</p><p></p><p>Look at Google search suggestions, Google Trends, social media discussions, news developments, forums and questions people are asking online.</p><p></p><p>You can also examine what established websites in your niche are publishing.</p><p></p><p>The goal is not to copy them.</p><p></p><p>Instead, identify gaps.</p><p></p><p>If people are searching for an answer but existing articles are confusing, outdated or incomplete, that could be an opportunity for you.</p><p></p><h3>Step 4: Create Strong Headlines</h3><p></p><p>Your headline is often the first thing a potential reader sees.</p><p></p><p>A weak headline can make people scroll past even when the article contains valuable information.</p><p></p><p>Compare:</p><p></p><p>"Remote Work Skills"</p><p></p><p>with:</p><p></p><p>"7 High-Income Skills You Can Learn in 2026 to Start Working Remotely"</p><p></p><p>The second title immediately tells the reader what they will get from the article.</p><p></p><p>Good headlines should be clear, specific and interesting without making false promises.</p><p></p><h3>Step 5: Make Your Introduction Count</h3><p></p><p>Once someone clicks your article, the introduction has an important job: convince them to continue reading.</p><p></p><p>Avoid starting every article with a long, generic introduction.</p><p></p><p>Instead, quickly establish the problem and explain what the reader will gain.</p><p></p><p>For example:</p><p></p><p>«Finding a remote job can be difficult when you do not know which skills employers are looking for. The good news is that several digital skills can be learned online and turned into income opportunities.»</p><p></p><p>The reader immediately understands what the article is about.</p><p></p><h3>Step 6: Focus on Solving Problems</h3><p></p><p>People do not visit websites simply because someone has published an article.</p><p></p><p>They visit because they want something.</p><p></p><p>They want an answer, solution, explanation, recommendation, idea or piece of information.</p><p></p><p>Therefore, before publishing, ask:</p><p></p><p>What problem does this article solve?</p><p></p><p>If you cannot answer that question clearly, the article may need more work.</p><p></p><p>Useful content is more likely to be saved, shared, recommended and revisited.</p><p></p><h3>Step 7: Learn Basic SEO</h3><p></p><p>Search engine optimization, commonly known as SEO, can help people discover your content through search engines.</p><p></p><p>You do not need to become an SEO expert before publishing your first article.</p><p></p><p>Start with the basics.</p><p></p><p>Use the main topic naturally in your:</p><p></p><p>- Title</p><p>- Introduction</p><p>- Subheadings</p><p>- Body of the article</p><p>- Meta description</p><p>- Image alt text, where appropriate</p><p></p><p>You should also create clear URLs and link to relevant articles on your own website.</p><p></p><p>Most importantly, do not fill your article with keywords simply to manipulate search rankings. Write for humans first.</p><p></p><h3>Step 8: Create Content Around Topics, Not Just Individual Articles</h3><p></p><p>One article may bring a visitor to your website.</p><p></p><p>A collection of related articles can keep that visitor there.</p><p></p><p>For example, if you write about remote work, you could create several connected articles:</p><p></p><p>Main topic: Remote Work</p><p></p><p>Supporting articles:</p><p></p><p>- Best Skills to Learn for Remote Work</p><p>- How to Create a Remote Work Portfolio</p><p>- Where Beginners Can Find Remote Jobs</p><p>- Common Remote Job Application Mistakes</p><p>- How to Prepare for a Remote Job Interview</p><p></p><p>You can then link these articles to one another.</p><p></p><p>This creates a content network that helps readers discover more of your work.</p><p></p><h3>Step 9: Promote Every Article</h3><p></p><p>Do not stop after clicking "Publish."</p><p></p><p>Share your article across platforms where your audience is active.</p><p></p><p>You can promote it through:</p><p></p><p>- LinkedIn</p><p>- Facebook</p><p>- X</p><p>- WhatsApp</p><p>- Telegram communities</p><p>- Relevant online communities</p><p>- Email newsletters</p><p></p><p>However, avoid repeatedly dropping links without context.</p><p></p><p>Instead, write a short post explaining why the article is useful and give people a reason to click.</p><p></p><p>For example, rather than saying:</p><p></p><p>"Read my new article: [link]"</p><p></p><p>you could say:</p><p></p><p>"Many beginners want remote jobs but don''t know which skills employers are actually looking for. I broke down seven skills that can help you get started and where you can learn them."</p><p></p><p>Then direct interested readers to the full article.</p><p></p><h3>Step 10: Build an Email Audience</h3><p></p><p>Social media followers are useful, but building an email list gives you a direct way to reach people who want your content.</p><p></p><p>Offer readers a reason to subscribe.</p><p></p><p>You could provide:</p><p></p><p>- A free guide</p><p>- Writing tips</p><p>- Weekly career opportunities</p><p>- Remote job updates</p><p>- AI tool recommendations</p><p>- Industry news</p><p>- Exclusive articles</p><p></p><p>Send useful emails consistently rather than filling people''s inboxes with constant promotions.</p><p></p><p>Over time, your email list can become one of your most valuable traffic sources.</p><p></p><h3>Step 11: Turn One Article Into Multiple Pieces of Content</h3><p></p><p>You do not always have to create something completely new for every platform.</p><p></p><p>One detailed blog post can become several pieces of content.</p><p></p><p>For example, a blog about "How to Get Your First Remote Job" can become:</p><p></p><p>- A LinkedIn post</p><p>- Five short social media posts</p><p>- An Instagram carousel</p><p>- A short video script</p><p>- An email newsletter</p><p>- A discussion topic</p><p>- A list of interview tips</p><p></p><p>This strategy allows you to reach different audiences without starting from scratch every time.</p><p></p><h3>Step 12: Build Relationships With Other Creators</h3><p></p><p>You do not have to build your audience alone.</p><p></p><p>Connect with other writers, bloggers, creators, professionals and businesses within your niche.</p><p></p><p>Comment thoughtfully on their posts.</p><p></p><p>Join relevant conversations.</p><p></p><p>Share useful insights.</p><p></p><p>Collaborate when possible.</p><p></p><p>Networking can introduce your work to people who would never have discovered it otherwise.</p><p></p><h3>Step 13: Study Your Analytics</h3><p></p><p>Do not rely only on likes and comments to decide whether your content is successful.</p><p></p><p>Look at your website and social media analytics to understand what is actually happening.</p><p></p><p>Pay attention to:</p><p></p><p>- Page views</p><p>- Search traffic</p><p>- Click-through rate</p><p>- Time spent on pages</p><p>- Returning visitors</p><p>- Most popular articles</p><p>- Traffic sources</p><p></p><p>If one topic consistently performs better than others, investigate why.</p><p></p><p>You may discover that your audience wants more content around that subject.</p><p></p><h3>Step 14: Update Your Best Articles</h3><p></p><p>Getting traffic is not only about publishing new content.</p><p></p><p>Older articles can continue bringing visitors if they remain useful.</p><p></p><p>Review your best-performing articles periodically.</p><p></p><p>Update outdated information, improve the introduction, add useful sections, replace broken links and make sure the article still answers the reader''s question.</p><p></p><p>An article published months ago can sometimes become more valuable after a good update.</p><p></p><p></p><h3>A Simple 30-Day Traffic-Building Plan</h3><p></p><p>If you are completely new to content writing, you can start with a simple plan.</p><p></p><p>Week 1: Find Your Direction</p><p></p><p>Choose your niche.</p><p></p><p>Identify your target audience.</p><p></p><p>Research at least 20 questions or topics your audience is interested in.</p><p></p><p>Choose five strong topics from your research.</p><p></p><p>Week 2: Create Valuable Content</p><p></p><p>Write two or three detailed articles.</p><p></p><p>Focus on solving specific problems instead of simply trying to hit a word count.</p><p></p><p>Make your headlines clear and interesting.</p><p></p><p>Apply basic SEO to each article.</p><p></p><p>Week 3: Promote Your Content</p><p></p><p>Share your articles across your active social platforms.</p><p></p><p>Turn each article into smaller posts.</p><p></p><p>Join relevant conversations and communities.</p><p></p><p>Start connecting with other people in your niche.</p><p></p><p>Week 4: Study and Improve</p><p></p><p>Check your analytics.</p><p></p><p>Identify which articles received the most attention.</p><p></p><p>Look at where your visitors came from.</p><p></p><p>Study which headlines and topics performed best.</p><p></p><p>Then use what you have learned to plan your next month''s content.</p><p></p><p></p><h3>Don''t Chase Viral Traffic Alone</h3><p></p><p>One mistake new writers make is believing that going viral is the ultimate goal.</p><p></p><p>Viral traffic can be exciting, but thousands of visitors who never return may not be as valuable as a smaller audience that regularly reads, shares and recommends your work.</p><p></p><p>The real goal should be to build consistent traffic and a loyal audience.</p><p></p><p>One reader who discovers your article today could become a subscriber, client, customer or regular reader tomorrow.</p><p></p><p>That is how an audience grows.</p><p></p><p>The beginning of a content writing career can feel frustrating.</p><p></p><p>You may write articles that receive little traffic, struggle to find clients, question your ability and wonder whether anyone is paying attention.</p><p></p><p>Those challenges are normal.</p><p></p><p>What separates writers who eventually build an audience from those who give up is often their willingness to keep learning and improving.</p><p></p><p>Do not focus only on writing more.</p><p></p><p>Learn how to research better topics, understand your audience, write useful content, optimise it for search, promote it effectively and study your results.</p><p></p><p>Traffic does not usually happen overnight. It is built through valuable content, consistency, distribution and patience.</p><p></p><p>Start with one audience. Solve one problem at a time. Keep improving your writing and your strategy.</p><p></p><p>With time, the few people reading your content today can become the larger audience you are working toward tomorrow.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789536551/echo-gist/c8a6cb2b-83cc-4b91-8bb9-b3e2ec813a12/bd4px7h7ewlinhmbjbya.png', 'career', '{}', 'c8a6cb2b-83cc-4b91-8bb9-b3e2ec813a12', TRUE, FALSE, 10, 3, 1, 0, '2026-09-16T05:31:08.531Z', '2026-09-19T22:58:18.509Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('4d83fd51-4617-4819-b7e4-0f9e81955601', 'The Things Nigerian Parents Never Say but Somehow Expect You to Understand', 'the-things-nigerian-parents-never-say-but-somehow-expect-you-to-understand-h0ry', 'What was your experience like growing up with your parents?', '<p>Growing up Nigerian means learning a lot of things nobody actually teaches you.</p><p>There was no conversation. No explanation.</p><p>You just… knew. For example, visitors.</p><p>If someone comes to the house, my mum didn''t have to say, “Please go inside.” She''d just give us that sign.</p><p>And we understood immediately. Everybody disappears.</p><p>No questions, no complaints. You just enter inside and continue whatever you were doing there.</p><p>Same thing when you''re watching a film and suddenly a “corrupt” scene comes on. Nobody says, “leave the room.”</p><p>You already know. You stand up and go inside, and you stay there until the scene is over. If your mum is in a good mood, she might call you back.</p><p>If not, well… congratulations. You''ve been dismissed for the rest of the movie. 😭</p><p>Then there''s the look. Nigerian parents don''t need to shout every time, and it''s always our mother.</p><p>Sometimes your mum just looks at you, That''s it.</p><p>You immediately remember every questionable thing you''ve done since January. And when she calls your full name?</p><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788888991/echo-gist/a0da860a-2376-461b-9925-b688da024c37/jjwjqrtxtajdbylrcr2t.jpg" alt="930086.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; border-radius: 8px; margin: 24px 0px; display: block;"><p>Just start preparing your defence.</p><p>Another thing we somehow learned was how to behave when adults were around. You could be making noise five seconds ago, but the moment an adult enters the room, suddenly you''re sitting properly like you''ve never made a sound in your life.</p><p>And greeting?... You better greet, because apparently, someone can see you every day and still be offended that you didn''t greet them properly. You can imagine that.</p><p>The funniest part is that none of these rules were ever formally explained. You just picked them up along the way.</p><p>A hand gesture meant come here, certain look meant don''t even try it.</p><p>Your name being called twice meant this is serious, and sometimes, “Go inside” wasn''t even said. You just knew.</p><p>Maybe that''s one of the funniest parts of growing up Nigerian. Our parents didn''t always explain the rules.</p><p>They just expected us to understand them, and somehow…</p><p>we did.</p><p>What was your experience like growing up with your parents? What are those unspoken rules you somehow just knew? Share yours with us in the comments.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788888962/echo-gist/a0da860a-2376-461b-9925-b688da024c37/vy1d46kzivgowrqqks6l.jpg', 'lifestyle', '{"family","nigerianparents","nigerianculture","nigerianchildhood","relatablestories"}', 'a0da860a-2376-461b-9925-b688da024c37', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 71, 1, 1, '2026-09-08T17:37:57.927Z', '2026-09-17T07:43:39.719Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('d89dfeb1-3df6-4bba-a471-16df16f11565', 'HAKDASHA 1', 'hakdasha-1--bk4v', 'In a village not too far away from the city, there lived a dedicated, focused, God fearing and skillful young man whose name is Andrew. Andrew was very dedicate…', '<p>In a village not too far away from the city, there lived a dedicated, focused, God fearing and skillful young man whose name is Andrew. Andrew was very dedicated and set his heart to do anything he wanted to do which he did it with all his might.</p><p></p><p></p><p> You could refer to Andrew as every lady’s spec. This is because not only was he very God fearing, diligent and skillful, he was quite handsome just like Absalom who was described as a very fine young man in the scriptures. </p><p></p><p></p><p>Although, Andrew was the most handsome young man in his village, there was something missing, Andrew wanted more, he wanted to make it quick, explore the city, chill with those he assumed as high and mighty. </p><p></p><p>He truly wanted to show his village people that he was a fine boy in every sense, and not just a local champion wasting his life in a village settlement with no good water, good roads or beautiful infrastructure. He decided to put it to God in prayers concerning his desire to move to the city. </p><p></p><p></p><p>He wept as he prayed, he said, “God I want to go to the city, I need to be part of the “what''s up gang.” Then God replied, “Is that the only reason you want to go to the city? Is it to join the high and mighty? How about staying in this village for a little while, equip yourself the more, soak yourself in me, gain roots in my word deeply and when it is time, you will begin to bear much fruits that people from all over the world will begin to call your name.</p><p></p><p></p><p> You will travel to nations and continents and not just the city, it is then you will know that it is I who has announced you. By then you will become <strong>UNSTOPPABLE</strong> because the oil upon your head will begin to overflow.” </p><p>Did Andrew stay back or he decided to travel? </p><p></p><p>You can give a guess in the comments section and stay tuned for part 2😊🎉🎉🎉</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788701976/echo-gist/7d2ad71d-e26d-4985-9fbe-8b4c1570f087/zuj40bp0tuekwsojh1uy.jpg', 'writing', '{}', '7d2ad71d-e26d-4985-9fbe-8b4c1570f087', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 11, 2, 2, '2026-09-06T13:39:56.229Z', '2026-10-01T20:39:44.400Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('b9c9b405-4a2c-453a-a400-730c96d1289f', 'Consistency is the magic', 'consistency-is-the-magic-zxrb', 'Don’t underestimate what can happen when you keep showing up day after day, even when the results feel invisible and the effort seems thankless. Quiet consisten…', '<p>Don’t underestimate what can happen when you keep showing up day after day, even when the results feel invisible and the effort seems thankless. Quiet consistency compounds in ways you rarely notice until suddenly everything shifts.</p><p>The very thing you’re struggling with today, the frustration, the doubt, the slow progress, might be the exact process that transforms your life months from now. What feels like resistance right now is often the forge that’s shaping the version of you who can handle the breakthrough when it arrives.</p><p>So keep going. Trust the work you’re putting in, even when you can’t yet see the payoff.</p>', NULL, 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 1, 17, 0, 0, '2026-08-12T10:52:32.763Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:02.177Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('ea00093a-8d57-4152-800a-50a48a73082e', 'Ehm.', 'ehm-fkb1', '…', '', NULL, 'technology', '{}', '7d2ad71d-e26d-4985-9fbe-8b4c1570f087', FALSE, FALSE, 1, 35, 0, 0, '2026-08-31T11:26:22.179Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:01.348Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('a52d6560-0066-4747-a6f1-1931b7a3a4a7', 'The Things We Learn Too Late', 'the-things-we-learn-too-late--l6ts', 'Some lessons only make sense after life has already taught them to us.', '<p>There are some lessons we wish we had learned earlier.</p><p>Not because someone didn''t try to tell us.</p><p>Not because we weren''t warned.</p><p>But because some truths are difficult to understand until life places us in a moment where we finally see them for ourselves.</p><p>Someone can tell you to appreciate time, but you may not truly understand it until a moment you wish you could have back.</p><p>Someone can tell you not everyone is meant to stay, but you may not accept it until you watch someone you thought would always be there walk away.</p><p>Someone can tell you to be kinder to yourself, but you may not understand it until you look back and realize how hard you were on a younger version of yourself who was simply trying to survive.</p><p>Life has a way of teaching lessons slowly.</p><p>And sometimes, the lessons that arrive late become the ones we remember forever.</p><h3>We Learn That Time Is More Valuable Than We Realize</h3><p>When we are younger, we often believe there will always be more time.</p><p>More time to call someone.</p><p>More time to visit.</p><p>More time to say the things we should have said.</p><p>We postpone moments because we assume they will still be waiting for us.</p><p>"I''ll do it next week."</p><p>"I''ll visit when I''m less busy."</p><p>"I''ll call when I have more time."</p><p>But life doesn''t always wait for our perfect timing.</p><p>There are people who have looked back and wished they had spent more evenings with loved ones instead of chasing things that seemed urgent at the time.</p><p>They realize that the message they delayed sending, the conversation they avoided, or the moment they ignored was actually more valuable than they understood.</p><p>The truth is, time is one of the few things we spend without knowing how much we have left.</p><p>We often chase achievements, money, and success while forgetting that some of the most meaningful moments are the simple ones.</p><p>A conversation.</p><p>A laugh.</p><p>A meal together.</p><p>A memory created with someone we love.</p><p>Sometimes, the things we think are ordinary today become the things we miss tomorrow.</p><h3>We Learn That Not Everyone Is Meant to Stay</h3><p>One of the hardest lessons life teaches us is that some people are only meant to be part of a chapter, not the entire story.</p><p>And accepting that can hurt.</p><p>There are friendships we thought would last forever.</p><p>Relationships we thought would never change.</p><p>People we imagined growing old with.</p><p>But sometimes, people leave.</p><p>Sometimes, we outgrow each other.</p><p>Sometimes, the person who once felt like home becomes someone we no longer recognize.</p><p>At first, it can feel like a loss.</p><p>You wonder what went wrong.</p><p>You replay conversations.</p><p>You think about what you could have done differently.</p><p>But with time, you begin to understand something:</p><p>Not every goodbye is a punishment.</p><p>Some endings create space for growth.</p><p>Some people enter our lives to teach us something.</p><p>They teach us about love.</p><p>They teach us about boundaries.</p><p>They teach us about ourselves.</p><p>And even though we may wish certain chapters had ended differently, we can still be grateful for what those chapters gave us.</p><p>A person leaving does not mean the relationship had no value.</p><p>Sometimes, something can be meaningful and still not be permanent.</p><h3>We Learn That We Should Have Been Kinder to Ourselves</h3><p>Many of us are much kinder to others than we are to ourselves.</p><p>We forgive other people''s mistakes.</p><p>We encourage others when they feel lost.</p><p>We remind people that they are doing their best.</p><p>But when it comes to ourselves, we often become our biggest critics.</p><p>We remember every mistake.</p><p>Every failure.</p><p>Every moment we wish we had handled differently.</p><p>We tell ourselves we should have known better.</p><p>We should have done more.</p><p>We should have been further ahead.</p><p>But the person you were years ago was making decisions with the knowledge, experience, and strength they had at that time.</p><p>You cannot judge your past self using everything you know today.</p><p>Growth means becoming wiser.</p><p>It means learning.</p><p>It means changing.</p><p>And sometimes, the person you need to forgive the most is yourself.</p><p>You were learning.</p><p>You were trying.</p><p>You were becoming.</p><p>Give yourself the same grace you freely give to others.</p><h3>We Learn That Chasing Approval Can Make Us Lose Ourselves</h3><p>There is a quiet exhaustion that comes from constantly trying to make everyone happy.</p><p>Trying to be accepted.</p><p>Trying to meet expectations.</p><p>Trying to become the version of yourself that makes everyone else comfortable.</p><p>Many people spend years making choices based on what others will think.</p><p>They choose careers because they want approval.</p><p>They hide parts of themselves because they fear judgment.</p><p>They stay in situations that make them unhappy because they are afraid of disappointing people.</p><p>But eventually, many people learn a difficult truth:</p><p>You can spend your entire life trying to please everyone and still not be enough for some people.</p><p>Someone will always have an opinion.</p><p>Someone will always have advice.</p><p>Someone will always see things differently.</p><p>And that is okay.</p><p>Your life was not created for you to constantly perform for an audience.</p><p>There comes a time when you have to ask yourself:</p><p><strong>“Am I living a life that feels true to me, or am I living a life that makes everyone else comfortable?”</strong></p><h3>We Learn That Small Moments Are Actually Big Moments</h3><p>Sometimes, we spend so much time waiting for big moments that we forget to appreciate the small ones happening around us.</p><p>We wait for the perfect day.</p><p>The perfect opportunity.</p><p>The perfect season.</p><p>But life is mostly made up of ordinary moments.</p><p>The morning conversations.</p><p>The random laughter.</p><p>The quiet moments when everything feels peaceful.</p><p>The people who check on us.</p><p>The little victories nobody else notices.</p><p>One day, we may realize those moments were not small at all.</p><p>They were the things that made life meaningful.</p><p>Happiness is not always found in the biggest achievements.</p><p>Sometimes, it is hidden in the simple things we were too busy to notice.</p><h3>We Learn That Healing Takes Time</h3><p>Another lesson life teaches us is that healing cannot be rushed.</p><p>We often want to move on quickly.</p><p>We want to forget the pain.</p><p>We want to wake up one day and feel completely okay.</p><p>But healing is not always a straight line.</p><p>Some days are better than others.</p><p>Some memories return when we least expect them.</p><p>Some wounds take longer than we thought they would.</p><p>And that''s okay.</p><p>Healing is not about pretending something never happened.</p><p>It is about learning how to carry your story without allowing it to control your future.</p><p>You are allowed to take your time.</p><p>You are allowed to rebuild slowly.</p><p>You are allowed to become whole again.</p><h3>The Lessons We Learn Are Often the Ones We Needed Most</h3><p>Looking back, many of us realize that the moments we thought would break us were also the moments that shaped us.</p><p>The disappointment taught us strength.</p><p>The loss taught us appreciation.</p><p>The mistakes taught us wisdom.</p><p>The endings taught us acceptance.</p><p>Life rarely gives us lessons in the way we expect.</p><p>Sometimes it teaches through unexpected changes.</p><p>Sometimes through difficult seasons.</p><p>Sometimes through experiences we would never choose.</p><p>But every lesson adds something to our story.</p><p>Maybe there are things you wish you knew earlier.</p><p>Maybe there are mistakes you wish you could undo.</p><p>Maybe there are moments you wish you had handled differently.</p><p>But remember this:</p><p>You were learning.</p><p>You were growing.</p><p>You were becoming.</p><p>And even the lessons that arrived late still arrived at the right time.</p><p>Because the person you are today exists because of everything you have experienced.</p><p>So be patient with yourself.</p><p>Appreciate the people who matter.</p><p>Protect your peace.</p><p>Say the words you need to say.</p><p>Create the memories you don''t want to lose.</p><p>And never stop learning.</p><p>Because life is a story, and every chapter — even the difficult ones — is teaching you something worth knowing.</p><p><strong>Some lessons come late, but they still have the power to change everything. ✍️</strong></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788022999/echo-gist/26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2/sccdrx2gyzcfvypnja1q.png', 'technology', '{"personalgrowth","selfdiscovery","lifereflection","emotionalwellness"}', '26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2', TRUE, FALSE, 6, 10, 7, 6, '2026-08-29T17:04:37.058Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:10.076Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('a3cdbb10-88f3-4e11-a8fd-996932374835', 'Broken Mirror, Smile Face 4', 'broken-mirror-smile-face-4-8q3h', 'She didn’t sleep well that night, or many nights after. She would lie awake in the wide bed that had once held two people and now held only her and the shape of…', '<p>She didn’t sleep well that night, or many nights after. She would lie awake in the wide bed that had once held two people and now held only her and the shape of her own thoughts, and she would listen to the house breathe around her — the settling of old wood, the far-off hum of the refrigerator, the wind finding every gap in the window frames it had found for eleven years running. And she would think about the woman in the mirror. The seven women, really. The one who had smiled too easily for too long, agreeing before she’d even heard the question. The one who flinched at raised voices in other rooms, other houses, other lives that weren’t even hers. The one — this was the one that frightened her most — who had, for one clean and terrible second, wanted to put her own fist through more than glass.</p><p>She was not a woman who had ever thought of herself as capable of rage. That was the story she’d told herself for thirty-one years: that she was patient, accommodating, easy to love because she made so little demand on the world’s affection. But rage, she was discovering now, doesn’t announce itself politely. It doesn’t ask if this is a convenient time. It simply arrives, sudden as broken glass, and shows you a version of yourself you’d spent a lifetime carefully not becoming.</p><p>The question that kept her awake, night after night, staring at the ceiling fan’s slow rotation, was not why did I break it. She knew why. The question was quieter than that, and far more dangerous:</p><p>Which one of these women is actually me.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1784709256/echo-gist/b3132995-e2de-4cc1-9705-e9671bcf3423/uiaybde3pddcqiy6lgdh.png', 'opinion', '{}', 'b3132995-e2de-4cc1-9705-e9671bcf3423', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 3, 2, 1, '2026-07-22T08:36:28.705Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:11.374Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('637e3b92-dac7-4434-b930-74161694e0ac', 'The Little Seed That Almost Gave Up', 'the-little-seed-that-almost-gave-up-sxm4', 'Sometimes the greatest growth happens in places no one can see', '<p>Every morning before the sun had fully climbed over the rooftops Mia walked to the corner of her garden with a watering can. She knelt beside the patch of soil where she had planted her seed. Gave it enough water to darken the earth.</p><p>Days passed.</p><p>Then weeks.</p><p>Nothing happened.</p><p>The soil looked the same. The garden looked the same. The roses bloomed, the tomatoes climbed their stakes and butterflies visited every flower except the patch in the corner. Mia started to believe her seed would never grow.</p><p>One evening as the sky turned orange and the crickets began to sing she sat down beside the patch of soil. She pulled her knees to her chest. Whispered, "Maybe I was wasting my time."</p><p>Her grandfather, who was trimming the hedges nearby, heard her. He set down his shears, walked over and lowered himself onto the grass beside her with a groan.</p><p>“Just because you cannot see something happening " he said, "doesn’t mean nothing is happening."</p><p>Mia looked at him confused.</p><p>“Grandpa, I’ve watered it every day. There’s nothing."</p><p>He smiled the way he always did when he knew something she didn’t. Not to tease her. Because he remembered being her age and not knowing it too.</p><p>“Some of the important growth happens where nobody can see it, " he said. "Under the ground in the dark a seed is working harder than it will above the soil. It’s growing roots before it ever shows a leaf. Roots take patience."</p><p>Mia didn’t fully understand.. Something in his voice made her want to try one more day.</p><p>The morning she filled her watering can again and returned to the same patch of soil even though her heart wasn’t quite sure it mattered anymore.</p><p>A day later she noticed something</p><p>A tiny green leaf, no bigger than her fingernail had pushed through the soil.</p><p>She dropped to her knees, careful not to touch it.</p><p>“Grandpa! Grandpa, come look!"</p><p>He walked over slowly, peered down at the leaf and nodded like he had expected it all along.</p><p>“There it is, " he said. "It was never doing nothing. It was just doing the kind of work you can’t see."</p><p>From that day Mia watered the seed with a different kind of patience.</p><p>Not the anxious waiting kind. The quiet trusting kind.</p><p>Weeks turned into months. The leaf became a stem. The stem grew taller and stronger and one morning Mia woke up to find a bud had formed at its tip.</p><p>Not after the bud opened into the most beautiful flower in the entire garden. Bright, full and unlike anything growing there.</p><p>Mia sat beside it. Finally understood what her grandfather meant.</p><p>Growth is not always visible at the beginning.</p><p>Sometimes before life shows the results it is building something underneath. Something patient, something real.</p><p>The seed did not grow because Mia watered it once.</p><p>It grew because she kept showing up even when she saw no evidence of change.</p><p>Sometimes our lives are the same.</p><p>The work happening inside us may be invisible today. That does not mean it is not happening.</p><p>The dreams we are building, the lessons we are learning and the person we are becoming are all growing in ways we may not see yet.</p><p></p><p><strong>Lesson:</strong></p><p>Do not give up on yourself just because you cannot see progress yet.</p><p>Some seasons are for growing.</p><p>Keep showing up. Keep learning. Keep trying.</p><p>Your roots are growing, even when nobody can see them. </p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788177885/echo-gist/26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2/spizcm4u6w3ad6i0rhx4.png', 'lifestyle', '{"lifelessons","personalgrowth","inspirationalstories","selfdiscovery","triciaswordsdiary"}', '26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 10, 5, 4, '2026-08-31T12:07:42.682Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:00.966Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('f1cf82c2-b2cf-4c8d-b4fd-5ea5aced1d4b', 'Jaded', 'jaded-lhkv', 'Some things don’t end when you leave them behind…', '<p>Five months later, I was still lying on my bed, staring at the ceiling, unable to sleep or feel much of anything.</p><p>Why did he do that to me?</p><p>I had asked myself the question so often it no longer sounded like one. It was simply another thought living in my head.</p><p>My phone’s harsh cry broke through the silence.</p><p>I had forgotten to put it on silent.</p><p>I reached for it and saw the name on the screen.</p><p><strong>Beatrice.</strong></p><p>Of course.</p><p>She had been calling every day since I returned from Lagos. Sometimes I answered. Most times I didn’t.</p><p>She was worried I might harm myself.</p><p>Once, after I ignored her calls for a few days, she came banging on my door.</p><p>“Rapu! I know you are in there! You will open this door this minute, ozigbo, ozigbo! You don’t have to be doing this to me and yourself!”</p><p>That was Beatrice.</p><p>Loud. Dramatic. Full of life.</p><p>The kind of person who could walk into a room and make friends with everyone before she had even found a seat.</p><p>We were opposites in almost every way.</p><p>Beatrice was boisterous. I was quieter.</p><p>She could argue with a wall and somehow win. I preferred to smile, observe and let people think I wasn’t listening.</p><p>Yet somehow, we worked.</p><p>We were girls’ girls.</p><p>Lunch dates. Staycations. Long phone calls. Talking about jobs. Making fun of men. Swearing off men. Then meeting men and forgetting everything we had said.</p><p>Beatrice used to complain that people noticed me without me doing anything.</p><p>“You just stand there looking pretty and people come to you,” she would say. “Meanwhile, I have to do all the work.”</p><p>I would laugh.</p><p>I couldn’t really argue with her.</p><p>I wasn’t the loudest person at parties, but once I settled in, I could become the life of the party. I loved good music, good food and good company. I liked making people laugh. I liked making people feel comfortable.</p><p>I was also embarrassingly romantic.</p><p>Beatrice hated that about me.</p><p>She thought I was too soft.</p><p>“Rapu, you will enter one man’s car because he called you beautiful three times and then come and tell me he has a good heart.”</p><p>She was always saying things like that.</p><p>I would roll my eyes.</p><p>“You’re just jealous because nobody is calling you beautiful.”</p><p>“Please, I don’t need a man to validate me.”</p><p>“You need one.”</p><p>“I need Jesus.”</p><p>We would laugh until our stomachs hurt.</p><p>I miss those days.</p><p>Today, however, I wasn’t in the mood for Beatrice’s drama.</p><p>I picked up my phone to put it back on silent, but then I noticed the missed calls.</p><p>Beatrice.</p><p>And Marcus.</p><p>A message from Marcus sat beneath the calls.</p><p><em>Dear, I hope all is well? Remember we have a date by 5 PM and I have been trying to reach you.</em></p><p>I sighed.</p><p>Marcus.</p><p>I had completely forgotten about the date.</p><p>He had always liked me.</p><p>Scratch that.</p><p>Loved me.</p><p>And still did.</p><p>Marcus had been there before Frank.</p><p>Before everything.</p><p>Before Lagos.</p><p>Before the hospital.</p><p>Before I learned that sometimes the person you trust to hold you together is the very person who tears you apart.</p><p></p><p>I met Frank during the period I was having issues with my former boyfriend.</p><p>I had recently lost my remote job and was spending most of my days sending applications, attending virtual interviews and refreshing my email like an offer might magically appear if I stared at it long enough.</p><p>It was frustrating.</p><p>I hated being idle.</p><p>I hated having to explain to people that I was job hunting when I didn’t even know what job I was going to get next.</p><p>It was on one of those difficult evenings that I found myself outside Salivate Restaurant, waiting for a cab after a long argument with Ebuka, my boyfriend.</p><p>The weather wasn’t promising at all. The heavens were rumbling, ready to cry their eyes out for my predicament.</p><p>I stood there with my arms tightly clasped around my bosom, trying to shield myself from the cold.</p><p>Frank drove past me.</p><p>Then he reversed.</p><p>He wound down his window.</p><p>“You’re going to stand here until the rain carries you away?”</p><p>I laughed despite my pain.</p><p>“I’ve been waiting for a cab.”</p><p>“You can enter. I’ll drop you somewhere you can get one.”</p><p>I hesitated for barely a second.</p><p>I didn’t need much convincing.</p><p>Frank was charming.</p><p>Tall. Handsome. Chocolate-skinned with nice biceps, exactly how I liked them.</p><p>But it wasn’t his looks that got me.</p><p>It was the conversation.</p><p>He was a good conversationalist, and somehow, talking to him felt easy. Before we reached our destination, I was telling him about Ebuka and the problems in my relationship.</p><p>He listened.</p><p>He didn’t interrupt.</p><p>He didn’t tell me what I should have done.</p><p>He just listened.</p><p>When he dropped me off, he asked if he could call me.</p><p>I said yes.</p><p>That was how it started.</p><p>Weeks later, I was able to get over Ebuka with Frank by my side.</p><p>I started a relationship with him a few weeks after I called things off with Ebuka.</p><p>Frank was the perfect man.</p><p>At least, he knew how to make himself look like one.</p><p>He sent good morning texts.</p><p>He called to check if I had eaten.</p><p>When I was job hunting, he became almost as invested in my applications as I was.</p><p>“Send me your CV,” he’d say.</p><p>Then he’d send me links.</p><p><em>Try this one.</em></p><p><em>This company is hiring.</em></p><p><em>Apply here too.</em></p><p>Sometimes, he would call and ask, “Have you checked your email today?”</p><p>He stocked my fridge when I complained about being too tired to go shopping.</p><p>He stocked my kitchen.</p><p>He remembered things I forgot.</p><p>Once, I casually mentioned that I needed a new phone charger. I completely forgot about it.</p><p>Two days later, he showed up with one.</p><p>“You said you needed it.”</p><p>I remember looking at him and smiling like an idiot.</p><p>It was the kind of thing that made me feel seen.</p><p>And I loved being seen.</p><p>Frank remembered little things.</p><p>My favourite snacks.</p><p>How I liked my eggs.</p><p>The song I played when I was sad.</p><p>The perfume I once pointed out in a shop window and said was too expensive.</p><p>He remembered everything.</p><p>He was also always there when I wanted to talk.</p><p>I could yap for hours, jumping from one topic to another, and Frank would listen.</p><p>Sometimes he’d laugh.</p><p>Sometimes he’d tell me I was overthinking.</p><p>Sometimes he’d just hold me.</p><p>It was everything I thought love was supposed to be.</p><p>Or so I thought.</p><p>There were, however, things Frank said that I didn’t pay enough attention to.</p><p>He didn’t like how close Beatrice and I were.</p><p>Not openly.</p><p>Never openly.</p><p>He was too calculated for that.</p><p>“You know I love that you and Beatrice are close, right?” he would say.</p><p>Then he’d pause.</p><p>“But you don’t have to tell your friends everything we do.”</p><p>I’d laugh.</p><p>“Why?”</p><p>“Nothing. I just think some things should stay between us.”</p><p>Another time, he said, “I see how close you both are, and that’s beautiful. Just remember that not everybody is happy when they see someone else doing well.”</p><p>I frowned.</p><p>“Beatrice is happy for me.”</p><p>“I didn’t say she wasn’t.”</p><p>He kissed my forehead.</p><p>“I’m just saying, be careful. Sometimes people can love you and still be jealous of you.”</p><p>I dismissed it.</p><p>That was Frank.</p><p>Every body in my corner was not happy for me, in his words.</p><p>He simply planted little doubts and watered them occasionally.</p><p>I didn’t notice.</p><p>I was too busy falling in love.</p><p></p><p>Life with Frank went smoothly until one night when I was at his house.</p><p>Frank’s elder brother came in with another relative of theirs.</p><p>They were both cold towards me, and Frank wasn’t home at the time.</p><p>Jide, Frank’s elder brother, paced around the house for a while before walking over to where I was seated, attending to my nails.</p><p>“You need to get out of this house now!”</p><p>I was gobsmacked by his outburst.</p><p>I looked up, chuckled and continued with what I was doing.</p><p>Jide grew red with anger.</p><p>He snatched my manicure set from me and shouted, “I paid for this house, and so I have the right to ask you to leave!”</p><p>His other relative intervened at that point and took him out of the house.</p><p>I was furious.</p><p>I felt humiliated.</p><p>I packed my bag to leave, but I met Frank at the exit.</p><p>After I narrated what had happened, he apologized and confronted his brother about the ownership of the house.</p><p>He called Jide’s bluff and, to make up for what had happened, promised us a trip to Lagos.</p><p>I was excited.</p><p>I needed a new air.</p><p></p><p>The airport was bustling with people.</p><p>We managed to move past the busy crowd and made our way to the exit to get a taxi to convey us to Frank’s house.</p><p>Yes.</p><p>His house.</p><p>Frank had boasted a lot about his magnificent house in Lagos, so I couldn’t wait to get there and relax from work.</p><p>The first taxi we approached was too expensive, so we opted for another that conveyed us to the estate.</p><p>We got to the apartment and were welcomed by his friend.</p><p>After settling in, I looked around the apartment.</p><p>Something felt different.</p><p>It looked like a rented apartment.</p><p>Again, I confronted Frank.</p><p>He got so offended that he shouted at me for not believing him.</p><p>That night, we had a big fight.</p><p>The next morning, I woke up before him.</p><p>Fortunately for me, his phone was unlocked.</p><p>Frank was always selfish with his phone, and now I had an opportunity to go through it.</p><p>I went through his texts, and my suspicions were confirmed.</p><p>The apartment was rented.</p><p>I also found out that Frank was a big casanova and a leech.</p><p>When he woke up, we had another bout of argument.</p><p>After a while, he apologized about the apartment.</p><p>He convinced me that he truly had an apartment but had not taken me there because he was unsure of me and whether I was with him for his money.</p><p>I loved Frank so much that I foolishly believed him.</p><p>But I had my reservations.</p><p>I suggested that we move to a cheaper apartment to save money, since I already knew that the one we were staying in wasn’t his.</p><p>Living with Frank in that apartment opened my eyes to a lot of red flags.</p><p>The man who used to send me good morning texts began yelling at me.</p><p>The man who remembered my favourite snacks began hitting me.</p><p>The man who listened to me yap for hours began kicking and hissing at me.</p><p>He didn’t even care about my well-being when I was sick.</p><p>I received a series of calls from different people and vendors asking me to tell Frank to pay what he owed them.</p><p>Frank had even borrowed money from a microfinance bank and used me as a guarantor without my knowledge.</p><p>Whenever I confronted him, he always had an explanation.</p><p>There was always a reason.</p><p>There was always someone else to blame.</p><p>When I couldn’t take it anymore, I searched for Frank’s ex-girlfriend online and sent her a message asking about him.</p><p>As I feared, she confirmed my suspicions.</p><p>She told me that targeting pretty, young women who were well-to-do and leeching off them was Frank’s modus operandi.</p><p>My heart shattered.</p><p>Everything suddenly made sense.</p><p>The attention.</p><p>The gifts.</p><p>The listening.</p><p>The way he seemed to know exactly what I needed before I even asked.</p><p>I wondered how much of it had been real.</p><p></p><p>Frank wasn’t at the hotel after I reached out to his ex-girlfriend.</p><p>I was feeling feverish, so I went to the hospital.</p><p>The result came out.</p><p>I was a month and two weeks gone.</p><p>My heart skipped as soon as I saw the word <strong>POSITIVE</strong> boldly written in red.</p><p>I was devastated.</p><p>I sat there staring at the result.</p><p>Pregnant.</p><p>There was a strange mixture of fear and disbelief in me.</p><p>I thought about Frank.</p><p>I thought about the baby.</p><p>I thought about everything I had imagined my life would become.</p><p>Frank was still not back when I returned to the hotel.</p><p>He finally came back late that night, reeking of alcohol.</p><p>He pounced on me immediately I opened the door for him.</p><p>With each blow that landed on me, he muttered, “You think you are special, eh? You think?”</p><p>He stopped, took off his shorts, dragged me by my hair to the ground and tore my pants amidst my wailing and pleading.</p><p>A heavy slap landed on my face each time I tried to push him off me.</p><p>“You are going around asking people about me, you stupid girl. I’ll show you what you are looking for today!” he cussed.</p><p>I gathered enough strength to tell him I was pregnant because I thought it would make him stop.</p><p>I was wrong.</p><p>That was the last thing I could remember.</p><p>I woke up the next day not knowing what had happened.</p><p>I believe I had passed out from the beatings after I told him I was pregnant.</p><p></p><p>There was a knock at the door.</p><p>The knock from the receptionist and security man resuscitated me.</p><p>I was a mess.</p><p>The room was a mess too, and it smelled of old blood.</p><p>I managed to open the door.</p><p>They told me that we had been owing for two weeks.</p><p>I was bewildered.</p><p>Frank had been borrowing money from me to pay, claiming that there was an issue with his bank account that he needed to rectify.</p><p>Frank wasn’t in the room.</p><p>I looked for my phone to call him but couldn’t find it.</p><p>That was when I realized it.</p><p>Frank was <strong>GONE.</strong></p><p>None of his belongings was in the room, and I had no way of reaching him.</p><p>I was escorted to the hotel lobby to stay there until I could clear the bill.</p><p>There, I borrowed one of the staff’s phones and logged into my social media accounts.</p><p>I tried contacting Frank.</p><p>He had blocked me.</p><p>I reached out to Beatrice.</p><p>She called immediately.</p><p>I couldn’t say anything.</p><p>I just whimpered and handed the phone to the receptionist to speak with her.</p><p>I felt terrible.</p><p>I hadn’t listened to Beatrice when she warned me about Frank.</p><p>And now she was the one picking up the pieces.</p><p>She cleared my debt and booked the next flight for me to return home.</p><p></p><p>It has been five months since that ugly experience, but I still haven’t gotten over it.</p><p>It is too much for me.</p><p>Mourning the murder of my unborn child by his own father is another weight I don’t know how to carry.</p><p>Beatrice wasn’t gentle with me when I got back.</p><p>Not at first.</p><p>She was angry.</p><p>Furious, actually.</p><p>“I warned you, Rapu.”</p><p>I remember sitting there quietly while she cried.</p><p>“I told you.”</p><p>She wasn’t trying to make me feel worse.</p><p>She was hurt.</p><p>She had watched me walk straight into something she had tried to protect me from.</p><p>And now she had to watch me suffer through it.</p><p>I knew Beatrice meant well.</p><p>I knew she wanted me to start living again instead of wallowing in misery.</p><p>But I just couldn’t.</p><p>I felt so dead inside.</p><p>I wanted to stay on my bed and cry.</p><p>Some days, I couldn’t even bring myself to shower.</p><p>Some days, I stared at my phone for hours without replying to anyone.</p><p>Marcus kept checking on me.</p><p>That was another thing I couldn’t bear.</p><p>He had always been there.</p><p>Before Frank.</p><p>During Frank, even when I didn’t realize how much I needed him.</p><p>And now, after everything, he was still there.</p><p>That was exactly why I didn’t want to see him.</p><p>I felt guilty.</p><p>Out of all the people who had loved me, I had chosen someone else.</p><p>Marcus had been there, and I had chosen Frank.</p><p>I didn’t want Marcus to look at me with pity.</p><p>I didn’t want him to see what was left of me.</p><p>I didn’t want him to look at me and think, <em>I told you so.</em></p><p>Even if he never would.</p><p>I simply couldn’t bear it.</p><p>I didn’t want to have anything to do with men again.</p><p>Not now.</p><p>Maybe not ever.</p><p>My phone rang again.</p><p>Beatrice.</p><p>I stared at her name for a few seconds before answering.</p><p>“Hello?”</p><p>“Rapu?”</p><p>“Hmm.”</p><p>“Have you eaten?”</p><p>I smiled faintly.</p><p>Same old Beatrice.</p><p>Always asking if I had eaten.</p><p>“Yes.”</p><p>“Liar.”</p><p>I laughed.</p><p>It was small.</p><p>Barely there.</p><p>But it was a laugh.</p><p>There was silence on the other end.</p><p>Then she said, “Come out with me.”</p><p>“I don’t want to.”</p><p>“I didn’t ask if you wanted to.”</p><p>I smiled again.</p><p>“Where?”</p><p>“My favourite lounge.”</p><p>I looked around my room.</p><p>It was a mess.</p><p>My clothes were scattered everywhere. Empty bottles sat on the table. The curtains had barely been opened in days.</p><p>I sighed.</p><p>“I need to clean up first.”</p><p>“Good.”</p><p>“And then?”</p><p>“And then you will meet me.”</p><p>I shook my head.</p><p>“You’re so annoying.”</p><p>“I know.”</p><p>The call ended.</p><p>I stared at the phone in my hand.</p><p>For five months, I had been waiting to feel like myself again.</p><p>Maybe that was the problem.</p><p>Maybe I was waiting for the old Rapu to come back.</p><p>But perhaps she wasn’t coming back.</p><p>Perhaps I had to become someone else.</p><p>Someone who knew that surviving something didn’t mean pretending it hadn’t happened.</p><p>I got out of bed.</p><p>Slowly.</p><p>I picked up the first piece of clothing from the floor.</p><p>Then another.</p><p>It wasn’t much.</p><p>But it was movement.</p><p>And for now, that was enough.</p><p>Tonight, I would meet Beatrice at her favourite lounge.</p><p>Not because I was healed.</p><p>Not because I was ready to fall in love again.</p><p>Not because everything was suddenly okay.</p><p>Just because, for the first time in five months, I wanted to leave my room.</p><p>Maybe surviving is not always about becoming the person you were before.</p><p>Maybe sometimes, it is simply deciding to get out of bed.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788182855/echo-gist/a9152831-b554-4cef-ac89-d66fb7927777/yv77ehhmz6qvwidc5j66.jpg', 'books', '{}', 'a9152831-b554-4cef-ac89-d66fb7927777', TRUE, FALSE, 12, 22, 4, 1, '2026-08-31T13:28:23.503Z', '2026-09-22T09:39:28.540Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('9236e63b-1ded-4341-9ef5-6e91d52b6371', 'Sometimes you outgrow people without warning
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('c0bf7c96-608b-4a82-95c9-2e240b5a6ca0', 'He Ignored the First Warning. Then It Happened Again.', 'he-ignored-the-first-warning-then-it-happened-again-ze22', 'The first notification didn''t scare him.He was on his laptop, going about his day, when a Google security prompt appeared.Was this you trying to sign in?He igno…', '<p>The first notification didn''t scare him.</p><p>He was on his laptop, going about his day, when a Google security prompt appeared.</p><p>Was this you trying to sign in?</p><p>He ignored it.</p><p>Maybe it was nothing.</p><p>Maybe it was just one of those random security notifications that show up every now and then.</p><p>Then it happened again.</p><p>That''s when he stopped.</p><p>Something wasn''t right.</p><p>Someone or something was trying to access his Gmail account.</p><p>And this wasn''t an account he could afford to lose.</p><p>It was his main work account. The one connected to important information, documents, accounts, and other things he had built around his work.</p><p>So he didn''t wait to find out what would happen next.</p><p>He started securing everything.</p><p>He changed his password.</p><p>He checked his account.</p><p>He strengthened his two-factor authentication.</p><p>He went through his security settings and made sure he could lock down the account before the situation became worse.</p><p>And thankfully, he was online when it happened.</p><p>That part stayed with me.</p><p>Because what if he wasn''t?</p><p><strong><em>The notification we almost always ignore</em></strong></p><p>Think about how many security notifications we receive.</p><p>"New sign-in detected."</p><p>"Is this you?"</p><p>"Someone is trying to sign in to your account."</p><p>"We''ve noticed unusual activity."</p><p>After seeing enough of them, they can start to feel like background noise.</p><p>You look at your phone.</p><p>You see the notification.</p><p>You think:</p><p>"I''ll check it later."</p><p>And swipe it away.</p><p>But sometimes, that notification isn''t just an inconvenience.</p><p>It''s the warning.</p><p>Someone could be trying to get into your account right now.</p><p>And the difference between an attempted takeover and a successful one might simply be whether you noticed the warning in time.</p><p><strong><em>Your email account is more important than you think</em></strong></p><p>There''s something else this experience made me think about.</p><p>We often treat our email like it''s just another app.</p><p>It isn''t.</p><p>Your main email address can be connected to your social media accounts, work platforms, cloud storage, financial services, subscriptions, documents, and countless other accounts.</p><p>And when you forget a password?</p><p>Where does the reset link usually go?</p><p>Your email.</p><p>That means your primary email can become something of a master key to your digital life.</p><p>If someone gets control of it, the problem may not stop with your inbox.</p><p>They could potentially use access to your email to attempt to take over other accounts connected to it.</p><p>That''s why protecting your main email isn''t something you should put off until you have time.</p><p><strong><em>And here''s the uncomfortable part</em></strong></p><p>My friend ignored the first notification.</p><p>I don''t blame him.</p><p>I probably would have wondered if it was just another random security alert too.</p><p>That''s what makes this story so relatable.</p><p>Cybersecurity isn''t always about someone sitting in a dark room writing complicated code.</p><p>Sometimes, it''s much less dramatic.</p><p>Sometimes it''s someone trying to sign into your account while you''re busy doing something completely unrelated.</p><p>And sometimes, the only thing standing between them and your account is a notification you haven''t decided whether to take seriously.</p><p><strong><em>So what should you do?</em></strong></p><p>You don''t need to become a cybersecurity expert overnight.</p><p>But there are a few things you shouldn''t keep postponing.</p><p>Turn on two-factor authentication.</p><p>If someone somehow gets your password, 2FA gives your account another layer of protection.</p><p>Don''t approve a login request you didn''t initiate.</p><p>If your phone asks whether you''re trying to sign in and you''re not, don''t approve it just to make the notification disappear.</p><p>Use a unique password for your primary email.</p><p>If you reuse the same password everywhere, one compromised account can create problems elsewhere.</p><p>Pay attention to security alerts.</p><p>Especially unexpected ones.</p><p>Check recent sign-ins and devices connected to your account if something doesn''t look right.</p><p>And perhaps most importantly:</p><p>Don''t wait until something happens before taking your account security seriously.</p><p>My friend''s account wasn''t taken over.</p><p>He noticed the warning.</p><p>He acted quickly.</p><p>And the story ended there.</p><p>But it made me think about how many people might see that first notification, swipe it away, and continue with their day.</p><p>Maybe nothing happens.</p><p>Or maybe the next notification never comes.</p><p>Because the person trying to get in already succeeded.</p><p><em>Sometimes cybersecurity starts with something as simple as noticing the warning and not ignoring it.</em></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1786936020/echo-gist/6110aa8e-6f0d-4df4-ad0b-2ac775af342f/pcvehppnqyvxhzo3w98l.png', 'cybersecurity', '{"technology","cybersecurity"}', '6110aa8e-6f0d-4df4-ad0b-2ac775af342f', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 14, 2, 1, '2026-08-17T03:24:57.853Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:56.735Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('72935056-e67f-4021-80ef-55ae9b2e9b0d', 'Step by step', 'step-by-step-klp5', 'Step by step until I reach my highest peak ✨', '', 'https://picsum.photos/seed/step-by-step-klp5/800/500', 'technology', '{}', '356fed5b-9025-44e0-a253-1d1075958f4c', FALSE, FALSE, 1, 16, 4, 1, '2026-07-13T22:09:01.349Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:24.665Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('c9107bd3-6a89-44ea-b52f-8b0c87ac2901', 'Am I Weird? How I Screwed My First Date (4)', 'am-i-weird-how-i-screwed-my-first-date-4-2my4', 'Turns out, the date wasn''t the embarrassing part.', '<hr><hr><p> I spent the rest of that evening thinking about everything I''d said.</p><p>Every laugh.</p><p>Every awkward silence.</p><p>Every answer I probably should have worded differently.</p><p>And especially that one question.</p><p><em>Do you like me?</em></p><p>Why did he even ask me that?</p><p>Why did I answer the way I did?</p><p>Why couldn''t I just behave like a normal human being?</p><p>I checked my phone.</p><p>Nothing.</p><p>I put it down.</p><p>Picked it up again.</p><p>Still nothing.</p><p>Put it down.</p><p>Picked it up.</p><p>Nothing.</p><p>At this point, I was no longer waiting for a text. I was conducting an investigation.</p><p>Then, finally, my phone buzzed.</p><p>His name.</p><p>My heart jumped.</p><p>I opened the message.</p><p><strong>“I had a really nice time today.”</strong></p><p>I stared at it.</p><p>Then smiled.</p><p>Then immediately started overthinking again.</p><p><em>Was he just being nice?</em></p><p>Before I could convince myself that the entire date had been a social experiment, another message came in.</p><p><strong>“Can I see you again?” </strong></p><p>And just like that, all the embarrassment I''d been carrying around disappeared.</p><p>I smiled at my phone.</p><p>Maybe I hadn''t screwed up the date after all.</p><p>Maybe I''d been too busy worrying about how I looked, what I said and whether he liked me to actually enjoy the fact that someone wanted to spend time with me.</p><p>So I replied.</p><p><strong>“Yes.”</strong></p><p>Simple.</p><p>No overthinking.</p><p>No rehearsing.</p><p>Just yes.</p><p>Except...</p><p>That wasn''t the end of the story.</p><p>Because the second date was where I discovered <strong>that screwing up a first date and screwing up a relationship are two completely different things.</strong></p><p>And if you think I was awkward on the first date...</p><p>You haven''t heard anything yet.</p><p>The End.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788103028/echo-gist/ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731/lvlwzvi5ijhcowltewrx.png', 'lifestyle', '{}', 'ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 3, 1, 0, '2026-08-30T15:34:08.864Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:05.439Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('34498aeb-8b08-4365-95bc-68c96a9ffef9', 'Peter Obi Endorses Fellow Presidential Candidate Seyi Makinde.', 'peter-obi-endorses-fellow-presidential-candidate-seyi-makinde-a6c1', 'Peter Obi, the former Anambra governor and 2023 Labour Party presidential candidate now running for president in 2027 under the Nigeria Democratic Congress with…', '<p>Peter Obi, the former Anambra governor and 2023 Labour Party presidential candidate now running for president in 2027 under the Nigeria Democratic Congress with running mate Rabiu Kwankwaso, made a notable show of cross party solidarity on September 7. He appeared in Abuja at the launch of Seyi Makinde''s campaign office for the Allied Peoples Movement and told the crowd that Makinde was qualified to become president, calling him his "dear brother" and urging Nigerians to make their own free choice at the polls. The gesture wasn''t a withdrawal from his own race both men remain active presidential candidates on separate party platforms, with Obi framing the appearance as a call for opposition figures to set aside rivalry and unite around free, fair elections rather than personal ambition.</p><p>The same day, Obi pushed a doubt  from within his own former camp, rejecting a claim from ex running mate Datti Baba-Ahmed that no opposition leader is capable of fixing Nigeria and pointing instead to his record governing Anambra State. The pushback came against the backdrop of a busy campaign swing through Port Harcourt, Kogi, Benue, and Enugu, and just days after he struck a defiant tone on social media, declaring on September 4 that he remains willing to risk everything, even his life, for the country''s future. Together, the Makinde endorsement, the rebuttal to Baba-Ahmed, and the multi-state tour paint a picture of an increasingly assertive Obi trying to position himself as a unifying figure in a crowded and fractious opposition field ahead of 2027.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788802023/echo-gist/40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1/aeqk0w5wr7s0z0kljbox.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 18, 0, 0, '2026-09-07T17:27:36.974Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:29.952Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('081e369a-a9bd-42b0-b26c-907556551bbd', 'A US Newsroom Wants African Voices in the AI Policy Debate — And Will Pay $10,000 for a Year of Work', 'a-us-newsroom-wants-african-voices-in-the-ai-policy-debate-and-will-pay-10000-fo-mxq5', 'A year-long, $10,000 remote fellowship for journalists, researchers and policy professionals working on tech and democracy — with African and Global Majority applicants specifically encouraged. Applications close 24 September 2026.', '<p>Most conversations about how artificial intelligence and technology should be regulated happen without much input from Africa. Tech Policy Press is trying to change that with its 2027 Fellowship, a paid, year-long programme for journalists, researchers and policy professionals — remote, and open to applicants worldwide. Applications close 24 September 2026.</p><h2>What Is the Fellowship, Exactly?</h2><h3>What does Tech Policy Press actually do?</h3><p>Tech Policy Press has run this fellowship since 2023, and says it has supported 25 fellows from 11 countries so far, producing work on AI governance, digital rights, and the relationship between governments, corporations and technology. The 2027 cohort is the next round of that programme.</p><h3>What will a fellow actually spend the year doing?</h3><p>Each selected fellow pursues an independent reporting or research project sitting at the intersection of technology and democracy — not a fixed curriculum, but a self-directed body of work built around one issue the fellow chooses. Tech Policy Press has specifically flagged interest in work covering regions and communities usually left out of global tech-policy debates, which puts African and Nigerian-focused proposals squarely in scope.</p><h3>What could that work actually look like for a Nigerian applicant?</h3><p>The organisation doesn''t hand fellows a prescribed topic list, but plausible directions include how AI is being adopted inside government services, data protection and digital identity policy, surveillance technology, platform regulation, technology''s role in elections, or the effect of automation on African workers. The through-line has to be technology''s relationship to democracy and society — the specific angle is up to the applicant to define and defend.</p><h2>Who Should Actually Apply?</h2><h3>Is this only for tech people?</h3><p>No. Tech Policy Press is explicit that this isn''t aimed at software developers. It''s built for mid-career journalists, researchers, and public policy professionals — including lawyers working in tech policy, digital-rights researchers, and writers who cover technology''s social impact. What matters more than job title is a demonstrated track record on a specific policy issue or geography.</p><h3>Do you need to move to the United States?</h3><p>No. The fellowship is remote and part-time, meaning a Nigerian journalist or researcher can take part without relocating or stepping away from existing work. Tech Policy Press specifically encourages applicants from the Global Majority and from communities underrepresented in media.</p><h3>Is this realistic for someone new to the field?</h3><p>Not really — and it''s worth being honest about that upfront. Applicants need existing, publicly accessible published work to point to. This fellowship is built for people who can already show they know how to report or analyse a technology issue, not a starting point for someone new to the space.</p><h2>What Does the Money Actually Cover?</h2><h3>What is the $10,000 for?</h3><p>It''s a stipend supporting the fellow''s work over the year, not a training fee or tuition payment. Fellows work as independent contractors, using the funding to sustain a year of reporting and analysis on their chosen issue.</p><h3>What else comes with it, beyond the stipend?</h3><p>Fellows get editorial guidance directly from Tech Policy Press, a cohort of international peers working on related issues, monthly group sessions, and monthly one-on-one editorial check-ins — structured enough to push the work forward, without being a rigid course.</p><h2>What Does the Fellowship Actually Expect in Return?</h2><h3>Is this a light commitment?</h3><p>No. Fellows are expected to produce at least monthly analysis or reporting tied to their focus area, attend a mandatory monthly cohort meeting, and complete a monthly check-in with the editorial team, for the full twelve months. Anyone hoping for an occasional workshop and a certificate at the end should look elsewhere — this fellowship expects real, ongoing output.</p><h2>How Do You Actually Apply?</h2><h3>What does the application require?</h3><p>Four things: a CV or résumé, a cover letter no longer than two pages, links to previously published work, and two professional references. The cover letter carries real weight — it needs to explain what the applicant intends to investigate, why the issue matters, and why they''re positioned to do that work, not just restate their résumé.</p><h3>Is there a way to learn more before applying?</h3><p>Tech Policy Press is running two online information sessions for prospective applicants: 9 September 2026 at 8:00 a.m. EDT, and 16 September 2026 at 7:00 p.m. EDT. Registration is required for both.</p><h3>What happens after you submit?</h3><p>Applications close 24 September 2026. Tech Policy Press says selected applicants will be notified in early December 2026, with some candidates invited to an online interview as part of the review. The selected cohort begins its twelve-month term in January 2027.</p><h2>Why This One Is Worth the Effort</h2><p>Submitting an application here is not a guarantee of selection — the process is competitive, and the strength of the proposed work matters as much as past experience. But for a Nigerian journalist or researcher already working around technology policy, this isn''t just a stipend. It''s a rare, fully backed year to pursue one issue in depth, with editorial support and an international network behind it — and a direct route to putting Nigerian and African tech-policy questions in front of a much wider global audience.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788867942/echo-gist/c537c717-a71c-461f-aabf-05570c40e16b/pkjw4q28sen2cgdzpcwq.jpg', 'technology', '{"tech","policy","stipend","remote","fellowship"}', 'c537c717-a71c-461f-aabf-05570c40e16b', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 35, 3, 2, '2026-09-08T11:51:26.484Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:22.412Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('30bd326a-a088-4a5b-82bf-cb840d42519b', 'Love as a business?', 'love-as-a-business--it46', 'When your heart is the currency, losses feel different.', '<p>Love</p><p>For most of my life, I approached love like an entrepreneur.</p><p>Personal relationships and friendships felt like investments.</p><p>Time, energy, care. I put them in and expected returns.</p><p>And for a while, business was good.</p><p>I was getting results.</p><p>People showed up.</p><p>The returns made it feel worth it.</p><p>And there is nothing wrong with wanting to be treated the way you treat people.</p><p>That is human.</p><p>But at some point, it started to feel like I was expecting a turtle to jump for me just because I jumped for it.</p><p>Then I came across something that changed everything.</p><p>People do not love you based on how lovable you are.</p><p>They love you based on their capacity to love.</p><p>The limit of what someone can give you is not what you deserve.</p><p>It is what they have.</p><p>And if you are not careful, you start defining your worth</p><p>through the way other people show up for you.</p><p>That realization destroyed my entire business model.</p><p>Because recently, some of my biggest investments failed.</p><p>I am talking about years of effort.</p><p>Time, care, presence.</p><p>And it all came crashing down.</p><p>I tried to fix it.</p><p>Tried to piece things back together.</p><p>For a moment, it looked like things were stabilizing.</p><p>But the cracks never really held.</p><p>And the pieces kept falling apart.</p><p>I am not saying everything has been bad.</p><p>There were seasons where the returns were incredible.</p><p>But when your heart is the currency,</p><p>losses feel different.</p><p>They do not just affect your portfolio.</p><p>They shake your existence.</p><p>Would I do it again?</p><p>Maybe.</p><p>Would I advise someone else to?</p><p>Probably not.</p><p>It is not that I do not care anymore.</p><p>If I am being honest, I would still subconsciously take a bullet for the people I love.</p><p>But it hurts when what you give and what you receive feel worlds apart.</p><p>And I am learning to accept that.</p><p>Because loving is not the same as expecting.</p><p>And that has been my mistake.</p><p>I thought I was loving,</p><p>but I was measuring.</p><p>Now, I am trying to love because I can.</p><p>Even though somewhere deep down,</p><p>there is still a part of me that hopes it will be returned.</p><p>I just try not to let that part lead anymore.</p><p>And if you are judging my business skills at this point,</p><p>let me be clear.</p><p>There were profits.</p><p>Massive ones.</p><p>Other areas of my life were thriving.</p><p>But in all of that,</p><p>I gave myself the least investment.</p><p>I neglected myself while pouring into everything else.</p><p>So now, I am shifting.</p><p>I will become my own best investment.</p><p>And from what I build within myself,</p><p>I will give to others.</p><p>Not the other way around.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1786827786/echo-gist/82b4b527-06d6-42d5-8cba-cb7e09cda146/u4sfrpsdnpyp0zo5ntrd.jpg', 'philosophy', '{"loveandrelationship","emotionalinvestment","selfworth","personalgrowth","boundaries"}', '82b4b527-06d6-42d5-8cba-cb7e09cda146', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 45, 6, 5, '2026-08-15T21:06:39.523Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:57.945Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('56514bd9-7cfb-401c-910b-b134ebc6cadc', 'Broken Mirror, Smile Face', 'broken-mirror-smile-face-bun7', 'Part Two: The Widower’s Daughter', '<p>It was the housekeeper, Mama Nkechi, who first mentioned the widower’s daughter — offhand, the way people mention things they assume you already know, while she wrung a cloth over the kitchen sink and Adaeze sat at the table pretending to read a newspaper she had no intention of finishing.</p><p>“That mirror,” Mama Nkechi said, not looking up, “it was hers. Papa Obinna’s daughter. The one who died.”</p><p>Adaeze set the newspaper down slowly. “Died? Chidi never told me anyone died in this house.”</p><p>“Ah, Madam Adaeze.” Mama Nkechi’s hands stilled over the basin. “Chidi does not know. He bought the house from Papa Obinna in a hurry — the old man wanted to be rid of it fast, before he changed his mind, before the memory got its claws in deeper. He didn’t tell young Oga the whole story, and young Oga didn’t ask. Men like that never ask. They only see good bones and a fair price.”</p><p>The kitchen had gone very quiet. Outside, the harmattan wind pushed dust against the window glass in soft, insistent waves, and somewhere upstairs a loose shutter knocked twice against its frame, like knuckles on a door.</p><p>“Tell me,” Adaeze said.</p><p>Mama Nkechi dried her hands on her wrapper and sat down across from her, the chair creaking beneath the shift of her weight, and for a long moment she said nothing at all, as though deciding how much of the past a woman standing at the edge of her own unraveling could safely be given.</p><p>“Her name was Chiamaka,” she said finally. “Papa Obinna’s only daughter. Beautiful girl — too beautiful, people said, the kind of beautiful that makes other women’s husbands forget themselves. She was engaged to be married. A good man, everyone said. Rich man. And then, one week before the wedding, she was found in the room upstairs — your room now — in front of that same mirror. They said her heart simply stopped. Young and strong and her heart simply stopped, as though something had frightened the life clean out of her.”</p><p>Adaeze’s fingers had gone cold around the edge of the newspaper. “What frightened her?”</p><p>“Nobody knows, Madam. Papa Obinna never spoke of it again after the burial. He kept her room exactly as she left it for years — the mirror, the bed, her books — until finally the silence in that room became too heavy even for a grieving father to carry, and he sold the house rather than walk past that door one more day.” Mama Nkechi’s eyes, when she finally lifted them to meet Adaeze’s, were older than her face. “Some houses hold on to things, Madam. Some rooms remember the last face they saw.”</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1784809219/echo-gist/b3132995-e2de-4cc1-9705-e9671bcf3423/jyuqohtuvy4e6pleqbhd.png', 'opinion', '{}', 'b3132995-e2de-4cc1-9705-e9671bcf3423', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 12, 2, 1, '2026-07-23T12:21:18.823Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:10.886Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('119e2470-6344-4fed-9d7e-82af95ab3b07', 'Tiny little miracles ✨', 'tiny-little-miracles--17fs', 'Think about the good things of life too. Think about the best scenario of how beautiful it can be. Life can get better. Stay grateful for the tiny little miracles, like fresh air, family, friends, a walk, a quiet moment, food, laughter. The tiny little miracles are mostly what keeps us going, not necessarily hope, but a light of still being here. One day it will all make sense and the day is soon 🤍', '', NULL, 'technology', '{}', '40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1', FALSE, FALSE, 1, 9, 3, 1, '2026-07-17T07:12:00.896Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:12.452Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('410f9318-34f3-451a-979e-78d0d6de7e89', 'WELCOME TO OCTOBER!', 'welcome-to-october-smok', 'It''s not too late for you to continue what you left half way. Get up and get to work🤭🤭😀 I''m rooting for you! 
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('e4b48f35-ec22-4dc0-8600-3ed651001316', 'A place for all.', 'a-place-for-all--i87s', 'They say writing is the description of your soul.But these days, it’s been so much more for me.It has become a medium. A way to process things. A way to underst…', '<p>They say writing is the description of your soul.</p><p>But these days, it’s been so much more for me.</p><p>It has become a medium. A way to process things. A way to understand how life, in all its complexity, has shaped me. Sometimes, it’s not even about me. It’s about the stories of people around me. The way I see their struggles, the way I try to make sense of their experiences. And if I could play God for a moment, I’d probably rewrite parts of their lives just to make things better.</p><p>Recently, I started writing on Echo Gist, and it has been more than I expected.</p><p>At first, it was the community. We all crave belonging, that feeling of finding “our people.” And Echo Gist gives you that. But what surprised me the most was something deeper.</p><p>It was seeing people relate.</p><p>Seeing someone read something I wrote and feel understood. Having people reach out to say, “This made me feel less alone.” That feeling is beyond fulfilling. The idea that something as simple as words can shift someone’s perspective, or even give them hope, is something I don’t take lightly.</p><p>Now, when ideas come to me and I don’t write them down, or when I leave drafts sitting in my notes, I feel a certain kind of guilt. Because maybe, just maybe, that piece could have been someone’s ladder out of a dark place. Or the reassurance they needed to know they’re not alone.</p><p>And the beautiful thing is, it doesn’t have to be emotional or motivational writing.</p><p>At Echo Gist, there’s space for every kind of writer.</p><p>Whether you’re into business, tech, comics, or you enjoy reviewing books and movies, there’s a place for you to grow. It’s not just about writing. It’s about finding your voice and building something meaningful with it.</p><p>Echo Gist is more than a blog.</p><p>It’s a community that supports growth. A space where you don’t have to be perfect to start. You just have to begin.</p><p>We believe in you exactly as you are right now.</p><p>And we want to see you grow into everything you can be.</p><p>So if you’ve been holding onto an idea, a story, or a thought…</p><p>Share it.</p><p>Join us at <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://echo-gist.com">echo-gist.com</a></p><p>We’ve believed in you, even before you clicked subscribe.</p>', NULL, 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 9, 1, 2, '2026-07-24T08:32:48.183Z', '2026-09-15T15:57:10.532Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('c09a4bec-fe01-4b23-8d16-2741ea5da496', 'In Arcadia', 'in-arcadia-w16u', 'Reading In Arcadia by Ben Okri made me question my own existence. I found myself asking, What is my purpose? What am I truly afraid of? What is my own Arcadia? …', '<p>Reading <em>In Arcadia</em> by Ben Okri made me question my own existence. I found myself asking, <em>What is my purpose? What am I truly afraid of? What is my own Arcadia?</em> At first, I didn’t think the novel was for me, but as I read on, its psychological and philosophical reflections completely changed my perspective.</p><p>What struck me most was the journey of the characters. Each of them is searching for something different, and through their conversations and experiences, I found myself reflecting on happiness, fulfilment, and death. Why are we so afraid of death? If we all leave this world one day, what does it really mean to continue existing? The novel also changed the way I see art. A painting is never just a painting; it carries a message that can outlive its creator. Nicolas Poussin’s <em>Et in Arcadia Ego</em> reminded me that even in paradise, death is always present, yet so is the legacy we leave behind.</p><p>My biggest takeaway was that our struggles do not always define us. Sometimes the life we imagine for ourselves is different from the purpose we eventually discover. I finished the book asking myself, <em>What if my disappointments are not failures, but directions toward my own Arcadia?</em> If you enjoy books that challenge the way you think about life, purpose, and existence, <em>In Arcadia</em> by Ben Okri is well worth reading.</p>', NULL, 'books', '{}', 'c86a857f-2f41-4b97-a75c-dd640ca58744', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 2, 0, 0, '2026-09-16T08:01:33.036Z', '2026-09-30T10:02:20.747Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('bf393b42-d8a3-49cf-b9d6-a83fcbf10f62', 'The Girl He Chose', 'the-girl-he-chose-s9sg', 'By the time Ethan made me his favourite person in school, I had already become the favourite target of every girl who wanted him. And there were plenty of them. Ethan was handsome, effortlessly charmi…', '<p>By the time Ethan made me his favourite person in school, I had already become the favourite target of every girl who wanted him.</p><p></p><p>And there were plenty of them.</p><p></p><p>Ethan was handsome, effortlessly charming, and, according to the endless whispers that followed him through the corridors, one of the richest boys in school. Girls found excuses to sit beside him in class. They waited outside his lectures. Some even fought over the smallest attention from him.</p><p></p><p>Yet somehow, he kept coming back to me.</p><p></p><p>My mother had warned me from the beginning.</p><p></p><p>“Never take advantage of that boy,” she had said. “And whatever you do, don''t let your guard down.”</p><p></p><p>I tried.</p><p></p><p>I really did.</p><p></p><p>Especially after Kate cornered me one afternoon.</p><p></p><p>She folded her arms and looked me up and down, her expensive perfume lingering in the air between us.</p><p></p><p>“Stay away from my man,” she said. “You should know your place.”</p><p></p><p>I said nothing.</p><p></p><p>What could I possibly say?</p><p></p><p>Kate came from one of the wealthiest families in school. She was beautiful, confident, and accustomed to getting whatever she wanted.</p><p></p><p>I was no match for her.</p><p></p><p>But Ethan didn''t seem to care.</p><p></p><p>The more I avoided him, the closer he came.</p><p></p><p>If I changed seats, he found me during break. If I left the lecture hall early, he was waiting outside. If I ignored his messages, he would simply appear beside me the next morning with that irritating smile on his face.</p><p></p><p>And in front of everyone, he made one thing painfully clear.</p><p></p><p>I was the girl he had chosen.</p><p></p><p>That afternoon, the library was unusually quiet.</p><p></p><p>I sat opposite Ethan, pretending to read while he worked on his laptop. The soft turning of pages and occasional tapping of his keyboard were the only sounds around us.</p><p></p><p>Then I felt it.</p><p></p><p>Someone was watching me.</p><p></p><p>I slowly lifted my eyes.</p><p></p><p>Kate stood several shelves away.</p><p></p><p>She wasn''t smiling.</p><p></p><p>Her eyes were fixed on me with such coldness that, for a moment, I imagined they could cut straight through my chest.</p><p></p><p>I quickly looked away.</p><p></p><p>“Ethan.”</p><p></p><p>He looked up.</p><p></p><p>“Hmm?”</p><p></p><p>I hesitated before closing my book.</p><p></p><p>“Why do you keep coming back to me?”</p><p></p><p>His brows furrowed.</p><p></p><p>“After everything?” I continued. “I''ve turned you down so many times. I''ve tried to stay away from you. So why won''t you leave me alone?”</p><p></p><p>For a few seconds, he simply stared at me.</p><p></p><p>Then he laughed softly.</p><p></p><p>I frowned.</p><p></p><p>“What?”</p><p></p><p>He leaned back in his chair.</p><p></p><p>“Because you''re different.”</p><p></p><p>I rolled my eyes. “That''s not an answer.”</p><p></p><p>“It is.”</p><p></p><p>He closed his laptop and lowered his voice.</p><p></p><p>“I don''t care about a girl''s surname, her father''s money, or how many people know her name.”</p><p></p><p>My fingers tightened around the edge of my book.</p><p></p><p>“I like a girl with a brain,” he continued. “Someone who can stand on her own feet. Someone humble enough to understand that money doesn''t make a person better than everyone else.”</p><p></p><p>My cheeks grew warm.</p><p></p><p>His eyes never left mine.</p><p></p><p>“I don''t want someone who needs people to solve every problem for her.”</p><p></p><p>He paused.</p><p></p><p>“I want you.”</p><p></p><p>My heart stumbled.</p><p></p><p>“Ethan…”</p><p></p><p>“I don''t want you to be my girlfriend just because that''s what people expect.”</p><p></p><p>He reached across the table, his fingers stopping just short of mine.</p><p></p><p>“I want you to be my woman.”</p><p></p><p>I forgot how to breathe.</p><p></p><p>“And I won''t let anyone disrespect you,” he added. “Not even if that person thinks she can buy the whole school.”</p><p></p><p>I glanced towards the shelves.</p><p></p><p>Kate was gone.</p><p></p><p>A strange uneasiness settled in my stomach.</p><p></p><p>Ethan noticed.</p><p></p><p>“Don''t worry about her.”</p><p></p><p>“You don''t understand.”</p><p></p><p>“No,” he said quietly. “I understand perfectly.”</p><p></p><p>He smiled.</p><p></p><p>“That''s why I''m asking you to stop running from me.”</p><p></p><p>For the first time, I didn''t have an excuse.</p><p></p><p>Maybe my mother had been right to warn me.</p><p></p><p>Maybe Ethan was dangerous in ways I hadn''t yet discovered.</p><p></p><p>Or maybe, for once, I had finally found someone who saw me for who I was rather than what I could offer him.</p><p></p><p>I looked at him and smiled despite myself.</p><p></p><p>“Fine,” I whispered.</p><p></p><p>His smile widened.</p><p></p><p>But as we left the library together, I couldn''t shake the feeling that someone was following us.</p><p></p><p>And when I glanced over my shoulder, I saw Kate standing at the end of the corridor.</p><p></p><p>She wasn''t crying.</p><p></p><p>She wasn''t angry.</p><p></p><p>She was smiling.</p><p></p><p>That was the moment I realised accepting Ethan might have been the easiest part.</p><p></p><p>Keeping him might be the real battle.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1790516021/echo-gist/32ac00d9-97a3-48f3-9d37-d6e2b5977d82/jjiqfhmvgnsd7lozykqv.jpg', 'lifestyle', '{"shortstory","romance","fantasy","writing","love"}', '32ac00d9-97a3-48f3-9d37-d6e2b5977d82', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 5, 3, 0, '2026-09-08T21:23:18.309Z', '2026-10-02T07:05:00.378Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('1454f876-8bc1-48a4-85f7-5bb49005a666', 'I have it all, but at what Cost.', 'i-have-it-all-but-at-what-cost-q9xm', 'An arm, a leg, and everything in between,  that''s what success cost me', '<p style="text-align: justify;">“All my life, I have strived to have it all; wealth, power, and fame. And now, I do, all before the age of forty. I bloody have it all. Sweet, isn’t it? Yeah, it is, except for this sudden headache that started a week ago. Aspirin didn’t do anything, so I went for a checkup.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">I’m waiting in the doctor’s office, staring at the calendar hanging behind his chair on his blue-colored wall. It was one of those nutritional facts calendars. Quite calming except for the &nbsp;&nbsp;smell of the cheap antiseptic. “<em>The best doctor in the country, but his office reeks of cheap antiseptic,”</em> I thought to myself.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“You are sick,” the doctor said as he came back in. “You have an experimental terminal illness called ‘Ifotomime’. We’ll try our best with treatment, but full disclosure, it doesn’t look so good.”</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><em>“Ifotomime? Experimental? Who the heck named it?”</em></p><p style="text-align: justify;">“How long do I have?” I asked.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“A couple of years, could be more, could be less, but we need to start treatment right away, sir.”</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“<em>What treatment, if it’s experimental?”</em></p><p style="text-align: center;"><em>***</em></p><p style="text-align: justify;">I &nbsp;was supposed to be in Tahiti or the Bahamas now, drinking expensive wine and living luxuriously. Instead, tubes were being attached to me, and I was being poked, prodded, and stuck with all sorts of needles and tiny tubes.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“Your cells are dying faster than they can reproduce,” the doctor said.</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><em>“I know. They are being killed by your so-called bloody ‘Ifotomime’.”</em></p><p style="text-align: center;">***</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Six months of scientific treatment and these needles-wielding butchers were failing. “Screw this, I’m done with this bullcrap called science,” I told the useless doctor and had myself discharged.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">I left to look for other alternatives: spirituality, and religion, I even smoked weed and bounced like a Rastaman. “<em>Well, I wouldn’t call it bouncing, with the life-threatening illness and all that”</em>. Still, nothing worked.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">And when everything seemed bleakest, I found it: a tiny light at the end of the tunnel. But the bloody track was filled with broken glass and thorns. I met an Oungan in New Orleans, and he said to me in a voice as deep as a grave: “You made a deal with the devil.”</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“What?… Damn,” I remembered. I had signed a contract with a joker in a black robe eleven years ago. He told me I would have everything, literally everything, but I might not enjoy it. “F**k it,” I said, and I did it. He didn’t tell me it was real, that it would take me nine years to achieve everything I wanted, and just two years to enjoy it before I died, minus the enjoyment.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“What’s the solution?” I asked the voodoo doctor.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Bring me a newborn,” he said. “I will use the newborn’s life to extend yours.”</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“Will it be okay?” I asked.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“Meh. You will have to…” he replied.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“No, I can’t do that. I just can’t. No way am I doing that cannibalistic deed, not to a newborn,” I said and left him.</p><p style="text-align: center;">***</p><p style="text-align: justify;">It’s been a year now. I’m shrinking in size, my skin is paler than chalk, I freaking shit blood, and to make matters worse, these useless doctors told me I only had less than a month to live. I’ve got no friends to talk to, no family to turn to, and no wife or children to look to. The only ones close to me are freaking vultures who want to nibble on my carcass and wealth. They’d rather let me die.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“<em>Damn, I don’t want to die. I want to live. I’ll do it.”</em></p><p style="text-align: justify;">I paid a greedy nurse to get me what the voodoo man asked for.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">I took it back to him, he prepared some rituals and says some strange incantations. Perhaps, it was a weird sermon, who knows? He placed the infant in my arms and asked me to..., the baby didn''t cry, it smiled at me. I want to cuddle it so bad but I had to do it. I did what he asked. It was horrible, I know, but I had no choice.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">The voodoo man then gave a sinister laugh, sounding like a hyena cackling at its prey. He abruptly stopped and shook his head pitifully, saying, “What a shame. Human beings and vanity.”</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“What do you mean?“ I asked. “You asked me to do it.”</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“You could have refused and died with a little dignity and a lot less sin,” he replied.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“Wha..” I felt dizzy and drowsy, and then I blacked out. When I regained consciousness, it was silent - awfully silent. I was inside a giant tube filled with a transparent liquid that burned but didn''t char, with a breathing tube shoved down my throat. I tried to move, but I couldn''t. I attempted to shout, but my voice wouldn''t cooperate. I could only tilt my head and glance to my left and right, seeing hundreds or even thousands of tubes like the one I was in, each containing a human being. The tubes were filled with a liquid that resembled water but wasn''t water, and all of them had numerous wires attached to them in every direction.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">I locked eyes with the woman in the tube beside me, but we couldn''t communicate. “Hello,” I said, or at least thought I did. The expression on her face was one of hopelessness, regret, and despair. I tried to fathom why, but I was in a similar situation.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">The breathing tube retracted, and the liquid rushed in. I drowned, blacked out, and died. When I awoke, I uttered, “What''s happening?” or thought I did. It was déjà vu all over again. I opened my eyes, looked left and right, and saw many people in my doomed situation. The breathing tube retracted once more. I tried to hold my breath, but it was pointless. The liquid rushed in, causing excruciating pain. I drowned, blacked out, and died.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">It happened hundreds, thousands, perhaps millions of times - all over again and again and again. It was silent, deathly silent, until I heard it; music to my ears, literal music, echoing in my ears:</p><p style="text-align: justify;"><em>“We ain''t nothin'' but mammals</em></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><em>Well, some of us are cannibal</em></p><p style="text-align: justify;"><em>Who cut other people open like cantaloupes”</em></p><p style="text-align: justify;">Eminem had never sounded so good. And then it stopped.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“Hello,” a booming and gruff voice said. “You might be wondering where you are and what is happening. You are all now the property of AYOCORP, a science company aiming to clean our society of all kinds of filth by all means necessary. You all agreed to participate in an illusion-induced experiment that tests your reactions to unique situations, and you all failed. As per the contract you signed, you will be here until you are all reformed or until we run out of electricity. So don''t fight it, just go with the flow.”</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“No!”I screamed or thought I did, but it didn''t stop. The agony continued, happening all over again."</p><p style="text-align: justify;">&nbsp;</p><p style="text-align: justify;"></p><p style="text-align: justify;"></p><p style="text-align: justify;"></p><p style="text-align: justify;">&nbsp;Note: No AI was used in writing this short story </p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788126324/echo-gist/89f720e3-1f90-484c-80c1-ed14f3ce9ec4/kwecydacc67qsaoubfwt.jpg', 'writing', '{}', '89f720e3-1f90-484c-80c1-ed14f3ce9ec4', TRUE, FALSE, 6, 54, 4, 0, '2026-08-30T21:51:17.131Z', '2026-09-28T04:07:47.647Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('4e4f5249-70c3-49e6-9691-c8904b357da5', 'My family''s secret', 'my-familys-secret-c8p4', 'My family''s secret should have been kept secret. Some things are never meant to see the light of day.', '<p>First of five I was. Born in poverty, but wasn''t my parents’ fault. Blame life for that.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">We strived, all of us, but the cosmos always seems to be against us. I wanted to become a writer so I wrote, about lots of things: “strange new world”, “a deal with the devil”, “how to make it in a not so legal way”, “my family''s secret that should be kept secret”, “a unicorn that can jump through time”, and many more. All, fiction, of course except for “my family''s secret that should be kept secret”.&nbsp;</p><p style="text-align: justify;">43 years of age, I am now, my mother, 70, father, 72, siblings, two years after the other.&nbsp; Strived, we did, but our conditions never seem to improve; although our family size did, we are no longer 7 members; now we are 18. We should have been way more, but we''re 18.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">And then I got it, a publishing contract with a company, one of the biggest in our country.&nbsp;All my works, they rejected except for my “family’s secret that should be kept secret”.&nbsp;</p><p style="text-align: justify;">I was gonna consider it with some variations of course. But they refused. They wanted the raw story. “6 figures, they offer me, hell, a movie deal might also come in”. I’ll get rich but it’ll cost me a lot if I go through with this. Why did I even submit this manuscript, I asked myself but I couldn’t come up with an answer. If this get published in its raw form, it will ruin us.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">My family secret should be kept secret. What’s this secret you might ask. Let’s just say, the world frown upon it, even the law is against it.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">My family, I loved, my dream, I also loved. I don’t know what to do but I must make a decision. And yes, I made it. It was tough but I choose myself and my dream. 6 figures upfront, I got. My book hit, millions of people read, but some made the connections quickly.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">They figure out why we never marry outsiders And why our extended family never seems to get bigger despite the fact we give birth to several children each year,&nbsp; and why we all look so much alike.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">My family got arrested. Their Fate, you can guess. I went underground, disappeared completely, and moved to a town nobody has heard of. Yes, I was selfish and left my family to a terrible fate, but any one of you would have done the same.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">“Did I enjoy my life?” No, I didn’t because eight months afterwards, I was hit by a truck and got paralysed. Whoever did it, nobody knows.</p><p style="text-align: justify;">&nbsp;</p><p style="text-align: justify;">&nbsp;</p><p style="text-align: justify;">&nbsp;</p><p style="text-align: justify;">Note: No AI was used in the writing of this short story</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788403892/echo-gist/89f720e3-1f90-484c-80c1-ed14f3ce9ec4/tfhycq0lwquf5fzyefpq.png', 'writing', '{"shortstorynigerianshortstory"}', '89f720e3-1f90-484c-80c1-ed14f3ce9ec4', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 52, 3, 0, '2026-09-03T02:56:14.050Z', '2026-10-02T07:29:11.431Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('ad7f6f4d-f0bb-4834-9d19-1f8514c6e33a', 'Happy Independence Day, Nigeria.', 'happy-independence-day-nigeria--5tv5', 'Comment HI, if you are Nigerian. 🤭🤭', '<p>Comment HI, if you are Nigerian. 🤭🤭</p><p></p><p>And you are not, say Hello. 🤭🤭</p>', NULL, 'culture', '{}', '7d2ad71d-e26d-4985-9fbe-8b4c1570f087', TRUE, FALSE, 1, 1, 0, 1, '2026-10-01T20:53:23.636Z', '2026-10-01T21:10:04.816Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('53624db5-39a7-4ff7-8b48-83eddf54ddf1', 'Age factor in our society', 'age-factor-in-our-society--y60p', 'Some people don’t really get bothered by age differences, while others are very particular about age. They feel disrespected when someone younger than them speaks to them in a certain way, regardless of the person’s intentions.
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('bb565579-f079-4b88-9587-4e6fda5a4301', 'Skin Tone, Like Volume Control', 'skin-tone-like-volume-control-miiu', 'Brightening not Bleaching', '<p>Think of your skin the way you think of a song playing through your headphones. Some days you want it soft and low. </p><p>Some days you turn it up just a notch. You are not trying to change the song—you are just adjusting the volume until it sits right in the room.</p><p>That is what brightening should feel like: practical, measurable, and completely in your control. You do not need to bleach the melody out of your skin. You do not need to tone your face into a stranger’s face. You only need to turn the dial a little.</p><p>If you are dark-skinned and want to deepen into a warm, even brown glow, treat it like raising the volume from three to five, not from three to ten. Start with a whitening soap that cleanses gently and lifts dullness without stripping your natural color. Let it do the daily work of clearing dead skin, sweat, and the grayish film that makes your tone look tired. Then leave the heavy brightening creams alone. Creams that promise three shades in three weeks are usually doing the work of bleach in a prettier jar, and they come with a price: thin skin, dark patches, stretch marks, and regret.</p><p>Instead, moisturize with something simple and nourishing. Protect yourself from the sun, because the sun is the static that distorts your song. Drink your water. Sleep. Exfoliate with patience. Brightening is maintenance, not transformation. You are not becoming someone new. You are simply making sure your own skin sounds clear.</p><p>So turn the dial slowly. Listen to your body. A shade or two of radiance is enough when the radiance is healthy. The goal is not to disappear into another color. The goal is to glow, confidently, in the one you already are.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788273560/echo-gist/6ab7749a-4f8a-421e-8a47-80d578b9ee69/j6cjemheq5k721ob1zyi.jpg', 'health', '{}', '6ab7749a-4f8a-421e-8a47-80d578b9ee69', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 15, 2, 0, '2026-09-01T14:40:19.414Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:54.801Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('f5507dd1-590e-4a6f-b85c-62c933e69511', 'Life of a first child', 'life-of-a-first-child--lo8k', 'Being the first child comes with a lot of expectations. You’re often expected to know better, act better, and sometimes even lead when you’re still trying to figure yourself out.
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('f0d233d1-7d75-487d-9625-5c29bd8c517f', 'Dream bigger, sis!”  Adeife Adeoye’s bold call to Nigerian women.', 'dream-bigger-sis-adeife-adeoyes-bold-call-to-nigerian-women-8enf', 'There''s a growing conversation online about whether young Nigerians, especially women, are being told to "dream bigger" in ways that ignore the ground they''re a…', '<p>There''s a growing conversation online about whether young Nigerians, especially women, are being told to "dream bigger" in ways that ignore the ground they''re actually standing on. When public figures encourage people to look beyond familiar career paths, government jobs, tech, even fields like aviation or space science, the advice is often well intentioned. Nobody wants young women boxed into a narrow set of "acceptable" jobs simply because that''s what''s always been done. But intention and impact aren''t always the same thing, and a lot of people online feel that ambition without context can come off as tone deaf.</p><p>The pushback isn''t really about discouraging big dreams. It''s about the gap between motivational language and lived reality. In a country wrestling with double digit inflation, currency devaluation, and a job market that hasn''t kept pace with population growth, telling someone to "aim for the stars" can land very differently depending on who''s saying it and what safety net they have. Critics argue that real empowerment isn''t just about expanding what people believe is possible, it''s about acknowledging the actual barriers (cost of education, lack of infrastructure, limited opportunities) that stand between a dream and its execution. Otherwise, the advice risks sounding like it''s coming from a place of privilege rather than solidarity.</p><p>At the same time, defenders of this kind of messaging make a fair point too, expectations shape outcomes, and if young girls are only ever told to aim for "safe" or traditional roles, that ceiling becomes self fulfilling. Somebody has to be the first Nigerian woman in a given field, and that person needed someone to plant the idea before the resources ever lined up. The real tension seems to be less about whether to dream big, and more about whether the people encouraging that dream are also doing the work of showing a realistic path there.</p><p>What do you think, is this kind of "dream beyond your zone" messaging genuinely motivating, or does it gloss over the real economic walls young Nigerians are up against?</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787384928/echo-gist/40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1/y43b2tmvxrmfcusfdknd.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 17, 2, 0, '2026-08-22T07:49:21.986Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:45.294Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('f1010001-0000-4000-a000-000000000014', 'Production Dockerization: Multi-Stage Builds, Security Hardening, & Best Practices', 'production-dockerization-multi-stage-builds-security-hardening', 'How to craft ultra-slim, secure production Docker containers with multi-stage builds, non-root user execution, and layer caching optimization.', '<h1>Production Dockerization: Multi-Stage Builds, Security Hardening, & Best Practices</h1>
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('571aeac4-2e99-4b33-b48a-e0d01c827883', 'It Was Never Really About the Stranger', 'it-was-never-really-about-the-stranger-qtov', 'Some people leave your life, but the version of you they met doesn''t leave with them.', '<p>If you had asked JSS3 me what changed me, I probably would have pointed at the stranger.</p><p>I would have told you about the friendship that came out of nowhere, how exciting it was, how quickly everything happened, the game that ruined it, and the day I told my classmates about it only to have them laugh.</p><p>Back then, it felt like a big deal.</p><p>It was one of those experiences I thought I would remember forever.</p><p>But life moved on.</p><p>School continued.</p><p>Break times came and went.</p><p>There were new conversations, new people and new things to get excited about.</p><p>Eventually, I stopped thinking about the stranger as much.</p><p>We never became friends again.</p><p>There was no dramatic reunion.</p><p>No big conversation where we finally explained everything to each other.</p><p>Nothing.</p><p>The friendship simply ended.</p><p>And maybe that''s why, looking back now, I don''t think the stranger was the most important part of the story.</p><p>What stayed with me was everything that came after.</p><p>I was young.</p><p>I was curious.</p><p>I wanted to explore the things my classmates talked about.</p><p>I wanted to make friends.</p><p>I wanted to feel seen.</p><p>And I was still figuring out what all of that meant.</p><p>The stranger was just one chapter in that process.</p><p>A very strange chapter, yes.</p><p>But still just a chapter.</p><p>Funny how some people enter your life for such a short time and somehow leave you with a story you can still remember years later.</p><p>I don''t know what became of him.</p><p>I don''t even know if he remembers me.</p><p>But I remember that girl.</p><p>The JSS3 girl who was just trying to find her way through friendship, social media, curiosity and growing up.</p><p>And maybe that''s who I was really trying to understand all along.</p><p>Not the stranger.</p><p>Me.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1786987239/echo-gist/ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731/i0mx7cmuu4xaqqzvqt6n.png', 'lifestyle', '{}', 'ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 10, 1, 0, '2026-08-17T17:20:05.166Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:55.341Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('3c9bc62c-1d29-43ab-9325-85f2432fb15e', 'Architects of Our Own Crisis', 'architects-of-our-own-crisis-powr', 'How Collective Responsibility Can Defeat Antimicrobial Resistance', '<h3>Part Two: The prescription pad is part of the battlefield</h3><p><strong>We have spent years telling patients not to misuse antibiotics.</strong></p><p>Don''t self-medicate.</p><p>Don''t demand antibiotics for every illness.</p><p>Don''t stop treatment unnecessarily.</p><p>Don''t share your antibiotics.</p><p>All of that matters.</p><p>But there is an uncomfortable question we also need to ask:</p><p><strong>What happens on the other side of the prescription?</strong></p><p>If patients have a responsibility in the antimicrobial resistance crisis, are healthcare providers completely free from responsibility?</p><p>Of course not.</p><p>Antimicrobial resistance is too complex to be reduced to a single group of people.</p><p>Patients can misuse antimicrobials.</p><p>Healthcare professionals can prescribe them inappropriately.</p><p>Health facilities can lack diagnostic capacity.</p><p>Agricultural systems can use antimicrobials improperly.</p><p>Pharmaceutical and regulatory systems can have gaps.</p><p>And weak surveillance can allow the problem to grow without us fully seeing it.</p><p>If the damage is collective, then the repair must also be collective.</p><p>And this is where one of our most important weapons against antimicrobial resistance enters the conversation:</p><h1><strong><em>Antimicrobial Stewardship</em></strong></h1><p>Antimicrobial stewardship programmes are designed to improve how antimicrobials are selected, prescribed, administered and monitored.</p><p>The goal is not simply to use fewer antibiotics.</p><p><strong>The goal is to use them intelligently.</strong></p><p>That distinction matters.</p><p>A patient who genuinely needs an antibiotic should receive the appropriate treatment.</p><p>The problem begins when antimicrobials are used when they are <em>unnecessary</em>, when the <em>wrong drug</em> is selected, when the <em>dose</em> is <em>inappropriate</em>, when treatment continues <em>longer than necessary</em>, or when therapy is <em>not reassessed</em> as new information becomes available.</p><p>A practical way to think about this is through the <strong>5 Ds: Diagnosis, Drug, Dose, Duration and De-escalation.</strong></p><h1>1. Diagnosis: Is an antimicrobial actually needed?</h1><p>Before the prescription comes the question:</p><p><strong>What are we actually treating?</strong></p><p>Not every fever is bacterial.</p><p>Not every cough requires an antibiotic.</p><p>Not every infection-like symptom means that an antibacterial drug is appropriate.</p><p>Whenever feasible, antimicrobial treatment should be guided by appropriate clinical assessment and diagnostic testing.</p><p>There are, of course, situations where treatment cannot wait for laboratory confirmation.</p><p>A critically ill patient cannot always afford to wait several days for a culture result.</p><p>That is why empirical therapy exists.</p><p>But empirical treatment should not become an excuse for abandoning diagnosis altogether.</p><p>And this is where I have a message for the healthcare professional who says:</p><blockquote><p>“Doctor, I''ve been prescribing this way for 30 years.”</p></blockquote><p>With all due respect, experience matters.</p><p><strong><em>But the microorganisms aren''t exactly newcomers either.</em></strong></p><p>Bacteria have been evolving and adapting for <strong><em>billions of years.</em></strong></p><p><strong>Run the appropriate tests whenever the clinical situation allows it.</strong></p><p>The question is not whether experience is valuable.</p><p>The question is whether experience should replace evidence.</p><p>It shouldn''t.</p><h1>2. Drug: Is this the right antimicrobial?</h1><p>Once an antimicrobial is considered necessary, the next question is:</p><p><strong>Which one?</strong></p><p>The strongest antibiotic is not automatically the best antibiotic.</p><p>Treatment should target the confirmed or most likely pathogen while minimizing unnecessary effects on the patient''s normal microbiota.</p><p>This is particularly important when broad-spectrum antibiotics are used.</p><p>Think about it this way:</p><p><strong>Using a broad-spectrum antibiotic for every infection is like using a sledgehammer to swat a fly on a glass table.</strong></p><p>Yes, you may kill the fly.</p><p>But you might also <strong>destroy </strong>the table.</p><p>Broad-spectrum antimicrobials have important clinical uses, particularly when the pathogen is unknown or the patient is seriously ill.</p><p>The problem is not that broad-spectrum drugs exist.</p><p>The problem is <strong>using them when a narrower, appropriate option would do the job.</strong></p><p>Antimicrobial stewardship therefore asks:</p><ul><li><p>What organism are we trying to treat?</p></li><li><p>What is the likely susceptibility pattern?</p></li><li><p>Is there a narrower agent that would be effective?</p></li><li><p>Can unnecessary antimicrobial exposure be avoided?</p></li></ul><p>Precision matters.</p><h1>3. Dose: Is the amount appropriate for this patient?</h1><p>Antimicrobial dosing is not simply a matter of memorizing a number.</p><p>The appropriate dose can depend on factors such as:</p><ul><li><p>the type and severity of infection;</p></li><li><p>the site of infection;</p></li><li><p>the patient''s age and weight where relevant;</p></li><li><p>kidney function;</p></li><li><p>liver function;</p></li><li><p>the pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic properties of the drug; and</p></li><li><p>other patient-specific considerations.</p></li></ul><p>Giving everyone the same dose without considering these factors is like putting the same quantity of fuel into every vehicle and expecting every engine to perform perfectly.</p><p>Some may receive too little.</p><p>Some may receive too much.</p><p>And some may experience avoidable toxicity.</p><p>There is another important point here:</p><p><strong>The drug needs to reach the infection at an effective concentration.</strong></p><p>Treating an infection is not simply about putting an antimicrobial into someone''s bloodstream.</p><p>The drug must reach the relevant site of infection in sufficient concentration while remaining safe for the patient.</p><p>The brain, lungs, bones, urinary tract and other tissues do not all present the same pharmacological challenges.</p><p>So the question isn''t merely:</p><blockquote><p>“What dose kills the organism?”</p></blockquote><p>It is:</p><blockquote><p><strong>“What exposure will effectively treat this infection in this particular patient?”</strong></p></blockquote><h1>4. Duration: How long should treatment continue?</h1><p>This is where one of the most persistent assumptions about antibiotics needs to be challenged:</p><p><strong>Longer does not automatically mean better.</strong></p><p>Antimicrobial stewardship promotes treatment durations supported by evidence for the specific infection and clinical circumstances.</p><p>Continuing therapy unnecessarily exposes patients to additional antimicrobial pressure and potential adverse effects without necessarily providing additional benefit.</p><p>Imagine leaving a fire hose running for three days after the flames have already been extinguished.</p><p>You''re not making the building safer.</p><p>You''re just flooding it.</p><p>The same principle applies to antimicrobial therapy.</p><p>Once an appropriate evidence-based treatment period has been completed, continuing “just to be safe” is not automatically safer.</p><p>The appropriate duration depends on the infection, the antimicrobial, the patient and the clinical response.</p><p>Therefore:</p><p><strong>The right duration is not necessarily the longest duration.</strong></p><p>It is the duration supported by evidence for that clinical situation.</p><h1>5. De-escalation: Now that we know more, can we narrow the treatment?</h1><p>This may be one of the most important moments in antimicrobial stewardship.</p><p>A patient may initially present with a serious infection where the causative organism is unknown.</p><p>In such a situation, empirical therapy may need to cover several possible pathogens.</p><p>But the clinical picture does not remain static.</p><p>The laboratory may return culture and antimicrobial susceptibility results.</p><p>The patient''s condition may improve.</p><p>The suspected pathogen may become known.</p><p>And suddenly, the question changes.</p><p>It is no longer:</p><p><strong>“What could possibly be causing this?”</strong></p><p>It becomes:</p><p><strong>“Now that we have more information, what is the most appropriate treatment?”</strong></p><p>That is where de-escalation comes in.</p><p>Can the broad-spectrum antimicrobial be narrowed?</p><p>Can an unnecessary antimicrobial be stopped?</p><p>Can treatment be changed from intravenous to an appropriate oral option?</p><p>Can therapy be adjusted according to susceptibility results?</p><p>Imagine continuing to call in an airstrike after the target has already been identified and contained.</p><p>More force does not necessarily mean better treatment.</p><p>Sometimes, <strong>precision is the stronger weapon.</strong></p><p>De-escalation is therefore not “weakening” treatment.</p><p>It is using new information to make treatment more precise.</p><h1>The laboratory is not a side character</h1><p>There is an important lesson running through all five Ds:</p><p><strong>Antimicrobial stewardship cannot function effectively without diagnostics.</strong></p><p>The prescriber needs information.</p><p>The laboratory provides information.</p><p>The pharmacist contributes expertise in antimicrobial selection, dosing and interactions.</p><p>Nurses monitor administration, response and patient factors.</p><p>Infection prevention teams address transmission.</p><p>Hospital leadership provides the systems and resources that allow all of this to happen.</p><p>This is why effective antimicrobial stewardship is not simply about giving doctors another guideline and telling them to “prescribe responsibly.”</p><p>It requires <strong>multidisciplinary collaboration.</strong></p><h1>AMR is bigger than the hospital</h1><p>And this brings us to the bigger picture.</p><p>Antimicrobial resistance does not exist exclusively inside hospitals.</p><p>It does not belong exclusively to doctors.</p><p>It does not belong exclusively to patients.</p><p>It does not even belong exclusively to human medicine.</p><p>Antimicrobial resistance moves across interconnected systems involving <strong>people, animals and the environment.</strong></p><p>That is why the response must be grounded in a <strong>One Health approach.</strong></p><p>Healthcare institutions need the capacity to support antimicrobial stewardship through:</p><ul><li><p>trained multidisciplinary teams;</p></li><li><p>reliable diagnostic services;</p></li><li><p>evidence-based prescribing guidelines;</p></li><li><p>antimicrobial-use surveillance;</p></li><li><p>regular audit and feedback;</p></li><li><p>infection prevention and control;</p></li><li><p>continuing professional education; and</p></li><li><p>institutional accountability.</p></li></ul><p>At the same time, medical, pharmacy, nursing and other health-professional programmes need to prepare future healthcare workers to understand antimicrobials as a <strong>finite and shared resource.</strong></p><p>The conversation must also extend beyond hospitals.</p><p>Agriculture matters.</p><p>Veterinary medicine matters.</p><p>Environmental contamination matters.</p><p>Public policy matters.</p><p>Regulation matters.</p><p>Public awareness matters.</p><p>And education matters.</p><h1>We cannot educate our way out of AMR alone</h1><p>One of the easiest things to do in the AMR conversation is to blame the patient.</p><p>“People buy antibiotics without prescriptions.”</p><p>“People stop taking antibiotics when they feel better.”</p><p>“People share medication.”</p><p>All of these behaviors deserve attention.</p><p>But stopping there would be a mistake.</p><p>We cannot educate our way out of AMR while leaving healthcare systems, prescribing practices, diagnostics, agriculture, regulation and environmental pathways untouched.</p><p>Similarly, we cannot simply blame healthcare professionals while ignoring the pressures under which healthcare systems operate.</p><p>AMR is a systems problem.</p><p>And systems problems require systems solutions.</p><h1>We are architects of the crisis. We can also be architects of the solution.</h1><p>Antimicrobial resistance is often presented as though humanity is simply waiting for bacteria to defeat our medicines.</p><p>But there is another way to look at it.</p><p>Every unnecessary prescription creates antimicrobial pressure.</p><p>Every poorly targeted treatment creates an opportunity for selection.</p><p>Every preventable infection creates another reason to use antimicrobials.</p><p>Every missed opportunity for diagnosis makes precision more difficult.</p><p>Every failure to de-escalate wastes information that could have improved treatment.</p><p>And every effective stewardship intervention represents an opportunity to preserve antimicrobial effectiveness.</p><p>The solution therefore does not rest on one profession.</p><p>It does not rest on one laboratory.</p><p>It does not rest on one new antibiotic.</p><p>And it certainly does not rest on blaming patients alone.</p><p>It requires collective discipline.</p><p><strong>Better diagnosis.</strong></p><p><strong>Better prescribing.</strong></p><p><strong>Better stewardship.</strong></p><p><strong>Better infection prevention.</strong></p><p><strong>Better surveillance.</strong></p><p><strong>Better regulation.</strong></p><p><strong>Better agricultural practices.</strong></p><p><strong>Better public understanding.</strong></p><p>And better collaboration across the One Health spectrum.</p><p>The future of antimicrobial therapy will not be protected by a miracle drug alone.</p><p><strong>It will be protected by the millions of decisions we make before, during and after that drug is prescribed.</strong></p><p>We are, in many ways, <strong>architects of our own crisis.</strong></p><p>And that means we have an opportunity to become architects of the solution.</p><p><strong>We may not need a miracle drug to save the medicines we already have.</strong></p><p><strong>We need the collective discipline to protect the miracles we already have.</strong></p><p><em>Part Two of the series on antimicrobial resistance.</em></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1790155169/echo-gist/822d6b89-834b-40bf-a86b-a9a08d95380a/jtjze0z0ofp4206krfvt.jpg', 'science', '{"sciencecommunication","onehealth","amr","microbiology"}', '822d6b89-834b-40bf-a86b-a9a08d95380a', TRUE, FALSE, 7, 2, 0, 0, '2026-09-23T09:34:40.171Z', '2026-09-24T03:30:17.560Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('b39c6afd-6ec0-4c3e-bffb-43bf525da188', 'A riddle', 'a-riddle--nixc', '2 weeks without bathing 
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('c1634775-1160-41bf-a513-68a69b910a48', 'We Have Made Life Look Like We Only Came Here to Find Money and Die', 'we-have-made-life-look-like-we-only-came-here-to-find-money-and-die-q4lu', 'When I was little, I thought growing up was going to be beautiful.I didn’t really understand money then. I didn’t know you needed money to keep the lights on, p…', '<p>When I was little, I thought growing up was going to be beautiful.</p><p>I didn’t really understand money then. I didn’t know you needed money to keep the lights on, pay rent, eat, move around, buy data, fix things when they broke, or basically exist without constantly calculating your next expense.</p><p>I just thought you grew up.</p><p>You made money.</p><p>You were happy.</p><p>You had friends.</p><p>You went out.</p><p>You had a nice house.</p><p>And you lived.</p><p>Simple.</p><p>Nobody told me that somewhere between growing up and actually becoming an adult, life would become this endless cycle of <em>find money, pay bills, complain about money, find more money.</em></p><p>And somehow, everybody is tired.</p><p>People wake up, go to work, come back exhausted, pay bills, sleep, and do it all over again.</p><p>Then when you ask them to hang out, they say they don’t have money.</p><p>Ask them to visit you, they say they are busy.</p><p>Ask them when last they saw their friends, and they genuinely have to think about it.</p><p>And I sometimes wonder, is this really what we thought adulthood was going to be?</p><p>Because we are working so hard to afford life that we barely have the time or energy to actually live it.</p><p>I know money is important. Very important, actually.</p><p>Especially in Nigeria.</p><p>Let’s not pretend.</p><p>Nigeria is hard.</p><p>Things are expensive. Rent is expensive. Food is expensive. Transportation is expensive. The value of the naira keeps falling, and even when you finally find a job, sometimes the salary barely survives the journey from your employer’s account to your bank account.</p><p>You earn ₦300,000 and before you know it, transport has taken some, food has taken some, data has taken some, skincare has taken some, bills have taken some.</p><p>What is left?</p><p>You start wondering if you were actually paid at all.</p><p>And jobs? Finding a good-paying job sometimes feels like finding a needle in a very large, very frustrating haystack.</p><p>So yes, people need money.</p><p>I need money.</p><p>You need money.</p><p>We all need money.</p><p>But somewhere along the line, I think we started confusing <strong>having money</strong> with <strong>being allowed to live.</strong></p><p>And those are not the same thing.</p><p>We started telling ourselves that we would enjoy life when we have enough money.</p><p>When we get the better job.</p><p>When we move into the nice apartment.</p><p>When we can afford to travel.</p><p>When we buy the car.</p><p>When we get the perfect body.</p><p>When we can afford the expensive restaurant without checking the menu prices first.</p><p>When we finally “make it.”</p><p>And before you know it, your entire life becomes a waiting room.</p><p>Wait.</p><p>Just wait.</p><p>Things will be better soon.</p><p>And I understand why.</p><p>We are scared of slowing down.</p><p>Because everybody keeps telling us that the time to make money is when you’re young. So you work while you’re young, save while you’re young, sacrifice while you’re young, so that when you’re old you can finally enjoy yourself.</p><p>Because who knows what will happen later?</p><p>The care culture is dying too.</p><p>There was a time when your neighbour could be your brother. When people looked out for each other. When seeing someone struggling could actually move you to help them.</p><p>Now you can practically be dying on the road and people will walk past you.</p><p>Everybody is trying to survive.</p><p>Everybody is trying to secure themselves because, honestly, who knows who will be there for you tomorrow?</p><p>So people hustle.</p><p>And hustle.</p><p>And hustle.</p><p>But then something happens.</p><p>You become so busy securing your future that you stop experiencing your present.</p><p>And I don’t think we’re meant to live like that.</p><p></p><p>I also think social media has made this worse.</p><p>Because now it’s not enough to live a good life.</p><p>Your life has to <strong>look</strong> good.</p><p>You have to look like you’re living.</p><p>You need the latest phone.</p><p>The expensive hair.</p><p>The nice apartment.</p><p>The restaurant pictures.</p><p>The vacation pictures.</p><p>The perfect body.</p><p>The perfect outfit.</p><p>The perfect lighting.</p><p>And then, of course, you need the perfect picture to prove that you have all these things.</p><p>Nobody wants to post a real picture anymore.</p><p>Everybody is waiting for that one picture where they look like a baddie.</p><p>The picture where the stomach is flat, the hair is laid, the face is giving, the lighting is perfect and everything looks expensive.</p><p>And honestly, I get it.</p><p>Who doesn’t want to look good?</p><p>But sometimes I wonder when we stopped using social media to share our lives and started using it to present evidence that our lives are worth envying.</p><p>We used to post nonsense.</p><p>Real nonsense.</p><p>A stupid picture of your friend.</p><p>A blurry picture.</p><p>A joke that only five people understood.</p><p>Something embarrassing that happened during the day.</p><p>You could make a dumb post and your friends would laugh at you in the comments.</p><p>People weren’t trying so hard to look perfect.</p><p>We were just having fun.</p><p>Now everybody is so serious.</p><p>Even our silliness has been curated.</p><p>And the likes matter too.</p><p>People take pictures, edit them, spend money on hair, clothes, photographers and sometimes even gadgets, then sit down refreshing the post to see how many people liked it.</p><p>And sometimes the picture doesn’t even do well.</p><p>So you’ve spent all that money trying to create a moment that will impress people, and three hours later, the algorithm has swallowed it.</p><p>I know a girl who spent most of her money preparing for her birthday pictures.</p><p>Hair.</p><p>Makeup.</p><p>Outfit.</p><p>Pictures.</p><p>Everything.</p><p>She wanted the birthday pictures.</p><p>And she got them.</p><p>Beautiful pictures.</p><p>But by the time the birthday was over, she didn’t have money for a bedframe.</p><p>And I don’t even say this to make fun of her.</p><p>Because honestly, I understand her.</p><p>We’ve all been sold this idea that certain things make you look like you’re doing well.</p><p>And sometimes we spend money trying to <strong>look like we are living a beautiful life while the actual life underneath the picture is struggling.</strong></p><p>That is the part that worries me.</p><p></p><p>And I’m not even going to sit here and act like I have figured it out.</p><p>I haven’t.</p><p>I postpone happiness too.</p><p>Many times.</p><p>Sometimes I want to buy a bottle of wine or get something small for myself and I’ll start calculating.</p><p><em>“If I buy this now, when it finishes, my money will be gone.”</em></p><p>Sometimes I’m exhausted and I just want to take a ride home instead of entering a bus.</p><p>Then I check the price.</p><p><em>“For what?”</em></p><p>And I enter the bus.</p><p>Sometimes I want to see my friends but I don’t have money for transport.</p><p>Sometimes I want to take a picture but I don’t have the hair I think I need for the picture, so I tell myself I’ll take it later.</p><p>When?</p><p>When I get the hair.</p><p>When I have the money.</p><p>When things are better.</p><p>And that’s the exact thing I’m talking about.</p><p>We keep waiting.</p><p></p><p>The funny thing is, I think I’m actually a very low-maintenance girl.</p><p>I hate admitting it because the internet has made it seem like every woman must be a particular kind of “baddie” to be considered desirable.</p><p>But honestly?</p><p>Sometimes I just want to live.</p><p>I can wear my natural hair.</p><p>I can do all-back and be fine.</p><p>I love wigs. I love nice hair. I love beautiful things. But I can survive without them.</p><p>I don’t need a new gadget every time something new comes out.</p><p>Give me food in my house.</p><p>Give me money in my account.</p><p>Give me data.</p><p>Let me work out.</p><p>Let me have somewhere comfortable to sleep.</p><p>Let me have people I love around me.</p><p>Honestly, I’m good.</p><p>And maybe that’s not enough for Instagram.</p><p>But it can be enough for life.</p><p></p><p>You are also allowed to get excited over small things.</p><p>I think we have forgotten that.</p><p>A good cup of coffee.</p><p>Clean sheets.</p><p>A meal you’ve been craving.</p><p>Getting home after a long day and taking off your bra.</p><p>A quiet weekend with absolutely nothing planned.</p><p>Seeing someone you haven’t seen in a long time.</p><p>Sitting with your friends in someone’s house, nobody looking at their phone, everybody talking rubbish and laughing.</p><p>Making fun of one another.</p><p>Remembering something stupid you did years ago and laughing about it all over again.</p><p>These things don’t sound impressive.</p><p>You probably can’t make a “soft life” TikTok out of most of them.</p><p>But they are still life.</p><p>And sometimes happiness is just noticing the little things that are still there.</p><p>I was talking to a friend recently, and she told me she had been feeling depressed because she didn’t know how her life was going.</p><p>She doesn’t have an apartment of her own.</p><p>And the funny thing is, I was actually admiring her.</p><p>This girl had started learning hair installation. She’s also learning makeup.</p><p>Meanwhile, I’m sitting here thinking, <em>this babe is doing something with herself.</em></p><p>And she’s sitting there thinking her life is going nowhere.</p><p>It made me realize how strange we are.</p><p>We are constantly looking at somebody else’s life and thinking they are doing better, while that person might be looking at us and thinking the exact same thing.</p><p>Everybody is tired.</p><p>Everybody thinks they’re behind.</p><p>Everybody thinks they haven’t done enough.</p><p>Maybe sometimes we need to stop and look at what is actually happening.</p><p>Maybe your life isn’t as empty as you think it is.</p><p></p><p>I learned something about this when I was dealing with my own health problems.</p><p>When you’re healthy, you don’t wake up and say, “Thank God, I can eat without pain today.”</p><p>You just eat.</p><p>You don’t say, “I’m grateful that I woke up feeling normal.”</p><p>You just wake up.</p><p>You don’t think about how beautiful it is to sit with your family, laugh with your friends, walk around freely, or simply feel okay.</p><p>Until something takes that normality away from you.</p><p>Then suddenly, the things you thought were ordinary become precious.</p><p>And maybe we don’t have to wait for something to go wrong before we notice what is going right.</p><p></p><p>This isn’t me saying we should stop working.</p><p>Please.</p><p>Make your money.</p><p>Get the job.</p><p>Build the business.</p><p>Save.</p><p>Invest.</p><p>Buy the house.</p><p>Travel.</p><p>Get the nice hair.</p><p>Buy the iPhone if you want it.</p><p>Do all the things you’ve dreamed about.</p><p>Wanting more is not a bad thing.</p><p>But maybe we need to stop making <strong>more</strong> the entry fee for happiness.</p><p>You can want a better life without hating the one you currently have.</p><p>You can be ambitious and still rest.</p><p>You can be broke and still laugh.</p><p>You can be working towards something and still enjoy a random Tuesday.</p><p>You can wear your natural hair and take the picture anyway.</p><p>You can post the silly picture.</p><p>The ugly picture.</p><p>The picture where you weren’t posing.</p><p>The picture that only makes sense to you and your friends.</p><p>You don’t have to look like a baddie every day to prove that you’re living.</p><p>And maybe we should start going to our friends’ houses again.</p><p>Not every hangout needs a restaurant.</p><p>Not every gathering needs drinks and a photographer.</p><p>Sometimes everybody can bring whatever they have.</p><p>Someone cooks.</p><p>Someone brings a drink.</p><p>Someone brings absolutely nothing but their presence.</p><p>And you sit down.</p><p>You talk.</p><p>You laugh.</p><p>You remember old stories.</p><p>You make new ones.</p><p>And for a few hours, nobody is thinking about rent.</p><p>Nobody is thinking about Instagram.</p><p>Nobody is checking how many likes their last picture got.</p><p>You’re just there.</p><p>Alive.</p><p></p><p>Maybe this is the part we’ve forgotten.</p><p>Life is not only the big things.</p><p>It is also the little things happening while you’re waiting for the big things.</p><p>The coffee.</p><p>The friends.</p><p>The laughter.</p><p>The food.</p><p>The clean sheets.</p><p>The breeze coming through an open window.</p><p>The random phone call.</p><p>The stupid joke.</p><p>The good news.</p><p>The quiet evening.</p><p>The ability to wake up and try again.</p><p>We have become so obsessed with building a life worth living that we’ve forgotten to live while we’re building it.</p><p>So yes, make the money.</p><p>Please make the money.</p><p>But don’t spend your entire youth preparing for a future where you finally get to be happy.</p><p>Call your friend.</p><p>Go and see them.</p><p>Sit somewhere and talk nonsense.</p><p>Post the picture.</p><p>Open the windows.</p><p>Eat the food.</p><p>Laugh too loudly.</p><p>Take the ride home once in a while if you’re too tired for the bus.</p><p>Buy the small thing that makes you happy sometimes.</p><p>Let yourself be excited about something that will not change your life.</p><p>Because maybe happiness was never supposed to be this grand thing we finally unlock after we’ve achieved everything.</p><p>Maybe sometimes it is just realizing that, despite everything, <strong>there are still good things here.</strong></p><p>And maybe that’s enough reason to live a little.</p>', NULL, 'culture', '{}', 'a9152831-b554-4cef-ac89-d66fb7927777', TRUE, FALSE, 9, 41, 4, 0, '2026-08-31T16:04:48.209Z', '2026-09-28T04:13:31.552Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('125afaba-dcd8-4405-9739-3156da13cc6a', 'My Brother Used to Beg for Everything — So I Taught Him a Very Expensive Lesson', 'my-brother-used-to-beg-for-everything-so-i-taught-him-a-very-expensive-lesson-38k7', 'He never stopped begging until one bottle of 7up changed everything.
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('dba8ff5e-a962-4e0d-aa3c-84c35125fff1', 'WHY "SOFT LIFE" BECAME MORE THAN A TREND (AND WHY SO FEW PEOPLE ARE ACTUALLY LIVING IT)', 'why-soft-life-became-more-than-a-trend-and-why-so-few-people-are-actually-living-8hrc', 'The dream of ease, the pressure to afford it, and the reality nobody posts about.', '<p>She posted a video sipping tea by the window, captioned "choosing peace this season." Ten minutes later, she was back on three client calls, an unpaid invoice, and a landlord message she had not answered in four days. That''s soft life now, not the absence of stress, but the art of hiding it well. If you''ve ever posted a calm, glowing picture of your life while your chest was tight with anxiety the whole time, you already know exactly what this article is about.</p><p>WHY DOES EVERYONE SUDDENLY WANT A "SOFT LIFE"?</p><p>It didn''t come from nowhere. An entire generation grew up watching their parents work themselves into exhaustion and call it responsibility. The aunty who worked two jobs and still fainted from stress, the uncle who "provided" but was never around, the quiet understanding that suffering equals maturity. Soft life is the loud, public rejection of that story. The desire underneath it is real, people are genuinely tired of proving their worth through struggle. The problem is what happened to that desire once it hit the internet, it got packaged, filtered, and sold back as a lifestyle you could buy into with the right skincare routine and the right caption.</p><p>Have you wondered if Soft Life Is Real, or Is It Just Good Lighting and a Caption? </p><p>Both. That''s the uncomfortable answer, yes you read right. </p><p>For a small number of people, soft life is genuinely funded by money, by privilege, by circumstances that remove the daily grind most people can''t opt out of. For a much larger number of people online, it''s a fifteen-second clip of the one peaceful hour in an otherwise chaotic day, posted because that hour is the only part worth showing anyone. You''re not seeing a lie exactly. You''re seeing a highlight reel of someone''s best moment, presented as if it were their whole life. And you''re comparing it to your entire, unfiltered day.</p><p>AM I THE ONLY ONE WHO CAN''T AFFORD TO "CHOOSE PEACE"?</p><p>No. And this is the part nobody wants to admit out loud, because it sounds like complaining, peace costs money, and rest requires a level of financial and emotional stability that a lot of young Nigerians simply don''t have yet.</p><p>You can want to "choose yourself" all you want, the rent still needs paying, the family still calls with needs, the job still doesn''t care how tired you are. Feeling locked out of soft life is not a personal failure to manifest calm. It''s a fairly accurate read of your actual circumstances.</p><p>IS IT NORMAL TO FEEL LIKE YOU''RE FAILING AT RELAXING?</p><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787398881/echo-gist/817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4/btexnb8jcwmvkhmwxlg6.png" alt="1000734737.png" style="max-width: 100%; border-radius: 8px; margin: 24px 0px; display: block;"><p>Yes, and it''s one of the strangest side effects of this trend. People are now stressed about not being relaxed enough. Rest became a performance metric. Instead of resting because you''re tired, you rest so you can post that you rested, and somehow that turns relaxation into another task on the list. If your "self-care Sunday" feels more exhausting than your actual work week, that''s not you doing soft life wrong. That''s soft life, as currently sold to you, being a bit of a trap.</p><p>Nobody Tells You This: Soft Life Was Never About Doing Less, It Was About Doing Less Loudly</p><p>Here''s the hard truth, a lot of what gets called soft life online isn''t rest. It''s rest with an audience. The tea by the window only counts if someone sees it. The "day off" only registers if it''s documented. Real rest, the kind that actually restores you, usually looks boring, unposted, and slightly unglamorous, sleeping in without telling anyone, sitting in silence with no caption, saying no to something and never explaining why. The version that goes viral is rarely the version that''s actually working.</p><p>HOW TO BUILD AN ACTUAL SOFT LIFE (NOT JUST POST ONE)</p><p>You don''t need a new aesthetic. You need boundaries nobody can see. Protect one unposted hour a day. Something that''s just for you, with no camera and no caption. If it feels strange to keep something private, that''s worth noticing. Separate rest from performance. Ask yourself honestly: am I doing this because I need it, or because I need people to see me doing it Build the softness before you advertise it. Save quietly. Set the boundary quietly. Let the calm be a byproduct of real changes, not the opening scene of a story you have not lived yet. Soft life is not fake. But most of what''s sold to you under that name is a photo of a destination, not a map to get there. The real version is slower, less photogenic, and unlike the trend, actually sustainable. Be honest, is your soft life real, or is it just your best camera angle?</p><p>Tell us in the comments. And send this to the friend who''s been "resting" since January but hasn''t stopped hustling for a day.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787398352/echo-gist/817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4/izp9h0rv2b84h7usmdxq.png', 'lifestyle', '{}', '817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 45, 4, 2, '2026-08-22T11:43:23.975Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:45.056Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('269db756-eebd-49e1-a228-aa1ecb8118db', 'The Rude Bus Driver', 'the-rude-bus-driver-r0b9', 'The Mutiny', '<p>Travelling by road? </p><p>There is an undeniable charm to road trips in Nigeria. </p><p>The rhythmic hum of the engine, the refreshing breeze cutting through the open window, the vibrant energy of roadside hawkers thrusting plantain chips and cold drinks through the glass, and the endless stretch of green scenery, it was almost enough to make Diana forget her severe motion sickness.</p><p>Almost.</p><p>She was traveling home to visit relatives, settled into a middle seat of a commercial bus. But any hope of a peaceful journey was shattered before they even cleared the city limits, courtesy of the driver.</p><p>He was a bitter, abrasive man who treated his weathered vehicle like a pristine luxury vehicle and his passengers like stray livestock.</p><p>"<em>Abeg</em>, make una eat carefully o!" he barked into the rearview mirror, his voice dripping with venom as he glared at a middle-aged woman carefully holding a takeaway container. "<em>Kai</em>! Madam, no go stain my seat with that beans!"</p><p>The woman paused mid-bite, her spoon hovering in the air. "<em>Haba</em>, driver! I no fit chop again? Why you dey talk to me like this?"</p><p>The driver only grunted, continuing his barrage. A few rows back, a young student leaned his head backward against the headrest, trying to sleep off the heat.</p><p>"Hey! You!" the driver shouted. "Adjust your head! Before you go drool come stain my car seat!"</p><p>The young man snapped his eyes open, thoroughly unimpressed. "This your <em>ajepako</em> car wey dey cry for help? Na him you dey pride yourself on top?"</p><p>By noon, the bus was a battlefield of loud arguments and back-to-back insults. But nobody clashed with the driver quite like the mother of three sitting near the front.</p><p>"You are extremely bad at driving, rude, and completely mannerless!" she yelled over the engine roar.</p><p>"And you, this unmarried woman with plenty children," the driver sneered back, "don''t even try me. Go and control your noisy kids!"</p><p>"Who told you I''m not married?!" she fired back, pulling out her phone. "In fact, I will call my husband now! He’s in the army, and he will deal with you at the next checkpoint!" She dialed furiously, but the highway signal was dead.</p><p>Realizing her threat was falling flat, the woman resorted to pure malice. Diana watched in utter disbelief as the woman covertly induced vomit in one of her young children, deliberately letting the mess spill onto the floor mat just to spite the driver.</p><p>When the driver noticed, he lost his mind. He slammed on the brakes, pulling over to the shoulder, and began screaming at the terrified child until the kid burst into tears.</p><p>"Don''t you dare speak to my child like that!" the mother roared, shielding her crying kid.</p><p>With a final curse, the driver shoved the bus back into gear and tore down the road. An hour later, when another one of the woman''s children whimpered that he needed to pee, the driver stubbornly refused to pull over, speeding past every safe stop.</p><p>The noise inside the bus reached a boiling point. Everyone was shouting at once. That was when Diana, unable to take the chaos any longer, raised her voice over the noise.</p><p>"If you know you are this frustrated and sparkling neat," Diana called out, "why are you driving us, the dirty passengers?"</p><p>That was the final straw.</p><p>The driver swerved hard onto the dirt shoulder, cutting the engine right in the middle of a dense, isolated forest stretch. He threw open his door, stepped out, and crossed his arms.</p><p>"All of you should come out of my car!" he shouted through the open windows. "I am not moving again! Walk to where you are going!"</p><p>A sudden, tense silence fell over the passengers. Pride urged them to step down and leave the arrogant man behind, but reality quickly set in. They were stranded in an insecure bush area with no network, no towns in sight, and zero passing commercial vehicles. Leaving the bus meant putting their lives at risk.</p><p>The passengers began whispering among themselves, gathering in a quick, urgent consensus. This driver was an unhinged safety risk to every single person on board.</p><p>"I know this road," a calm voice called out from the back. A young man stood up. "And I have a valid license. I can drive us."</p><p>Before the driver could comprehend what was happening, three male passengers stepped off the bus, grabbed the driver by his shirt, and forcefully bundled him into the back row.</p><p>"What are you doing?!" the driver screamed, his arrogance suddenly turning to panic. "This is my car! I will have all of you arrested for kidnapping and carjacking!"</p><p>"Shut up there!" a passenger barked, landing a light slap on the side of his head as they pinned him down. Even the mother of three leaned over to throw a firm slap of her own for good measure.</p><p>"Nobody is stealing your precious car," Diana said coldly as the new driver started the ignition and put the bus in drive. "We are only taking ourselves to our destination. When we get there, you can have your sparkling clean vehicle back."</p><p>The bus rolled smoothly back onto the highway, leaving the former king of the road trapped in his own backseat, forced to endure the rest of the trip in complete, humiliated silence.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787430206/echo-gist/64a4435f-e4df-4c17-8422-16439c81d14b/nbylzhwifzrivsp7xg7k.jpg', 'writing', '{"writing","relatable","drama"}', '64a4435f-e4df-4c17-8422-16439c81d14b', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 9, 2, 4, '2026-08-22T20:34:02.160Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:43.649Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('b3fbcb05-a3f7-4a28-9156-7d6e11f72f6f', 'My Crush Has Mouth Odor', 'my-crush-has-mouth-odor--c49q', 'The Illusion of Perfection (Comedic)', '<p>To Rita, Louis was not just a guy; he was a masterpiece walking the halls of the university. Tall, light skinned, and sleekly built, he moved with an effortless poise. With his almond eyes, sharp nose, perfectly contoured pink lips, and subtle, elf-like ears, he looked like he had stepped right out of a high-fantasy novel.</p><p>He was quiet, almost ghostlike yet his aura dominated every room he entered. Nobody really knew what his voice sounded like, though rumors claimed a lucky few had heard him speak.</p><p>"Louis is the one for me," Rita declared proudly, resting her chin on her hands as she watched him cross the courtyard.</p><p>Martha, her roommate looked up from her notebook with a slight smirk. "Hmm. Rita, small-small o. Have you ever actually spoken to him?"</p><p>"Not yet. But I have a plan."</p><p>"What if he snubs you?" Martha asked.</p><p>"He won''t," Rita said confidently. "He’s just reserved."</p><p>"Toh, go get him then," Martha chuckled. "Though, word on the street is that a couple of girls used to crush on him hard before they suddenly dropped him and ran."</p><p>Rita waved a hand dismissively. "Doesn''t matter, Martha. I know what I want."</p><p>Rita launched her full-scale campaign. She tried approaching him after class, but he glided away effortlessly. Undeterred, she began slipping handwritten love letters into his bag. When she finally managed to get his WhatsApp number, her enthusiastic paragraphs were met with ice-cold single-word replies: "Ok," "Alright," and the occasional thumbs-up emoji.</p><p>Then came the gifts. She left him sleek pens, notebooks, silver chains, woven bracelets, and designer beanies.</p><p>Soon, coursemates started whispering, sending anonymous messages taunting her desperation. Rita ignored them all. She was goal-oriented, and Louis was the target.</p><p>Her persistence paid off on a hot Thursday afternoon. A text popped up on her screen:</p><p>"Passageway behind Faculty of Engineering. 3:00 PM.”</p><p>Rita’s heart leaped. The moment had finally arrived. She practically flew to the venue.</p><p>When she reached the quiet passageway, Louis was waiting, leaning casually against the concrete pillar.</p><p>"Thank you for meeting me," Rita gushed, her heart doing backflips. </p><p>"I hope you''ve been getting my messages and gifts. I really just want us to vibe, you know? You''re always so isolated."</p><p>Before she could finish her sentence, Louis stepped forward and pulled her into a tight, sudden embrace. Rita froze, her cheeks burning as their hearts beat in sync.</p><p>Then the first sign hit her. A sharp, sour odor wafted from his armpits, cutting through the warm afternoon air. It’s just sweat, Rita reasoned quickly, trying to stay calm. He''s been in lectures all day.</p><p>Without warning, Louis leaned down and pressed his lips to hers.</p><p>What was supposed to be a romantic moment ended instantly. Rita jerked backward, gasping for air as her eyes widened in complete horror.</p><p>As he stood there, completely unbothered by her reaction, he began to speak. His voice was shockingly deep, a low rumble that sounded almost completely inaudible like a faint reverberation coming from deep inside his chest.</p><p>"I see how much effort you put in for me," he mumbled, his deep tones barely reaching her ears. "I think we should be together. By the way, my real name isn''t Louis... it''s Brombromlagwe."</p><p>Rita could barely hear the words, nor did she care to. She was completely paralyzed not by the kiss or his muffled, deep voice, but by the overwhelming wave of stench that erupted with every quiet syllable. </p><p>It tasted metallic, sour, and heavy, like something rotting deep within his gut. Beneath those pristine pink lips, his tongue was coated in a thick white layer of bacteria, and the smell was pure, unadulterated halitosis.</p><p>Before he could take another step toward her, Rita turned on her heel and sprinted back to her hostel.</p><p>The moment she burst into her room, she scrubbed her teeth until her gums bled, used half a bottle of mouthwash, and flossed repeatedly. Still feeling utterly contaminated, she tore open a fresh pack of mints and shoved three into her mouth at once.</p><p>When Martha walked in later that evening, she found Rita sitting on her bunk, aggressively chewing through a mountain of mint wrappers.</p><p>"What''s with all the mints?" Martha asked, raising an eyebrow.</p><p>"My crush has terrible mouth odor. And body odor," Rita gagged, holding her chest. "It’s not normal odor, Martha, it’s coming straight from his internal organs! And he kissed me!"</p><p>Martha stared at her for a second before bursting into hysterical, breathless laughter.</p><p>"Is it not what you wanted?" Martha wheezed, clutching her stomach. "You said you didn''t care!"</p><p>"Gosh, I feel so dirty!" Rita whined, tossing another mint into her mouth. "He even told me his real name is Brombromlagwe! I couldn''t even process it!"</p><p>Martha laughed so hard she nearly fell off her stool.</p><p>Rita sank back against her pillow, staring blankly at the ceiling. "No wonder all those other girls ran away."</p><p>"So..." Martha grinned, wiping a tear from her eye. "Is your love not unconditional anymore?"</p><p>"It was never love o!" Rita cried out. "It was just pure foolishness. Omo, I have moved on!"</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787426129/echo-gist/64a4435f-e4df-4c17-8422-16439c81d14b/fhwb9o5oncwtmqfex6qs.jpg', 'writing', '{"writing","drama","stories","funny"}', '64a4435f-e4df-4c17-8422-16439c81d14b', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 3, 1, 0, '2026-08-22T19:21:40.471Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:44.222Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('974198a9-e227-488e-8e1d-1814ec616b41', 'The Girl They Thought Would Never Rise', 'the-girl-they-thought-would-never-rise--ehc8', 'PART TWO: WHAT SHE BUILT IN SILENCE', '<p>For seconds neither woman moved.<br>Meera stood in the doorway with one hand resting on the glass handle.<br><br>Nora looked at her from behind the desk.<br><br>There was something about seeing a person from your past standing in front of you after so many years. They could look the same and somehow feel like a stranger.<br><br>Meera was the first to speak.<br><br>“You own this company?”<br><br>Nora closed the folder in front of her.<br><br>“Yes.”<br><br>Meera looked around the office as if the answer might be hiding somewhere.<br><br>The large windows overlooked half the city. There were framed designs on the walls shelves filled with packaged products and employees moving quietly outside the glass doors.<br><br>This was not the girl Meera remembered.<br>The girl she remembered had always been in the kitchen. Always carrying something. Always apologizing. Always waiting.<br><br>Meera looked back at her.<br><br>“How?”<br><br>Nora almost smiled.<br><br>It was a question.<br><br>It took her back years.<br><br>How?<br><br>She had asked herself the question many times. How was she supposed to survive? How was she supposed to pay rent?<br> How was she supposed to build anything when she had started with nothing?<br><br>The answer had never been one moment.<br><br>There were hundreds of them. Most of them nobody had seen. After leaving home Nora worked at the diner during the day. Studied at night. The room above the laundromat became her classroom.<br><br>She borrowed books from the library and watched lessons on a secondhand computer whenever she could afford the electricity to charge it.<br><br>She studied business. Marketing. Design. Accounting. Customer service. Anything that might help her build a life. She didn''t have anyone to explain things to her so she taught herself.<br><br>Some nights she was so tired that she would fall asleep with her head on a book. Nights she couldn''t sleep at all. Those were usually the nights when memories of home came back.<br><br>Priya''s birthday. Her fathers silence. Meera''s voice. The school she had been forced to leave.<br><br>The life she might have had if someone had simply decided that she was worth investing in. Sometimes she cried. Quietly.<br><br>She had learned how to cry without making noise.<br><br>Every morning she still got up. The diner needed her. The rent was due. Nobody was coming to save her.<br><br>She had eventually learned that the hardest part of being alone wasn''t having nobody to hold your hand.<br><br>It was having nobody to tell you that you were doing okay. So she learned to tell herself.<br><br>“You''re doing okay Nora.”<br><br>When she wasn''t sure she believed it.<br><br>The first course she paid for was supposed to help her get into the design industry.<br><br>She saved for months.<br><br>Every week she put money aside and refused to touch it. When she finally had enough she enrolled. The course was nothing like she had expected.<br><br>The lessons were rushed. The materials were incomplete. Whenever she asked questions the instructor acted as though she was wasting his time.<br><br>Still Nora kept attending. She needed that certificate. She needed something she could put on a résumé.<br><br>When the course ended she realized she had learned little. When she complained the instructor told her she had misunderstood what she was paying for.<br><br>Nora knew she hadn''t. Knowing she had been cheated didn''t give her the money back.<br><br>She walked home that evening with the envelope containing what remained of her savings pressed tightly inside her bag.<br><br>When she got to her room she emptied the money onto the mattress. Counted it.<br><br>Then she counted it again. She sat there for a time. She could have cried. Instead she pulled out her notebook. On a page she wrote:<br><br>What did this teach me?<br><br>She stared at the question. Eventually she wrote:<br><br><strong>Don''t pay for promises. Pay for proof.</strong><br><br>She closed the notebook. It wasn''t the lesson she wanted. It was one she would never forget.<br><br>A year later Nora found a job helping a small clothing business manage its orders. It wasn''t much. She was grateful.<br><br>The owner told her she would be paid at the end of every week. For the few weeks Nora worked harder than anyone had asked her to.<br><br>She wrote product descriptions. Answered customers. Organized orders. Took photographs. Updated the business page. Sometimes she stayed until midnight because she wanted the business to succeed.<br><br>It did.<br><br>Orders started coming in. Customers returned. The owner seemed pleased. Then payday came. Nothing. Nora waited. The following week came. Still nothing. She called. No answer. She sent messages. No response.<br><br>Eventually she went to the shop. The shutters were down. She called again. This time the owner answered. </p><p></p><p>“I''ll pay you when things improve, " he said. </p><p>Nora stared at the shop.<br><br>“You told me you''d pay me every week.”<br><br>“I know.”<br><br>“. I''ve done all the work.”<br><br>“I said I''ll pay you.”<br><br>The call ended. He never did.<br></p><p>Nora walked home angry enough to cry. The money he owed her wasn''t a fortune to him. To her it was rent. Food. Electricity. A little breathing room.<br><br>That night she sat on the floor beside her mattress and cried. Not because she was weak.<br><br>Because she was tired of having to fight for things other people received without thinking.<br><br>In the morning she went back to work at the diner. She couldn''t afford to stay in bed and be angry. </p><p>Bills didn''t care that she had been cheated. Hunger didn''t care that she was heartbroken.<br><br>So she kept moving. The rejections came too. Nora applied for design jobs at agencies and larger companies. She sent applications until she could predict the rejection emails before opening them. Some companies responded. Most didn''t.<br><br>During one interview a man looked through her portfolio. Asked where she had trained.<br><br>“I taught myself, " Nora said.<br><br>He looked up.<br><br>“Do you have anyone in the industry who can recommend you?”<br><br>“No.”<br><br>“Any connections?”<br><br>Nora shook her head.<br><br>He gave her a smile.<br><br>“We''ll get back to you.”<br><br>They didn''t.<br><br>A week later she found out someone else''s had gotten the position.<br><br>It hurt.<br><br>After a while Nora stopped asking why life wasn''t fair. Fairness wasn''t going to pay her rent. She needed something. She needed a way forward.<br><br>So she started with what she had. A small table. A secondhand computer. A few design tools. The skills she had picked up along the way.<br><br>Her first products were simple. Handmade journals. Gift boxes. Small accessories. Custom packaging.<br><br>Nothing that looked impressive from the outside. She made them in the room above the laundromat. She took photographs beside the window because that was where the light was best.<br><br>Then she posted them online. For two days nothing happened. Nobody ordered. Nobody even asked a question.<br><br>On third day a woman bought a journal. Nora stared at the payment notification. She checked it twice. Then a third time.<br><br>It was real.<br><br>Someone had actually paid for something she had made. She smiled. It wasn''t an amount of money. It felt huge to her.<br><br>She wrapped the journal carefully in paper and tied it with the nicest ribbon she had.<br><br>That month she barely made a profit. After buying materials and paying for deliveries almost everything was gone.<br><br>The second month was better.<br><br>The third month was better, than the past two months. Then someone asked her to make gift boxes for an office event. Nora agreed before she had figured out how she was going to make them.<br><br>She spent most of the night working. The order wasn''t perfect. One box was the size.Several labels had to be redone.<br><br>The customer loved them. They recommended her to someone else. That was how it started. One customer. Then another. Then another.<br><br>Nora learned as she went. She learned that making something beautiful wasn''t enough. She had to know how much it cost.<br><br>She had to know how much to charge. She had to keep records. She had to deal with suppliers. She had to answer customers without losing her temper.<br><br>She made mistakes. A few of them cost her money. One time she sent an order to the wrong address.<br><br>Another time a supplier delivered the materials and Nora had to replace everything herself.<br><br>There were weeks when she wondered whether she was making progress at all. She kept going. The business slowly grew.<br><br>First it was Nora.<br><br>Then she hired one person to help with packaging. Later, another person joined.<br><br>She moved from the room above the laundromat into a workspace behind a printing shop.<br><br>It wasn''t beautiful. The walls needed painting. The roof leaked whenever it rained.<br><br>The first time Nora unlocked the door in the morning and realized the place was hers she stood there smiling.<br><br>She had a business.<br><br>A real one.<br><br>She registered the company. Spent an entire evening trying to decide what to call it.<br>Eventually, she chose one</p><p><strong>Still Rising.</strong><br><br>When someone asked why she simply said, "It reminds me to keep going.”<br><br>She didn''t tell them about the bus station. She didn''t tell them about the notebook. She didn''t tell them about the girl who had once believed she would never become anything.<br><br>That part of her life belonged to her.<br><br>Rising grew slowly. Then it grew quickly. People began recommending Nora''s work. A few businesses started placing orders.<br><br>Someone wrote about her products online. Another customer shared her work. Nora hired people. For the time she had employees depending on her. It scared her.<br><br>She remembered what it felt like to depend on people who didn''t care whether she succeeded. She promised herself she would never make her employees feel that way.<br><br>Then came Daniel. He joined the company when it was still small. He was good with clients. Understood the business. Importantly he believed in Nora.<br><br>At least she thought he did.<br><br>He had seen the company when it had a handful of orders and a lot of uncertainty. He had seen Nora working late. He had seen her struggle.<br><br>So when Daniel told her "One day people are going to know your name " she believed him. She trusted him. That trust would cost her.<br><br>The biggest opportunity Still Rising had ever received came from a luxury hotel group.<br><br>They wanted a company to handle custom gift packages and branded products for several of their properties.<br><br>It was the kind of contract that could change everything. Nora spent weeks preparing. She worked on designs until two in the morning. She revised proposals. She made samples. She attended meetings.<br><br>Daniel helped manage the account.<br><br>When the client finally said they wanted to move Nora sat in her office after the meeting and stared at the email.<br><br>She read it again. Then she laughed. Not loudly. Just a small disbelieving laugh. They had made it. Not completely.<br><br>Enough to know that they were going somewhere. A day later Daniel stopped answering his phone. Then the client stopped responding.<br><br>Nora became worried. She sent emails. Nothing. She called. Nothing. Then one morning she saw a photograph online.<br><br>Her stomach dropped. The same designs. The same presentation. The same concept she and her team had spent months creating.<br><br>But the company listed underneath wasn''t Still Rising. It was Daniels'' company. Nora sat still. She called him. This time he answered.<br><br>“How could you do this?”<br><br>There was a pause.<br><br>Then Daniel said, "It''s business, Nora.”<br><br>“You took my work.”<br><br>“I improved it.”<br><br>“You took my client.”<br><br>“You weren''t moving enough.”<br><br>Nora could barely speak.<br><br>“You knew how much this meant to me.”<br><br>“I knew how much it meant to the company.”<br><br>The call ended.<br><br>Nora put the phone down. For minutes she just stared at the wall. Then the cancellations started. Two clients left. Then another.<br><br>A few employees followed Daniel. The money began disappearing when it came in.<br><br>For the time since she had left home Nora wondered if she had finally reached the end of what she could survive.<br><br>That evening she stayed in the office long after everyone had gone. The building was quiet. The machines were off. The lights above her desk buzzed softly.<br><br>Nora opened the company''s account. The numbers were frightening. She opened her email. More cancellations. Then she opened her notebook. The old one.<br><br>The one she had carried from home. The cover was bent now. Some pages were stained. She turned through it until she found the sentence she had written years ago.<br><br><strong>This is not where my story ends.</strong><br><br>Nora stared at it.<br><br>She remembered the bus station. The cold bench. The flickering light. The bag against her chest.<br><br>She remembered being eighteen and having no idea what would happen next. That girl had been scared too. She had still walked into the diner.<br><br>She had still taken the job.<br><br>She had still bought the notebook.<br><br>She had still tried.<br><br>Nora wiped her face.<br><br>“You didn''t come this far to stop now " she whispered.<br><br>In the morning she called her lawyer. Then she called the people who had stayed. They rebuilt. It wasn''t quick. It wasn''t easy.<br><br>Slowly the company began recovering. Nora became more careful. She stopped trusting people simply because they said the things. She learned to put agreements in writing. She learned to protect her work.<br><br>Importantly she stopped being afraid of starting again. Then, months later, something happened that Nora wasn''t prepared for.<br><br>A major lifestyle company discovered, Still Rising.<br><br>They had seen one of her products at an event. They wanted to meet. Nora thought it was a mistake at first. She checked the email three times.<br><br>It wasn''t.<br><br>They wanted to discuss a partnership. For the time in years Nora allowed herself to hope without immediately telling herself to be careful.<br><br>The meeting went well.<br><br>Very well.<br><br>A week later the partnership was confirmed. Orders came in faster than Nora could process them. People who had never heard of Still Rising were suddenly talking about it.<br><br>Her team celebrated.<br><br>Nora stood quietly in the middle of the office. Watched everything happening around her. She thought about the girl above the laundromat. The girl who had sold one notebook. The girl who had once counted coins before buying food.<br><br>She wished she could go back and tell her:<br><br>Keep going.<br><br>That night Nora stayed late. She was about to leave when her phone rang. An unfamiliar number. She almost ignored it. Instead she answered.<br><br>“Hello?”<br><br>There was silence.<br><br>Then a mans voice came through.<br><br>“Nora?”<br><br>Her heart stopped. She knew that voice. She hadn''t heard it in years. Her fathers voice. Nora sat slowly in her chair.<br><br>“Where did you get this number?" she asked.<br><br>“I saw your name in the newspaper.”<br><br>She said nothing.<br><br>“I saw what you''ve built.”<br><br>Still Nora said nothing.<br><br>Her father cleared his throat.<br><br>“I wanted to call.”<br><br>Nora looked around her office.<br><br>At the company name on the glass wall.<br><br><strong>STILL RISING.<br></strong><br>At the people who now depended on the thing she had built. At the life she had created without him.<br><br>“I don''t know what to say " her father admitted.<br><br>Nora looked down at her mothers ring hanging around her neck. There had been a time when she would have given anything to hear those words from him.<br><br>A time when she would have waited by the phone. A time when one call from her father could have made her feel chosen.<br><br>That girl was gone.<br><br>“I just wanted to know how you are " he said.<br><br>Nora took a breath.<br><br>“I’m fine.”<br><br>Another silence.<br><br>Then he asked the question she had never expected to hear.<br><br>“Can we meet?”<br><br>Nora looked toward the window.<br><br>The city lights stretched across the darkness. For years she had imagined what it would feel like to stand in front of her father after becoming successful.<br><br>She had imagined proving him wrong. She had imagined him regretting everything. Now the moment had finally come.<br><br>Strangely she wasn''t sure she wanted it anymore.<br><br>“I''ll think about it, " she said.<br><br>Her father exhaled.<br><br>“I understand.”<br><br>The call ended.<br><br>Nora remained in her chair. She didn''t cry. She didn''t smile. She simply sat there staring at the phone. Because for the time she realized something.<br><br>Her father had finally come looking for her. She wasn''t the girl who had been waiting anymore.<br><br>Nora had no idea what would happen when she finally opened that door.<br></p><p></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789418922/echo-gist/26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2/t0umbxmz1psg9dakzvhf.jpg', 'books', '{"thegirltheythoughtwouldneverrise","whatshebuiltinsilence","emotionalstory","nevergiveup"}', '26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2', TRUE, FALSE, 12, 6, 1, 0, '2026-09-14T20:51:26.256Z', '2026-09-28T18:13:07.974Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('a4f7c8b1-9cd6-44b0-8cea-c0cfc2b87840', 'Breaking news!!!
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('0720d38e-d24e-4f81-8ec7-8ec4d8b68676', 'There''s no limit to how far you can grow', 'theres-no-limit-to-how-far-you-can-grow-qpp1', 'Whether you''re building a career in tech, growing a business, working toward a degree, or simply thinking of ways to better your future, you''re on a good path, …', '<p></p><hr><p>Whether you''re building a career in tech, growing a business, working toward a degree, or simply thinking of ways to better your future, you''re on a good path, and you''re doing great. It can get overwhelming sometimes, we know, but the process is exactly what shapes who you become. This week, Ecosgist is encouraging everyone to stretch their limits, because your only limitation is you. We believe in your dreams, and we believe they''re achievable, especially when they''re spoken into words and shared with people who see them too. We''re glad you''re part of this community, and we can''t wait to see what we''ll build together. Here''s to a great week ahead. Stay safe, and follow Echogist  for all the trending news on tech, entertainment, jobs, and so much more.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1786955113/echo-gist/40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1/drhbbxobhuv45pawgp9t.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 1, 10, 0, 0, '2026-08-17T08:25:58.837Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:56.276Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('c89a27be-56a8-4523-87ac-0532bea3874d', 'The Gift of Loving and Being Loved', 'the-gift-of-loving-and-being-loved-md7y', 'Not all love is romantic, and some of the most important love never is.', '<p></p><p>Romantic love is beautiful.</p><p>It’s loud.</p><p>It’s intense.</p><p>It’s the one everyone talks about like it’s the main event.</p><p></p><p>And somehow, every other kind of love becomes background noise.</p><p></p><p>But I don’t think it should be that way.</p><p></p><p>Because the friend you call when everything feels off…</p><p>the one who somehow makes things lighter without even trying…</p><p></p><p>the one who listens and makes you feel seen, not judged…</p><p></p><p>or the one that steps in and makes sense of the chaos when your head is all over the place…</p><p></p><p>That kind of love?</p><p></p><p>It’s powerful.</p><p></p><p>The collection of people like that in your life</p><p>honestly beats a love that is only fueled by desire…</p><p>or what we now casually call love but is really just lust.</p><p></p><p>And sometimes, I feel a slight pang of pity.</p><p></p><p>When someone says, “I’m in a relationship,”</p><p>and then slowly disappears into it.</p><p></p><p>Everything becomes about one person.</p><p></p><p>One source of warmth.</p><p>One source of validation.</p><p>One source of companionship.</p><p></p><p>It sounds sweet at first…</p><p></p><p>but it’s almost like the blueprint for suffocation.</p><p></p><p>My friends would probably hear this and come for me immediately,</p><p>saying this is coming from someone who has been single since the break of dawn 🤣</p><p></p><p>But I promise you, it’s not that.</p><p></p><p>I’m not against having your person.</p><p></p><p>In fact, I hope everyone experiences that kind of connection.</p><p></p><p>The one you think about when you close your eyes.</p><p>The one that somehow sits behind your heartbeat 🤣</p><p></p><p>That kind of love is real.</p><p></p><p>But so is this one.</p><p></p><p>The quiet, consistent, everyday love</p><p>that comes from your people.</p><p></p><p>The ones you call over the smallest discomfort</p><p>and it doesn’t feel weird.</p><p></p><p>It feels normal.</p><p></p><p>The ones you share deep, personal things with</p><p>without second-guessing yourself.</p><p></p><p>And yes… the <em>money bags</em> too 🤣</p><p>The ones who show up when life is squeezing you,</p><p>who make sure you’re good in ways that go beyond words.</p><p>Even the ones that call you out of nowhere</p><p>and somehow make you feel like the most important person on earth in that moment.</p><p></p><p>That is love too.</p><p></p><p>And it’s something we should invest in.</p><p></p><p>Because at the end of the day,</p><p>no one person should have to carry the weight</p><p>of being everything to you.</p><p></p><p>It’s not fair to them.</p><p>And it’s not healthy for you.</p><p></p><p>So yes, fall in love.</p><p></p><p>Have your person.</p><p></p><p>But don’t neglect the people</p><p>who have been loving you in ways</p><p>that didn’t need romance to feel real.</p><p></p><p>I probably don’t say this enough…</p><p></p><p>especially since it’s spread across both genders</p><p>and guys like to act weird about these things 🤣</p><p></p><p>But I genuinely love you all.</p><p></p><p>And I feel privileged</p><p>to have access to that kind of love.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787510995/echo-gist/82b4b527-06d6-42d5-8cba-cb7e09cda146/cypewp1z3t3sczxm4fot.jpg', 'lifestyle', '{"community","friendship","loveandrelationships"}', '82b4b527-06d6-42d5-8cba-cb7e09cda146', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 21, 3, 0, '2026-08-23T18:52:38.727Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:41.469Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('ecd90726-f87c-4404-9dff-72533cdff1ee', 'Rock, Paper, Scissors', 'rock-paper-scissors-jti5', 'Sometimes, life doesn''t give you the option to choose the perfect move.', '<p>Rock.</p><p>Paper.</p><p>Scissors.</p><p>Three simple choices.</p><p>You pick one, hoping it beats whatever the other person picked.</p><p>Sometimes you win.</p><p>Sometimes you lose.</p><p>And sometimes, you both pick the same thing and have to start again.</p><p>Funny how life works almost the same way.</p><p>There are days when you feel like you''ve figured everything out. You have a plan. You know what you''re doing. You make a decision, convinced it is the right one.</p><p>Then life throws something completely different at you.</p><p>And suddenly, your carefully planned rock meets someone else''s paper.</p><p>You lose.</p><p>Not because you were foolish.</p><p>Not because you didn''t try hard enough.</p><p>You simply made a move that didn''t work this time.</p><p>And that''s one thing nobody really prepares us for: sometimes, the right decision can still produce the wrong outcome.</p><p>You can be hardworking and still not get the opportunity.</p><p>You can love someone genuinely and still lose them.</p><p>You can prepare for something for months and still fail.</p><p>You can do everything you know to do and still find yourself staring at a result you didn''t expect.</p><p>It doesn''t always mean you chose badly.</p><p>Sometimes, it just means life played scissors when you brought paper.</p><p>And perhaps that''s why the game doesn''t end after one round.</p><p>You don''t throw your paper on the table, see that you''ve lost, and decide you''ll never play again.</p><p>You simply laugh, reset your hand, and try again.</p><p>Maybe that''s the part we forget.</p><p>Losing one round isn''t the same as losing the game.</p><p>So, if today feels like one of those days where everything you chose seems to have worked against you, take a breath.</p><p>Reset.</p><p>Pick again.</p><p>Rock.</p><p>Paper.</p><p>Scissors.</p><p>Whatever comes next, don''t let one bad round convince you that you were never meant to win.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787494271/echo-gist/ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731/qr5qi98i4goj3wmeldjf.png', 'lifestyle', '{}', 'ca2f991e-546d-4a4c-947e-d4cc42e71731', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 10, 3, 0, '2026-08-23T14:13:46.643Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:41.887Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('685fd458-f737-4981-87fb-9e93c67b65a5', 'The Internet Made Everyone a Creator, Now Everyone Is Tired', 'the-internet-made-everyone-a-creator-now-everyone-is-tired-lllz', 'Nobody warned us that being online would eventually feel like a job.You wake up, check your phone, and before you’ve even brushed your teeth, you’re already con…', '<p></p><p>Nobody warned us that being online would eventually feel like a job.</p><p>You wake up, check your phone, and before you’ve even brushed your teeth, you’re already consuming somebody else’s life.</p><p>Then you remember you’re supposed to post too.</p><p>Your business needs content.  </p><p>Your personal page needs content.  </p><p>Your brand needs content.  </p><p>And apparently, even your breakfast needs to be content.</p><p>So you take the picture.</p><p>You record the video.</p><p>You write the caption.</p><p>You post.</p><p>Then you wait.</p><p>Why only 47 views?</p><p>Maybe I should delete it.</p><p>Maybe the picture isn’t good enough.</p><p>Maybe I should have used a trending sound.</p><p>Maybe I’m not posting enough.</p><p>And suddenly, something that was supposed to be fun starts feeling like work.</p><p>There was a time when being online was just… being online.</p><p>You opened Instagram to see what your friends were doing. You watched a few videos. Maybe you posted a picture once in a while.</p><p>Nobody expected you to have a content strategy.</p><p>Now?</p><p>Everyone is a creator.</p><p>You’re expected to have a personal brand. A niche. A content calendar. Good lighting. Good pictures. Good captions. And somehow, you’re supposed to “show up consistently.”</p><p>Even when you’re tired.</p><p>Even when nothing interesting happened that day.</p><p>Even when you’re just trying to survive.</p><p>The internet turned ordinary people into brands.</p><p>Your hobby can become content. Your morning routine can become content. Your business can become content. Your relationship can become content.</p><p>Sometimes, even your struggles become content.</p><p>And at first, it felt exciting.</p><p>Everyone suddenly had a voice.</p><p>You didn’t need a television station, a publishing company or a huge audience to tell your story. You could simply pick up your phone and speak.</p><p>But somewhere along the way, creating stopped feeling like creating.</p><p>It started feeling like a job.</p><p>We have become so obsessed with being visible that we rarely give ourselves permission to disappear for a while.</p><p>If you haven’t posted in three days, you start feeling guilty.</p><p>If your business page is quiet, you worry that customers will forget you.</p><p>If you don’t post your achievements, it almost feels like they didn’t happen.</p><p>We’re constantly documenting our lives while barely having time to live them.</p><p>And perhaps the strangest part is that the internet never actually said everyone had to become a creator.</p><p>We just started believing it did.</p><p>You don’t have to turn every experience into a post.</p><p>You don’t have to build a personal brand around every part of your life.</p><p>You don’t have to be interesting every single day.</p><p>Sometimes, you can just exist.</p><p>No content.</p><p>No strategy.</p><p>No perfectly edited video.</p><p>Just you, living your life without wondering how it would perform on the internet.</p><p>Because maybe the next big thing isn’t creating more.</p><p>Maybe it’s learning when to put the phone down.</p><p>If you made it to the end, thank you for reading. ❤️</p><p>Kindly share this with someone who might enjoy reading it too.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788909497/echo-gist/43014ec1-637a-4b51-9800-065d611de765/eamzcopo2xmlwmyaxn6r.png', 'technology', '{}', '43014ec1-637a-4b51-9800-065d611de765', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 22, 4, 4, '2026-09-08T23:18:25.843Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:19.362Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "posts" ("id", "title", "slug", "excerpt", "content", "cover_image", "category", "tags", "author_id", "published", "featured", "read_time", "view_count", "like_count", "comment_count", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES ('4f6fc8cf-f1cd-4458-94c1-dea2c1995a59', '🇳🇬 October 1, 1960: The Day Nigeria Became Her Own', '-october-1-1960-the-day-nigeria-became-her-own-nkdb', 'Before the coups, before Biafra, there was October 1, 1960.', '<h3>Imagine waking up in Nigeria in 1960.</h3><p>The country you knew had spent decades under British colonial rule. Decisions about government, administration and the country’s future had largely been shaped within a colonial system.</p><p>Then came <strong>October 1, 1960</strong>.</p><p>Nigeria became an independent nation.</p><p>But independence was not simply about lowering one flag and raising another. It was the beginning of a completely new chapter—and one that would eventually lead Nigeria through political crises, military coups and, only seven years later, a devastating civil war.</p><p>So, <strong>how did Nigeria become independent, and what was the country like on that historic day?</strong></p><p><strong>Before Independence</strong></p><p>Before 1960, Nigeria was under British colonial rule.</p><p>The territory had been brought together under British administration over time, and by the mid-20th century, Nigerian political movements were increasingly demanding greater self-government.</p><p>Nigerian politicians and nationalists pushed for greater representation and eventually complete independence.</p><p>Among the prominent political figures of the period were <strong>Nnamdi Azikiwe, Obafemi Awolowo and Abubakar Tafawa Balewa</strong>, who represented different political movements and regions of the country.</p><p>Nigeria’s political system had also developed around three major regions: the <strong>Northern Region, Western Region and Eastern Region</strong>. Each had considerable autonomy, while the federal government at the centre had more limited powers than it would have in later decades.</p><p>The road to independence was therefore not simply a single event. It was the result of years of political negotiations, constitutional changes and growing demands for self-government.</p><p><strong>🇳🇬 October 1, 1960</strong></p><p>Then came the historic day.</p><p>On <strong>October 1, 1960, Nigeria gained independence from Britain</strong>. The United Nations records Nigeria as having attained independence that day, while the Nigerian government formally applied for UN membership on the same date.</p><p>The country’s new government operated under a parliamentary system.</p><p><strong>Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa became Nigeria’s Prime Minister</strong>, while <strong>Queen Elizabeth II remained the country’s head of state</strong>, represented in Nigeria by a Governor-General.</p><p>Dr. <strong>Nnamdi Azikiwe</strong> would later become Governor-General in November 1960.</p><p>So although Nigeria was now independent, the country initially remained within the Commonwealth and retained the British monarch as its constitutional head of state.</p><p>This is an important detail that is sometimes forgotten when we talk about independence.</p><p>Nigeria was <strong>sovereign</strong>, but it did not immediately become a republic.</p><p>That happened three years later.</p><p><strong>The Man Who Spoke for the New Nigeria</strong></p><p>At the centre of the independence celebrations was Nigeria’s Prime Minister, <strong>Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa</strong>.</p><p>Balewa delivered the country’s independence speech on October 1, 1960. The National Library of Nigeria preserves a photograph of him delivering that historic speech.</p><p>His words reflected the hopes of a newly independent country.</p><p>Nigeria was entering the international community as a sovereign state, and its leaders wanted the country to establish friendly relationships with other nations while contributing to the development of Africa.</p><p>Just six days later, on <strong>October 7, 1960</strong>, Balewa addressed the United Nations General Assembly in New York as Nigeria’s Prime Minister.</p><p>Nigeria was admitted as the <strong>99th member of the United Nations</strong> that same day.</p><p>In his speech, Balewa emphasized Nigeria’s desire for peaceful relations with other countries and its intention to work with other African nations.</p><p>It was a significant moment.</p><p>Nigeria was no longer speaking to the world as a British colony.</p><p>It was speaking as <strong>Nigeria</strong>.</p><p><strong>What Changed After Independence?</strong></p><p>Independence meant that Nigerians could now take control of their own government.</p><p>The new constitution established a federal system with elected institutions, including a Prime Minister and Parliament. The three major regions retained substantial powers of their own.</p><p>But independence did not magically solve Nigeria’s problems.</p><p>The country was incredibly diverse, with different ethnic groups, languages, religions, political interests and regional identities.</p><p>The challenge was now clear:</p><p><strong>Could all these different groups successfully build one united nation?</strong></p><p>For a while, Nigeria continued under civilian democratic rule.</p><p>But political tensions increased over the following years.</p><p>In 1963, Nigeria took another major constitutional step.</p><p>The country became a <strong>republic</strong>, ending the British monarch’s role as head of state. <strong>Nnamdi Azikiwe became Nigeria’s first President.</strong></p><p>Nigeria had moved from colony, to independent federation, to republic in just three years.</p><p><strong>But Independence Was Only the Beginning</strong></p><p>Looking back today, October 1, 1960 can feel like the beginning of modern Nigeria.</p><p>And in many ways, it was.</p><p>But the optimism of independence would eventually collide with serious political and ethnic tensions.</p><p>In <strong>January 1966</strong>, Nigeria experienced its first military coup.</p><p>The country’s political crisis deepened.</p><p>In 1967, the Eastern Region declared itself the independent Republic of <strong>Biafra</strong>, leading to the Nigerian Civil War, which lasted until 1970.</p><p>Thousands of lives were lost, and millions of Nigerians were affected by the conflict.</p><p>So when we celebrate October 1, we are not simply celebrating a date.</p><p>We are remembering the beginning of a journey that has included <strong>hope, political struggles, military rule, war, democracy, setbacks and remarkable resilience</strong>.</p><p><strong>🇳🇬 The Meaning of October 1</strong></p><p>On October 1, 1960, Nigeria became independent from Britain.</p><p>The British colonial era had ended, and Nigerians took control of their own national government.</p><p>The country was still young.</p><p>Its leaders had enormous responsibilities ahead of them.</p><p>And the people had enormous expectations.</p><p>They had inherited a country with enormous cultural diversity, natural resources and human potential.</p><p>The question was whether Nigeria could transform that potential into a peaceful and prosperous nation.</p><p>More than six decades later, that question is still part of Nigeria’s story.</p><p>Because <strong>October 1, 1960 wasn’t the end of Nigeria’s history.</strong></p><p></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788220494/echo-gist/832b7482-a3ed-4fe1-b023-27c200c96d62/kj4roi2rwczjm7vhepmj.png', 'technology', '{}', '832b7482-a3ed-4fe1-b023-27c200c96d62', FALSE, FALSE, 4, 32, 0, 0, '2026-09-01T00:07:51.670Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:59.680Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
