WHEN EVERYTHING FELL APART', '-part-four-when-everything-fell-apart--rjlz', 'After the funeral, everything she knew began to disappear.', '<p><em>After her father’s burial, she thought the hardest part was over. She was wrong.</em></p><p>When her father died, the family didn’t realize just how many lives he had touched until the day of his burial.</p><p>People came from different places to mourn him. Among them were schoolchildren—children her father had helped with transportation money on their way to school. Some of them cried so deeply that it shocked the family.</p><p>They were not his biological children.</p><p>But he had made a difference in their lives.</p><p>That was when she began to understand how respected and well-known her father had truly been.</p><p>By the grace of God, the family did everything they could to give him a proper burial. But the burial itself came with another burden.</p><p>They had already spent a lot of money during his illness. Properties had been sold. Loans had been taken. Debts had accumulated.</p><p>After he was buried, the family had to face those debts.</p><p>And that was when everything began to fall apart.</p><p>While her father was alive, his presence had commanded a certain level of respect. People were careful about how they treated the family.</p><p>After his death, that changed.</p><p>People began to disrespect them.</p><p>People started gossiping.</p><p>Some began taking advantage of the family.</p><p>Things people would never have dared to do while her father was alive suddenly became normal.</p><p>Her father’s mother also began experiencing difficulties. When she became sick, the family took her to the hospital and spent money on her treatment.</p><p>But even outside the hospital, she was no longer treated with the same respect.</p><p>Village meetings became difficult.</p><p>There were times she would go for meetings and people would lock her in.</p><p>There was gossip, humiliation and accusation.</p><p>It was as if everyone knew that the man who had once stood between his family and the world was gone.</p><p>And without him, they believed the family could be pushed around.</p><p>Meanwhile, her father’s third wife was left trying to manage the shop and take care of the younger children.</p><p>Her brothers were also trying to survive.</p><p>The family had lost the person who had held so many things together.</p><p>Thankfully, her father had left one important thing behind—the borehole.</p><p>He had opened it so water could be pumped and sold, and it became one of the things helping the family survive.</p><p>But even that became difficult to maintain after his death.</p><p>There was no longer anyone to handle things the way he used to.</p><p>And while all of this was happening, <strong>she decided to leave home and look for a way to survive.</strong></p><p>She thought she was simply going out to hustle.</p><p>She didn’t know she was stepping into another chapter of suffering.</p><p>After her father’s burial, a friend approached her.</p><p>The friend told her about a place where there was money and assured her that she would be fine.</p><p>She trusted her.</p><p>She followed her.</p><p>She had no idea that decision would become the beginning of another painful chapter of her life.</p><p>She began doing whatever work she could find.</p><p>She sold akara.</p><p>She sold pure water.</p><p>She hawked.</p><p>She did anything she could to survive.</p><p>Eventually, the friend who had taken her there disappeared.</p><p>She was left alone.</p><p>And once again, she had to figure out how to survive by herself.</p><p>She worked as a waitress.</p><p>She worked as a farmer .</p><p>She worked as a cleaner.</p><p>She worked in cafés and canteens.</p><p>She took whatever honest work came her way.</p><p>Little by little, she began saving money.</p><p>Eventually, she was able to rent a place of her own.</p><p>For the first time in a long time, she felt that she was beginning to build a life for herself.</p><p>But life had not finished testing her.</p><p>She had another friend staying with her.</p><p>She trusted this friend enough to accommodate her.</p><p>Then she had to return to the village for the prayers and ceremonies connected to her father’s death.</p><p>While she was away, the friend disappeared with her belongings.</p><p>When she came back, she was left with almost nothing.</p><p>Once again, she had to start from the beginning.</p><p>Another loss.</p><p>Another betrayal.</p><p>Another chapter of survival.</p><p>But she kept going.</p><p>Because by then, she had learned something life had been trying to teach her since her father’s death:</p><p><strong>Sometimes, the person you expect to save you will be the person who leaves you behind.</strong></p><p>And sometimes, the only person left to save you is yourself.</p><p>There were still many things she had not told anyone.</p><p>There were things that happened while she was working and struggling to survive—things that would eventually become some of the darkest memories of her life.</p><p>But those memories would come later.</p><p>For now, she was still trying to survive.</p><p><strong>And her story was far from over.</strong></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1790141754/echo-gist/832b7482-a3ed-4fe1-b023-27c200c96d62/as3pplkzusjqwrv4ecke.jpg', 'lifestyle', '{"echo-gistcom","evho-gistcom"}', '832b7482-a3ed-4fe1-b023-27c200c96d62', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 10, 0, 0, '2026-09-23T05:43:44.311Z', '2026-10-01T12:36:27.682Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

I was wrong.

The worst thing was believing that, after losing her, I had lost my father too.

I was only ten when my mother''s blood stained the floor beneath me.

I remember the cupboard.

I remember the darkness.

And I remember the sound of gunshots.

Bang!

Bang!

Bang!

Each shot tore through the room, followed by my mother''s cry. I pressed both hands over my mouth, terrified that even the slightest sound would give away my hiding place.

I could see her through the narrow opening between the cupboard doors.

She staggered backwards.

Another gunshot rang through the house.

My mother clutched her chest, blood spreading across her clothes. Her knees weakened, and she slowly sank to the floor.

Then her eyes found mine.

For one terrible second, everything became still.

She knew where I was.

Her lips parted, but no words came out.

Tears slipped down her cheeks as she stared at me, and somehow, even with death taking the strength from her body, I knew what she was telling me.

Stay hidden.

Don''t come out.

I bit down on my hand to stop myself from screaming.

My mother''s eyes slowly closed.

She was gone.

But the men did not leave.

They searched the entire house, throwing things aside and opening every door. Their footsteps grew closer to the cupboard, and I could barely breathe.

One of them stopped.

My heart nearly stopped with him.

He opened another cabinet.

Then another.

But he never opened mine.

When they found nothing, they poured something across the floor.

A moment later, flames rose.

The house was burning.

Smoke filled the cupboard, and my lungs began to tighten. I could no longer feel my fingers. My body trembled uncontrollably as the heat grew stronger.

I was going to die there.

Just like my mother.

Then, through the smoke, I heard someone shouting my name.

"Julian!"

My father''s voice.

The cupboard door was ripped open.

My father pulled me into his arms and carried me out of the burning house.

I remember looking over his shoulder.

I remember seeing my mother''s body disappear behind the flames.

Then everything went black.

When I opened my eyes again, I was in my grandmother''s house.

There was no hospital.

My grandmother treated me herself.

And when I was strong enough to ask for my father, he was already gone.

He never came back.

Not once.

Flowers arrived.

Gifts arrived.

Letters sometimes arrived.

But my father never did.

As the years passed, I began to wonder whether he blamed me.

Maybe he thought I should have protected my mother.

Maybe he thought I had failed her.

Maybe he simply didn''t want me anymore.

"Grandma," I cried whenever the loneliness became too much. "I want to see my dad."

She would hold me tightly, but she never gave me the answer I wanted.

Instead, she trained me.

I learnt how to fight before I fully understood why I needed to.

I learnt how to move quickly, how to defend myself and how to use a gun.

My father had assigned a kung-fu master to train me.

Another man taught me how to handle firearms.

I was being raised like a warrior while everyone around me pretended it was normal.

Even my identity was kept hidden.

At ten years old, I did not understand any of it.

I only understood that my mother was dead.

And my father was gone.

For years, I hated him for it.

Then, on my eighteenth birthday, everything changed.

A package arrived from my father.

My heart jumped when I saw his name.

For the first time in years, I thought he was finally coming for me.

Instead, there was an envelope.

Inside it was a flash drive and a handwritten note.

My father''s handwriting.

I stared at it for a long time.

He had promised that one day he would explain everything.

Why my mother died.

Why he had stayed away from me.

Why he had sent me to my grandmother.

Why he could only love me from a distance.

But I never opened the note.

I was angry.

If my father had something to say to me, he should say it to my face.

I didn''t want an explanation written on a piece of paper.

I wanted him.

So I left.

I went looking for my father.

I didn''t know that the moment I stepped out of my grandmother''s house, someone was already watching me.

The news reached my father before I could get very far.

His daughter was gone.

He immediately began searching for me.

But by the time I reached the train station, I had a much bigger problem.

They found me first.

The men stepped out from different directions.

Black clothes.

Covered faces.

Guns in their hands.

My blood ran cold.

I knew those masks.

I had seen them once before.

Ten years ago.

The night my mother died.

One of them raised his gun.

I moved before he could pull the trigger.

My body reacted almost instinctively.

I ducked.

The bullet flew past my head.

I kicked the gun from his hand and drove my elbow into his chest.

Another man rushed towards me.

I turned.

Blocked his attack.

Then struck him hard enough to send him crashing to the ground.

Another came.

Then another.

There were too many of them.

But I had spent years preparing for this moment without even knowing it.

One by one, they fell.

By the time I heard hurried footsteps behind me, most of the men were already on the ground.

I turned.

My father stood several feet away.

He stared at me.

For the first time in my life, I saw something I had never seen in his eyes before.

Pride.

A small smile appeared on his face.

He walked towards me.

I wanted to remain angry.

I had spent years telling myself that I hated him.

But the moment he opened his arms, all those years of anger suddenly felt fragile.

I had missed him.

More than I wanted to admit.

I stepped into his arms.

He held me tightly.

And for the first time since my mother''s death, I felt like I had my father back.

Then I heard it.

Bang!

My father''s body jerked in my arms.

His weight suddenly collapsed against me.

"Dad?"

I caught him before he hit the ground.

Blood spread across his back.

My eyes widened.

A sniper.

The security officers at the station immediately sprang into action. Some ran towards the direction of the shot while others surrounded us.

My father gripped my arm.

"Run," he whispered.

"What?"

"Run, Julian."

"No!"

"Leave no trace."

I shook my head violently.

"No, Dad. You need a hospital."

"They''ll find you."

"Who?"

His breathing became weaker.

"They''re all..." He coughed, blood staining his lips. "They''re all in..."

"In what?"

His eyes struggled to remain open.

"The letter."

My heart stopped.

"The letter?"

He nodded faintly.

"They''re... in the letter."

"Dad, please. Save your strength. You can tell me everything later."

His fingers tightened around mine.

"I love you, sweetheart."

Tears filled my eyes.

"Don''t say that. You''re going to be fine."

"Don''t... try saving me."

"There is no way I''m letting you die."

I screamed towards the security officers.

"Call an ambulance!"

For a few seconds, everything became a blur.

Then the sound of the ambulance siren pierced through the station.

"Please!" I shouted as the paramedics rushed towards us. "Over here! My father has been shot!"

They placed him on a stretcher.

I followed them, still holding his bloodstained hand.

But as they lifted him into the ambulance, his eyes suddenly opened.

He looked directly at me.

There was fear in his eyes.

Not for himself.

For me.

"Julian..."

"I''m here."

His lips moved, but the words were barely audible.

"Whatever you do..."

He swallowed painfully.

"Don''t let them get you."

The ambulance doors slammed shut.

And as the siren began to fade into the distance, I finally understood one thing.

My father hadn''t stayed away from me because he hated me.

He had been running from someone.

Someone who had been hunting my family for ten years.

And whatever was inside that letter...

was the reason they had finally found me.', '<p>Julian thought her father abandoned her after her mother''s murder. But when she finally goes looking for him, the same masked men who killed her mother come after her. After a sniper shoots her father, Julian is forced to uncover the dangerous secret hidden in the letter she refused to read.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789057755/echo-gist/32ac00d9-97a3-48f3-9d37-d6e2b5977d82/wkbtznjzyoqpags6ewfd.png', 'lifestyle', '{"shortstory","technology","fantasy","familysecret","fiction"}', '32ac00d9-97a3-48f3-9d37-d6e2b5977d82', TRUE, FALSE, 1, 11, 2, 0, '2026-09-10T16:31:29.103Z', '2026-10-02T07:05:46.600Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
<p>In high-availability web services, locking tables during database migrations causes user-facing downtime and request timeouts. Implementing safe, multi-phase migration patterns guarantees zero downtime.</p>

<h2>The Expand-and-Contract Migration Pattern</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Expand:</strong> Add the new column or table alongside the old one without removing the old column.</li>
  <li><strong>Dual-Write:</strong> Update application code to write to both the old and new columns simultaneously.</li>
  <li><strong>Backfill:</strong> Run a background worker to copy historical data in small batches.</li>
  <li><strong>Read Switch:</strong> Update application queries to read exclusively from the new column.</li>
  <li><strong>Contract:</strong> Deprecate and drop the old column once all systems are verified.</li>
</ol>', NULL, 'backend', '{"database","migrations","postgresql","devops","backend"}', 'a0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380a11', TRUE, TRUE, 12, 894, 48, 0, '2026-08-22T06:43:48.846Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:46.866Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
