A childhood sibling rivalry escalates into one unforgettable prank involving a bottle of 7up and it is not fiction.', '<p>Disclaimer: don’t try this at home, we were unserious children 😭<br><br>My younger siblingcan beg. I mean, beg beg. Most especially, my younger brother. He can beg for your eyessss.<br><br>When we were much younger, my brother begged for everything even if we were both given the same thing. He’d make sure to finish his own first, then watch me until I started eating mine, before stretching his hand out and saying, “Come and give me.” If you refused, he’d start crying, and your mum would have to come out and say, “Give him now, don’t be stingy.” It always made me so annoyed and frustrated. Eventually, I’d have to give him.<br><br>I had had it up to hereeeee. So one day, I decided enough was enough.<br><br>I bought a bottle of 7up — the plastic bottled one. My brother bought his own, Fanta. As usual, he didn’t even wait for food before bubbling his all up. I drank a little out of mine and kept it in the fridge after eating, with a serious warning that no one should touch my 7up.<br><br>Tell me why I went back there later and the quantity I left was no longer the same. Someone had taken my 7up🥹. I was livid. I knew it was no other person but my brother.<br><br>“Tobi,” “Tobiiiiii,” I called my brother.<br><br>When he came, I asked, “Did you drink my 7up?”<br><br>He said, “Ahnnn, why will I do that? Someone drank your 7up?” Feigning ignorance too. Mehnnnn, smoke was coming out of my nose from all the anger.😡 <br><br>I poured my remaining 7up somewhere else and devised a plan. I went inside the kitchen, poured kerosene into the 7up bottle, and cleaned the outside. Then I went back to where I’d kept my remaining drink, poured a little of it into the kerosene-filled bottle, and coated the mouth with 7up too, so it wouldn’t give off any smell.<br><br>I dropped it on the table and called my brother to get me snacks — acting like I wanted to eat the snacks with the drink. As usual, he first grumbled. Then he agreed, on the condition that I’d give him some of it. I said no problem.<br><br>When he got back, I gave him a little of the snacks, then specifically said, “The drink is already small, so make sure you drink small out of it.”<br><br>He collected it, unsuspecting of anything, and as the greedy person that he is, instead of sipping it small small, he gobbled a big chunk of the 7up mixed with kerosene.<br><br>He screameddddd and dropped the whole drink, then started crying. </p><p><em>Oh, the joy that gave me. Is this how criminals are birthed?🌚</em><br><br>The noise attracted my parents, who came to the scene almost immediately, asking what happened. None of us could explain, but my brother was pointing at the drink while fainting.<br><br><em>Ahhhh, fainting ke? Modaran. This was not part of the plan nowwww.</em><br><br>My mum sniffed the drink and immediately knew what had happened. By this time, people were already gathering and offering solutions. They quickly gave him palm oil which was apparently some kind of antidote. I think it worked, because he opened his eyes a few minutes later. I was legit scared I had killed my brother.<br><br>After everything had died down, I was given the beating of my life, but my brother also learned the lesson of his life. So, win-win, yeah?🌚😌<br><br><em>Oh, and this is not fiction, btw.</em><br><br></p><p></p><p></p><p></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788427481/echo-gist/a16fce57-7b1d-41de-934e-3b6d207eac01/vky5uda0hojzdvqj1s66.jpg', 'lifestyle', '{"storytime","siblings","funnystories","childhood","lifestyle"}', 'a16fce57-7b1d-41de-934e-3b6d207eac01', TRUE, FALSE, 3, 44, 9, 7, '2026-09-03T09:33:08.737Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:48.064Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
Tragic Fire Engulfs Calabar SPAR Mall as Traders Watch Fortunes Go Up in Flames', 'breaking-news-tragic-fire-engulfs-calabar-spar-mall-as-traders-watch-fortunes-go-pk61', 'In a heartbreaking incident that has left the city of Calabar in shock, the popular SPAR Mall (also referred to as Calabar Mall) was engulfed by a devastating f…', '<p></p><p>In a heartbreaking incident that has left the city of Calabar in shock, the popular SPAR Mall (also referred to as Calabar Mall) was engulfed by a devastating fire in the early hours of Saturday, August 22, 2026. </p><p>The blaze reportedly started inside a section of the shopping complex that houses several businesses, including a pharmacy, clothing store, perfumery, and phone shop. The mall, widely regarded as the main (and for many, the only major) modern shopping destination in Calabar, Cross River State, also anchors the multinational SPAR supermarket chain alongside other outlets. What began in one wing quickly spread from shop to shop, overwhelming the mall’s internal fire hydrant system and leaving firefighters struggling to contain the flames.</p><p>Eyewitness accounts and circulating videos paint a painful picture,  distressed shop owners and traders stood outside, some weeping uncontrollably, as their hard earned goods worth millions of naira were reduced to ashes. </p><p> Despite the Calabar Fire Service being reportedly just a short drive described by some as a mere three minutes from the mall, initial reports indicated that only one fire service truck was on the scene in the early stages, battling an already spreading blaze. Subsequent footage showed additional firefighters and police personnel arriving to manage the crowd, restore order, and prevent attempted looting by some bystanders. Law enforcement agents worked to clear the area as the flames continued their destructive path.</p><p>As of the latest updates, the exact cause of the fire remains unknown, and no official confirmation of casualties has been issued by the Cross River State Government or emergency authorities. The full extent of structural damage and total financial losses is still being assessed, but the human cost is already clear, livelihoods destroyed, years of investment wiped out in hours, and a community left questioning the state of emergency preparedness.</p><p>This tragedy has reignited longstanding concerns about infrastructure and emergency response capacity in Calabar and across Nigeria. In a well functioning system with adequate equipment, rapid deployment, functional hydrants, and sufficient fire trucks, such a blaze so close to a fire station might have been contained far more quickly, limiting the scale of destruction. Instead, traders are left wailing over irreplaceable losses while the city watches one of its key commercial hubs burn.</p><p>Residents, shop owners, and observers continue to call for thorough investigations into the cause, stronger investment in firefighting resources, and better preventive measures in commercial facilities. For now, the focus remains on bringing the fire fully under control and supporting those whose businesses and dreams have been reduced to smoke and rubble.</p><p>The people of Calabar deserve better. This avoidable scale of loss serves as a painful reminder of the urgent need for improved public safety systems.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787403342/echo-gist/40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1/ijbmisxgvv7knetneswi.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 12, 1, 0, '2026-08-22T13:00:49.519Z', '2026-09-28T13:27:41.296Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
After 20 Years.', 'lionel-messi-retires-from-argentina-national-team-after-20-years--zyfh', 'Lionel Messi has closed one of the most extraordinary chapters in football history. Twenty years after first pulling on the Argentina jersey, the 39 year old an…', '<p>Lionel Messi has closed one of the most extraordinary chapters in football history. Twenty years after first pulling on the Argentina jersey, the 39 year old announced on Monday that he is retiring from international duty, following the emotional heartbreak of losing this summer''s World Cup final to Spain and, more recently, the passing of his father, Jorge. In a handwritten note shared to Instagram, Messi revealed he had first written the words on July 21st, just two days after that final but it was grief and reflection in the weeks since that made him certain the time had finally come to say goodbye.</p><p>What Messi leaves behind is staggering, 125 goals in over 200 caps, a World Cup title in 2022, and Copa América glory in both 2021 and 2024. But beyond the numbers lies the story of a boy from Rosario who carried a nation''s hopes through years of heartbreak before finally delivering. He didn''t just win trophies for Argentina, he healed a complicated relationship with a country that once doubted him and, in the end, adored him completely.</p><p>The tributes have poured in from teammates, CONMEBOL, FIFA, and fans across the globe, with the phrase "Gracias eternas, Leo"  eternal thanks, Leo echoing across social media as a nation says farewell to its favorite son. Messi won''t disappear from football entirely, he''ll continue turning out for Inter Miami, and he''s already promised to watch future tournaments from the stands, cheering for the very team he once carried on his back. It''s a bittersweet ending, but also a fitting one a champion stepping aside not in defeat, but in peace, leaving the door open for the next generation to carry the torch he lit two decades ago.</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788200426/echo-gist/40e9bddb-450e-4128-88bb-1165336bdbf1/zk92ioxw142zm2d47noa.jpg', 'technology', '{}', 'c0de57af-f011-0e5a-ff55-c0de57aff555', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 10, 2, 0, '2026-08-31T18:21:01.524Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:00.031Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
A lot of you would’ve been in that mob throwing stones. 

 In some companies, calling your boss "Sir" is what will get you FIRED. 
Yes. First name basis is the culture and the training. 

Respect isn’t in “Sir” or “Ma”. Respect is in how you work, how you deliver, and how you treat people. 

In Nigeria we judge by titles and in the  corporate world, we judge by results and by culture. 
But here is the balance- Context matters and there''s always need to read the room! Showing a little courtesy will earn one more than it would cost one. It wouldn''t have cost the lady in question a dime to add a little courtesy in addressing the CEO.

Sometimes, all we just need is to say the right things at the right time. 
A contact list came from this angle and I decided to share.


©️Penque', '', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787335195/echo-gist/7d2ad71d-e26d-4985-9fbe-8b4c1570f087/ts0swdbzmjbg7tvexjqr.jpg', 'technology', '{}', '7d2ad71d-e26d-4985-9fbe-8b4c1570f087', FALSE, FALSE, 1, 14, 1, 0, '2026-08-21T18:03:05.012Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:47.822Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
It''s the answer that ends the conversation, not the one that''s true.', '<p>Most times you just want to end the conversation and avoid saying the truth.You have said it in the group chat. You have said it to your mother when she asked why your voice sounds different. You have said it to yourself, lying awake at 1 a.m. doing math you didn''t ask your brain to do, your rent, that email you haven''t replied to, why your chest feels tight for no reason. I''m just stressed. It''s the most dangerous sentence in your vocabulary, because it''s the one that lets you keep going without ever asking what''s actually happening to you.</p><p>Here''s the truth nobody tells you, stress has a job. It''s supposed to spike, help you handle the thing in front of you, then leave. What you''re describing, the version that doesn''t leave, that''s been living in your shoulders for eight months, is not stress doing its job anymore. It''s your body sending a different message entirely, and you''ve been translating it wrong.</p><p>THE SIGNS YOUR BODY SENDS BEFORE YOU "BURN OUT"</p><p>Burnout doesn''t announce itself. It arrives disguised as small, forgivable things. You put the milk in the cupboard. You reread the same paragraph four times and retain none of it. You say "I''m fine" to your partner in a tone that makes them ask if you''re actually fine. None of these feel like a crisis on their own, that''s exactly why they are dangerous. You are not falling apart in one dramatic moment; you''re leaking, quietly, one forgotten name and one skipped meal at a time.</p><p>AM I THE ONLY ONE WHO FEELS LIKE THIS?</p><p>You''re not, and that''s not a comforting cliché, it''s the part nobody says out loud because everyone''s too busy performing "handling it." Your colleague who always seems composed has cried in her car in the office parking lot. </p><p>That friend who posts the productive morning routine has also lain on his bathroom floor at 11 p.m. because getting up felt like too much. Normal stress doesn''t make you Google "why do I feel like crying for no reason" at your desk. If you''re here, reading this, you already know which category you''re in.</p><p>IS IT NORMAL TO SNAP, FORGET, OR FEEL NUMB?</p><p>Occasionally, yes. Constantly, no, and the difference is duration, not intensity. It''s normal to snap at your partner once during a brutal week. It''s not normal when snapping becomes your default setting and you don''t recognize your own tone anymore. It''s normal to forget a name under pressure. It''s not normal to walk into a room and forget why, three times in one day. And feeling numb, not sad, not angry, just flat, like you''re watching your own life through glass is one of the quietest signs, because it doesn''t look like suffering. It looks like coping. It isn''t.</p><p>HOW TO TELL THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN STRESS AND SOMETHING DEEPER </p><p>Ask yourself one question: does it end? Real stress has an off switch, the deadline passes, the exam ends, and your body comes back down. What you might be dealing with doesn''t come back down. You finish the project and you''re still tight in the chest. You get through the week and Monday still arrives with dread instead of relief. That persistence, not the intensity of any single bad day, is the actual signal. Stress is a reaction to something. What you''re describing has started reacting to nothing, on its own schedule, without your permission.</p><p>WHY "I''M JUST STRESSED" KEEPS YOU STUCK Here''s what nobody tells you, minimizing it is not resilience, it''s avoidance wearing resilience''s clothes. Every time you say "just stressed," you give yourself permission to skip the harder, more honest question, why has this been going on for so long, and why haven''t I told anyone the real number of weeks? Naming it accurately is not dramatic. It''s the only thing that actually gets you help instead of more coping mechanisms stacked on top of the original problem.</p><p>WHAT TO DO ONCE YOU NOTICE THE SHIFT You don''t need a full breakdown to justify getting support, that''s the trap. Waiting for it to get "bad enough" is how people end up in the parking-lot-crying stage before they say anything to anyone. The move is smaller and less dramatic than you think. Tell one person the real version, not the "I''m fine" version. Ask a doctor to check the physical side, sleep, hormones, thyroid because chronic stress mimics and masks other things. And stop measuring whether you deserve help by comparing your life to someone who "has it worse." You don''t need permission to take this seriously. You just need to stop calling it something smaller than it is.</p><p></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787229318/echo-gist/817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4/jxasmr6k5hwiylbgdiws.png', 'productivity', '{}', '817fe3a7-791d-4781-acd2-9654cee9aed4', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 32, 4, 0, '2026-08-20T12:42:57.175Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:50.911Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

