Here’s to the first daughters the unofficial second mothers, the protectors, the role models, and the ones who often carry more than they say. 🥂✨', '', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1787750508/echo-gist/58012914-4c47-4043-ac58-0c171dc635a4/nfehtuwwm0jzr6jtenbs.png', 'writing', '{}', '58012914-4c47-4043-ac58-0c171dc635a4', FALSE, FALSE, 1, 1, 1, 0, '2026-08-26T13:23:17.814Z', '2026-09-15T15:56:23.565Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

The adventure teaches children an important lesson about empathy, friendship, responsibility, and appreciating others. Kelechi learns that sometimes, we become so used to having someone or something around us that we forget to notice its value.

The biggest takeaway is simple: don''t wait until something leaves before you realize how much it means to you. Treat people with kindness, listen to them, and remember that everyone deserves to feel seen and appreciated.', '<p>The first time Kelechi''s shadow ran away, he thought it was a joke.</p><p>It happened on a Tuesday morning.</p><p>Kelechi was brushing his teeth when he noticed something strange.</p><p>He moved his toothbrush to the left.</p><p>His shadow moved to the right.</p><p>He stopped.</p><p>His shadow didn''t.</p><p>Kelechi slowly lowered his toothbrush.</p><p>His shadow raised its hand.</p><p>Kelechi''s mouth fell open.</p><p>The shadow waved.</p><p>“Stop that!” Kelechi whispered.</p><p>The shadow waved again.</p><p>Kelechi stepped backward.</p><p>The shadow stepped forward.</p><p>Then it turned around…</p><p>…and <strong>ran away.</strong></p><p>Kelechi screamed.</p><p>“MUMMY!”</p><p>His mother rushed into the bathroom.</p><p>“What happened?”</p><p>“My shadow!”</p><p>His mother looked at the floor.</p><p>There was no shadow.</p><p>She looked at Kelechi.</p><p>Then at the floor again.</p><p>“Kelechi, you need to stop watching cartoons before school.”</p><p>“But it ran away!”</p><p>His mother sighed.</p><p>“Go and wear your uniform.”</p><p>Kelechi stared at the empty floor.</p><p>“I knew it,” he whispered.</p><p>“My shadow has finally had enough of me.”</p><p>At school, everything became worse.</p><p>Kelechi tried standing in the sunlight.</p><p>Nothing.</p><p>He stood beside his friends.</p><p>Nothing.</p><p>He jumped.</p><p>Nothing.</p><p>He even danced.</p><p>Still nothing.</p><p>His friend Tobi stared at him.</p><p>“Bro.”</p><p>“Yes?”</p><p>“You look suspicious.”</p><p>“How?”</p><p>“You don''t have a shadow.”</p><p>Kelechi looked around.</p><p>“Exactly!”</p><p>Tobi''s eyes widened.</p><p>“So…”</p><p>“So what?”</p><p>“You''re a ghost.”</p><p>Kelechi nearly fell over.</p><p>“I''m NOT a ghost!”</p><p>Tobi stepped backward.</p><p>“That''s exactly what a ghost would say.”</p><p>By lunchtime, half the class had heard the story.</p><p>By break time, the whole school had heard it.</p><p>And by the end of the day, someone had started a rumor that Kelechi had become invisible.</p><p>Kelechi was furious.</p><p>“I am NOT invisible!”</p><p>His teacher looked at him.</p><p>“We can see you.”</p><p>“Exactly!”</p><p>She paused.</p><p>“But where is your shadow?”</p><p>Kelechi sighed.</p><p>“That is what I''m trying to find out.”</p><p>That evening, Kelechi followed a trail of strange little footprints behind his house.</p><p>They weren''t human footprints.</p><p>They weren''t animal footprints either.</p><p>They looked like…</p><p><strong>shadow footprints.</strong></p><p>They led through the garden.</p><p>Past the mango tree.</p><p>Behind the old water tank.</p><p>And straight into the abandoned storage room.</p><p>Kelechi pushed the door open.</p><p>It creaked.</p><p>Inside was darkness.</p><p>“Hello?”</p><p>Something moved.</p><p>Kelechi swallowed.</p><p>“Shadow?”</p><p>A tiny voice answered.</p><p>“Go away.”</p><p>Kelechi froze.</p><p>It was his shadow.</p><p>“What are you doing here?”</p><p>“I''m tired.”</p><p>“Tired of what?”</p><p>“Following you everywhere!”</p><p>Kelechi blinked.</p><p>“What?”</p><p>His shadow stepped out from behind a box.</p><p>It looked exactly like him, except completely black.</p><p>“You wake up. I wake up.”</p><p>“You eat. I stand behind you.”</p><p>“You run. I run.”</p><p>“You dance badly. I have to dance badly too.”</p><p>Kelechi frowned.</p><p>“I don''t dance badly.”</p><p>The shadow stared at him.</p><p>“You danced yesterday for twenty minutes while wearing one sock.”</p><p>Kelechi looked away.</p><p>“That was different.”</p><p>“No, it wasn''t.”</p><p>The shadow crossed its arms.</p><p>“I want my own life.”</p><p>Kelechi couldn''t believe what he was hearing.</p><p>“You''re my shadow!”</p><p>“I know.”</p><p>“You can''t just leave me!”</p><p>“Why not?”</p><p>“Because…”</p><p>Kelechi stopped.</p><p>He didn''t actually know.</p><p>The shadow smiled.</p><p>“Exactly.”</p><p>Then it walked away.</p><p>Kelechi followed.</p><p>“Wait!”</p><p>The shadow disappeared through a crack in the wall.</p><p>Kelechi squeezed through after it.</p><p>On the other side was something impossible.</p><p>A whole city.</p><p>A city made entirely of shadows.</p><p>Shadow houses.</p><p>Shadow trees.</p><p>Shadow cars.</p><p>Even shadow chickens.</p><p>And thousands of shadows walked around without people attached to them.</p><p>Kelechi stared.</p><p>“What is this place?”</p><p>His shadow smiled.</p><p>“Home.”</p><p>For the first time, Kelechi understood.</p><p>His shadow wasn''t broken.</p><p>It wasn''t lost.</p><p>It had simply been somewhere else all along.</p><p>He walked through the strange city.</p><p>Then he noticed something.</p><p>Some shadows were sitting alone.</p><p>Some were crying.</p><p>Some were hiding.</p><p>One tiny shadow sat beneath a tree, trembling.</p><p>Kelechi walked over.</p><p>“What''s wrong?”</p><p>The little shadow pointed toward the city.</p><p>“I can''t find my person.”</p><p>Kelechi frowned.</p><p>“Your person?”</p><p>The shadow nodded.</p><p>“She disappeared.”</p><p>Suddenly, Kelechi understood why his own shadow had run away.</p><p>Maybe shadows weren''t supposed to spend their whole lives simply following.</p><p>Maybe they needed to know they mattered too.</p><p>Kelechi turned to his shadow.</p><p>“You know what?”</p><p>“What?”</p><p>“I''m sorry.”</p><p>The shadow looked surprised.</p><p>“For what?”</p><p>“For never thinking about you.”</p><p>The shadow smiled.</p><p>Then something enormous appeared behind them.</p><p>A giant shadow towered over the city.</p><p>Kelechi grabbed his shadow.</p><p>“What is THAT?”</p><p>His shadow whispered:</p><p>“The Shadow Eater.”</p><p>The creature opened its enormous mouth.</p><p>And every shadow in the city screamed.</p><p>“RUN!”</p><p>Kelechi and his shadow raced through the streets.</p><p>The Shadow Eater chased them.</p><p>Faster.</p><p>Faster.</p><p>It swallowed houses.</p><p>It swallowed trees.</p><p>It swallowed shadows.</p><p>Kelechi suddenly stopped.</p><p>“I have an idea!”</p><p>His shadow stared at him.</p><p>“Is it a good one?”</p><p>“No!”</p><p>“Wonderful.”</p><p>Kelechi ran toward the creature.</p><p>The Shadow Eater opened its mouth.</p><p>At the last second, Kelechi jumped into the sunlight pouring through a crack in the ceiling.</p><p>The giant creature stopped.</p><p>It couldn''t move into the light.</p><p>Kelechi turned around.</p><p>“Everyone! Follow me!”</p><p>Hundreds of shadows rushed toward the light.</p><p>The Shadow Eater shrank.</p><p>Smaller.</p><p>Smaller.</p><p>Until it became nothing more than a tiny dark spot.</p><p>Then.....</p><p><strong>POP!</strong></p><p>It disappeared.</p><p>The shadow city cheered.</p><p>Kelechi''s shadow walked over.</p><p>“You saved everyone.”</p><p>Kelechi smiled.</p><p>“We saved everyone.”</p><p>His shadow raised an eyebrow.</p><p>“Does that mean I have to go back to following you?”</p><p>Kelechi laughed.</p><p>“No.”</p><p>The shadow smiled.</p><p>“Good.”</p><p>“But…”</p><p>Kelechi pointed toward the doorway leading back home.</p><p>“Can you at least come back sometimes?”</p><p>The shadow pretended to think.</p><p>“Maybe.”</p><p>The next morning, Kelechi stood outside his house.</p><p>The sun rose.</p><p>And there it was.</p><p>His shadow.</p><p>Right beside him.</p><p>Kelechi smiled.</p><p>“Welcome back.”</p><p>The shadow waved.</p><p>Then Kelechi noticed something strange.</p><p>His shadow was smiling.</p><p>But Kelechi wasn''t.</p><p>He stopped.</p><p>The shadow kept smiling.</p><p>“Kelechi…”</p><p>He slowly looked down.</p><p>His shadow whispered:</p><p><strong>“I think something followed us home.”</strong></p><p>Kelechi''s smile disappeared.</p><p>Behind him, something moved.</p><p>He turned.</p><p>Nothing.</p><p>He looked at the ground.</p><p>His shadow was gone.</p><p>Again.</p><p>Then a second shadow appeared beside his feet.</p><p>It wasn''t shaped like him.</p><p>It was shaped like something with <strong>three long arms.</strong></p><p>Kelechi screamed.</p><p>And somewhere behind the house…</p><p>something laughed.</p><p><strong>The End.</strong></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789638642/echo-gist/666033c2-c4c4-474e-aa8d-e03147d91ca7/h8rzgsrbecvw1mpmfkm2.jpg', 'comic', '{"kidsstories","childrenadventure","fantasystories","bedtimestories","magicalstories"}', '666033c2-c4c4-474e-aa8d-e03147d91ca7', TRUE, FALSE, 4, 3, 0, 0, '2026-09-17T09:53:16.737Z', '2026-10-04T10:46:33.406Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
Their mediocre post went viral.', 'your-best-post-flopped-their-mediocre-post-went-viral--1vfp', 'The war that doesn''t end, the war between algorithms and a dedicated writer.', '<p>Your best post flopped.</p><p>Their mediocre post went viral.</p><p>You blamed yourself.</p><p>You shouldn''t have.</p><p>There is a name for what happened to you.</p><p>And most writers will never know it exists.</p><p><strong>Diasimocracy vs Meritocracy.</strong></p><p>A war between dedicated writers and the algorithms.</p><p>(Adapted from Professor Douglas Giles''s article on Medium)</p><p>You ever seen a post with nothing to do with excellence or brilliance?</p><p>A total disorder.</p><p>Screaming complete lack of professionalism.</p><p>And yet, not only is it showing up on your feed, it is actually performing better than every brilliantly crafted piece around it.</p><p>Welcome to the world of Diasimocracy.</p><p>Still searching for what that means?</p><p>You can stop now — I got you as always. 😊</p><p>From the Greek word Diasimos — meaning "famous" or "celebrity" — Professor and Philosopher Douglas Giles coined the term Diasimocracy to define a particular modern landscape shaped heavily by social media algorithms.</p><p>A world where being a talented writer does not make you a successful one.</p><p>A world where you pour your soul into a masterpiece — and your post performs woefully.</p><p>A world where fame outranks quality.</p><p>Every single time.</p><p>Now let me ask you something.</p><p>Were you operating under the assumption that the best content always wins?</p><p>That excellence is eventually rewarded?</p><p>That if you write well enough, consistently enough, the platform will notice?</p><p>That is called Meritocracy.</p><p>And I hate to be the one to tell you this —</p><p>You have been playing the wrong game. 😅</p><p><strong>The Algorithmic Monsters</strong></p><p>Every platform has its own personalised, ever-evolving algorithm.</p><p>LinkedIn has its own.</p><p>Medium has its own.</p><p>Every platform that uses a recommender program has their own.</p><p>And these monsters are stone-headed and stone-hearted.</p><p>They have no affection for the brilliance in your writing.</p><p>No concern for the hours you spent crafting your sentences.</p><p>No appreciation for the research behind your words.</p><p>All they care about is engagement signals, posting frequency, follower count, and algorithmic familiarity.</p><p>In short, they care about who you are, not what you wrote.</p><p>But here is the good news.</p><p>The algorithm is not unbeatable.</p><p>It is dynamic — and so can you be.</p><p>I will be making another post very soon breaking down exactly how to navigate the algorithm, stay ahead of the battle, and make the monster work for you instead of against you.</p><p>Stay tuned. 👀</p><p>💬 Have you ever had a brilliant post flop while a mediocre one went viral? Tell me about it in the comments.</p><p>♻️ Repost — every writer in your network needs to see this today.</p><p>#ScientificWriting #ContentCreation #ScienceCommunication #Writing #Nigeria</p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788983230/echo-gist/822d6b89-834b-40bf-a86b-a9a08d95380a/dmkiyvvjnzysnghinbgw.png', 'technology', '{"writing","algorithms","machinelearning","scientificwriter"}', '822d6b89-834b-40bf-a86b-a9a08d95380a', TRUE, FALSE, 2, 27, 2, 0, '2026-09-09T19:49:20.789Z', '2026-09-15T15:55:14.555Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

The Girl Nobody Chose', '<p>Nora carried the tray of dirty glasses into the kitchen while candles on her stepsisters cake were still smoking. Twenty‑two of them.</p><p>Nora had counted without meaning to. It was Priya''s birthday. It was Nora''s birthday too. Nobody had mentioned it.</p><p>Nora had spent the morning polishing the silver for Priya''s party, the afternoon ironing the tablecloth Meera had chosen and the evening carrying trays to guests who told Priya how beautiful Nora looked and never once wondered who the girl serving them was.</p><p>Meera said from the sink not turning around "Those plates go in the cabinet, not the rack.. Wipe the counters before you go upstairs. I do not want the kitchen smelling like fish in the morning."</p><p>"Okay " Nora replied.</p><p>It was the answer.</p><p>In that house many words were considered disrespectful. Few words made people forget Nora was there.</p><p>Her father sat in the living room with a newspaper on his knees, his glasses pushed into his hair. He arrived at the party in twenty minutes. Long enough to shake hands with guests. Enough to tell Priya she looked beautiful. Long enough to smile when people complimented the daughter he had chosen to celebrate.</p><p>He had not looked toward the kitchen once.</p><p></p><p>He had not wished Nora a happy birthday.</p><p>Nora was not sure which hurt more. That his father had forgotten or that his father remembered and decided silence was easier.</p><p>For years Nora had tried to convince herself that her fathers silence meant his father was staying out of it.</p><p>That a man who never defended Nora was also a man who would never intentionally hurt her.</p><p>Nora had stopped believing that a long time ago.</p><p>Silence was still a choice.</p><p>Her fathers choice had always been the same.</p><p>Her father watched.</p><p>Her father knew.</p><p>Her father did nothing.</p><p>Nora was up the stairs when she heard Meera''s voice through the kitchen door.</p><p>The kind of voice people use when they believe a wall is doing more work than it actually is.</p><p>"She is eighteen now. Let her go her own way. We have done more than enough for her."</p><p>Nora stopped. She waited.</p><p>Not because she expected her father to defend her. Because some small, foolish part of her still hoped her father would</p><p>The house stayed quiet.</p><p>No argument.</p><p>No disagreement.</p><p>No voice saying, "She is my daughter."</p><p>Nothing.</p><p>Nora walked upstairs without making a sound.</p><p>That night Nora sat on the edge of her bed for hours.</p><p>The room at the end of the hallway had once been used for storage. It still felt that way sometimes. A place where things nobody cared about were kept.</p><p>Nora looked around at the space she had called home for years.</p><p>The old curtains.</p><p>The cracked mirror.</p><p>The tiny shelf where she kept the things that belonged only to her.</p><p>Nora thought about the girl she used to be.</p><p>The girl who sat in the front row at school with her hand raised before the teacher finished asking questions.</p><p>The girl who believed being talented mattered.</p><p>The girl who dreamed without apologizing for it.</p><p>Then life slowly taught her otherwise.</p><p>The school fees that somehow disappeared whenever it was her turn.</p><p>In the afternoon Nora was called out of class because her fees had not been paid and she never returned.</p><p>The books she wanted but could not have.</p><p>The opportunities given to Priya because she was "worth investing in."</p><p>Years of hearing that Nora would never become anything.</p><p>Until eventually Nora started saying it to herself.</p><p>Maybe they were right.</p><p>Maybe Nora was not special.</p><p>Maybe some people were simply born to stand behind others.</p><p>Noras eyes landed on the notebook hidden beneath her clothes.</p><p>The only thing in that room that still belonged to her.</p><p>The pages were worn. The corners were soft from years of being touched.</p><p>Inside were dreams she had written before she learned to be ashamed of wanting things.</p><p>A shop she wanted to open.</p><p>Places she wanted to visit.</p><p>A life she wanted to build.</p><p>Nora had not opened it in over a year.</p><p>Nora told herself it was because she had grown up.</p><p>But down, she knew the truth.</p><p>She was afraid. Afraid of wanting something she might never get. Afraid of hoping</p><p>That night she opened it.</p><p>For the first time in a long time, she allowed herself to imagine a different life. Before sunrise she packed a bag.</p><p>Nora did not own much. Two changes of clothes. Her mothers old coat that Meera had once thrown into a donation bag because it was "outdated."</p><p>Nora had quietly taken it back. Hidden it away. Her mothers ring, hanging from a string around Nora''s neck because it did not fit her finger yet.</p><p>The notebook. That was everything. Before leaving she stood in the doorway of her room. And looked back.</p><p>There were no memories attached to the space. No moments where Nora had felt truly loved. No reason to stay.</p><p>Nora had spent years waiting for someone in that house to choose her. Maybe it was time Nora chose herself.</p><p>Nora left while the sky was still dark. She did not write a note. She did not say goodbye.</p><p>Nora doubted anyone would notice she was gone until they needed something from her.</p><p>The bus station at five in the morning was cold and nearly empty. One overhead light flickered above Nora buzzing like it could not decide whether to stay on.</p><p>Nora sat on a bench with her bag pressed against her chest and her mothers ring held tightly in her palm.</p><p>"This is a beginning, " Nora told herself.</p><p>"Not an ending."</p><p>Nora did not believe it yet. she repeated it anyway. Because it was the one thing she still had control over.</p><p>Nora slept sitting up that night waking every time footsteps came close.</p><p>In the morning Nora walked until she found a small diner with a handwritten sign taped to the window.</p><p>HELP WANTED. CLEAN TABLES</p><p>Nora stood outside for five minutes. Fear told her to keep walking. Pride told her she deserved better. Hunger reminded her that survival came before pride.</p><p>So Nora walked inside. The woman behind the counter looked at her carefully.</p><p>"Looking for work?"</p><p>Nora nodded.</p><p>"I can work hard."</p><p>The woman studied Nora for a moment before handing her a piece of paper.</p><p>"Come back this afternoon."</p><p>It was not a miracle. It was not a rescue. It was a chance. Sometimes a chance is enough.</p><p>Nora''s first shift lasted six hours.</p><p>Nora cleaned tables, washed dishes, carried plates and smiled at customers who never learned Nora''s name.</p><p>The money was not much. Less than what Meera used to spend on flowers for the dining table.</p><p>When Nora held that first paycheck in her hand she felt something she had not felt in years.</p><p>Pride.</p><p>Because nobody had given it to her. Nobody could take credit for it. It was Noras.</p><p>At the end of the week Nora rented a room above a laundromat. The mattress smelled like someone''s life. The walls were thin. The room was nothing like the home Nora once dreamed of.</p><p>It was hers. With her salary she bought a notebook. A cheap one. Nothing fancy. The kind people walk past without noticing.</p><p>Nora sat on the edge of the mattress that night listening to the sound of washing machines below her. She thought about her fathers silence. Meera''s words. Nora''s former self.</p><p>Then she opened the notebook. Wrote one sentence.</p><p>"This is not where my story ends."</p><p>Nora pressed hard that the words marked the page underneath. She did not know what her  story would become.</p><p>Nora did not know how many  ttimes she would have to repeat those words when life tested her again.</p><p>Nora knew one thing. A person who writes down a dream has already made a decision. Somewhere beneath the fear. Somewhere beneath the pain. She has decided to try.</p><p><br><strong>Years Later</strong></p><p>The elevator doors opened onto the floor.</p><p>Meera stepped out wearing her coat already preparing the impatient tone she would use if anyone made her wait too long.</p><p>She was there for a business meeting. A connection someone had arranged.</p><p>A company that according to everyone was becoming impossible to ignore. She had not bothered asking who owned it. It did not seem important.</p><p><strong>Until now.</strong></p><p>The glass doors at the end of the hallway carried a name Meera did not recognize. Inside was an office overlooking the city.</p><p>Behind the desk sat a woman reading through documents with confidence. The kind of confidence that belonged to someone who no longer needed permission to take up space.</p><p>The woman looked up.</p><p>Meera stopped breathing.</p><p>Because Meera knew that face.</p><p>Meera knew those eyes.</p><p>The girl Meera had once told would never become anything was no longer standing in the corner of a kitchen. The girl was no longer carrying trays. The girl was no longer asking for permission.</p><p>The girl was sitting behind the desk. The name on the door belonged to Nora. The woman looked at Meera silently.</p><p>For the time, in many years Meera had no words at all. </p><p>Because the girl they had left behind, the girl they had counted out, was now the woman they would have to answer to.</p><p></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788983586/echo-gist/26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2/vhywv6zrmf2as34od0co.png', 'books', '{"storytime","emotionalstory","unnoticed","norasstory","triciasdiary"}', '26f2332d-e264-4fd9-846f-30c5452124f2', TRUE, FALSE, 7, 16, 2, 0, '2026-09-09T20:52:27.328Z', '2026-09-23T14:22:30.526Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

That morning, I stood by my balcony, watching the world wake up beneath me.

Children ran across the compound, chasing one another with careless joy. Their laughter floated up to me, loud and unrestrained. One little girl stumbled, scraped her knee, and, after a few seconds of tears, got back up and ran after the others as though nothing had happened.

I smiled.

There was something beautiful about being young enough to believe that every day would be kind to you.

I rested my arms against the railing and watched them for a little longer.

"Enjoy it," I whispered, though they could never hear me. "Enjoy every moment you can. One day, you''ll look back and wish you could have one more ordinary morning like this."

I turned away from the balcony and walked back into my room.

Then I saw it.

The photograph.

It sat quietly on the shelf, covered in a thin layer of dust. My fingers brushed across the glass, stopping over the faces I had spent years trying not to remember too often.

My family.

For a moment, the room disappeared.

There was the screech of tyres.

The violent crash.

Glass shattering.

Then silence.

I was fifteen when the accident took everything from me.

Fifteen years had passed since that day, yet sometimes, all it took was one photograph to make me feel like that little girl again, standing in the ruins of the life she once knew and wondering how she was supposed to keep breathing.

But somehow, I did.

I survived the accident.

I survived the empty house.

I survived the days when grief was the only thing that came home with me.

And when I thought I had finally survived the worst of it, my uncle took what remained of my family''s inheritance.

Everything.

The house. The money. The things my parents had worked their entire lives to leave behind.

I still remember standing outside with my belongings beside me, watching the door close.

That was the day I learned that losing everything did not always happen in a single accident.

Sometimes, people took the pieces that were left.

I walked towards the mirror.

For a few seconds, I simply stared at the woman looking back at me.

Then I laughed softly.

"This babe has really been through shit."

My smile faded.

I looked closer.

The woman in the mirror wasn''t the frightened girl from fifteen years ago.

She had scars she didn''t talk about.

Memories she carried quietly.

Dreams she had nearly abandoned.

Yet somehow, she was still here.

Still dreaming.

Still trying.

Still becoming.

I placed my palm against the mirror.

Maybe I had broken more times than I cared to remember.

Maybe there were moments when I thought I wouldn''t make it to the other side.

But I did.

And perhaps that was the part of my story I had been too busy surviving to notice.

I wasn''t standing in front of the mirror because I had finally become everything I wanted to be.

I was standing there because, after everything life had taken from me, I was still becoming her.

And for the first time, that was enough.

I smiled at my reflection.

"Look at you," I whispered.

Still here.

Still standing.

Still chasing the dream.

Fifteen years later, the girl who thought her story had ended was still writing it.', '', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1788734282/echo-gist/32ac00d9-97a3-48f3-9d37-d6e2b5977d82/zg7cjphbmorv3qek4pjz.jpg', 'lifestyle', '{"story","life","writing","writerscommunity","truelifestory"}', '32ac00d9-97a3-48f3-9d37-d6e2b5977d82', TRUE, FALSE, 1, 34, 5, 2, '2026-09-06T22:41:00.079Z', '2026-10-02T06:49:02.817Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

