Key takeaway: Sometimes, five ordinary minutes with someone we love can become a memory we treasure forever.', '<h2>The Girl Who Borrowed Five Minutes From Tomorrow</h2><p>Maya was always late.</p><p>Not <em>a little</em> late.</p><p><strong>Very late.</strong></p><p>Late for school.</p><p>Late for breakfast.</p><p>Late for church.</p><p>Late even when her mother told her, “Maya, we are leaving in five minutes,” and she replied, “Okay!” while still looking for the other half of her shoe.</p><p>One Tuesday morning, she was sitting on her bed, trying to finish a drawing.</p><p>It was a picture of her and her grandmother sitting under the old mango tree behind their house.</p><p>Maya had been drawing it for days.</p><p>She wanted to give it to Grandma before she left for her trip the next morning.</p><p>But the clock was moving too quickly.</p><p>7:41.</p><p>Her school bus came at 7:45.</p><p>Maya looked at the drawing.</p><p>One corner was still unfinished.</p><p>“Just five more minutes,” she whispered.</p><p>From the hallway, her mother shouted,</p><p>“Maya!”</p><p>“I''m coming!”</p><p>“Maya!”</p><p>“I said I''m coming!”</p><p>She looked at the clock again.</p><p>7:43.</p><p>Her heart sank.</p><p>“I just need five minutes.”</p><p>She squeezed her eyes shut.</p><p>“I wish I could borrow five minutes from tomorrow.”</p><p>The room went quiet.</p><p>Maya opened one eye.</p><p>Nothing.</p><p>She laughed at herself.</p><p>“See? Even my wishes are late.”</p><p>She picked up her pencil.</p><p>Then she heard a tiny <strong>tick</strong>.</p><p>Not from the wall clock.</p><p>From underneath her bed.</p><p>Maya slowly leaned down.</p><p>There, hidden in the darkness, was a small silver alarm clock.</p><p>She had never seen it before.</p><p>Its hands were spinning backward.</p><p>Then it stopped.</p><p>The clock displayed:</p><p><strong>TOMORROW — 7:43</strong></p><p>Maya stared at it.</p><p>“What?”</p><p>The little clock gave a soft <strong>ding</strong>.</p><p>A piece of paper popped out of its side.</p><p>Maya picked it up.</p><p>There were only five words written on it:</p><p><strong>YOU MAY HAVE FIVE MINUTES.</strong></p><p>Maya''s mouth dropped open.</p><p>“Seriously?”</p><p>The clock ticked once.</p><p>Maya grabbed her pencil.</p><p>And suddenly, everything around her stopped.</p><p>The fan froze.</p><p>The curtains stopped moving.</p><p>Even the sound of her mother''s voice disappeared.</p><p>Maya looked at the clock.</p><p>Five minutes.</p><p>She smiled.</p><p>She finished the drawing.</p><p>She added the mango tree.</p><p>Grandma''s earrings.</p><p>Her yellow dress.</p><p>And a tiny bird sitting on one of the branches.</p><p>Then she signed her name.</p><p><strong>MAYA.</strong></p><p>The moment she finished, everything started moving again.</p><p>The fan spun.</p><p>The curtains moved.</p><p>Her mother''s voice returned.</p><p>“MAYA! THE BUS IS HERE!”</p><p>Maya grabbed her bag and ran.</p><p>She made it onto the bus just in time.</p><p>She smiled all the way to school.</p><p>She had done it.</p><p>She had borrowed five minutes from tomorrow.</p><p>Easy.</p><p>Or so she thought.</p><p>The next morning, Maya woke up feeling strange.</p><p>She looked at the clock.</p><p>7:40.</p><p>She jumped out of bed.</p><p>“I''m early!”</p><p>She brushed her teeth.</p><p>She got dressed.</p><p>She even found both shoes without crying.</p><p>Then she remembered.</p><p>Today was the day Grandma was leaving.</p><p>Maya ran downstairs.</p><p>Grandma was sitting at the dining table, drinking tea.</p><p>Maya handed her the drawing.</p><p>Grandma looked at it.</p><p>For a moment, she didn''t say anything.</p><p>Then her eyes became shiny.</p><p>“Oh, Maya.”</p><p>She pulled Maya into a hug.</p><p>“This is beautiful.”</p><p>Maya smiled.</p><p>“I made it for you.”</p><p>Grandma kissed her forehead.</p><p>“I''ll keep it forever.”</p><p>Maya hugged her tighter.</p><p>Then she noticed something strange.</p><p>Grandma was staring at the clock.</p><p>Her smile disappeared.</p><p>“What is it?” Maya asked.</p><p>Grandma pointed at the wall clock.</p><p>7:43.</p><p>Maya''s stomach dropped.</p><p>“What?”</p><p>Grandma whispered,</p><p>“It''s 7:43.”</p><p>“So?”</p><p>Grandma looked at her.</p><p>“I''ve been seeing that time everywhere this morning.”</p><p>Maya said nothing.</p><p>Grandma stood up.</p><p>She walked toward the window.</p><p>Then she suddenly stopped.</p><p>“Maya…”</p><p>“Yes?”</p><p>“Did you ask tomorrow for something?”</p><p>Maya''s heart began beating faster.</p><p>“How do you know?”</p><p>Grandma turned around.</p><p>Because she wasn''t smiling anymore.</p><p>“Because,” she said quietly, “tomorrow doesn''t like lending things.”</p><p>Before Maya could answer, the lights went out.</p><p>The house became silent.</p><p>Then the little silver clock under Maya''s bed began ringing.</p><p><strong>DING.</strong></p><p><strong>DING.</strong></p><p><strong>DING.</strong></p><p>Maya ran upstairs.</p><p>She pulled the clock out.</p><p>The screen had changed.</p><p>It no longer said:</p><p><strong>TOMORROW — 7:43</strong></p><p>It said:</p><p><strong>DEBT DUE.</strong></p><p>Maya''s fingers went cold.</p><p>“What debt?”</p><p>A tiny piece of paper slipped out.</p><p>She opened it.</p><p>This time, there were six words.</p><p><strong>You borrowed five minutes. Someone must lose them.</strong></p><p>Maya stared at the message.</p><p>“No.”</p><p>The clock ticked.</p><p>“No, no, no.”</p><p>She grabbed it.</p><p>“Give them back!”</p><p>Nothing happened.</p><p>She shook it.</p><p>“Give them back!”</p><p>Suddenly, the clock began glowing.</p><p>A bright light filled the room.</p><p>And Maya heard a voice.</p><p>Not her mother''s.</p><p>Not Grandma''s.</p><p>A voice that sounded like hundreds of clocks ticking at once.</p><p><strong>“Five minutes cannot disappear.”</strong></p><p>Maya whispered,</p><p>“Then where did they go?”</p><p>The voice answered:</p><p><strong>“Tomorrow.”</strong></p><p>That night, Maya couldn''t sleep.</p><p>She kept thinking about the message.</p><p><em>Someone must lose them.</em></p><p>At exactly midnight, she heard footsteps in the hallway.</p><p>She opened her bedroom door.</p><p>Grandma was standing there.</p><p>But something was wrong.</p><p>Grandma looked confused.</p><p>“Maya?”</p><p>“Yes?”</p><p>“What day is it?”</p><p>“Wednesday.”</p><p>Grandma frowned.</p><p>“No.”</p><p>She looked at her hands.</p><p>“I was supposed to leave yesterday.”</p><p>Maya''s heart stopped.</p><p>“What?”</p><p>Grandma looked at her.</p><p>“I already said goodbye to you.”</p><p>Maya shook her head.</p><p>“No, Grandma. You haven''t left yet.”</p><p>Grandma stared at her.</p><p>Then she smiled sadly.</p><p>“I think I know what happened.”</p><p>Maya''s eyes filled with tears.</p><p>“What?”</p><p>“You borrowed five minutes from tomorrow.”</p><p>Grandma touched her cheek.</p><p>“And now tomorrow is missing five minutes.”</p><p>Maya swallowed.</p><p>“Can we give them back?”</p><p>Grandma nodded.</p><p>“But you have to find them.”</p><p>“Where?”</p><p>Grandma pointed toward the window.</p><p>“Where else would lost time go?”</p><p>Outside, the moon was shining.</p><p>And somewhere beyond the clouds, a clock was ticking.</p><p>Maya climbed onto the roof.</p><p>She had no idea how she got there.</p><p>She only knew she had to fix what she had done.</p><p>Above her was a sky filled with stars.</p><p>Then she saw it.</p><p>A tiny golden door floating in the air.</p><p>Maya reached for it.</p><p>The door opened.</p><p>Behind it was tomorrow.</p><p>Not a place.</p><p>Not a day.</p><p>A strange, endless hallway filled with clocks.</p><p>Hundreds of them.</p><p>Thousands.</p><p>Some were moving forward.</p><p>Some were moving backward.</p><p>Some had no hands at all.</p><p>At the end of the hallway stood a little boy.</p><p>He looked about Maya''s age.</p><p>He was holding a broken clock.</p><p>“You took my five minutes,” he said.</p><p>Maya''s eyes widened.</p><p>“I didn''t know.”</p><p>“I know.”</p><p>“I''m sorry.”</p><p>The boy looked at her.</p><p>“I waited for you.”</p><p>“For me?”</p><p>He nodded.</p><p>“I needed those five minutes to say goodbye to someone.”</p><p>Maya''s heart sank.</p><p>“Who?”</p><p>“My grandmother.”</p><p>Maya looked at the floor.</p><p>“I''m really sorry.”</p><p>The boy smiled a little.</p><p>“Then give them back.”</p><p>Maya held out her hands.</p><p>“I don''t have them.”</p><p>The boy pointed at her.</p><p>“You do.”</p><p>Maya looked down.</p><p>The drawing.</p><p>She was still holding it.</p><p>The picture of her and Grandma under the mango tree.</p><p>The boy smiled.</p><p>“You used the five minutes to make that.”</p><p>Maya understood.</p><p>The five minutes weren''t inside the clock.</p><p>They were inside the memory she had created.</p><p>She held the drawing out.</p><p>The little boy touched it.</p><p>The picture began to glow.</p><p>Five tiny golden sparks floated out of the paper.</p><p>One.</p><p>Two.</p><p>Three.</p><p>Four.</p><p>Five.</p><p>They flew into the hallway.</p><p>Every clock stopped.</p><p>Then....</p><p><strong>TICK.</strong></p><p>Everything started again.</p><p>The boy smiled.</p><p>“Thank you.”</p><p>Maya smiled back.</p><p>Then the golden door began closing.</p><p>“Wait!” Maya shouted.</p><p>The boy waved.</p><p>“Don''t borrow time again.”</p><p>Maya laughed.</p><p>“I won''t.”</p><p>The door disappeared.</p><p>The next morning, Grandma left for her trip.</p><p>Maya hugged her tightly.</p><p>“Come back soon.”</p><p>“I will.”</p><p>Grandma kissed her forehead.</p><p>“And Maya?”</p><p>“Yes?”</p><p>“Thank you for the picture.”</p><p>Maya smiled.</p><p>This time, she wasn''t thinking about five extra minutes.</p><p>She was thinking about the five minutes she already had.</p><p>Because sometimes, we spend so much time wishing for more that we forget to love the little time sitting right in front of us.</p><p>Maya watched Grandma''s car disappear down the road.</p><p>Then she looked at the clock.</p><p>7:43.</p><p>She smiled.</p><p>And for once…</p><p><strong>she wasn''t late.</strong></p><p><strong>The End.</strong></p>', 'https://res.cloudinary.com/db14mxnqt/image/upload/v1789640353/echo-gist/666033c2-c4c4-474e-aa8d-e03147d91ca7/npjgter7x1udaad5dfib.jpg', 'comic', '{"lifelessions","bedtimestories","timetravel","magicalstories","kidstories"}', '666033c2-c4c4-474e-aa8d-e03147d91ca7', TRUE, FALSE, 5, 1, 0, 0, '2026-09-17T10:22:00.608Z', '2026-09-17T10:22:16.464Z') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

